window.STUDY_SEARCH={"courses":[{"slug":"classical-myth","name":"Classical Myth in Art","home":"/study/art-history/classical-myth.html","img":"/study/art-history/classical-myth/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"color-theory","name":"Color Theory","home":"/study/color-theory.html","img":"/study/color-theory/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"composition","name":"Composition","home":"/study/composition.html","img":"/study/composition/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"fauvism-and-expressionism","name":"Fauvism and Early Expressionism","home":"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"impressionism","name":"Impressionism","home":"/study/art-history/impressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/impressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"japanese-woodblock-prints","name":"Japanese Woodblock Prints","home":"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints.html","img":"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"oil-vs-acrylic","name":"Oil vs Acrylic","home":"/study/oil-vs-acrylic.html","img":"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"pigments-and-materials","name":"Pigments and Materials","home":"/study/pigments-and-materials.html","img":"/study/pigments-and-materials/img/","lane":"Technique"},{"slug":"post-impressionism","name":"Post-Impressionism","home":"/study/art-history/post-impressionism.html","img":"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"realism","name":"Realism","home":"/study/art-history/realism.html","img":"/study/art-history/realism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"romanticism","name":"Romanticism","home":"/study/art-history/romanticism.html","img":"/study/art-history/romanticism/img/","lane":"Art history"},{"slug":"value-and-light","name":"Value and Light","home":"/study/value-and-light.html","img":"/study/value-and-light/img/","lane":"Technique"}],"items":[[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-gods-of-olympus.html","The Gods of Olympus · Overview","The Gods of Olympus","","h1_aphrodite","Who the gods are, and how artists made each one recognizable · The Greeks pictured their gods as a family of twelve living on Mount Olympus, and the Romans took them over under Latin names. Because the gods look human, artists marked each one with a fixed set of objects, animals and poses, so a viewer could name the god at a glance. · Ancient statues set the standard types that later painters copied. · Renaissance and Baroque artists revived the gods for princely palaces and collections. · Learn the attributes here and the rest of the guide becomes easier to read. · Attribute · An object, animal or feature that identifies a figure, such as Zeus’s thunderbolt or Athena’s owl and aegis. · Sandro Botticelli · The Birth of Venus · c. 1485"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/jupiter-of-smyrna.html","The Gods of Olympus","Jupiter of Smyrna","Roman, sculptor unknown","h1_zeus","Roman, sculptor unknown · 2nd century CE · Marble, 270 cm high · Louvre, Paris · An over-life-size statue, carved in the province of Asia and possibly copied from a Greek original about three centuries older. Much of what you see was rebuilt for Louis XIV. · Recorded at Smyrna (modern İzmir) in 1680, owned by a Dutch merchant, and bought for Louis XIV. · Pierre Granier restored it in 1686: the shoulder, raised arm and thunderbolt, nose, feet and base are his. · Stood on the Allée Royale at Versailles from 1687; moved to the museum after the Revolution. · Look for the deep drill work in the hair and beard, typical of 2nd-century Roman carving. · Since the thunderbolt is modern, the museum also lists Asclepius, god of healing, as a possible identity. · Himation · A large rectangular cloak worn by Greek men. · Zeus (Roman Jupiter) is king of the gods and god of sky and thunder, who took power by overthrowing his father Cronus. Sculptors showed him as a mature, bearded man, bare-chested, with scepter and thunderbolt."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/juno-and-argus.html","The Gods of Olympus","Juno and Argus","Peter Paul Rubens","h1_hera","Peter Paul Rubens · c. 1610 · Oil on canvas, 249 × 296 cm · Wallraf-Richartz-Museum, Cologne · Painted soon after the artist came home to Antwerp from eight years in Italy, the picture is a showcase for the Venetian colour he had just studied. · Juno lifts the eyes from the headless body of Argus, helped by Iris, goddess of the rainbow, and a crowd of putti. · A rainbow runs through the centre; the museum notes the picture is built on blue, yellow and red. · Its design echoes the title page he drew for a 1613 book on optics. · Given to the museum in 1894 by a group of Cologne art lovers; the original patron is not known. · Hera (Roman Juno) is queen of the gods and goddess of marriage. She set hundred-eyed Argus to guard Io, one of Zeus’s loves; when Hermes killed him, she placed his eyes on her sacred bird, the peacock."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/bronze-god-from-cape-artemision.html","The Gods of Olympus","Bronze God from Cape Artemision","Greek, sculptor unknown","h1_poseidon","Greek, sculptor unknown · c. 460 BCE · Bronze, 209 cm high · National Archaeological Museum, Athens · One of the very few full-size Greek bronzes to survive, raised from the sea off northern Euboea. Most ancient bronzes were melted down; this one lasted because it sank. · Fishermen netted an arm around 1926; the nearly complete statue was brought up in September 1928. · The museum groups it with bronzes lost when Roman ships carrying art from Greece were wrecked. · Look at the arms: stretched to balance a throw, with a span (210 cm) slightly wider than the figure is tall. · The weapon in the right hand is lost, so it may be Zeus with a thunderbolt or Poseidon with a trident. · Poseidon (Roman Neptune), brother of Zeus, rules the sea, earthquakes and horses."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/varvakeion-athena.html","The Gods of Olympus","Varvakeion Athena","Roman, after Pheidias","h6_varvakeion","Roman, after Pheidias · 200–250 CE · Marble with traces of paint, about 1 m high · National Archaeological Museum, Athens · A small copy of the most famous statue in Athens, the gold-and-ivory Athena Parthenos, which stood about 12 m high inside the Parthenon. The original is lost, so copies like this are the best evidence of how it looked. · Found in Athens near the Varvakeion, in the ruins of a Roman house. · Traces of paint stood in for the gold and ivory of the original. · Its details match the ancient description by Pausanias: Victory in one hand, a shield at her feet and a serpent beside it. · Cambridge’s cast collection describes such statuettes as souvenirs of the famous original. · Athena (Roman Minerva) is goddess of wisdom, crafts and strategic war, born fully armed from Zeus’s head. Athens, her city, built the Parthenon in her honour."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/minerva-expelling-the-vices-from-the-garden-of-virtue.html","The Gods of Olympus","Minerva Expelling the Vices from the Garden of Virtue","Andrea Mantegna","h1_athena","Andrea Mantegna · completed 1502 · Tempera on canvas, 160 × 192 cm · Louvre, Paris · Made for Isabella d’Este’s studiolo in Mantua, a small private study she filled with paintings on moral themes. · One of five canvases for the room; it hung opposite his Parnassus. · Look for the Vices in flight, among them armless Sloth and Ignorance carried by Ingratitude. · Hidden images: a tree in human form and a face in a cloud. · Inscriptions in several alphabets label the figures. · Later owned by Cardinal Richelieu; entered the French state collections in 1801. · Minerva was a natural champion of virtue over vice. Here the myth is used as a moral lesson rather than a story."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/apollo-belvedere.html","The Gods of Olympus","Apollo Belvedere","Roman, after a Greek bronze","h1_apollo","Roman, after a Greek bronze · early 2nd century CE · Marble · Vatican Museums, Vatican City · The original dates from about 330 BCE and was probably by Leochares. Found in Rome in 1489, it became for three centuries the model of ideal male beauty. · Cardinal Giuliano della Rovere owned it and, as Pope Julius II, set it in the Vatican’s Belvedere court by 1508. · Montorsoli rebuilt the missing arm and hand in 1532–33; the left hand is now a cast of an ancient fragment found at Baia. · Look at the pose: the god has just loosed an arrow; the bow in his left hand is lost. · Taken to Paris under Napoleon and returned in 1816. · Chlamys · A short cloak pinned at the shoulder, here hanging from Apollo’s arm. · Apollo, son of Zeus, is god of light, music, prophecy and archery. He is always young and beardless, often with bow, lyre or laurel."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/diana-and-her-nymphs.html","The Gods of Olympus","Diana and Her Nymphs","Johannes Vermeer","h1_artemis","Johannes Vermeer · c. 1653–54 · Oil on canvas, 97.8 × 104.6 cm · Mauritshuis, The Hague · One of the artist’s earliest paintings, made just after he joined the Delft painters’ guild and years before his quiet interiors. · Long sold as a work by Nicolaes Maes; in 1885 the “N.M.” monogram proved a forgery over Vermeer’s signature. · Look for the small crescent moon on Diana’s head and a nymph washing her feet. · A later blue sky was covered in 1999–2000 to bring back the night setting. · The canvas was cut down on the right, cropping the kneeling figure at the edge. · Artemis (Roman Diana), Apollo’s twin, is goddess of the hunt, wild places and the moon, and a virgin protector of young women."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/mars.html","The Gods of Olympus","Mars","Diego Velázquez","h1_ares","Diego Velázquez · c. 1638 · Oil on canvas, 179 × 95 cm · Museo del Prado, Madrid · The painter turns the god of war into a weary man sitting on a bed, naked except for his helmet. The painting was recorded in Philip IV’s hunting lodge, the Torre de la Parada. · Recorded there in 1701–03, beside his Aesop and Menippus of similar size. · Look for armour, shield and sword dropped at the foot of the bed and the baton resting on the floor. · The museum traces the pose to the ancient Ludovisi Ares and to Michelangelo’s Pensieroso. · Loose brushwork builds the body with soft outlines. · Ares (Roman Mars) is the god of war, admired by the Romans more than the Greeks."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-birth-of-venus.html","The Gods of Olympus","The Birth of Venus","Sandro Botticelli","h1_aphrodite","Sandro Botticelli · c. 1485 · Tempera on canvas, 172.5 × 278.5 cm · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · One of the first large paintings of a pagan goddess since antiquity, very probably made for a member of the Medici family. It is painted on canvas, a support then used for decoration in great houses. · First recorded in 1550 by Vasari at the Medici villa of Castello. · Venus’s pose comes from ancient statues; gold highlights run through her hair. · The two winds at left are modelled on an ancient gem owned by Lorenzo the Magnificent. · Orange trees, a Medici emblem, and roses blown on the wind. · The cloaked woman is sometimes identified as the Hora of spring or one of the Graces. · Aphrodite (Roman Venus) is the goddess of love and beauty. In Hesiod she rises full-grown from the sea foam and comes ashore on Cyprus."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/vulcans-forge.html","The Gods of Olympus","Vulcan’s Forge","Diego Velázquez","h1_hephaestus","Diego Velázquez · 1630 · Oil on canvas, 223 × 290 cm · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Painted in Rome during the painter’s first Italian trip, without a commission, then sold by the artist to Philip IV in 1634. · Apollo, in orange with a laurel wreath and a halo of light, tells Vulcan that his wife Venus is Mars’s lover. · The armour on the anvil is being made for Mars. · Look at whole bodies freezing mid-movement, not only faces, to show the shock. · His first large work on a lead-white ground, giving it a lighter tone. · Hephaestus (Roman Vulcan) is the lame smith god, maker of the gods’ weapons. Apollo is the god who sees everything."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/hermes-and-the-infant-dionysus.html","The Gods of Olympus","Hermes and the Infant Dionysus","Attributed to Praxiteles","h1_hermes","Attributed to Praxiteles · 4th century BCE or later (disputed) · Parian marble, about 2.1 m high · Archaeological Museum of Olympia · Found in 1877 in the Temple of Hera at Olympia, where the ancient travel writer Pausanias recorded a Hermes by Praxiteles. Whether this is that original, a later work or a copy is still debated. · Excavated in May 1877; the right foot turned up in later digs. · Pausanias is the only ancient source to mention it. · The baby sits on his left arm; the lost right hand probably dangled grapes. · Proposed dates run from the mid-4th century BCE to about 100 BCE. · Hermes (Roman Mercury) is the messenger god and guide of travellers. Zeus gave him the infant Dionysus to carry to safety, away from Hera’s jealousy."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/titans-giants-and-the-first-mortals.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals · Overview","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","","h3_phaeton","Wars in heaven, and the making and punishing of humankind · Greek creation stories begin with violence: the Titans overthrow the first gods, and Zeus and the Olympians overthrow the Titans and then defeat the Giants. Mortals arrive in the middle of this struggle, made by Prometheus and burdened by Pandora. · Battles of gods against monsters gave artists a licence for twisting, colossal bodies. · Prometheus and Pandora explain why humans have fire, and why they suffer. · Gigantomachy · The war between the Olympian gods and the Giants, a favourite subject for temple sculpture. · Titanomachy · The earlier war in which Zeus and his siblings defeated the Titans. · Peter Paul Rubens · The Fall of Phaeton · c. 1604–08"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/sala-dei-giganti-room-of-the-giants.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","Sala dei Giganti (Room of the Giants)","Giulio Romano (design) and Rinaldo Mantovano","h3_giulio","Giulio Romano (design) and Rinaldo Mantovano · 1532–35 · Fresco on walls and vault · Palazzo Te, Mantua · A room in Federico II Gonzaga’s pleasure villa where fresco covers every wall and the vault without a break, so visitors stand inside the collapse of the Giants. Much of the painting was done by the designer’s assistant. · Look up: Jupiter hurls thunderbolts from the vault, ringed by alarmed gods. · On the walls Giants are crushed by falling columns, rocks and a collapsing temple. · Monkeys on one wall come from a period misreading of Ovid. · Scaffolding came down for Emperor Charles V’s visit in 1532 while parts were unfinished; the room is often read as flattering him. · Restored in the 1780s and again in 1988. · The Giants, children of Earth, attacked Olympus. Ovid’s version of the story is the painting’s source."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-fall-of-the-titans.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","The Fall of the Titans","Cornelis van Haarlem","h3_titans","Cornelis van Haarlem · 1588–90 · Oil on canvas, 239 × 307 cm · SMK, National Gallery of Denmark, Copenhagen · A Dutch Mannerist showpiece of tumbling nude bodies by a Haarlem painter whose circle was among the first to work from nude models. No commission is recorded. · Bought in 1621 by King Christian IV of Denmark. · Look at the bodies falling from light into darkness and piling up, mouths open and hands raised. · Foreground figures frame the ones behind and pull the eye deep into space. · Butterflies and insects cover the figures’ groins, which the museum links to Hell. · Long in storage; restored and rehung in 1988. · The Titans, the generation before the Olympians, were thrown down into Tartarus, the deepest part of the underworld."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-fall-of-phaeton.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","The Fall of Phaeton","Peter Paul Rubens","h3_phaeton","Peter Paul Rubens · c. 1604–08 · Oil on canvas, 98.4 × 131.2 cm · National Gallery of Art, Washington · Painted by the young artist in Italy and reworked over several years; painted-over reins and straps still show through. The picture catches the instant the sun’s chariot breaks apart. · Phaeton falls headfirst, wearing only a red sash, as the four horses scatter. · The butterfly-winged women at left are the Horae, the seasons, reacting in terror. · At lower right the earth catches fire; Jupiter’s thunderbolt strikes from outside the frame. · Probably the “Phaeton by Rubens” listed in an Antwerp inventory of 1657; bought by the gallery in 1990. · Phaeton begged his father, the sun god, to let him drive the sun’s chariot for a day. He lost control, scorched the earth, and was struck down by Jupiter."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/pergamon-altar-athena-fighting-the-giants.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","Pergamon Altar: Athena Fighting the Giants","Pergamene workshop","h3_perg_c","Pergamene workshop · c. 175–150 BCE · Marble high relief · Pergamonmuseum, Berlin · Part of the Great Frieze, more than 100 m of relief that wrapped the monumental altar, built under King Eumenes II. It is the largest surviving telling of the war against the Giants. · Athena grabs the winged Giant Alkyoneus by the hair while her serpent bites his chest. · Earth (Ge) rises from the ground pleading for her son; Victory flies in to crown Athena. · Scholars link the Giants to enemies of the Attalid kings, such as the Gauls. · In Berlin since 1930; the altar hall is closed for renovation and due to reopen in spring 2027. · In the Gigantomachy the Olympians were helped by Heracles. Athena’s victory over Alkyoneus was one of its best-known episodes."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/hydria-with-zeus-fighting-typhon.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","Hydria with Zeus Fighting Typhon","Chalcidian workshop","h3_typhon","Chalcidian workshop · c. 540–530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta, 46 cm high · Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich · The home of its workshop is still debated. One side shows Zeus’s last great battle. · Zeus strides in with a thunderbolt against Typhon. · Typhon is painted with wings and serpent legs; the hundred heads of the poems are not shown. · The other side shows the hero Peleus wrestling the huntress Atalanta. · Where it was found is not recorded in the sources checked. · Black-figure · A vase technique in which figures are painted in black slip and details scratched through to the clay. · Hydria · A three-handled jar for carrying water. · Typhon, the last child of Earth, challenged Zeus for rule of the world. Zeus defeated him and buried him beneath a mountain, often said to be Etna."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-myth-of-prometheus.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","The Myth of Prometheus","Piero di Cosimo","h4_piero","Piero di Cosimo · c. 1510–15 · Oil on poplar panel, 66.4 × 118.2 cm · Alte Pinakothek, Munich · A spalliera panel, a painting set into the panelling or furniture of a room, and the first narrative painting of the Prometheus story in modern times. · Several episodes run across one wide landscape. · At centre stands the statue of a youth Prometheus has made. · Minerva talks with Prometheus and then carries him up to heaven to find the spark of life. · The story continues on the Strasbourg panel: the figure brought to life, the theft of fire and Prometheus bound. · Bought by the museum in 1918. · In one tradition Prometheus, a Titan, shaped the first humans from clay; Minerva helped him. The painter followed Boccaccio’s retelling of the myth."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/prometheus-bound.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","Prometheus Bound","Peter Paul Rubens and Frans Snyders","h4_rubens","Peter Paul Rubens and Frans Snyders · c. 1611–18 · Oil on canvas, 242.6 × 209.6 cm · Philadelphia Museum of Art · One of the first major works from Rubens’s Antwerp studio, which he thought among his most important pictures. The eagle was painted by his colleague. · The eagle tears open the chest and digs a talon into one eye, while the other eye stares at the bird. · The tumbling body comes from Titian’s Tityus; the muscles from Michelangelo. · The left arm almost leaves the canvas, pushing the figure toward the viewer. · Look for the torch, sign of the stolen fire. · Bought by the museum in 1950. · For giving fire to humans, Zeus chained Prometheus to a mountain in the Caucasus, where an eagle ate his liver every day. The story is told in the play attributed to Aeschylus."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/eva-prima-pandora.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","Eva Prima Pandora","Jean Cousin the Elder","h4_cousin","Jean Cousin the Elder · after 1549 · Painting on wood panel, 97.5 × 150 cm · Louvre, Paris · A French Renaissance reclining nude by a painter who worked in the town of Sens. Its title fuses Eve, the first woman of the Bible, with Pandora. · The tablet above her carries the title (Eve, the first Pandora). · She leans on a skull, a reminder of death. · A serpent and apple point to Eden; a ruined landscape lies behind. · Bought by the museum in 1922 from Sens. · Zeus had Pandora made as the first woman to punish humans for Prometheus’s gift. She opened a jar that released every evil, leaving only Hope inside."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-deluge.html","Titans, Giants and the First Mortals","The Deluge","Michelangelo","h4_deluge","Michelangelo · c. 1508–09 · Fresco · Sistine Chapel ceiling, Vatican City · One of the nine Genesis scenes painted for Pope Julius II, and among the first the artist finished. It is far more crowded than the scenes he painted later. · People climb a hillside at left to escape the rising water. · A group shelters under a makeshift tent at right; a small boat is about to capsize. · The ark waits in the background. · Julius first wanted apostles on the ceiling; the artist proposed Old Testament scenes. · The Greeks had their own flood story: Zeus drowned a wicked humanity, and only Deucalion and Pyrrha survived to repeople the earth. Renaissance viewers saw it as a pagan echo of Noah."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/love-desire-and-transformation.html","Love, Desire and Transformation · Overview","Love, Desire and Transformation","","h5_europa","From Pollaiuolo to Waterhouse, with a Greek marble · Many myths turn on desire: gods pursue mortals, often in disguise, and the chase ends in a change of form. Ovid gathered these stories in the Metamorphoses, and from the Renaissance on they gave painters a respectable reason to paint the nude. · Titian’s poesie set the standard for painting these stories. · Gods transform themselves to seduce; mortals are transformed to escape or as punishment. · Many of these paintings were made for private rooms and bedchambers. · Metamorphosis · A change of shape, the theme of Ovid’s poem of about 8 CE and the main source for these paintings. · Poesia · Titian’s word for his mythological paintings for Philip II of Spain: painted poems based on Ovid. · Titian · The Rape of Europa · 1559–62"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/danae-and-the-shower-of-gold.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","Danaë and the Shower of Gold","Titian","h5_danae","Titian · 1560–65 · Oil on canvas, 129.8 × 181.2 cm · Museo del Prado, Madrid · A new, horizontal version of the Danaë the painter had sent Philip II in 1553, painted with looser handling. The museum’s catalogue thinks it was made for a private patron who knew him, not for the king. · The old nurse catching the gold in her apron sets youth against age, nude against clothed. · Gold pours from the cloud like falling coins. · A small dog on the bed is read by the museum as a hint of lust. · Kept from 1827 in a restricted room of Madrid’s Academy of San Fernando. · King Acrisius locked his daughter Danaë away after an oracle said her son would kill him. Zeus reached her as a shower of gold, and she bore the hero Perseus."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-rape-of-europa.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","The Rape of Europa","Titian","h5_europa","Titian · 1559–62 · Oil on canvas, 178 × 205 cm · Isabella Stewart Gardner Museum, Boston · The sixth and last of the poesie, shipped from Venice in 1562. It is the only one of the series in America. · Europa clings to the bull’s horn and waves a red veil back to her friends on the shore. · Cupid riding a dolphin repeats her pose; the signature sits at lower left. · Given to the future Charles I of England in 1623, but it stayed in Madrid when the marriage talks failed. · Isabella Stewart Gardner bought it in 1896; restored in 2018–19. · Zeus took the form of a white bull, charmed the Phoenician princess Europa, and swam off with her to Crete. The continent of Europe was later named after her."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-abduction-of-ganymede.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","The Abduction of Ganymede","Correggio","h5_ganymede","Correggio · c. 1530 · Oil on canvas, 163.5 × 72 cm · Kunsthistorisches Museum, Vienna · Commissioned by Federico II Gonzaga of Mantua as part of a series on Jupiter’s loves, and probably given by him to Emperor Charles V. Its tall, narrow shape matches its companion, Jupiter and Io. · A startled dog at the bottom looks up and leads the eye skyward. · Ganymede turns to face us while the eagle, wings cut off by the edge, carries him off. · Owned by Philip II after 1585 and bought by Emperor Rudolf II in 1605. · Zeus, as an eagle, carried the Trojan prince Ganymede up to Olympus to be cupbearer to the gods."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/apollo-and-daphne.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","Apollo and Daphne","Piero del Pollaiuolo","h5_bernini","Piero del Pollaiuolo · probably 1470–80 · Oil on wood, 29.5 × 20 cm · National Gallery, London · A tiny panel meant to be held and studied up close, possibly made for Lorenzo de’ Medici, whose emblem used the laurel. It was once thought to come from a piece of furniture. · Daphne’s arms have already become great laurel branches, and one leg a trunk. · Apollo runs in a rich hunting costume picked out with gold. · Behind them is the Arno valley with a distant city, probably Florence. · Still in a 16th-century Italian frame; at the gallery since 1876. · Apollo pursued the nymph Daphne, who prayed to escape and was turned into a laurel tree. The laurel became Apollo’s sacred plant."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/diana-and-actaeon.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","Diana and Actaeon","Titian","h6_actaeon","Titian · 1556–59 · Oil on canvas, 184.5 × 202.2 cm · National Gallery, London, and National Galleries of Scotland (shared) · One of the poesie, designed to hang beside Diana and Callisto so the two landscapes ran on into each other. The two galleries bought it together in 2009 and show it in turn. · A red curtain is pulled aside as Actaeon bursts in on the bathing goddess. · A stag’s skull on a stone pillar foretells his fate. · Diana, with a crescent in her hair, glares sideways while an attendant draws a veil. · The hunter Actaeon stumbled on Diana bathing. She turned him into a stag, and his own hounds tore him apart."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/venus-and-adonis.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","Venus and Adonis","Titian","h6_adonis","Titian · 1550s–1560s · Oil on canvas, 106.7 × 133.4 cm · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The artist’s most repeated composition: at least 30 versions by him, his workshop and copyists are known. This is the “two-dog” type, a different design from the version he painted for Philip II. · Venus, seen from behind, clings to Adonis as he leaves for the hunt. · Cupid is awake and frightened; a rainbow and a burst of light fill the sky. · The Met dates it to the 1550s; the National Gallery of Art’s catalogue places it in the 1560s with workshop help. · Venus loved the mortal hunter Adonis and begged him not to go hunting. He was killed by a boar, and the anemone grew from his blood."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/venus-and-mars.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","Venus and Mars","Sandro Botticelli","h6_marsvenus","Sandro Botticelli · c. 1485 · Tempera and oil on wood, 69.2 × 173.4 cm · National Gallery, London · A spalliera, hung at shoulder height or above, probably made to celebrate a marriage and hung in a bedchamber. · Baby satyrs play with Mars’s armour: one wears his helmet, one sits in his breastplate. · One blows a conch shell in Mars’s ear, and he still sleeps. · Wasps swarm near his head, possibly a pun on the Vespucci family name. · Bought by the gallery in 1874. · Venus, though married to Vulcan, was the lover of Mars. Renaissance thinkers read the pair as love calming strife."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/pygmalion-and-galatea.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","Pygmalion and Galatea","Jean-Léon Gérôme","h6_pygmalion","Jean-Léon Gérôme · c. 1890 · Oil on canvas, 88.9 × 68.6 cm · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · One of three oil versions the artist painted of his own painted marble sculpture of the subject. Here a sculptor paints his sculpture coming alive. · Each of the three paintings views the statue from a different side, as if walking around it. · His marble version is at Hearst Castle, California. · Given to the museum in 1927. · In Ovid, the sculptor Pygmalion fell in love with the ivory statue he had carved, and Venus brought it to life. The name Galatea was given to her by later writers."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/echo-and-narcissus.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","Echo and Narcissus","John William Waterhouse","h6_echo","John William Waterhouse · 1903 · Oil on canvas, 109 × 189 cm · Walker Art Gallery, Liverpool · Shown at the Royal Academy in 1903 and bought by the gallery the same year. · Narcissus lies on the bank, lost in his own reflection. · Echo watches from across the water, hemmed in and unseen. · The landscape is built from bold, decorative shapes. · The nymph Echo, able only to repeat others’ words, loved Narcissus, who loved only his reflection. She faded to a voice; he wasted away and became a flower."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/venus-de-milo.html","Love, Desire and Transformation","Venus de Milo","Hellenistic sculptor","h6_milo","Hellenistic sculptor · c. 150–100 BCE · Parian marble, 204 cm high · Louvre, Paris · Found by a farmer on the island of Melos in April 1820, bought by the French ambassador, and given to Louis XVIII, who gave it to the museum in 1821. It is the most famous Aphrodite to survive from antiquity. · Carved in two blocks joined at the line of the drapery around the hips. · Drill holes once held metal jewellery, such as a bracelet. · The arms were never found; proposals include an apple or a shield used as a mirror. · Found with an inscription from a gymnasium, but where it stood is unknown. · Aphrodite was worshipped across the Greek world. Late Hellenistic sculptors showed her half-draped, a type that begins with Praxiteles’s famous nude Aphrodite of Knidos."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/wine-harvest-and-the-underworld.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld · Overview","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","","h10_patinir","From a Greek cup to Leighton · Two gods of the earth’s cycles, Demeter of the grain and Dionysus of the vine, stand close to the world of the dead. Their myths explain the seasons and promise renewal, while stories of journeys to the underworld test what happens after death. · Dionysus punishes those who refuse him. · Heroes and poets who visit the underworld bring back warnings for the living. · Underworld · The realm of the dead ruled by Hades (Roman Pluto), reached by crossing the river Styx. · Bacchanal · A painting of the drunken followers of Bacchus. · Joachim Patinir · Charon Crossing the Styx · 1520–24"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-abduction-of-proserpina.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","The Abduction of Proserpina","Rembrandt","h9_bernini","Rembrandt · 1631 · Oil on oak panel, 84.4 × 79.5 cm · Gemäldegalerie, Berlin · One of the artist’s small-figure history paintings from around the time he moved from Leiden to Amsterdam, and a rare subject in Dutch art. It was probably in the collection of the Dutch stadholder Frederik Hendrik by 1632. · Proserpina screams and claws at Pluto’s face while her companions grab at her cloak. · Bright blue sky at upper left (the earth) gives way to darkness at lower right (Pluto’s realm). · The blue is costly ultramarine, made from lapis lazuli, and is original. · Technical study shows he first painted four horses, later reduced to two. · The design likely depends on a composition by Rubens known from an engraving. · Pluto seized Proserpina, daughter of the harvest goddess Ceres, while she gathered flowers, and carried her down to rule as his queen."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-return-of-persephone.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","The Return of Persephone","Frederic Leighton","h1_persephone","Frederic Leighton · c. 1891 · Oil on canvas, 203 × 152.4 cm · Leeds Art Gallery · A squared-up tracing of 1891 at the Royal Academy matches the finished picture almost exactly. Sir James Kitson bought the painting and gave it to the gallery. · Hermes lifts Persephone up out of the underworld toward her mother Demeter, at left. · The painter made separate studies for the heavy drapery. · A 1905 guide counted nineteen preparatory drawings; many survive in British collections. · Published as a photogravure by the Fine Art Society in 1897. · Demeter’s grief for her daughter left the earth barren until Zeus sent Hermes to bring Persephone back. She must return below for part of each year, which is why winter comes."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/bacchus.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","Bacchus","Caravaggio","h9_bacchus","Caravaggio · c. 1598 · Oil on canvas, 95 × 85 cm · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · An early work: a young model in a white sheet posing as the wine god, offering a glass. Cardinal del Monte, the painter’s first patron, gave it to the Medici for the wedding of Cosimo II in 1608. · Rediscovered in the Uffizi storerooms in 1913 and attributed to the painter by Roberto Longhi. · The fruit bowl includes a half-rotten apple among pomegranates, quinces, figs and grapes. · Look closely at the carafe: a tiny reflected face, generally read as a self-portrait, was confirmed by infrared study. · Grapes and vine leaves sit in the dark curls; the fingernails are dirty. · Dionysus (Roman Bacchus), son of Zeus and the mortal Semele, is god of wine, release and theatre."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/midas-and-bacchus.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","Midas and Bacchus","Nicolas Poussin","h9_midas","Nicolas Poussin · c. 1624 · Oil on canvas, 98.5 × 135 cm · Alte Pinakothek, Munich · An early work from about the time the painter arrived in Rome in 1624. It shows the start of the story, when Midas tells Bacchus his wish. · In the right background Midas washes in the river Pactolus to undo the gift. · Owned by Elector Max Emanuel of Bavaria; it came to the museum from Nymphenburg Palace. · For returning his tutor Silenus, Bacchus granted King Midas a wish. Midas asked that all he touched turn to gold, then found he could not eat, and was cured by bathing in the Pactolus."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/dionysus-cup.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","Dionysus Cup","Exekias","h9_exekias","Exekias · c. 530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta, 30.5 cm diameter · Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich · Made in Athens and signed by its maker, the leading black-figure artist of his day. It was exported to Etruria and found in a tomb at Vulci. · Dionysus reclines in a ship whose mast sprouts a vine with seven bunches of grapes. · Seven dolphins circle the ship, the pirates he transformed. · A coral-red ground fills the whole interior, sea and sky in one. · Outside, big eyes on each side and warriors fighting over a fallen body. · As the drinker drained the cup, the god seemed to rise out of the red wine. · Kylix · A shallow two-handled drinking cup used at the symposium, the Greek drinking party. · Pirates kidnapped Dionysus, not knowing he was a god. He filled the ship with vines and turned them into dolphins as they leapt overboard."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/charon-crossing-the-styx.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","Charon Crossing the Styx","Joachim Patinir","h10_patinir","Joachim Patinir · 1520–24 · Oil on panel, 64 × 103 cm · Museo del Prado, Madrid · Here a pioneer of landscape painting in Antwerp turns the river of the dead into a moral choice. It probably belonged to Philip II and was saved from the Alcázar fire of 1734. · The picture divides into three bands: paradise with angels at left, the dark Styx in the middle, hell at right. · Cerberus guards the gate of hell on the right. · The small soul in Charon’s boat turns, in profile, toward the easy road to damnation. · Charon the ferryman carried the souls of the dead across the Styx for a coin."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/orpheus-eurydice-and-hermes.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","Orpheus, Eurydice and Hermes","Roman, after an Athenian relief","h10_orpheusrel","Roman, after an Athenian relief · 1st century BCE, after an original of the late 5th century BCE · Marble, 121 × 98 cm · Louvre, Paris · A copy, probably made in Italy, of a lost original, which may have belonged to the Altar of the Twelve Gods in Athens. A nearly identical copy is in Naples. · Orpheus has just turned to look; Hermes, guide of souls, takes Eurydice’s hand to lead her back. · Orpheus holds his lyre; Hermes wears a traveller’s hat and boots. · A Latin inscription added before the 1500s names the figures wrongly, as Zethus, Antiope and Amphion. · Hermes’s legs and the upper right corner are later restorations. · Formerly in the Borghese collection; bought by the museum in 1807. · Orpheus charmed the gods of the underworld with his music and was allowed to lead his dead wife Eurydice home, if he did not look back. He looked, and lost her for ever."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/orpheus-leading-eurydice-from-the-underworld.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","Orpheus Leading Eurydice from the Underworld","Camille Corot","h10_corot","Camille Corot · 1861 · Oil on canvas, 112.7 × 137.2 cm · Museum of Fine Arts, Houston · Shown at the Paris Salon of 1861, this is the painter’s soft, misty landscape style applied to myth. The mood foreshadows the ending rather than showing it. · Spirits of the dead gather beneath feathery trees. · A narrow range of grays, greens and blues gives a filtered, dreamlike light. · Signed “COROT” at lower left; bought by the museum in 1987."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/gods-and-stories/the-map-of-hell.html","Wine, Harvest and the Underworld","The Map of Hell","Sandro Botticelli","h10_map","Sandro Botticelli · c. 1480–95 · Silverpoint, pen and ink, and colour on parchment · Vatican Apostolic Library · The opening sheet of the artist’s illustrations to Dante’s Divine Comedy, made for Lorenzo di Pierfrancesco de’ Medici. It is one of the few sheets he finished in colour. · Hell is a giant funnel of circular ledges crowded with tiny figures. · Bought in 1650 for Queen Christina of Sweden; most of the other sheets are in Berlin. · Dante borrows freely from Greek myth: Charon, Minos and Cerberus all work in his hell."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/heroes.html","Heroes · Overview","Heroes","","h11_cellini","Greek vases to Moreau and Delacroix · Greek heroes are mortals, often half-divine, who win fame by killing monsters, finishing impossible tasks and outwitting fate. Their stories are also warnings: the same strength and pride that make a hero often destroy him or those he loves. · Monster-slaying gave rulers a ready image of order defeating chaos. · Later artists turned to the darker heroes. · Hero · In Greek usage, a figure of the age of legend, honoured with cult after death. · Apotheosis · Being raised to the status of a god, as Heracles was after his death. · Benvenuto Cellini · Perseus with the Head of Medusa · 1545–54"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/medusa.html","Heroes","Medusa","Caravaggio","h11_caravaggio","Caravaggio · c. 1595–98 · Oil on canvas mounted on a wooden shield · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · A real parade shield painted with the severed head of Medusa. Cardinal Francesco Maria del Monte gave it to Grand Duke Ferdinando I de’ Medici in 1598. · Medusa screams with eyes wide, caught between life and death at the moment of beheading. · Snakes writhe around her head and blood streams from the neck. · Kept in the Medici armoury, first without a name; listed as his work in 1631 and shown with a suit of Persian armour. · The Medici used the Medusa head as an emblem of power; the shield also recalls the shield Perseus used to see her safely. · Medusa’s gaze turned people to stone. Perseus cut off her head by watching her reflection in a polished shield, and Athena later set the head on her own shield."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/perseus-with-the-head-of-medusa.html","Heroes","Perseus with the Head of Medusa","Benvenuto Cellini","h11_cellini","Benvenuto Cellini · 1545–54 · Bronze, statue over 3 m high · Loggia dei Lanzi, Florence · Commissioned by Duke Cosimo I de’ Medici in 1545 for the Loggia dei Lanzi on Florence’s main square, beside Donatello’s Judith. It was unveiled in April 1554 and read as the triumph of the duke over the old republic. · Cast in separate pieces and joined; the dramatic pour the sculptor describes in his autobiography was the Perseus figure. · Blood pours from Medusa’s neck; Perseus holds the head high. · Signed on the strap across Perseus’s chest. · The original marble base and its statuettes are now in the Bargello; copies stand in the Loggia. · Perseus killed Medusa with gifts from the gods: winged sandals, a cap of invisibility and a curved sword."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/perseus-and-andromeda.html","Heroes","Perseus and Andromeda","Titian","h11_titian","Titian · probably 1554–56 · Oil on canvas, 173 × 186.8 cm · Wallace Collection, London · One of the six poesie, conceived as a partner to The Rape of Europa. It later passed through the collections of Van Dyck and the Orléans family before Lady Wallace left it to the nation in 1897. · Andromeda’s pale body, chained to the rock, stretches across the canvas. · Perseus dives at the sea monster over the water. · Its condition has suffered since the 16th century. · Reunited with the other poesie in 2020–21 in London, Madrid and Boston. · Queen Cassiopeia boasted that she and her daughter outshone the sea nymphs, and a monster was sent to ravage the coast. Perseus freed Andromeda from the rock and married her."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/farnese-hercules.html","Heroes","Farnese Hercules","Glykon of Athens, after Lysippos","h12_farnese","Glykon of Athens, after Lysippos · 2nd–3rd century CE · Pentelic marble, 317 cm high · National Archaeological Museum, Naples · An enlarged Roman copy, signed by its sculptor, of a 4th-century BCE bronze attributed to Lysippos. It was found in 1546 in the Baths of Caracalla in Rome, where it was probably displayed. · A colossal, heavily muscled hero leans on his club, small head bowed. · His right hand, behind his back, holds the golden apples of the Hesperides, his last labour. · It entered the Farnese collection and moved with it to Naples. · The photograph shown is by Giorgio Sommer, about the 1860s. · Heracles (Roman Hercules), son of Zeus, performed twelve labours to atone for killing his family in a madness sent by Hera."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/the-choice-of-hercules.html","Heroes","The Choice of Hercules","Annibale Carracci","h12_crossroads","Annibale Carracci · 1596 · Oil on canvas · Museo di Capodimonte, Naples · The central ceiling painting of the Camerino, a small private study in Palazzo Farnese in Rome that the painter decorated in 1595–97 for Cardinal Odoardo Farnese. It was later removed and taken to Parma, then to Naples. · Pleasure, at right, points to cards, music and theatre masks in a green, flowery setting. · Virtue, at left, points up a hard, rocky path toward Pegasus. · A laurel-crowned poet waits to record the hero’s fame. · The programme was devised by the palace librarian, Fulvio Orsini. · In a Greek parable told by Prodicus and retold by Cicero, the young Hercules meets Virtue and Pleasure at a fork in the road and chooses the harder way."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/krater-with-heracles-wrestling-antaeus.html","Heroes","Krater with Heracles Wrestling Antaeus","Euphronios","h12_antaios","Euphronios · c. 515–510 BCE · Red-figure terracotta, 45.5 cm high · Louvre, Paris · Signed by its painter, one of the first great red-figure artists, made in Athens and found in Etruria. The museum bought it with the Campana collection in 1861. · Heracles kneels and strangles Antaeus, who is half-lying on the ground. · Compare the faces: Antaeus’s eyes roll back and his teeth show; Heracles stays calm. · Heracles’s neat curls are built from tiny raised dots of clay. · The scene appeared on a Greek stamp for the 1906 Athens games. · Red-figure · A vase technique, invented in Athens about 530 BCE, in which figures are left in the red clay and the background painted black. · Krater · A large bowl for mixing wine with water. · The giant Antaeus, son of Poseidon and Earth, drew strength from touching the ground. In the poems Heracles lifted him into the air to defeat him; the painter shows a different moment."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/amphora-with-theseus-killing-the-minotaur.html","Heroes","Amphora with Theseus Killing the Minotaur","Group of London B 174","h13_theseusvase","Group of London B 174 · c. 540–530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta, 42.3 cm high · Louvre, Paris · Made in Athens and found at Vulci in Etruria, in excavations by Lucien Bonaparte, prince of Canino. The museum bought it in 1840. · Theseus, bearded and in an embroidered tunic, drives his sword into the wounded, half-kneeling Minotaur. · Look for the small bird in the field between them. · Two young women and two young men watch, the Athenian tribute. · The other side shows Heracles wrestling the Nemean lion. · Parts of the black surface were repainted in modern times. · Each year Athens sent young people to Crete to be eaten by the Minotaur in the Labyrinth. Theseus killed the monster and found his way out with Ariadne’s thread."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/landscape-with-the-fall-of-icarus.html","Heroes","Landscape with the Fall of Icarus","After Pieter Bruegel the Elder","h13_icarus","After Pieter Bruegel the Elder · c. 1600, after an original of c. 1560 · Oil on canvas, 73.5 × 112 cm · Royal Museums of Fine Arts of Belgium, Brussels · A 2011 technical study concluded this canvas is a copy by another hand, after a lost original. It is still the best-known record of the composition. · Bought in 1912 as a copy; for decades some argued it was an original. · The underdrawing was judged weak and hesitant, a copyist’s work. · Look for Icarus’s legs in the water near the ship at lower right, while the ploughman works on. · Another version is in the Van Buuren Museum, Brussels. · Daedalus made wings of feathers and wax for himself and his son Icarus. Icarus flew too near the sun, the wax melted, and he fell into the sea."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/oedipus-explains-the-riddle-of-the-sphinx.html","Heroes","Oedipus Explains the Riddle of the Sphinx","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","h14_ingres","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · 1808, enlarged 1827 · Oil on canvas, 189 × 144 cm · Louvre, Paris · Painted in Rome as a required work for the French Academy while the artist was a student at the Villa Medici. He showed it at the Salon of 1827. · Oedipus, nude and holding a spear, leans toward the Sphinx on her rock as he answers. · Bones of earlier victims lie among the rocks. · Signed and dated 1808 at lower left. · Owned by the duc d’Orléans; bequeathed to the museum in 1878. · The Sphinx killed travellers who could not answer her riddle. Oedipus solved it and became king of Thebes, unaware he had already killed his father and would marry his mother."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/jason.html","Heroes","Jason","Gustave Moreau","h14_jason","Gustave Moreau · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 204 × 115.5 cm · Musée d’Orsay, Paris · Shown at the Salon of 1865. Its dense, jewel-like detail is typical of his approach to myth. · Jason stands with his foot on the dead guardian, painted as an eagle rather than a dragon. · Medea stands behind him holding a small vial. · A column with a ram’s head stands for the Golden Fleece. · Given to the French state by Théodore Reinach in 1908. · Jason sailed with the Argonauts to Colchis to win the Golden Fleece. He succeeded only with the help of the king’s daughter, the sorceress Medea."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/medea-about-to-kill-her-children.html","Heroes","Medea about to Kill Her Children","Eugène Delacroix","h14_medea","Eugène Delacroix · 1838 · Oil on canvas, 260 × 165 cm · Palais des Beaux-Arts, Lille · A critical success at the Salon of 1838, where the French state bought it; it has been in Lille since about 1840. Lille also has the oil sketch and about thirty preparatory drawings. · Medea clutches her two struggling sons in a cave, the group forming a pyramid. · She glances back as if she hears danger; a shadow falls across her face like a mask. · The dagger, the only upright line in the picture, is set off by white cloth. · He painted a smaller replica in 1862, now in the Louvre. · When Jason abandoned her to marry a princess, Medea took revenge by killing their children. Euripides made the story a tragedy in 431 BCE."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/troy-and-the-odyssey.html","Troy and the Odyssey · Overview","Troy and the Odyssey","","h16_turner","The war over Helen, and Odysseus’s long voyage home · The Trojan War began with a wedding, a golden apple and a beauty contest, and ended ten years later with a wooden horse. Homer’s Iliad tells one episode of the war; his Odyssey follows the hero Odysseus on his ten-year journey home. · Greek vase painters told the Trojan stories in close, labelled detail. · Later painters chose the moments of grief and fate. · Odysseus’s wit, not strength, carries him through monsters and enchantresses. · Epic · A long narrative poem about heroes and gods, such as the Iliad and the Odyssey. · J. M. W. Turner · Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus · 1829"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/the-francois-vase.html","Troy and the Odyssey","The François Vase","Kleitias (painter) and Ergotimos (potter)","h15_francois","Kleitias (painter) and Ergotimos (potter) · c. 570–560 BCE · Black-figure terracotta volute krater, 66 cm high · Museo Archeologico Nazionale, Florence · Signed by both its painter and its potter, it carries about 270 figures and 121 painted name labels. It was found in pieces in an Etruscan tomb near Chiusi in the 1840s. · The main band shows the gods arriving at the wedding of Peleus and Thetis, the parents of Achilles. · Dionysus carries a wine jar and turns his face to the viewer, rare at this date. · On each handle, Ajax carries the dead Achilles. · In 1900 a museum guard smashed it into 638 pieces; it has been reassembled twice. · Volute krater · A krater whose handles curl into scrolls (volutes) above the rim. · The goddess Eris, left off the guest list for this wedding, threw a golden apple “for the fairest” among the guests, which led to the Judgement of Paris and the Trojan War."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/achilles-and-ajax-playing-a-game.html","Troy and the Odyssey","Achilles and Ajax Playing a Game","Exekias","h15_exekias","Exekias · c. 540–530 BCE · Black-figure terracotta amphora, 61.1 cm high · Vatican Museums, Vatican City · Signed by its maker as both potter and painter. Its scene is found nowhere in Homer. It was found at Vulci in Etruria and given to Pope Gregory XVI in 1834. · Words come from their mouths: “four” for Achilles, “three” for Ajax, so Achilles is winning. · Achilles still wears his helmet; Ajax has taken his off. · Very fine incised detail covers their cloaks. · On the other side, Castor and Pollux return home to their parents. · Achilles and Ajax were the strongest Greek fighters at Troy. Both died there: Achilles by Paris’s arrow, Ajax by his own hand."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/the-judgement-of-paris.html","Troy and the Odyssey","The Judgement of Paris","Peter Paul Rubens","h15_paris","Peter Paul Rubens · probably 1632–35 · Oil on oak panel, 144.8 × 193.7 cm · National Gallery, London · A late work that the painter reworked and enlarged himself, then another artist altered in France between about 1676 and 1727. The later hand toned it down: removing putti and satyrs and making Paris point the apple at Venus. · Venus, in the centre, receives the apple, with Cupid behind her. · Minerva’s owl sits in the tree; Juno’s peacock hisses at Paris’s dog. · The Fury Alecto lurks in the clouds, foretelling the war. · Bought by the gallery in 1844. · Paris, a Trojan prince, judged which goddess was fairest. Juno offered power and wealth, Minerva wisdom, and Venus the most beautiful woman alive, Helen, whose abduction started the war."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/andromache-mourning-hector.html","Troy and the Odyssey","Andromache Mourning Hector","Jacques-Louis David","h15_andromache","Jacques-Louis David · 1783 · Oil on canvas, 275 × 203 cm · Louvre, Paris (on deposit from the École des Beaux-Arts) · The artist’s reception piece for the French Royal Academy, the painting that won him membership, shown at the Salon of 1783. It was restored in 2025. · Andromache sits beside Hector’s body with their young son Astyanax. · Hector’s helmet and weapons are laid beside him. · The candelabrum at right carries a Greek inscription quoting Andromache’s lament from the Iliad. · Bequeathed to the École des Beaux-Arts in 1886; on deposit since 1969. · Hector, Troy’s greatest defender, was killed by Achilles. The Iliad ends with his funeral and the lament of his wife Andromache."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/laocoon.html","Troy and the Odyssey","Laocoön","El Greco","h15_laocoon","El Greco · c. 1610–14 · Oil on canvas, 137.5 × 172.5 cm · National Gallery of Art, Washington · The artist’s only known mythological painting, made in his last years and probably still in his studio when he died. He set the story outside Toledo, the city where he lived. · Elongated nude figures wrestle snakes on a rocky bank. · A tiny horse trots toward a city of pink walls. · The figures at right are unfinished; a 1955 cleaning revealed an extra head and leg. · Bought by the Kress Foundation and given to the gallery in 1946. · The Trojan priest Laocoön warned against the Greeks’ wooden horse. Sea serpents sent by the gods killed him and his sons, and the Trojans took the horse inside."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/ulysses-deriding-polyphemus.html","Troy and the Odyssey","Ulysses Deriding Polyphemus","J. M. W. Turner","h16_turner","J. M. W. Turner · 1829 · Oil on canvas, 132.5 × 203 cm · National Gallery, London · Painted for the Royal Academy exhibition of 1829, this scene from Homer’s Odyssey is mostly light and colour. It came to the nation with the Turner Bequest in 1856. · Ulysses stands on deck in a scarlet cloak, arms raised, under a red banner. · The giant’s shadowy body sprawls along the clifftop, one hand raised to throw a rock. · The rising sun is drawn by Apollo’s horses; sea nymphs with stars on their foreheads swim at the prow. · Still in its original 19th-century frame. · Odysseus blinded the Cyclops Polyphemus to escape his cave, then taunted him from the ship. Polyphemus prayed to his father Poseidon, who kept Odysseus from home for years."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/heroes-and-troy/circe-offering-the-cup-to-ulysses.html","Troy and the Odyssey","Circe Offering the Cup to Ulysses","John William Waterhouse","h16_circe","John William Waterhouse · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 148 × 92 cm · Gallery Oldham · Bought from the painter in 1892 by the Oldham collector Charles Edward Lees, whose daughter gave it to the town’s gallery in 1952. · Circe raises a goblet of potion in one hand and her wand in the other. · The round mirror behind her shows Ulysses approaching, from where we stand. · A letter from the painter to Lees survives in the gallery’s archive. · The enchantress Circe turned Odysseus’s men into pigs. Protected by an herb from Hermes, Odysseus resisted her drugs and made her restore them."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/reading-myth/interpreting-myth.html","Interpreting Myth · Overview","Interpreting Myth","","h18_helios","How later ages read the old stories as allegory and symbol · Every age reads the myths for its own purposes. Renaissance thinkers found hidden philosophy in them, Baroque patrons used them to flatter, and the Symbolists of the 1800s turned them into private dreams. · A myth can carry moral, political or spiritual meaning beyond its plot. · Many meanings are still argued, which is part of the pleasure. · Allegory · A picture or story in which figures stand for ideas. · Symbolism · A late-19th-century movement that used myth and dream to suggest inner states rather than describe the world. · Guido Reni · Aurora · 1613–14"],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/reading-myth/primavera.html","Interpreting Myth","Primavera","Sandro Botticelli","h18_primavera","Sandro Botticelli · c. 1480 · Tempera on poplar panel, 207 × 319 cm · Uffizi Gallery, Florence · Recorded in the Florence house of the heirs of Lorenzo di Pierfrancesco de’ Medici, hung above a lettuccio, a carved daybed. The museum says its exact meaning remains a mystery. · At right, the wind Zephyr seizes the nymph Chloris, who reappears transformed as Flora. · Venus stands in the centre; a blindfolded Cupid shoots above her. · At left, Mercury touches the clouds with his staff. · At least 42 plant species can be identified in the meadow. · Since June 2026 it faces The Birth of Venus across the renovated Botticelli rooms. · Many scholars read the painting through the Neoplatonic philosophy of Marsilio Ficino, as an image of love rising from the physical to the spiritual."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/reading-myth/aurora.html","Interpreting Myth","Aurora","Guido Reni","h18_helios","Guido Reni · 1613–14 · Fresco · Casino dell’Aurora Pallavicini, Rome · Painted on the ceiling of a garden pavilion built in 1612–13 for Cardinal Scipione Borghese as a summer retreat. It is still in private hands and opens to visitors on the first day of most months. · Veiled Aurora, the dawn, flies ahead scattering flowers. · Apollo drives the sun’s four-horse chariot, ringed by the Hours. · Phosphorus, the morning star, flies among the figures. · Most recently restored and unveiled in 2022. · Each morning Aurora opens the gates of heaven for the sun. The daily return of light became an image of joy and renewal."],[0,"/study/art-history/classical-myth/reading-myth/oedipus-and-the-sphinx.html","Interpreting Myth","Oedipus and the Sphinx","Gustave Moreau","h18_moreau","Gustave Moreau · 1864 · Oil on canvas, 206.4 × 104.8 cm · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The painting that made the artist’s name at the Paris Salon of 1864, and a major influence on later Symbolist artists and writers such as Odilon Redon and Oscar Wilde. · The Sphinx, woman-headed with blue wings, lion claws and a serpent tail, confronts Oedipus at close quarters. · Remains of earlier victims lie at the bottom: a hand, a foot, a crown. · An ornate urn sits on the column at right. · Bequeathed to the museum in 1920. · The painting turns Ingres’s contest of reason into a tense standoff between man and monster, the Symbolist reading of the same myth."],[1,"/study/color-theory.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/a-short-history-of-color-theory.html","A Short History of Color Theory · Overview","A Short History of Color Theory","","runge-farbenkugel","The thinkers and books that set the terms painters still use · Ideas about color come from physicists, poets, chemists and teachers, not only from painters. Each of them asked a different question: what light is, how the eye answers it, how neighboring colors affect each other, and how to name a color exactly. Their books and plates, like the painter Philipp Otto Runge's 1810 color sphere shown here with white and black at its poles, still shape how color is taught. · Newton's prism experiments and his circle of colors · Goethe's turn from light to the viewing eye · Chevreul's discovery at a Paris tapestry works · Munsell's measured system and the art-school teachers who followed · Plate · A full-page printed picture in a book, often printed separately from the text and sometimes colored by hand. · Philipp Otto Runge · Plate from Farben-Kugel (Color Sphere), Hamburg · 1810"],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-newton-splits-white-light.html","A Short History of Color Theory · History","Newton Splits White Light","","newton-opticks-circle","In Opticks (1704), Isaac Newton published his account of a prism spreading sunlight into a band of colors that can be gathered back into white. · He bent the band into a circle, sizing each color's arc like a step in a musical scale. Treating the colors in a mix of lights as weights on the ring, he proposed that the closer their balance point lay to the center, the whiter the mix. · On the plate the arcs read red, orange, yellow, green, blue (spelled “Blew”), indigo and violet. · Color, he wrote, lies in how surfaces reflect rays and rays stir the senses: “the rays to speak properly are not coloured” (1704). · He admitted a gap: two opposite colors alone had not yet given him true white, only a faint tint."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-goethe-and-the-seeing-eye.html","A Short History of Color Theory · History","Goethe and the Seeing Eye","","goethe-farbenlehre-plate1","Johann Wolfgang von Goethe's Zur Farbenlehre (Theory of Colours, 1810) treated color as something that happens in the viewer's eye, not only in light. · He opened with colors the eye produces itself, such as afterimages and colored shadows, which, he complained, earlier writers had treated as illusions. His six-part ring sets each color across from a partner. His English translator judged that the open attack on Newton kept many scientists from hearing him out, at least for a time. · In the ring, yellow faces violet, orange faces blue and red faces green. · Partners, in the 1840 translation, “reciprocally evoke each other in the eye”; see Afterimages, in the module on interaction. · That translator, Charles Eastlake of the Royal Academy, first meant to publish only the parts useful to painters."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-chevreul-and-colors-side-by-side.html","A Short History of Color Theory · History","Chevreul and Colors Side by Side","","chevreul-chromatic-circle","Michel-Eugène Chevreul, the chemist in charge of dyeing at the Gobelins tapestry works in Paris, set out his law of simultaneous contrast, on how neighboring colors alter each other, in a book of 1839. · Complaints came in that his black wools looked weak in blue and violet draperies. Blacks from other workshops proved no better: the fault lay not in the dye but in the hues around the black. Years of work on that finding grew into the book. · Charles Martel's English translation of 1854 carried the book to British readers. · This ring of 72 pure hues comes from a later volume of 1864 on color in the industrial arts. · The effect itself is explained on the Simultaneous Contrast page, in the module on interaction."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-from-munsell-to-the-bauhaus.html","A Short History of Color Theory · History","From Munsell to the Bauhaus","","munsell-sphere-1905","In A Color Notation (1905), the Boston painter Albert Munsell argued that color needed a scale and vocabulary as settled as music's. · His starting model was a color globe he patented in 1900, drawn in pastel for the book's opening plate. He also taught for decades at the Massachusetts Normal Art School. At the Bauhaus, the German design school, Johannes Itten taught color in the required first course from 1919 to 1923; Josef Albers, there from 1920, later carried his own method to America. · His system itself is set out on the Munsell's System page. · Itten later wrote The Art of Color (1961); his contrasts are covered on Itten's Seven Contrasts. · Albers's Interaction of Color (1963) taught through cut-paper exercises; both books are in copyright, so this course only describes them."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/light-and-seeing.html","Light and Seeing · Overview","Light and Seeing","","ct-light-path","What color is before it reaches the canvas, and how the eye reads it · Color begins as light. These pages follow it from a light source, off a surface and into the eye and brain. They also show why a color that seems fixed can shift when the light does. · The band of wavelengths we can see · Why lights add up to white while paints add up to dark · Three kinds of cone and the brain's comparison · Changing light, constancy, and colors that match under only one light · Diagram: how a red surface turns white daylight into red light"],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-light-and-the-spectrum.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","Light and the Spectrum","","bezold-plate-i-solar-spectrum","Visible light is the narrow band of electromagnetic waves the eye can detect, roughly 380 to 700 nanometres long. · Hue changes along the band, from red at the long end through orange, yellow, green and blue to violet. Sunlight carries the whole band and looks white; Newton Splits White Light shows a prism fanning it out. Dark lines, lettered A to H, mark light absorbed by gases in the sun and air. · A cut-glass crystal or a hose's spray in sunlight shows the band. · Magenta and purple appear nowhere in the band; the eye builds them from red and violet light together. · Beyond the two ends lie ultraviolet and infrared, waves too short or too long for the eye. · Nanometre (nm) · One millionth of a millimetre, the unit used for the wavelength of light."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-mixing-light-vs-mixing-paint.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","Mixing Light vs. Mixing Paint","","bezold-plate-i-mixtures","Additive mixing, as with lights, adds wavelengths with each new color; subtractive mixing, as with pigments, takes more away with each one. · Wilhelm von Bezold, a German physicist, tested this with gamboge yellow and Prussian blue: mixed as paint, only the green both reflect survives; mixed as light in the eye, they add to a dim white that reads as gray. Screens add red, green and blue light; printers subtract with cyan, magenta and yellow inks. · Look at a phone screen through a strong magnifier: tiny glowing red, green and blue points, not blended color. · In paint, only a color lighter than the mix raises its value. · Real paints mix less cleanly than ideal inks: red, yellow and blue together give a dark brown."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-how-the-eye-sees-color.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","How the Eye Sees Color","","ct-cones","Color vision starts with three kinds of cone cell in the retina, most sensitive to short, medium or long wavelengths; nerve cells, beginning in the retina itself, then compare their signals. · The three ranges overlap widely, so no single cone signals red or blue; color comes from the balance among them. That is why three lights can be mixed to match a huge range of colors. · Watch a garden at dusk: as cones give way to the more sensitive rods, colors fade before shapes do. · Color is seen most sharply at the center of gaze, where cones are packed most densely. · The eye's lens yellows with age, absorbing more of the shortest, blue-violet wavelengths before they reach the cones. · Retina · The light-sensitive layer lining the back of the eye."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-light-changes-color.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","Light Changes Color","","ct-light-changes-color","Color constancy is the brain's habit of seeing roughly a surface's own color even when the light falling on it, and so the light it sends to the eye, changes. · Lamplight is rich in long wavelengths, so a gray stone under it really does send orange-tinted light to the eye; blue shade does the reverse. The brain discounts the tint by judging each color against the whole scene. · Look at a white wall lit by a lamp at one end and daylight at the other; the ends differ. · Painters chasing a particular light paint the shift the brain hides, sometimes pushing it further; Monet's cathedral series is one example. · Constancy is incomplete: in strongly colored light, or in a patch seen out of context, the tint shows."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/rouen-cathedral-west-facade.html","Light and Seeing","Rouen Cathedral, West Façade","Claude Monet","monet-rouen-west-facade","Claude Monet · 1894 · Oil on canvas, 100.1 × 65.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Blue-violet strokes fill the shadowed stone, with flecks of peach where light catches the towers; the rose window is a dark blue disc. Across more than thirty views of this façade, the gallery says, color above all sets each canvas apart. · A companion canvas in the same gallery shows the front in full sun: cream, gold and pale pink under a deep blue sky. · The gallery's description compares the blurred surface to a reflection broken up on rippling water. · He reworked the series in the studio; a curator notes he exaggerated the light and used color for mood. · The gallery's audio guide traces the series' color from pinks and yellows to deep greens and blues as the light dims."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-metamerism.html","Light and Seeing · Concept","Metamerism","","ct-metamerism","Metamerism is when two colors match under one light but not under another, because their surfaces reflect different mixtures of wavelengths that happen to look alike. · The eye reduces every mixture of wavelengths to three cone signals, so quite different mixtures can give the same three signals. Change the light and each surface shifts by its own amount, so a passage matched under studio lamps can stand apart in daylight. · Check an important match both by daylight and under the light where the work will hang. · To match an earlier mixture, or to retouch, use the same pigments, not just a color that looks the same. · Viewers differ too: a pair that matches for one person may not for another, because cone responses vary."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/describing-color.html","Describing Color · Overview","Describing Color","","field-chromatography-plate-1","Words and numbers for telling one color from another · Everyday color names are too loose for a painter. This module sets out the properties every color has, the wheel painters use to arrange hues, and a system that locates each color by number. Later modules use these terms throughout. · Three properties of any color, and what white, gray or black do to it · Primaries, secondaries and tertiaries on the painter's wheel · A measured color space built around a gray axis · Achromatic · Without hue: white, black and the grays between them. · George Field · Plate I from Chromatography, London · 1835"],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-hue-value-and-saturation.html","Describing Color · Concept","Hue, Value and Saturation","","ostwald-farbenfibel-triangle","Every color has three properties: hue, its color family (red, blue-green); value, how light or dark it is; and saturation, how strong or grayed it is. · Each can change while the others hold still. The chemist Wilhelm Ostwald's 1921 triangle starts from one yellow: mixed toward white it makes tints along the top edge, toward black shades along the bottom, and toward gray the tones between. All three usually also lower saturation. · Name a color by all three, such as a light, grayed blue-green, rather than by an object like sage. · Black shifts hue as well as value: stirred into yellow, it gives olive greens."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-the-color-wheel.html","Describing Color · Concept","The Color Wheel","","field-chromatography-plate-1","A color wheel arranges hues in a closed ring, running from red through violet and back to red, so every hue has neighbors on both sides. · On the painter's traditional wheel, red, yellow and blue are the primaries. Two primaries mixed give a secondary: orange, green or violet. A primary mixed with the secondary beside it gives a tertiary, such as blue-green. Twelve steps are usual; finer wheels are possible. · Read it as a map of mixing; Analogous, in the module on harmony and contrast, covers how neighbors mix. · This wheel follows paint; light and printing ink start from other primaries (see Mixing Light vs. Mixing Paint). · George Field's 1835 tertiaries mix two secondaries: citrine, russet, olive. Today's are also called intermediates."],[1,"/study/color-theory/foundations/theme-munsells-system.html","Describing Color · Concept","Munsell's System","","munsell-atlas-chroma-branches","Devised by Albert Munsell, this system places each color on three scales. Hue runs around a circle, value up a gray axis from black (0) to white (10), and chroma outward from that axis. · A notation such as 5R 4/14 reads hue, then value over chroma: a strong red a little darker than middle gray. The circle rests on five principal hues, not the painter's three. Munsell compared a chart that ignores chroma to 'a map of Switzerland with the mountains left out.' · On his 1915 chart, red reaches ten steps from the axis at value 4; blue, at the same value, only six. · Every hue shares the one gray axis, so colors of any hue can be compared by value alone. · Chroma has no fixed end; Munsell expected branches to lengthen as stronger pigments appeared."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/value-and-temperature.html","Value and Temperature · Overview","Value and Temperature","","munsell-atlas-value-axis","Light against dark, warm against cool, and the quiet colors in between · Before it is a set of hues, a painting is a pattern of lights and darks. This module looks at that pattern, at the pull between warm and cool, and at the grays and browns that hold a picture together. Each idea is paired with a painting that depends on it. · Reading lights and darks across different hues · Temperature as a comparison rather than a fixed label · Grays mixed from opposites, and pigments dug from the earth · Key · A picture's overall value range: high key is mostly light, low key mostly dark. · Albert H. Munsell · Chart V from Atlas of the Munsell Color System, Malden, Massachusetts · 1915"],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-seeing-light-and-dark.html","Value and Temperature · Concept","Seeing Light and Dark","","munsell-atlas-value-axis","A painting's value pattern is the arrangement of its lights and darks, and it does more than hue to show form, light and depth. · Every hue has its own value at full strength: on Munsell's 1915 chart, yellow sits at 8 of 10, red and blue at 4, purple at 3. The brain reads depth and form largely from differences in lightness, so a well-built picture still holds together in a black-and-white photograph. · Squint: it blurs detail and mutes hue, so the main lights and darks stand out. · Many painters plan a picture in three to five values before they choose any colors. · Strong hues mislead the eye; a vivid red can look lighter than a gray of the same value."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/symphony-in-white-no-1-the-white-girl.html","Value and Temperature","Symphony in White, No. 1: The White Girl","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-white-girl","James McNeill Whistler · 1861–1863, 1872 · Oil on canvas, 213 × 107.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Dress and curtain are almost all white, so the figure is modeled in very small steps of value, in a high key. Red hair and lips, a few flowers and blue carpet pattern give the only strong color; the bearskin is a pale tan. · In February 1862 Whistler told George du Maurier that, apart from the red hair, it was \"one gorgeous mass of brilliant white.\" · A gray underlayer lies in selected areas beneath thick, heavily textured white paint. · Technical study suggests a more colorful floor covering once lay where the bearskin is now. · He later grouped it with two more white pictures as Symphony in White, and said such works were about line and color, not story."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-warm-and-cool.html","Value and Temperature · Concept","Warm and Cool","","field-chromatography-fig-2","Color temperature sorts hues into warm ones, around red, orange and yellow, and cool ones, around green, blue and violet. It always works by comparison. · Any hue can lean either way: a red with a touch of blue looks cool beside a red that leans to orange. Light has temperature too, and painters often see cool shadows under warm light and warm shadows under cool light. · Set two versions of one hue side by side; which one is warmer shows at once. · Many painters lay out a warm and a cool version of each primary, so mixtures can lean either way. · The split is a convention: George Field's 1835 ring runs it from “Hot” orange to “Cold” blue, and border hues such as yellow-green are disputed."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/the-fighting-temeraire.html","Value and Temperature","The Fighting Temeraire","Joseph Mallord William Turner","turner-temeraire","Joseph Mallord William Turner · 1839 · Oil on canvas, 90.7 × 121.6 cm. The National Gallery, London · Turner divided the picture by temperature: a blazing orange sunset fills the right, a cool blue sky with a thin moon the left. The pale old warship moves on the cool side. · He painted the ship white and gold, though its actual paintwork was black and yellow. · The tug's smoke repeats the copper of the clouds, tying the dark boat to the warm side. · Around the sun, the orange, brown, pink and yellow of the clouds go on heavily, in places raised well above the canvas. · The sun’s pale disk is answered by a dark buoy at lower right, a value contrast that anchors the warm side. · John Ruskin observed that Turner often used his reddest sunsets to signal death."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-neutrals-and-earth-colors.html","Value and Temperature · Concept","Neutrals and Earth Colors","","ct-neutrals","Neutrals are colors of very low saturation: grays, browns and near-grays. Earth colors are pigments such as ochres, siennas and umbers, dug from the ground. · Mixing two opposites cancels much of their hue, giving grays that still lean toward one parent. Earths owe their color mainly to iron compounds, with manganese adding the brown of umbers, and they are among the most permanent pigments. · Hold a pure swatch beside a wall or a shadow to see how gray the wall or shadow really is. · Burnt sienna and burnt umber are the raw earths heated, which darkens them and turns sienna from yellow-brown to red."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/ville-davray.html","Value and Temperature","Ville-d'Avray","Camille Corot","corot-ville-davray","Camille Corot · 1870 · Oil on canvas, 54.9 × 80 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Nearly every passage here is a neutral: gray-greens in the foliage, green-black shadow along the bank, and a pewter sky behind the leaves. Even the pond reads as a silvery gray. · Robaut likened the dark branches against the pale sky to a spider's web, a pattern drawn almost entirely in value. · The greens never reach full strength; they lean toward silver in the light and toward near-black in shadow. · By contrast, his early oil sketches, such as those made in Italy, are clearly defined and fresh, with bright colors in fluid strokes. · After about 1850 his style softened and his range of colors narrowed. Many later studio landscapes keep mostly to tinted grays and blue-greens."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/harmony-and-contrast.html","Harmony and Contrast · Overview","Harmony and Contrast","","ct-seven-contrasts","Choosing colors that settle together, or that pull apart · Some color choices tie a picture together; others make parts of it jump out. These pages set out the classic color families a painter can pick from the wheel. They then look at contrast, using one well-known list of its kinds. · One hue, or a run of neighboring hues · Opposites, triads and four-hue schemes · A checklist of seven contrasts · Dull against intense, and small patches against large fields · Color scheme · A planned set of hues, chosen by where they sit on the color wheel. · Seven kinds of color contrast, drawn as swatch pairs"],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-monochromatic.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Monochromatic","","ct-monochromatic","A monochromatic scheme keeps to a single hue and varies only its lightness and strength. · With hue held still, the picture's structure rests almost entirely on light and dark. The result tends to feel calm and unified, and it shows at once whether the values are working. · To spot one, check whether every passage could be one paint, only lightened or darkened. · In practice many allow slight shifts warmer or cooler, or a few small touches of another color. · Without a wide enough value range, a one-hue picture turns flat rather than quiet."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/nocturne-blue-and-silver-battersea-reach.html","Harmony and Contrast","Nocturne: Blue and Silver—Battersea Reach","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-battersea-reach","James McNeill Whistler · 1870–75 · Oil on canvas, 49.9 × 72.3 cm. National Museum of Asian Art (Freer Gallery of Art), Smithsonian Institution, Washington, DC · Moored barges and a far shore of chimneys dissolve into a haze of gray, faintly blue-green. Nearly the whole river at night is held in a single hue. · The title itself names the scheme: blue and silver. · Thin, fluid blue went over a brown underlayer on rough canvas; later reworking left the surface grayer than it began. · The only breaks are tiny dots of warmer color for the lamps along the far shore and their glints on the water."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-analogous.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Analogous","","ct-analogous","An analogous scheme uses hues that sit side by side on the color wheel, usually three to five neighbors. · Neighbors share a parent color, so they read as one family and feel harmonious with little effort. The risk is sameness, which painters offset with changes of light, dark and intensity inside the family. · Look for a picture that stays on one side of the wheel: all cool, or all warm. · Stretch the arc too far and the family breaks apart into colors that contrast."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/water-lilies-agapanthus.html","Harmony and Contrast","Water Lilies (Agapanthus)","Claude Monet","monet-water-lilies-agapanthus","Claude Monet · c. 1915–26 · Oil on canvas, 201.3 × 425.6 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Violets, blues and greens fill nearly every inch of this pond. Only the pale lily pads and a few blossoms step outside the cool family. · With no banks or horizon in view, the water's colors run to every edge. · Violet passages near the top and green ones below both contain blue, so the eye reads them as one surface. · Small orange-red blossoms at the lower right are the strongest accents in the picture. · At over four meters wide, the canvas surrounds anyone standing close with this one color family."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-complementary.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Complementary","","bezold-plate-iv-pairs","Complementary colors sit opposite each other on the color wheel, and side by side each makes the other look more intense. · Between them, each pair contains all three paint primaries: red is set against green, and green is yellow mixed with blue. Bezold's 1876 pairs sit opposite on his twelve-step circle; he notes only purple and green are true complements of light. · To spot a pair, find a picture's main hue and look for its opposite elsewhere in the picture. · Where opposites of matched lightness meet, the edge can flicker; Vibrating and Vanishing Edges explains why."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/the-night-cafe.html","Harmony and Contrast","The Night Café","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-night-cafe","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 72.4 × 92.1 cm. Yale University Art Gallery, New Haven · Blood-red walls press against a green ceiling, and green tables and a pale green bar stand out against the red. Here the opposites are meant to grate, not to please. · Writing to his brother Theo, he said he had tried to show the terrible passions of humanity with red and green. · A day later his letter broke the green into several kinds, from soft malachite tones to harsh blue-greens and yellow-greens. · Saturated yellow, thick on the floor and set in dashes around the lamps, adds a third note; he likened the air to a furnace of pale sulfur. · He called it one of the ugliest pictures he had made. The clash was deliberate: the café was to feel like a place where people ruin themselves."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-triads-and-wider-schemes.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Triads and Wider Schemes","","bezold-plate-iv-triads","Wider schemes space three or four hues around the color wheel. · A triad takes three evenly spaced hues, as in Bezold's four 1876 examples, spaced on his own twelve-step circle. A split complement pairs one hue with the two neighbors of its opposite, and a square takes four evenly spaced hues. These schemes are lively but hard to balance, so most painters let one hue lead and keep the others smaller or duller. · To spot a triad, name a picture's three main colors and check their spacing on the wheel. · A split complement gives much of the punch of opposites with less of a head-on clash. · Three or four distant hues at full strength and equal size compete and look like a flag."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/the-milkmaid.html","Harmony and Contrast","The Milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer","vermeer-milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer · c. 1660 · Oil on canvas, 45.5 × 41 cm. Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam · Yellow, blue and red, the painter's three primaries, carry the figure against a pale wall. The triad is plain to see, yet the picture stays calm. · Blue leads the composition; the yellow bodice and a smaller amount of red skirt support it. · The blue is natural ultramarine; as in several of his paintings, he used it even where cheaper azurite would have done. · The bodice is lead-tin yellow, and the skirt is madder lake laid over charcoal black. · The whitewashed wall, mixed from umber, black and lead white, is a neutral that lets the three hues stand apart."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-ittens-seven-contrasts.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Itten's Seven Contrasts","","ct-seven-contrasts","Johannes Itten sorted the ways colors can differ into seven contrasts: hue, light and dark, warm and cool, complements, simultaneous contrast, saturation and extension. · The list works best as a checklist for looking, not a rule book. Complements appear above, light–dark and warm–cool in Value and Temperature, and simultaneous contrast in Interaction; the last two follow here. · Hue contrast is plainest when hues sit far apart on the wheel at full strength, as in a triad. · For any two passages in a painting, ask which of the seven differences are doing the work. · The seven overlap: a red beside a green differs in hue and often in value at once."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-contrast-of-saturation.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Contrast of Saturation","","munsell-atlas-chroma-steps","This contrast sets pure, intense colors against dull, grayed ones. · An intense color looks stronger among muted ones and weaker among other intense colors. Painters use this to decide where the eye lands without changing hue or lightness. Since mixing rarely raises intensity, dulling the surroundings is often the easier route. · It needs no second hue: Munsell's red, stepped from gray to full strength, already contrasts with itself. · Painters gray down most of a picture so a few saturated passages can carry the focus. · Making every color intense backfires: when everything shouts, nothing stands out."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/manuel-osorio-manrique-de-zuniga.html","Harmony and Contrast","Manuel Osorio Manrique de Zuñiga","Francisco de Goya","goya-red-boy","Francisco de Goya · 1787–88 · Oil on canvas, 127 × 101.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A boy's red suit is the only large patch of intense color, set in a room of soft grays and browns. Much of its force comes from how muted its surroundings are. · The suit gave the portrait its nickname, the \"Red Boy,\" used in the museum's own publications. · The sash and lace collar are nearly colorless, so nothing else competes with the red. · The cats are mostly gray, black and white, with warm patches on one and amber eyes; the magpie is black and white. · The birdcage is a muted green, the red's opposite, answering the suit without matching its strength. · Small touches of red and yellow on the caged finches echo the suit on a tiny scale."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-contrast-of-extension.html","Harmony and Contrast · Concept","Contrast of Extension","","ct-extension","Here the contrast is one of area: a small patch of one color can balance a much larger field of another. · Light, strong colors need less room than dark ones to make an equal impression. Itten set area ratios for the complement pairs from rough estimates of how strongly each hue acts on the eye. He credited Goethe, but the figures appear to go back to Schopenhauer's 1816 essay on color. · Ask which color claims the most area, and which small patch still catches the eye. · Painters often break the balance on purpose: a small, strong accent in a wide field becomes the focus. · The ratios assume full-strength hues; dull a color or change its lightness and the balance shifts."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/river-with-a-distant-tower.html","Harmony and Contrast","River with a Distant Tower","Camille Corot","corot-river-tower","Camille Corot · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 54.6 × 78.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Gray-greens in the trees and a pale, dull river and sky take up nearly all of the canvas. Specks of red mark the boatman's cap and a child at the left. · Red sits opposite green on the painter's wheel, so these small accents gain force from the field around them. · The red covers only a sliver of the surface, yet the eye can move between these few points. · Around them the picture stays low in saturation, which leaves the reds unchallenged."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/interaction.html","Interaction · Overview","Interaction","","seurat-circus-sideshow","How neighboring colors change one another in the eye · No color is ever seen on its own. This module covers the main cases: colors side by side, colors seen one after another, small marks the eye blends, and shared edges. Each page has a diagram you can test on yourself. · One paint made to look like two · Ghost colors that follow a steady stare · Dots and fine lines that merge at a distance · Edges that shimmer or vanish, and flat shapes that look like glass · Context · The colors around a color, which change how it looks. · Georges Seurat · Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque) · 1887–88"],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-simultaneous-contrast.html","Interaction · Concept","Simultaneous Contrast","","bezold-plate-iii","A color seen beside another seems to shift away from it, so two paints can look alike, or far apart, on different grounds. · The eye exaggerates differences at a boundary. On Wilhelm von Bezold's 1876 plates, two different reds look nearly alike on his purple (now faded to crimson) and yellow (left); swap the grounds and they split apart (center); on neutral paper their true difference shows (right). How Chevreul came to it is told on Chevreul and Colors Side by Side, in Module 1. · Mask a color with a scrap of gray card to see it apart from its neighbors. · Painters judge a mixture on the canvas, beside what will surround it, not on the palette. · Knowing the two disks differ does not make them look different, even to a trained eye."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-afterimages.html","Interaction · Concept","Afterimages","","ct-afterimage","An afterimage is a ghost color seen after staring at a color, usually close to its opposite: a strong red leaves a pale blue-green. · Steady looking tires the parts of the eye that respond to that color, so a white surface seen next looks tinged with its opposite. Chevreul called this successive contrast, because it unfolds over time rather than side by side. Goethe saw a girl in a scarlet bodice reappear on a white wall in sea-green. · Stare at the dot in the red disk for half a minute, then look at the blank square. · After long work on one strong color, rest your eyes before judging the next mixture. · Afterimages fade in seconds and vary between viewers, so they are only a rough guide."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-optical-mixing.html","Interaction · Concept","Optical Mixing","","bezold-plate-v-fig-3","Small touches of different colors, seen from far enough away, merge into one color in the eye instead of on the palette. · The eye averages the light from each touch, so the result stays lighter than the same paints stirred together. Bezold printed a test case: from a distance, red ornament on blue shows “a tinge of purple,” yet vermilion and ultramarine mixed on the palette make “a dirty reddish brown.” · Walk toward a dotted passage and watch one color split into its parts. · Painters set dots or broken strokes side by side to keep mixtures clean and lively. · Dots of opposite colors tend to average toward gray, so painters after a clean mixture can pair neighboring hues."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/circus-sideshow-parade-de-cirque.html","Interaction","Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque)","Georges Seurat","seurat-circus-sideshow","Georges Seurat · 1887–88 · Oil on canvas, 99.7 × 149.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A night scene under gaslight, built from dots and dashes instead of blended paint. Up close the figures break apart into separate touches of blue, red and orange. · Cobalt blue, ground very fine and at times mixed with red lake or emerald green, supplies the strokes that read as black. · Each area began with large dots of its main color, followed by ever smaller touches. · Finishing touches of tiny orange dots, vermilion with chrome yellow, imitate gaslight on the people in the scene. · The cast is mostly violet and green, warmed in the lit areas. The canvas has never been varnished. · He called his method chromo-luminarism, stressing the glow he was after. He had read the color theories of Chevreul and Ogden Rood."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-the-bezold-effect.html","Interaction · Concept","The Bezold Effect","","bezold-plate-v-figs-5-6","Changing one color in a pattern can shift the look of all the others, seen most clearly when fine lines switch from white to black. · In Fig. 5 of Bezold's plate, from his book on color in art and the decorative trades, one red carries black ornament on the left and white on the right: it reads darker beside black, lighter beside white. In Fig. 6, red ornament on black, it darkens more still. This pull toward a neighbor is called assimilation, or spreading. · From across the room, the one red splits into a light and a dark version. · It is the reverse of simultaneous contrast, where a neighbor pushes a color away from itself. · Changing one thread or outline color can recolor a whole rug or textile design."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-vibrating-and-vanishing-edges.html","Interaction · Concept","Vibrating and Vanishing Edges","","ct-vibrating-edges","Where two colors of the same value meet, the edge can seem to shimmer or to dissolve. It shimmers when the hues are strong and far apart, and dissolves when they are dull and close. · The eye finds edges most sharply through differences in lightness. Without that cue, a bright red-green border flickers, while a muted pair gives the eye nothing firm to hold. Albers called these vibrating and vanishing boundaries. · Photograph the strips in black and white: matched values turn into nearly one flat gray. · Painters soften a contour by matching values across it, and sharpen one with a light-dark step. · Bezold advised a lighter or darker outline between closely related colors, so the eye can follow the design."],[1,"/study/color-theory/relationships/theme-transparency-and-space.html","Interaction · Concept","Transparency and Space","","ct-transparency","Flat, opaque colors can look like overlapping sheets of tinted glass when the shared area is painted in a color between the two. · The eye reads the in-between color as one sheet seen through another, so a flat design gains layers and depth. If the overlap looks more like one of the two colors, that color tends to seem on top and come forward. · Compare the right-hand pair: an unrelated color in the overlap breaks the illusion at once. · Painters and designers use it to suggest veils, water or tinted light without real glazes. · Albers's name for the in-between color is the middle mixture."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/paint-and-pigment.html","Paint and Pigment · Overview","Paint and Pigment","","titian-bacchus-ariadne","Where colors come from, and why some don't last · Paint is pigment held in a binder, and each pigment has its own strengths and weaknesses. The pages move from raw materials to the palette, then to what binder, grinding and light do to a color. The last page compares building color in layers with laying it on all at once. · Pigments from earth, minerals, dyes and modern chemistry · Limited and full palettes · How binder, grinding and light change a color · Glazing and direct painting · Binder · The oil, egg or gum that holds pigment and makes it stick to a surface. · Titian · Bacchus and Ariadne · 1520–23"],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-where-pigments-come-from.html","Paint and Pigment · Concept","Where Pigments Come From","","ct-pigment-sources","A pigment is a colored powder that does not dissolve in its binder; painters' pigments come from colored earths, crushed minerals, plant and insect dyes, and chemistry. · Ground minerals can keep something of the stone's color. Ultramarine is made from lapis lazuli, and azurite is a copper ore. Dyes from madder root or cochineal insects must first be made into lakes. From the early 1700s chemists added new colors; Prussian blue was among the first. · Read the tube: the pigment name says what is inside, and 'hue' marks a substitute. · Painters choose by more than color: opacity, drying time, cost and permanence all differ. · Several historic pigments are toxic, among them vermilion, a mercury compound, and lead white. · Lake · A pigment made by fixing a dye onto a fine, nearly colorless powder."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/bacchus-and-ariadne.html","Paint and Pigment","Bacchus and Ariadne","Titian","titian-bacchus-ariadne","Titian · 1520–23 · Oil on canvas, 176.5 × 191 cm. The National Gallery, London · Each of the strongest pigments then sold in Venice gets its own broad area of the canvas. Each is mixed only with white, and each is used at full strength. · Ultramarine, the strongest color in the picture, fills the sky, fading from deep blue at the top to a paler band above the horizon. · The distant hills are azurite, a slightly greener blue that keeps them apart from the sky. · The rust-brown tree at upper right is iron-oxide ochre, brown on purpose in a spring landscape; Titian may have wanted a warm contrast in that corner. · Skin tones, from pale Ariadne to a satyr's dark russet, recur across the canvas and tie the strong colors together."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-limited-palettes.html","Paint and Pigment · Concept","Limited Palettes","","ct-limited-palette","A limited palette is a deliberately small set of paints from which every color in a picture is mixed. · Because every mixture shares the same few ingredients, the colors hang together almost by themselves. The cost is range: without a true blue, cool colors can only be suggested, by setting grays from black and white beside warm reds and ochres. · Try it: paint a small study from white, ochre, a red and black, then add one paint at a time. · In the diagram's middle row, every mixture comes from the four paints above it. · Clear greens, violets and bright blues are out of reach, so landscapes and flowers usually need more paints."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/frieda-schiff-1876-1958-later-mrs-felix-m-warburg.html","Paint and Pigment","Frieda Schiff (1876–1958), Later Mrs. Felix M. Warburg","Anders Zorn","zorn-frieda-schiff","Anders Zorn · 1894 · Oil on canvas, 101 × 76.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A pink gown, warm skin and a background of dark red-brown at right and pale gray-beige at left keep the picture mostly warm. The coolest notes are the soft grays of the patterned hanging. · The pink of her first evening gown goes on in quick strokes that change length and direction. · Tradition says he used only four colors: white, yellow ochre, vermilion and black. · Research published by the Zorn Museum in 2023, based on his paints, palettes and orders, found more, including cobalt blue, viridian and cadmium yellow. · The museum record does not list the paints in this canvas, so its exact palette is not known."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-how-pigments-behave.html","Paint and Pigment · Concept","How Pigments Behave","","ct-pigment-behavior","The same pigment can look different depending on the binder that holds it, how finely it is ground and how long it has been exposed to light. · In oil, azurite can darken or drift toward green, while ultramarine keeps its hue but looks darker. A mineral ground finer scatters more white light and turns paler. Light breaks down dyes. Red lakes are among the least stable, so pinks drain toward white and violets mixed with them turn bluer. · Look for passages that seem oddly pale, dark or cool beside the rest of a picture. · Choose lightfast pigments; the ASTM standards for artists' paints set lightfastness requirements. · Fading cannot be reversed. Museums mainly slow it by keeping light low and filtering out ultraviolet."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/irises.html","Paint and Pigment","Irises","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-irises","Vincent van Gogh · 1890 · Oil on canvas, 73.7 × 92.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Van Gogh planned this bouquet as a soft harmony: violet irises on a pink ground, tied together by green leaves. Fading has upset that balance. · He painted a second irises canvas as its opposite: violet flowers against a bright lemon yellow. · In a May 1890 letter he set them side by side: the yellow one built on complements that strengthen each other, this one on related greens, pinks and violets. · Both the pink and the violets contained a red pigment that has faded. The flowers now read bluer and the ground near-white, far from the soft effect he described. · The yellow-ground version is in the Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam. The Met reunited the four bouquets in 2015 to show what fading had changed."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-glazing-and-direct-painting.html","Paint and Pigment · Concept","Glazing and Direct Painting","","ct-glazing","Glazing builds color in thin transparent layers over dry paint; direct painting mixes each color on the palette and lays it on opaque, often wet into wet. · Light passes through a glaze and reflects off the layer below, giving a glow an opaque mixture cannot match. Direct painting trades that depth for speed and freshness, since each stroke shows its final color at once. · Glazed darks look deep and jewel-like; direct painting shows solid, often thick strokes. · Transparent pigments such as red lakes suit glazing; white makes them opaque and chalkier. · Each glaze must dry before the next goes on, so the method is slow. · Alla prima · Italian for 'at the first attempt': a painting finished in one wet session."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/a-muse-calliope.html","Paint and Pigment","A Muse (Calliope?)","Cosimo Tura","tura-muse","Cosimo Tura · Probably 1455–60 · Oil over egg tempera on wood (poplar), 116.2 × 71.1 cm. The National Gallery, London · A crimson mantle, a dark blue dress, a green skirt and red sleeves shot with gold glow from layered paint. Oil was still uncommon in Italy in the 1450s, and here it carries the final layers of color. · Under the oil lies an earlier, partly finished design in egg tempera. · The richest passages are built up in translucent oil layers over more solid paint, an effect beyond egg tempera. · For pale colors he chose walnut oil, the less yellow of the two oils he used, to keep them bright."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/composition-and-meaning.html","Composition and Meaning · Overview","Composition and Meaning","","gauguin-day-of-god","What color does in a picture beyond describing things · A painter can use color to build depth, set a mood or send a message. Some of these effects rest on how the eye works; others rest on habits a culture teaches. Four paintings, from a seventeenth-century landscape to a modern image of anxiety, show each one at work. · Haze, value and hue as cues to distance · Color taken from life versus color invented · Palettes chosen to steer a viewer's feelings · Colors that stand for ideas, and who can read them · Paul Gauguin · Mahana no atua (Day of the God) · 1894"],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-color-and-space.html","Composition and Meaning · Concept","Color and Space","","ct-color-space","Atmospheric perspective, also called aerial perspective, shows depth by painting distant things lighter, bluer and lower in contrast than near ones. · Air scatters short-wavelength blue light more than other colors, so far-off dark forms take on a bluish cast. Thick haze from moisture or dust whitens the distance instead. Hue can also push a flat shape forward or back: on black, most viewers see red as nearer than blue, though some see the reverse. · Compare a distant ridge with the sky behind it; the value gap narrows as distance grows. · Landscape painters keep their darkest darks and sharpest edges in front and veil the background. · That red-blue effect is weaker on a white ground and tends to reverse, so it depends on the setting."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/sunrise.html","Composition and Meaning","Sunrise","Claude Lorrain","claude-sunrise","Claude Lorrain · possibly 1646–47 · Oil on canvas, 102.9 × 134 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Today the depth reads mostly through light: the brightest values sit at the low horizon, and the land steps darker toward the viewer. A pale blue-gray mountain at the left marks the farthest distance. · The Met's catalogue credits the low horizon, thin pink clouds and widely spaced, see-through trees for the sense of light and breadth. · The foreground and middle ground have darkened with age, a not uncommon change in Claude's canvases, while the horizon kept its glow; the steps from near to far are now harder to see. · Claude set his idealized countryside near Rome in the light of sunrise or sunset. A low sun puts the lightest passage at the most distant point."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-local-and-expressive-color.html","Composition and Meaning · Concept","Local and Expressive Color","","ct-local-expressive","Local color is the color an object has in plain daylight, apart from its surroundings; expressive color is a color the painter chooses for effect, whether or not the object could have it. · Between the two lies observed color: the object as actually lit and changed by its neighbors. Where a painter sits on this range tells the viewer how literally to take the picture. · Ask whether any real light could produce a color; in ordinary daylight, a pink hill under a green sky could not. · Painters often keep drawing and values believable while freeing hue, so the scene still reads. · Local color alone tends to look flat because it ignores light; many painters treat it as a starting point."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/mahana-no-atua-day-of-the-god.html","Composition and Meaning","Mahana no atua (Day of the God)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-day-of-god","Paul Gauguin · 1894 · Oil on linen canvas, 68 × 91 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · The pool along the bottom is not water-colored at all: it breaks into flat shapes of yellow, orange, pink, blue and near-black. The bank above it is pink. · He painted it from memory and fantasy rather than from a scene in front of him. · The Art Institute ranks it among his most abstract works; the pool's interlocking color zones show why. · The idol at the center is green and blue-black, unlike the warm browns of the people around it. · Farther back, sea, hills and sky stay nearer to natural color, so the invented foreground stands out."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-color-and-emotion.html","Composition and Meaning · Concept","Color and Emotion","","goethe-schiller-temperamentenrose","Color carries emotion when a painter sets hue, value and saturation to push viewers toward a feeling rather than to describe what is there. · Goethe and Schiller's 1798–99 rose matched hues to temperaments. Yet the effect comes from the whole scheme more than any one hue: how strong the colors are, how far apart their values sit, and whether they clash or settle. · Cover the subject and ask what the colors alone suggest before reading the story. · Painters often push one property hard, such as saturation or value contrast, and keep the rest quiet. · A palette alone rarely fixes a feeling; subject, drawing and title steer how viewers read it."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/the-scream.html","Composition and Meaning","The Scream","Edvard Munch","munch-scream","Edvard Munch · 1893 · Tempera and crayon on cardboard, 91 × 73.5 cm. Nasjonalmuseet, Oslo · Bands of red, orange and yellow fill the sky above the fjord, and the same curving strokes run through land, water and figure. The museum calls the colors non-realistic and ties them to the subject, anxiety. · In notebook lines written before the painting, Munch recalled a sunset sky turning blood red, with flames over the blue-black fjord. · A year earlier he used the same colors, format and landscape in Sick Mood at Sunset; chiefly the central figure changed. · In the later painted version at the Munch Museum, cadmium yellow strokes in the sky have faded to off-white, mainly from damp, not light. · Munch also made a black-and-white lithograph of the motif in 1895; some impressions were colored by hand in watercolor."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/theme-color-and-symbol.html","Composition and Meaning · Concept","Color and Symbol","","goethe-farbenkreis-1809","A color becomes a symbol when a culture attaches a set meaning to it, so the color can be read as a sign. · The meaning can come from tradition, from the cost of the material, or both. European painters often gave the Virgin Mary a mantle of ultramarine, the most expensive blue, which honored her and showed what a patron could afford. · A symbol needs a viewer who knows the code; without it, the same blue is just blue. · Painters can use a traditional color to name a figure at a glance, before any detail is read. · Meanings differ by culture and period: Goethe's 1809 circle calls red “schön” (beautiful) and blue “gemein” (common)."],[1,"/study/color-theory/color-in-painting/the-virgin-in-prayer.html","Composition and Meaning","The Virgin in Prayer","Sassoferrato","sassoferrato-virgin-prayer","Sassoferrato · 1640–50 · Oil on canvas, 73 × 57.7 cm. The National Gallery, London · Veil, dress and mantle supply nearly the whole palette: a creamy white, a rose red and an intense blue. The blue is ultramarine, the strongest color on the canvas. · Set against a background close to black and lit from one side, the blue looks brighter still. · In a 2014 National Gallery talk, curator Caroline Campbell linked ultramarine's intense blue to the meaning of devotional pictures like this. · According to the National Gallery, the mantle was once covered with cobalt blue, a softer color more to Victorian taste; taking that layer off uncovered the ultramarine. · The same pigment could carry a worldly meaning: in Pierre Mignard's 1691 portrait of the Marquise de Seignelay, also in London, a lavish ultramarine robe displays her wealth."],[2,"/study/composition.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/a-short-history.html","A Short History · Overview","A Short History","","dow-masters-harmonies","From Renaissance windows to Japanese notan · Advice on composition goes back six centuries: a window in the Renaissance, rules in the academies, and a new vocabulary from Japan. · Alberti's Window · The Academy's Rules · Dow, Japan and Notan · Plate · Dark and Light Harmonies from the Masters · 1914"],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-albertis-window.html","A Short History · History","Alberti's Window","","poore-picture-vision","In 1435 Leon Battista Alberti wrote that a painting works like an open window onto its subject. · He made composition one of the painter's three tasks: fitting the parts into one picture. Poore's drawing shows a related idea: the frame as a cut from the whole field of view. · To copy a view, Alberti advised looking through a thin veil divided by threads into squares. · Raphael ruled the Garvagh Madonna into four equal parts; the center line became the Virgin's axis (National Gallery, London)."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-the-academys-rules.html","A Short History · History","The Academy's Rules","","poore-chiaroscuro-forms","From the 1600s, academies and writers set rules for good pictures. · The French Royal Academy, founded in 1648, ranked subjects from history painting down to still life. · In 1783 Reynolds described blotting Venetian pictures: no more than a quarter light, a quarter dark, half in between. · In 1797 J. T. Smith coined a \"rule of thirds\": over two-thirds in shadow, and sky, land or water split about 2 to 1. · He meant a ratio of areas and lines, not today's grid of nine equal boxes."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-dow-japan-and-notan.html","A Short History · History","Dow, Japan and Notan","","dow-hokusai-three-values","Japanese prints reached Western artists after Japan reopened to trade in the 1850s. · Painters such as Degas borrowed their elongated formats and off-center layouts. In Boston, Arthur Wesley Dow built a course shaped by Japanese art; his book Composition appeared in 1899. · He taught three elements: line, notan and color. · Notan, Japanese for \"dark-light,\" is the arrangement of light and dark shapes. · His preface credits Ernest Fenollosa of Boston's Museum of Fine Arts, who lectured in Dow's first such classes, in 1889."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/the-frame.html","The Frame · Overview","The Frame","","poore-view-metre","The shape of the picture, and where its edges cut · The first decisions come before any subject: the shape of the picture and what its edges leave out. · Format and the Picture Plane · Cropping and the Edge · Plate · The View-Metre · c. 1915"],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-format-and-the-picture-plane.html","The Frame · Concept","Format and the Picture Plane","","dow-three-proportions","Format is the shape of the picture: horizontal, vertical, square or round. The picture plane is its flat surface. · Dow redrew one house and tree in three formats; in the tallest, the tree towers. Every picture is both a view into space and a flat pattern on a surface. · Pattern \"is often in conflict with pictorial space,\" says Steven Zucker of Smarthistory. · In a 2014 talk, curator Simon Kelly suggested painters in the late 1800s moved from picture-as-window to picture-as-surface."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/view-of-ipswich-or-a-bend-in-the-river.html","The Frame","View of Ipswich, or A Bend in the River","Arthur Wesley Dow","dow-view-of-ipswich","Arthur Wesley Dow · ca. 1895 · Color woodcut, image 12.7 × 5.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The Met links this tall, narrow frame, only about 13 cm high, to Japanese pillar prints. · Look how the narrow format stacks the landscape from bottom to top. · No black key block outlines the forms; flat patches of color sit side by side."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/the-alba-madonna.html","The Frame","The Alba Madonna","Raphael","raphael-alba-madonna","Raphael · c. 1510 · Oil on panel transferred to canvas, diameter 94.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A round picture, called a tondo, is \"really hard\" to fill comfortably, says Smarthistory's Beth Harris. · Look at the Virgin's bent leg and elbow and John's fur garment: the National Gallery of Art notes how they echo the round edge. · Smarthistory's Steven Zucker notes the figures \"don't feel cramped.\" · The circle, the museum adds, evokes the heavens."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/theme-cropping-and-the-edge.html","The Frame · Concept","Cropping and the Edge","","dow-rectangles-various-shape","Cropping is where the edge of the frame cuts the scene, and what it leaves out. · Dow framed one stand of trees five ways, and each edge slices the trunks differently. · Degas took inventive cropping from Japanese prints, the Met notes. · He also saw sixteenth-century painters sometimes cut off part of a figure."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/a-woman-seated-beside-a-vase-of-flowers-madame-paul-valpincon.html","The Frame","A Woman Seated beside a Vase of Flowers (Madame Paul Valpinçon?)","Edgar Degas","degas-woman-seated-beside-a-vase-of-flowers","Edgar Degas · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 73.7 × 92.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The woman is pushed to the right and cut off by the edge, while a large bouquet takes the center. · Look at the lower right corner: only a sliver of her hand is left in. · Met curator Alison Hokanson describes her as \"squished in at the right.\" · She argues Degas planned such skewed layouts to give an effect of chance."],[2,"/study/composition/foundations/paris-street-rainy-day.html","The Frame","Paris Street; Rainy Day","Gustave Caillebotte","caillebotte-paris-street-rainy-day","Gustave Caillebotte · 1877 · Oil on canvas, 212.2 × 276.2 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · The main figures stand off-center, and the man at the right edge is cut in half. · The Art Institute suggests photography may lie behind the bold cropping. · The central couple look toward something outside the frame. · Yet nothing back there is accidental: the Art Institute says each distant figure was positioned to make the street feel crowded."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/shape-and-value.html","Shape and Value · Overview","Shape and Value","","dow-massing-two-values","Shapes, gaps and the pattern of dark and light · Under the subject, every picture is a pattern of shapes and of dark and light. Strong pictures plan both. · Positive and Negative Space · Notan and Value Massing · Emphasis and Focal Point · Plate · Massing in Two Values, from Corot, Daubigny and Hokusai · 1914"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-positive-and-negative-space.html","Shape and Value · Concept","Positive and Negative Space","","dow-notan-figure-ground","Positive space is the subject; negative space is the area around it. · The gaps are shapes too. In Dow's black-and-white panels, neither tone is simply background: both are designed. · Dow wrote that good notan \"is dependent upon good spacing.\" · A plain background makes a figure stand out; in a 2014 talk, curator Simon Kelly suggested Van Gogh took this from Japanese prints."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/laziness-la-paresse.html","Shape and Value","Laziness (La Paresse)","Félix Vallotton","vallotton-laziness","Félix Vallotton · 1896 · Woodcut, image 17.8 × 22.2 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · There is no gray here: only black ink and bare paper. · The Art Institute notes that black and white alone give him pattern, space and solid form. · Look at the pale body: it is mostly bare paper, shaped by the black around it."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-notan-and-value-massing.html","Shape and Value · Concept","Notan and Value Massing","","dow-notan-spotting","Value massing groups a picture's lights and darks into a few big shapes, in two or three values: Dow's notan. · Here Dow cut pictures by Manet, Millet, Corot and others down to flat black and white; what survives is the design. For value itself, see Color Theory. · Dow kept notan apart from light and shadow: it is a pattern the artist designs, not a fact of nature. · It holds, he wrote, \"whether colored or not.\""],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/under-the-wave-off-kanagawa-the-great-wave.html","Shape and Value","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-under-the-wave-off-kanagawa","Katsushika Hokusai · ca. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 37.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Hokusai builds the scene from a handful of dark blue and pale shapes. · Look inside the wave: its dark blue gathers into broad bands. · White foam and pale sky make the light masses around them. · Behind Fuji, a band of dark gray sky sets off its white peak."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-emphasis-and-focal-point.html","Shape and Value · Concept","Emphasis and Focal Point","","poore-lion-desert","The focal point is the part that draws the most attention. Emphasis is how the painter makes it do so. · The main tools are contrast, isolation and placement. In Gérôme's desert, one small dark shape alone in an empty field stands out. · J. T. Smith (1797) advised one main light, with the others smaller and dimmer: \"Two distinct, equal lights, should never appear in the same picture.\" · Reynolds wrote that a light looks brightest when the most shade surrounds it; Color Theory explains why."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/the-night-watch.html","Shape and Value","The Night Watch","Rembrandt van Rijn","rembrandt-night-watch","Rembrandt van Rijn · 1642 · Oil on canvas, 379.5 × 453.5 cm. Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam (on loan from the City of Amsterdam) · In a crowd of guardsmen, a few lit details stand out. · The Rijksmuseum points to two: the captain's gesturing hand and a young girl in the background. · The museum identifies the girl as the company's mascot. · It also calls Rembrandt the first to show the sitters in a group portrait \"actually doing something.\""],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/the-denial-of-saint-peter.html","Shape and Value","The Denial of Saint Peter","Caravaggio","caravaggio-denial-of-saint-peter","Caravaggio · 1610 · Oil on canvas, 94 × 125.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The Met notes that his late paintings get their force from strong light against near-total darkness. · The Met reads the hands as three accusations: the soldier's pointing finger and the woman's two fingers, matching Peter's three denials. · Light catches faces, hands, the helmet and the robes; the room behind stays dark. · The Met calls it \"a marvel of narrative concision.\""],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/balance-and-structure.html","Balance and Structure · Overview","Balance and Structure","","poore-boats-balance","How a picture holds steady · Balance keeps a picture from tipping to one side. Underneath, a simple structure can hold the parts together. · Symmetric Balance · Asymmetric Balance and Visual Weight · Radial Balance · Armatures · Thirds, Grids and the Golden Ratio · Plate · Four Sailboat Sketches (untitled figure) · c. 1915"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-symmetric-balance.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Symmetric Balance","","poore-salute-wounded","Balance is how a picture's parts are spread so it feels steady. Symmetric balance mirrors one side in the other around a central axis. · Poore drew a center line through Detaille's scene to show two nearly equal groups. Dow called symmetry \"the most common and obvious way of satisfying the desire for order.\" · Dow noted it turns up in \"cheap and mean design\" where beauty of form is ignored. · Poore linked strict, equal balance more with decoration than with pictures."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/madonna-and-child-enthroned-with-saints-colonna-altarpiece.html","Balance and Structure","Madonna and Child Enthroned with Saints (Colonna Altarpiece)","Raphael","raphael-colonna-altarpiece","Raphael · ca. 1504 · Oil and gold on wood, main panel 172.4 × 172.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Look for the mirror: the Virgin sits on the central axis, with God the Father centered above. · Two female saints flank the throne; two weighty male saints stand in front, one on each side. · In the arched panel on top, angels and seraphim flank God the Father in the same way."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-asymmetric-balance-and-visual-weight.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Asymmetric Balance and Visual Weight","","poore-steelyard","Asymmetric balance uses unlike sides that still feel even. Visual weight is how strongly any part pulls on the eye. · Poore compared it to a steelyard, a scale where a small weight far from the pivot balances a big one close to it. · A shape alone weighs more than the same shape in a group, Poore said. · Strong contrast adds weight, Poore noted; the Getty adds color and texture. · The small balancing weight must not become a rival point of interest."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair.html","Balance and Structure","Little Girl in a Blue Armchair","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair","Mary Cassatt · 1878 · Oil on canvas, 89.5 × 129.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The big blue chair and the sprawling girl sit right of center; a small dark dog in a matching chair and an open stretch of floor answer them at left. · Cassatt moved the dog from the floor up to the chair, the National Gallery of Art found. · She wrote that Degas advised her; he added the room's corner, a wide diagonal that deepens the space."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-radial-balance.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Radial Balance","","dow-radial-circles","In radial balance, parts spread out from a central point, like spokes from a hub. · Dow's four two-value wheels keep one hub and vary the spokes and rings. Poore counted the circle and radiating lines among his basic forms. · The parts around the center are often alike. · A round format suits it, but a round picture need not be radial. · Look for it in rose windows, wheels and mandalas."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/mandala-of-jnanadakini.html","Balance and Structure","Mandala of Jnanadakini","Unknown artist, Tibet","mandala-of-jnanadakini","Unknown artist, Tibet · late 14th century · Distemper (glue-based paint) on cloth, image 74.9 × 83.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The six-armed goddess sits at the exact center, ringed by eight smaller figures. · Rings of lotus petals, thunderbolt symbols and flames circle outward from them. · Colors mark the four quarters, one for each direction. · Four guardian goddesses stand in the gateways."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-armatures.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Armatures","","poore-fundamental-forms","An armature is a simple shape that holds a picture's main masses together: a triangle, circle, cross, S-curve or L. · Poore's chart sets each form beside small pictures built on it. The triangle means stability; in landscape, he said, it lies on its side, pointing into the distance. · The L-shape, he thought, is the only form that needs an opposing mass to balance it. · An armature found by drawing over a print is a reading, not proof of the artist's plan."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/the-harvesters.html","Balance and Structure","The Harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder","bruegel-harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder · 1565 · Oil on oak, 119 × 162 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A stooping reaper, the Met's catalogue notes, forms a triangle that echoes the wheat she is cutting. · Look for the same triangle in the sheaves standing in the field. · The view itself is built: Flanders is flat, so \"this is not a real landscape,\" says the Met's Adam Eaker."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-thirds-grids-and-the-golden-ratio.html","Balance and Structure · Concept","Thirds, Grids and the Golden Ratio","","poore-strong-points","Placement rules promise the best spots in a frame; the evidence is thin. · Poore's grid argues that the center is weak and unequal divisions, such as thirds, are strong: a period opinion. · In 727 paintings, the rule of thirds \"seems to play only a minor, if any, role\" (2014 study). · In UC Berkeley tests, viewers preferred a single front-facing object centered. · Martin Kemp knows of no Renaissance drawing with a hidden golden ratio. · Lab tests find a real but fragile preference for it (Green, 1995)."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/circus-sideshow-parade-de-cirque.html","Balance and Structure","Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque)","Georges Seurat","seurat-circus-sideshow","Georges Seurat · 1887–88 · Oil on canvas, 99.7 × 149.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Golden-ratio claims about this painting come from measuring it. Roger Fischler's 1981 study found no sign Seurat used the ratio. · Look at the stiff horizontals, verticals and rows of lights: easy to fit a grid to, which is why overlays tempt. · Simple ratios such as 5:8 sit close to the golden ratio, so a near fit proves little. · His real planning shows in his studies for La Grande Jatte; see Checking a Composition."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/movement-and-depth.html","Movement and Depth · Overview","Movement and Depth","","poore-hogarth-line","Moving the eye across and into the picture · Lines, repeats and depth cues move the eye across the surface and back into space. How well they steer it is a fair question. · Line and Direction · Rhythm and Repetition · Leading the Eye · Depth by Design · Plate · Hogarth's Line of Beauty · c. 1915"],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-line-and-direction.html","Movement and Depth · Concept","Line and Direction","","poore-aesthetics-of-line","Lines, edges and gestures point the eye in a direction, and each direction can carry a mood. · Poore matched pictures to kinds of line: vertical for dignity, horizontal for calm, curve for movement, diagonal for speed. · The Getty defines movement as \"the path the viewer's eye takes through the work of art, often to focal areas.\" · In Leonardo's Last Supper, the lines of the room meet on Christ, drawing attention to him (Smarthistory)."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/the-death-of-socrates.html","Movement and Depth","The Death of Socrates","Jacques Louis David","david-death-of-socrates","Jacques Louis David · 1787 · Oil on canvas, 129.5 × 196.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The figures spread left and right along the couch in a flat band the Met calls \"friezelike\" and \"planar horizontal.\" · Against that calm horizontal, Socrates' raised arm points upward. · His other hand reaches for the cup of hemlock. · The disciple handing it to him turns away, unable to look. · Plato sits at the foot of the bed, closing the line at left."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-rhythm-and-repetition.html","Movement and Depth · Concept","Rhythm and Repetition","","dow-repetition","Repetition uses the same line or shape again. Rhythm is what it creates: a sense of organized movement. · Dow called repetition probably the oldest form of design. Equal spacing gives a pattern, like his border; uneven spacing is what you find in landscape, like his gables. · Some variety, the Getty adds, keeps a rhythm lively. · Look for repeats in a picture's edges and gaps, not only its objects."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/sudden-shower-over-shin-ohashi-bridge-and-atake.html","Movement and Depth","Sudden Shower over Shin-Ōhashi Bridge and Atake","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-sudden-shower","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 34 × 24.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Rain falls in rows of fine parallel lines across the whole print. · Under it, the bridge cuts a long diagonal, with small figures hurrying across. · The bridge's posts repeat in a second, slower beat below the deck."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-leading-the-eye.html","Movement and Depth · Concept","Leading the Eye","","poore-shepherdess-exit","Painters plan where the eye goes. Eye-tracking studies test whether it does. · Poore wanted one entrance and one exit for the eye. His Millet example has two, which he counted a fault: \"a case of divided interest.\" · In 1935 Buswell recorded 200 viewers' eye movements and found large differences between people. · A 2020 study of landscape art found composition pulls the eye to the main elements, but the order of looking varies."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/young-woman-with-a-water-pitcher.html","Movement and Depth","Young Woman with a Water Pitcher","Johannes Vermeer","vermeer-young-woman-with-a-water-pitcher","Johannes Vermeer · ca. 1662 · Oil on canvas, 45.7 × 40.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Light from the window at left falls on the headdress and collar, then turns the pitcher and basin gold. · On the Met's audio guide, the light is said to be \"guiding me around the room.\" · A dark, shadowy corner at lower left, the guide adds, lets you discover the picture a little at a time. · Notice which part you looked at first."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/theme-depth-by-design.html","Movement and Depth · Concept","Depth by Design","","comp-depth","Depth cues make a flat surface read as space. The painter decides which to use and how strongly. · Overlap puts one thing in front of another, and size shrinks things as they recede. Linear perspective runs receding lines to a vanishing point at the viewer's eye level. A near-far jump leaves out the middle ground. · Brunelleschi worked out linear perspective around 1420, and Alberti's book on painting explained it. · Color and distance are covered in Color Theory."],[2,"/study/composition/arranging-the-picture/plum-estate-kameido.html","Movement and Depth","Plum Estate, Kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-plum-estate-kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Woodblock print, image 34 × 22.6 cm. Brooklyn Museum, New York · A plum tree fills the foreground, close up, and the orchard beyond is seen through its branches. · Look past the trunk: small figures stand along a fence among the distant trees. · Van Gogh copied the print in 1887, keeping its composition."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/unity.html","Unity · Overview","Unity","","poore-stable-interior","Making the parts one picture, then testing it · The last step is making every part belong to one whole, at the right size, and then checking the result with fresh eyes. · Unity and Variety · Proportion and Scale · Checking a Composition · Plate · Stable Interior — A. Mauve · c. 1915"],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/theme-unity-and-variety.html","Unity · Concept","Unity and Variety","","poore-unity","Unity is the sense that every part belongs to one whole. Variety adds difference to hold attention. · Poore set a single, tight group beside a picture split between two centers. \"Of all pictorial principles,\" he wrote, \"none compares in importance with Unity or Balance.\" · Echoing a shape or angle in several places \"helps to unify the composition,\" says Beth Harris of Smarthistory. · Poore quotes the painter George Inness on his own struggle for \"unity, unity.\""],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/a-sunday-on-la-grande-jatte-1884.html","Unity","A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884","Georges Seurat","seurat-grande-jatte","Georges Seurat · 1884–86 · Oil on canvas, 207.5 × 308.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Dozens of different figures are held in one ordered scene. · The Art Institute says Seurat meant it as a modern answer to a classical Greek frieze. · He reworked it two years later, making some figures bigger and adding new ones, such as the monkey on a leash. · Look for the repeats that bind it: upright figures in profile, parasols, curving hats and shadows."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/theme-proportion-and-scale.html","Unity · Concept","Proportion and Scale","","dow-rectangles-proportion","Proportion is how sizes and amounts relate inside a picture. Scale is how big something is next to something else, or next to us. · Dow made proportion, or \"good spacing,\" the principle behind all his others. His tracings divide rectangles unequally. · A large painting pushes viewers back; a small one draws them in (Smarthistory). · In Bellini's Madonna of the Meadow, Smarthistory notes, the Virgin takes up a third of the picture and stands higher than the horizon, which marks her as the subject."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/view-from-mount-holyoke-northampton-massachusetts-after-a-thunderstorm-the-oxbow.html","Unity","View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm — The Oxbow","Thomas Cole","cole-oxbow","Thomas Cole · 1836 · Oil on canvas, 130.8 × 193 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Smarthistory's Bryan Zygmont traces a diagonal from lower right to upper left that splits the view into two unequal parts: storm and wilderness at left, sunlit farmland at right. · Find the painter, tiny at lower center: he has stopped sketching to look out at us. Smarthistory calls him \"easily overlooked.\" · Next to the huge valley, that small figure sets the scale."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/theme-checking-a-composition.html","Unity · Concept","Checking a Composition","","dow-two-value-sketches","Painters check a design by making it look strange: small, reversed, upside down or far away. · Dow told students to \"make many trial arrangements\" and keep the best; small two-value sketches are quick to compare. · Leonardo advised a mirror: reversed, a picture looks like another painter's work, so faults show. · Poore adds viewing from different distances and turning the picture upside down. · Neuroscientist Margaret Livingstone suggests the mirror and upside-down tricks work by stripping away depth cues, so you see flat shapes."],[2,"/study/composition/putting-it-together/oil-sketch-for-a-sunday-on-la-grande-jatte-1884.html","Unity","Oil Sketch for \"A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884\"","Georges Seurat","seurat-oil-sketch-grande-jatte","Georges Seurat · 1884 · Oil on panel, 15.5 × 24.3 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · One of more than 20 painted studies, it looks at first like the layout of the final canvas. · Its 20-odd figures, though, \"have little to do with the final version,\" the Art Institute notes. · The trio at right was completely rethought. · Compare it with the finished painting under Unity and Variety."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/the-wild-beasts.html","The Wild Beasts · Overview","The Wild Beasts","","matisse-woman-with-a-hat","Matisse, Derain and their circle, 1904–1912 · In 1905 a few painters in Paris began choosing color for its force rather than its accuracy. This part follows that change from its first trials to the quieter pictures that came after. · The summer of 1905 at Collioure, where Matisse and Derain worked side by side · The Salon d'Automne that autumn, and the uproar that named the group · Where the experiment led next, as Matisse turned toward calm, decorative harmony · Henri Matisse · Portrait of Madame Matisse. The Green Line · 1905"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/luxe-calme-et-volupte.html","The Wild Beasts","Luxe, calme et volupté","Henri Matisse","matisse-luxe-calme-et-volupte","Henri Matisse · 1904 · Oil on canvas, 98.5 × 118.5 cm. Centre Pompidou, Musée national d'art moderne, Paris (on deposit at the Musée d'Orsay) · Bathers rest and picnic on a shore beside a moored boat. It records his turn to divisionism, a method he wrote in 1905 had left him dissatisfied. · Painted in his Paris studio after a summer spent with Paul Signac at Saint-Tropez. · Look at the figures: flowing contour lines hold them, cutting across the field of dots. · Touches of nearly pure color sit side by side, unblended, over the whole canvas. · The palette already runs hot: pink, orange and yellow spread through sand, sky and bodies alike. Color chosen for intensity over accuracy is what the next year's pictures push further. · Signac bought it in 1905; his heirs kept it until it entered the national collection in 1982. · Divisionism · Painting in separate small touches of pure color, meant to mix in the viewer's eye rather than on the palette. · The title borrows a line from Charles Baudelaire's poem \"L'Invitation au voyage,\" about a dreamed-of place of calm and pleasure."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/femme-au-chapeau-woman-with-a-hat.html","The Wild Beasts","Femme au chapeau (Woman with a Hat)","Henri Matisse","matisse-woman-with-a-hat","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 80.7 × 59.7 cm. San Francisco Museum of Modern Art, San Francisco · A half-length portrait of his wife, Amélie, holding a fan under a huge, heaped-up hat. Its first public showing set off a scandal. · Hung in Gallery VII at the 1905 Salon d'Automne, the show that gave the group its name. · Look at the background: loose, unblended patches of pink, green and violet suggest no real room. · Thick strokes sit beside thin, scrubbed washes, and no edge is smoothly drawn. · The face is green, pink and ochre, with no tie to real skin tones. Color here carries structure and feeling, not likeness. · Leo and Gertrude Stein bought it in 1905; the museum was showing it on loan by 1936, and it joined the collection in 1991 as the bequest of Elise S. Haas. · Salon d'Automne · A yearly autumn exhibition in Paris that showed newer, less official art. · Fauvism · From fauves, French for \"wild beasts\": a movement in Paris that used bright, unnatural color for expressive force."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/portrait-of-madame-matisse-the-green-line.html","The Wild Beasts","Portrait of Madame Matisse. The Green Line","Henri Matisse","matisse-the-green-line","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 40.5 × 32.5 cm. Statens Museum for Kunst (SMK), Copenhagen · A small, frontal head of Amélie Matisse, the painter's wife, split down the middle by a stripe of green. It reduces a portrait to a few bold zones of color. · Probably painted in autumn 1905, just after the summer at Collioure. · Look past the sitter: three flat blocks of color stand in for any setting. · Paint goes on in broad, unblended strokes; the yellow cheek has no dark outline. · A single green stripe does the work that shading normally does, parting a warm yellow side from a pink one. Pure hue, not light and dark, models the face. · It belonged to the Danish collector Christian Tetzen-Lund before entering the SMK in 1936."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/open-window-collioure.html","The Wild Beasts","Open Window, Collioure","Henri Matisse","matisse-open-window-collioure","Henri Matisse · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 55.3 × 46 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Open glazed doors frame sailboats in the harbor, with potted flowers and vines on the balcony in front. The view looks out over Collioure, a Mediterranean town. · Exhibited in Paris at the Salon d'Automne of 1905, soon after it was painted. · Look at the room: a cool green wall faces a pink and violet one across the opening. · Thick, raised paint in the flowerpots contrasts with thin, quick marks for the distant masts. · Dabs of pure color are set apart and never blended into one another. Gaps of untouched canvas show between them, most clearly around the vines and the sea. · Mr. and Mrs. John Hay Whitney bought it in 1952; it came to the gallery as a gift in 1998."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/mountains-at-collioure.html","The Wild Beasts","Mountains at Collioure","André Derain","derain-mountains-at-collioure","André Derain · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 81.3 × 100.3 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Foothills rise in bands of orange, blue and yellow above a thicket of trees. Almost nothing in the scene keeps its everyday color. · NGA lecturer David Gariff sees van Gogh's restless, moving trees echoed in these twisting forms. · Look at the trunks: bright red lines twist through foliage of blue and green. · Short, separate dashes build the foliage and grass, and dark blue lines outline the hills. · Each hill is one flat band of intense color, and the trees lose any solid, rounded form. The place reads as a pattern of color more than a record of a site. · The dealer Ambroise Vollard possibly bought it from the artist; it came to the gallery in 1982 from John Hay Whitney's collection."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/pont-de-charing-cross.html","The Wild Beasts","Pont de Charing Cross","André Derain","derain-charing-cross-bridge","André Derain · c. 1906 · Oil on canvas, 81 × 100 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris (French State; assigned to the Orsay in 1986) · A London embankment curves past pink trees toward the bridge, under a yellow sky with towers rising on the far bank. · He painted this bridge more than once; another version, dated 1906, is in the National Gallery of Art, Washington. · Look at the river: a yellow surface broken by flecks of blue and green, not gray water. · Flat, curving bands of green and pink make the road and pavement, edged in dark blue. · Warm yellows and pinks are played against cool blues and greens across the canvas. These contrasts, more than London's own light, set the mood and hold the design together. · The dealer Ambroise Vollard once owned it; it was given to the French state in 1973."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/posters-at-trouville.html","The Wild Beasts","Posters at Trouville","Albert Marquet","marquet-posters-at-trouville","Albert Marquet · 1906 · Oil on canvas, 65.1 × 81.3 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Strollers in dark and white clothes pass striped beach tents and a long wall of advertising boards on the seafront. It is his only known painting of this spot. · He and Raoul Dufy painted side by side at Trouville in summer 1906, both looking at one poster hoarding. · Look at the posters: lettering shrinks to a few marks inside flat blocks of yellow, blue and red. · Paint is laid in flat, even areas rather than heavy, textured strokes. · Pure yellow, blue and red fill a calm, orderly street scene. The color is bold, but the drawing and space stay steady and easy to read. · Sold through John Rewald to the Whitneys in 1971, it entered the gallery with their 1998 gift."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/le-bonheur-de-vivre-also-called-the-joy-of-life.html","The Wild Beasts","Le Bonheur de vivre, also called The Joy of Life","Henri Matisse","matisse-joy-of-life","Henri Matisse · October 1905–March 1906 · Oil on canvas, 176.5 × 240.7 cm. Barnes Foundation, Philadelphia · Sixteen figures dance, make music and rest in a glade ringed by pink, orange and green trees. Its effect on Picasso is widely seen as the start of the two painters' rivalry. · Shown at the 1906 Salon des Indépendants, where Leo Stein bought it from the artist. · Look at the ring of small dancers in the distance, set against the large reclining figures in front. · Paint lies thin and even across most areas, so the lines carry much of the drawing. · Broad, flat fields of color, with almost no shading on figures or ground, turn an imagined paradise into a decorative surface. · Albert C. Barnes bought it in 1923 through the dealer Paul Guillaume."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/les-affiches-a-trouville.html","The Wild Beasts","Les Affiches à Trouville","Raoul Dufy","dufy-posters-at-trouville","Raoul Dufy · 1906 · Oil on canvas, 65 × 81 cm. Centre Pompidou, Musée national d'art moderne, Paris · Holidaymakers in boaters and dark coats crowd a promenade in front of a long row of posters. He painted the scene twice; the other is known today only through an old photograph in black and white. · Probably the canvas exhibited at the 1906 Salon d'Automne under the title Les Planches (Trouville). · Look at the lower right: an empty red folding chair stands apart from the crowd. · Figures are set down in a few quick, dark strokes, with almost no modeling. · Printed posters and their lettering become part of the painting. The posters carry most of the strong color; the sky and street stay gray and pale. · The museum bought it in 1956. · By a tradition recorded by Dorival, author of a 1957 article on this painting, the man in the panama hat at center is the playwright Henri Bernstein."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/paris/goldfish.html","The Wild Beasts","Goldfish","Henri Matisse","matisse-goldfish","Henri Matisse · 1912 · Oil on canvas, 82 × 93.5 cm. Statens Museum for Kunst (SMK), Copenhagen · Three orange fish in a glass bowl, a small vase of flowers and a pale figure sculpture share a dark tabletop. Painted seven years after the Fauve summer, it shows where that color led. · Look at the bowl: dark blue bands run down the glass, and pink light floats on the water's surface. · A deep plum ground covers most of the canvas, brushed thinly and loosely. · Hot orange appears only in the fish and one flower. The bright color of 1905 is now held to a few accents, and the mood is quiet and somber. · Goldfish bowls recur in his work from 1911 to 1915; another Goldfish of 1912 is in the Pushkin Museum, Moscow. · Once in the Copenhagen collection of Christian Tetzen-Lund, it was acquired by the SMK in 1928."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/bridge-to-the-future.html","Bridge to the Future · Overview","Bridge to the Future","","kirchner-street-dresden","Die Brücke in Dresden, with Paula Modersohn-Becker, 1905–1911 · In 1905 four young artists in Dresden formed a group and named it Die Brücke, \"the Bridge.\" They wanted an art that was direct and unpolished. In the same years, with no tie to the group, Paula Modersohn-Becker was simplifying form in her own way. · Modersohn-Becker's pictures of 1906, made apart from the Dresden painters · The Brücke painters' subjects: city streets, stage performers, studio sitters and landscape · Prints and quick drawings, made alongside the paintings · Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · Street, Dresden · 1908"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/self-portrait-on-her-sixth-wedding-anniversary.html","Bridge to the Future","Self-Portrait on Her Sixth Wedding Anniversary","Paula Modersohn-Becker","modersohn-becker-self-portrait-anniversary","Paula Modersohn-Becker · 1906 · Oil or oil tempera (sources differ), 101.8 × 70.2 cm. Paula Modersohn-Becker Museum, Bremen · A woman painter shows herself bare to the hips, in an amber necklace, hands around a rounded belly. The museum that owns it counts it as the earliest nude self-portrait by a woman in art history. · Look at the lower right: a painted inscription gives her age as 30 and names the anniversary. · Dry, matte paint gives the skin a chalky look against a dappled green ground. · The body is reduced to broad, heavy forms under a level gaze, so the picture reads as a frank statement about herself rather than a flattering likeness. · Curator Jay A. Clarke treats her nude self-portraits of 1906 as statements of independence. · She married the painter Otto Modersohn in May 1901, so this was really her fifth anniversary; the title's count includes the wedding day."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/still-life-with-apples-and-green-glass.html","Bridge to the Future","Still Life with Apples and Green Glass","Paula Modersohn-Becker","modersohn-becker-apples-green-glass","Paula Modersohn-Becker · 1906 · Tempera or oil (sources differ) on paperboard mounted on plywood, 33.2 × 43 cm. Probably Paula Modersohn-Becker Museum, Bremen · A small tabletop scene: apples on a patterned plate and on a red cloth, with a knife and a stemmed glass. Ordinary things are pressed into simple, weighty shapes. · Dated more closely to November or December 1906. · Look at the glass: despite the title, in this image it reads as a deep blue. · Paint is dragged on thickly, so the turquoise wall behind keeps a rough, scratched texture. · The tabletop tilts up toward the viewer and the whole apples swell into plain rounds, so the picture works through mass and color more than detail. A knife laid across the plate leads the eye toward the glass. · Its inventory number suggests it entered the museum in 1993; an older photo archive lists a private owner in Worpswede."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/street-dresden.html","Bridge to the Future","Street, Dresden","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-street-dresden","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1908, reworked 1919; dated on painting 1907 · Oil on canvas, 150.5 × 200.4 cm. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · Women in wide hats crowd a busy shopping street, passing one another without contact. The scene turns the big-city crowd into a picture of isolation. · The setting is the Königstrasse in Dresden, with a trolley closing off the back. · Look at the pink pavement: it tilts steeply upward, and there are almost no buildings. · Acid greens and oranges outline the figures in place of shading. · Faces turn into masks and the ground seems to slide under the walkers, so the street feels unsteady rather than simply busy. In a letter he wrote of strange faces surfacing here and there as points of interest in the throng. · It stayed with the artist until his death in 1938; the museum bought it in 1951."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/franzi-in-front-of-carved-chair.html","Bridge to the Future","Fränzi in Front of Carved Chair","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-franzi-carved-chair","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1910 · Oil on canvas, 71 × 49.5 cm. Museo Nacional Thyssen-Bornemisza, Madrid · A portrait of Fränzi, a young girl who lived in Friedrichstadt, a working-class part of Dresden, with her face painted a strong yellow-green. The museum ranks it as the strongest of his images of her. · Look behind her: the chair back is a carved wooden figure, most likely made by the artist. · Thin, quick strokes leave bare canvas showing at the edges and in the patterned dress. · A handmade studio object becomes part of the portrait, its carving framing the sitter's head, so craft and painting meet in one picture. · In 1910 a Heckel woodcut after one of Kirchner's images of her served as the cover image for the Brücke catalogue of a show at Galerie Arnold, Dresden. · The museum built a small focus exhibition around this painting in 1996–97."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/czardas-dancers.html","Bridge to the Future","Czardas Dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-czardas-dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1908, reworked 1920 · Oil on canvas, probably about 150 × 200 cm. Kunstmuseum Den Haag, The Hague · Four dancers in red and pink costumes perform on a stage, skirts flying. The museum counts it as a high point among his many pictures of dance. · Painted in Dresden; he later dated it 1905, earlier than it was actually made. · Look at the stage floor and arches: they flatten into bands of blue, orange and green. · When he returned to it, he filled areas he had left unpainted with bright color. · Legs and elbows break into angular, jerky shapes. The museum reads the large areas of color, clashing in some places and merging in others, as an image of modern city life. · The museum, then called the Gemeentemuseum, bought it in 1955."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/elias-cemetery-in-dresden.html","Bridge to the Future","Elias Cemetery in Dresden","Max Pechstein","pechstein-elias-cemetery","Max Pechstein · 1906 · Oil on canvas, 44.3 × 61 cm. Brücke-Museum, Berlin · An old Dresden graveyard in deep green, with headstones, a cross and trees, and no people anywhere in view. A quiet, still subject is given a restless, heavy surface. · He signed it at lower right with a small monogram and the year. · Look at the lawn: it rolls in wavy bands, as if the ground itself were moving. · Thick paint goes on in short, curving strokes that follow the shapes of leaves and stones. · The museum's record tags Van Gogh as a reference. That borrowed energy unsettles a resting place, so mood counts for more than an exact view. · The museum acquired it from a private collection in 1979."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/english-step-dancers.html","Bridge to the Future","English Step Dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-english-step-dancers","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1911 · Color woodcut printed in black, yellow, green and pink on blotting paper, image 40.5 × 27.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Two dancers in matching dark costumes step forward together, one hand on the other's shoulder, beside a large potted plant. It carries the stage theme from canvas into print. · Inscribed \"Eigendruck,\" meaning the artist printed this impression himself. · Look at the edges: the green and yellow areas do not quite meet the black outlines. · The soft, absorbent paper soaks up the ink, giving the colors a matte, uneven surface. · The bodies are cut into flat blocks of color, so the handmade process stays in plain view rather than being polished away. · Given to the Jena art society in 1918, it was taken in the 1937 purge of \"degenerate art\" and then sold abroad. · Woodcut · A print made by cutting away a wood block so that only the raised parts take ink."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/houses-in-dresden-with-people-strolling.html","Bridge to the Future","Houses in Dresden with People Strolling","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-houses-in-dresden","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1909 · Crayon on buff wove paper, sheet 32.4 × 44.3 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A rapid sketch of a street corner: a tall block with a pitched roof, trees on either side and a road curving away. It shows how much of a city scene a few broken lines can hold. · Look at the two small figures in hats at center: one coat is left blank, the other is shaded dark. · Hard-pressed crayon makes the outlines; lighter rubbing gives the gray in the roof and trees. · Windows shrink to single hooked strokes and trees swell into loose, wavy shapes. Nothing is worked up to a finish, in line with the group's 1906 program, which called for direct, unfalsified expression. · Signed at lower left in black chalk. · It passed from the artist's estate through a dealer in Como to Ruth Cole Kainen in 1990, and came to the museum with her gift in 2012."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/city-and-war.html","City and War · Overview","City and War","","kirchner-potsdamer-platz","Berlin streets, the First World War and the print as protest, 1902–1923 · In 1911 the Brücke painters left Dresden for Berlin. Within three years the First World War broke into their lives and their art. Prints became a way to grieve and to protest in public. · Kirchner's Berlin: the street and its crowds as a modern subject · The war's toll on artists, seen in a self-portrait and scenes of violence · Käthe Kollwitz and the print made for a wide public · Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · Potsdamer Platz · 1914"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/berlin-street-scene.html","City and War","Berlin Street Scene","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-berlin-street-scene","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1913–14 · Oil on canvas. Neue Galerie New York and Private Collection, New York · Two women in plumed hats and fur collars, read by the museum as prostitutes, walk a crowded pavement among men in dark coats. He painted it after the group's 1911 move from Dresden to Berlin. · Two sisters, Erna and Gerda Schilling, served as models; he met them in his first Berlin months. · Look at the faces: narrow, masklike and almost alike, with little expression to read. · Long, hatched strokes sit on a pinkish field between the figures, so the crowd seems to flicker. · The color is pitched at full strength: red, deep blue and green sit side by side, jolting the eye rather than matching the street. · The museum marked its acquisition with a 2007 exhibition and describes the painting as recently restituted."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/potsdamer-platz.html","City and War","Potsdamer Platz","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-potsdamer-platz","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1914 · Oil on canvas, 200 × 150 cm. Nationalgalerie, Staatliche Museen zu Berlin, Berlin · Two women stand on a round traffic island while men in dark suits pass along the street behind them. It is the largest of the painter's city scenes. · Real landmarks appear, among them the domed Café Piccadilly at left and the entrance to the Potsdam railway station. · Look at the green street rising steeply behind the women, as if seen from above. · The black widow's veil on the left-hand woman was painted in after the war began. · The two women seem cut off from the passers-by around them. Warped space and unnatural color make a busy square feel isolating. · The museum bought it in 1999 with help from several German cultural foundations and the federal government."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/street-scene-after-the-rain.html","City and War","Street Scene after the Rain","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-street-scene-after-rain","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1914 · Woodcut on Japanese paper, image 26.5 × 24.7 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A woman holding flowers walks between men in hats and dark coats, all built from jagged black shapes left standing on the block. The print takes the street subject of his paintings into black and white. · Look at the large hatted figure at lower left: his face is turned toward us while his body runs off the bottom edge. · Short, sawtooth cuts along the coats let the paper show through as bright notches. · Diagonal cuts split the background into splinters, so the strain of the street lives in sharp, broken forms rather than in described detail. · A red estate stamp on the back shows that this sheet passed through the artist's estate after his death. · The Washington collector Ruth Cole Kainen bought it in 1970 and gave it to the gallery in 2012."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/self-portrait-as-a-soldier.html","City and War","Self-Portrait as a Soldier","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner","kirchner-self-portrait-as-soldier","Ernst Ludwig Kirchner · 1915 · Oil on canvas, 69 × 63.3 cm. Allen Memorial Art Museum, Oberlin College, Oberlin, Ohio · The painter shows himself in artillery uniform, cigarette at his lips, raising a bloody stump where his painting hand should be. Lecturer David Gariff reads the severed hand as a symbol of trauma. · Inducted in 1915, he was let go for illness and depression, then spent time in clinics after a breakdown. · Look at the nude behind him, who may be a live model or a painting. · Flat zones of blue, red and ochre sit side by side with little shading. · Blank, dark eyes give the face an emptied look, putting inner state ahead of a faithful likeness. · In 1937 the Nazi regime stripped museums of modern art it branded \"degenerate\"; this canvas was seized and hung with mocking labels in the touring Degenerate Art show. The Allen bought it in 1950."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/the-night-die-nacht.html","City and War","The Night (Die Nacht)","Max Beckmann","beckmann-the-night","Max Beckmann · 1918–19 · Oil on canvas, 134 × 155 cm. Kunstsammlung Nordrhein-Westfalen, Düsseldorf · In a cramped room, a man is strangled with a cord, a woman is tied by her wrists, and a child is carried off. Violence breaks into a private home. · An inscription at lower left dates the work from August 1918 to March 1919. · Look at the two candles at the bottom of the picture, one lying flat on the floorboards. · Hard outlines and chalky flesh make the bodies look stiff, almost wooden. · Tilted beams and packed limbs leave no open space, so the viewer is pressed into the scene. The effect is discomfort, not description. · Bought by the museum in 1963. Its early-1930s ownership is unclear; heirs of possible earlier owners want it back, and the museum plans to refer the case to Germany's new arbitration panel."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/memorial-print-for-karl-liebknecht.html","City and War","Memorial Print for Karl Liebknecht","Käthe Kollwitz","kollwitz-memorial-sheet-liebknecht","Käthe Kollwitz · 1920 · Woodcut on Japanese paper, image 35.5 × 50 cm. Städel Museum, Frankfurt am Main · Working people bend over the body of the politician Karl Liebknecht, whom right-wing paramilitaries killed together with Rosa Luxemburg. The print turns a political murder into a scene of shared grief. · A separate strip under the image carries a German dedication from the living to the dead, recalling 15 January 1919. · The artist disagreed with his politics but respected his stand against the war. · She drew him in death and tried other printing methods before settling on woodcut. · Solid black and rough white cuts reduce the mourners to bowed heads, worn faces and hands, so gesture, not likeness, carries the image. · A separate pencil note marks this sheet as a trial proof and dates it (1919); it came to the museum in 1964 with a private Kollwitz collection."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/the-people-das-volk-plate-7-of-war.html","City and War","The People (Das Volk), plate 7 of War","Käthe Kollwitz","kollwitz-the-people","Käthe Kollwitz · 1923 · Woodcut on Japanese paper, image 36 × 30 cm. Kunsthalle Bielefeld, Bielefeld · At the center of a tight huddle, a woman wrapped from head to foot guards with one hand the small child peering out of her cloth. The print comes from Kollwitz's woodcut series on the First World War. · Behind the series lies a loss shared with many families: her own son had died at the front. · Published in Dresden by Emil Richter in 1923, the portfolio gathers seven woodcuts begun in 1918 and finished by 1922 or 1923. · Look at how faces and hands float out of near-solid black, with almost nothing else described. · The woman's masklike face among contorted ones, and the rough gouged edges, make alarm rather than a story the subject of this woodcut. · This sheet is signed and numbered 36 of 100."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/dresden-and-berlin/outbreak-losbruch-plate-5-of-peasants-war.html","City and War","Outbreak (Losbruch), plate 5 of Peasants' War","Käthe Kollwitz","kollwitz-outbreak","Käthe Kollwitz · 1902–03 · Line etching, drypoint, aquatint, emery and soft ground, corrected with pencil and opaque color, sheet 50.2 × 58.7 cm. Kupferstichkabinett, Staatliche Museen zu Berlin, Berlin · A woman flings up her arms as armed peasants surge past her into revolt. This sheet is a working proof of the second state, not a finished edition print. · She came to the subject in 1901 through Wilhelm Zimmermann's account of the Peasants' War. · Though numbered fifth, it was the first plate of the cycle she finished. · Look at the woman's back, turned to us, so her raised arms seem to launch the crowd forward. · Rather than illustrating a text, this history print centers on rising up against injustice, giving an old revolt a social purpose. · The leading woman is \"Black Anna,\" a figure from the revolt; this is probably the cycle's only plate showing a known person. · Etching · A print made from a metal plate whose lines are bitten into the metal with acid. · Aquatint · An etching method that bites areas of tone, not lines, into the plate."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/toward-abstraction.html","Toward Abstraction · Overview","Toward Abstraction","","kandinsky-blue-rider-almanac","Kandinsky and his circle, 1906–1913 · Munich drew Russian and German painters who joined in 1909 to form the New Artists' Association. In late 1911, after it turned down a large painting by Kandinsky, he and Franz Marc broke away and showed under the name of the almanac they were editing: the Blue Rider. Over these years color came loose from the things it described. · Summers in Murnau, where four painters worked side by side · Gabriele Münter, Kandinsky's partner, whose Murnau house hosted the circle and whose 1957 gift keeps much of its work in Munich · Improvisations and compositions, and the step toward non-objective painting · Wassily Kandinsky · Cover of the Blue Rider Almanac · 1912"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/riding-couple-reitendes-paar.html","Toward Abstraction","Riding Couple (Reitendes Paar)","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-riding-couple","Wassily Kandinsky · 1906–07 · Oil on canvas, 55.4 × 50.7 cm. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus und Kunstbau München, Munich · Two figures in Russian dress share a dappled horse among birch trunks, with a domed city like the Kremlin across the river. Scholar Vivian Endicott Barnett groups such Russian folk subjects with the work he made abroad. · Painted in winter 1906–07, while the artist lived at Sèvres, near Paris. · Look at the far bank: towers and onion domes glitter in a band of light above the dark water. · Small dabs of bright paint sit on a dark ground, like beads sewn on cloth. A tempera study and pencil sketches came first. · The museum connects it with Symbolism and Jugendstil, styles that put mood ahead of observation. The horse and rider, later a key motif in his art, already appear. · Left to Gabriele Münter by the artist, it was hers from 1926; she gave it to the museum in 1957."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/murnau-view-from-the-window-of-the-griesbrau-murnau-blick-aus-dem-fenster-des-griesbrau.html","Toward Abstraction","Murnau – View from the Window of the Griesbräu (Murnau – Blick aus dem Fenster des Griesbräu)","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-murnau-griesbraeu","Wassily Kandinsky · 1908 · Oil on cardboard, 49.8 × 70 cm. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus und Kunstbau München, Munich · A town's main street, painted from the inn where the artist lodged. The museum counts it among the first pictures he made in Murnau. · Made in summer 1908, when three painter friends shared the inn with him. · Look at the street: it curves away in strokes of violet and pink, with green fields and blue hills beyond. · Long, sweeping brushstrokes still keep a trace of the Jugendstil line. · The museum sees this summer as the moment three of the four found a freer, more expressive way of painting. Here forms flatten and colors clash in strong contrasts. · He had passed through the town on a cycling trip in 1904. The painting reached the museum through Münter's gift."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/portrait-of-the-dancer-alexander-sakharoff-bildnis-des-tanzers-alexander-sacharoff.html","Toward Abstraction","Portrait of the Dancer Alexander Sakharoff (Bildnis des Tänzers Alexander Sacharoff)","Alexej von Jawlensky","jawlensky-alexander-sakharoff","Alexej von Jawlensky · 1909 · Oil on cardboard, 69.5 × 66.5 cm. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus und Kunstbau München, Munich · A ballet dancer and friend of the painter, shown half-length in a red costume, tilts his head and fixes us with a painted stare. It is one of three portraits of him made in 1909 alone. · The museum places it at the start of the series of staring heads that runs through his work until 1914. · Look at the face: chalk-white skin, dark-ringed, heavy-lidded eyes and red lips, with a single rose below. · Clotilde von Derp-Sacharoff recalled it was painted in under half an hour. The dancer arrived before a show, already in makeup, and carried off the wet board. · The white, mask-like face trades likeness for a stage type; the museum also notes the sitter's androgynous look. · Bought by the Lenbachhaus in 1965."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/self-portrait-i-selbstbildnis-i.html","Toward Abstraction","Self-Portrait I (Selbstbildnis I)","Marianne von Werefkin","werefkin-self-portrait","Marianne von Werefkin · about 1910 · Tempera and lacquer bronze on paper on cardboard, 51 × 34 cm. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus und Kunstbau München, Munich · The painter turns to stare at us from under a wide red hat, her eyes burning. The museum ranks it among the most unusual self-portraits any woman has painted. · With Jawlensky she ran a salon in Munich's Schwabing district, where she shaped its talk about art theory. · Look at the eyes: vermilion irises with dark dot pupils, ringed in blue, set in a face of yellow and green. · The red of the mouth returns in the hat, and the busy background strokes show Van Gogh's influence. · Color serves intensity rather than likeness, even on her own face; the museum likens its untamed force to the Fauves and the Brücke. · It was with the Werefkin foundation in Ascona before the Lenbachhaus bought it."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/murnau-landscape-murnauer-landschaft.html","Toward Abstraction","Murnau Landscape (Murnauer Landschaft)","Alexej von Jawlensky","jawlensky-murnau-landscape","Alexej von Jawlensky · 1909 · Oil on cardboard, 50.5 × 54.5 cm. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus und Kunstbau München, Munich · A road, trees and hills near Murnau, broken into angular planes of invented color. For a time around 1908–09, the museum says, he went further than any of his painting companions. · Signed and dated '09 at lower right, near small color tests left in view. · Look at the shadows on the road: they get the same flat treatment as the trees that cast them. · Scholars have shown a debt to French Cubism, above all to Henri Le Fauconnier, a guest at the second NKVM show. · Colors are chosen apart from nature: violet meets yellow, green meets orange, a single tree glows vermilion, and a band of hills turns turquoise. · Münter owned it until her 1957 gift. · Neue Künstlervereinigung München (NKVM) · The New Artists' Association of Munich, an exhibiting group founded in 1909."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/cover-of-the-blue-rider-almanac-umschlag-fur-den-almanach-der-blaue-reiter.html","Toward Abstraction","Cover of the Blue Rider Almanac (Umschlag für den Almanach 'Der blaue Reiter')","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-blue-rider-almanac","Wassily Kandinsky · 1912 · Color woodcut on heavy paper, 31.8 × 23.1 cm. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus und Kunstbau München, Munich · The bound cover of a book of essays and pictures edited by the artist and Franz Marc. A horse and rider rear up beside the hand-cut lettering. · They planned the book during 1911, while both still belonged to the NKVM. · Look at the colors: black and one soft blue, printed over tan paper. · The museum's text on the final design notes eleven cover designs made in quick succession, the rider showing the influence of folk paintings on glass found in Murnau. · The book set art of different eras and fields side by side, music included, and its rider became the sign of a renewal movement. · Came to the museum in Münter's 1957 gift, bound with its spine intact. · Der Blaue Reiter (Blue Rider) · The Munich circle around Kandinsky and Marc; it held exhibitions from late 1911."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/improvisation-28-second-version.html","Toward Abstraction","Improvisation 28 (Second Version)","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-improvisation-28","Wassily Kandinsky · 1912 · Oil on canvas, 112.6 × 162.5 cm. Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum, New York · Black lines slash across bursts of color in a scene of disaster and rescue. By 1910 many of his canvases drew on the Book of Revelation, the Bible's vision of the world's end. · Look left for a boat in the waves, a serpent and possibly cannons; on the right the museum finds an embracing couple, a sun and candles. · Smarthistory's Steven Zucker calls it \"abstracted\" rather than abstract: objects are blurred, not removed, so color and line strike first. · The museum's essay ties his horse-and-rider motif, also on the cover of his book On the Spiritual in Art, to a campaign against conventional art and toward a more spiritual future. · Part of the Guggenheim's founding collection."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/improvisation-no-30-cannons.html","Toward Abstraction","Improvisation No. 30 (Cannons)","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-improvisation-30-cannons","Wassily Kandinsky · 1913 · Oil on canvas, 111 × 111.3 cm. Art Institute of Chicago, Chicago · A square canvas of swirling color in which a few objects can still be picked out. Its subtitle names the clearest of them. · First shown in London in July 1913, at the Allied Artists' Association salon in the Royal Albert Hall. · Look at the lower right for the cannons, then up at the leaning red and white buildings. · He told the collector Arthur Jerome Eddy the cannons probably reflected that year's war talk; the real content was what the forms and colors make us feel (Art Institute summary). · He called his improvisations of 1910–14 unconscious, spontaneous expressions; the guns show how much of the visible world could still slip in. · Eddy probably bought it from the artist; his family gave it to Chicago in 1931."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/composition-vii.html","Toward Abstraction","Composition VII","Wassily Kandinsky","kandinsky-composition-vii","Wassily Kandinsky · 1913 · Oil on canvas, about 200 × 300 cm. State Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow · A huge canvas of colliding shapes, lines and colors that swirl around a dark knot left of center. The Tretyakov ranks it with the central works of his early 1910s. · More than thirty studies came first, in pencil, pen, watercolor and oil, most from autumn 1913. · Look for faint traces of the end-of-the-world theme of his earlier pictures: angels blowing trumpets and a rider. · Once the studies were done, the large canvas took only a few days, 25 to 28 November. · Few recognizable objects remain. The museum's partner text describes blue and red accents on a diagonal, held together by white in many shades. · It came to the Tretyakov in 1929 from Moscow's State Museum of New Western Art. · Non-objective (abstract) art · Art that shows no recognizable objects, using only color, line and shape."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/animals-and-color.html","Animals and Color · Overview","Animals and Color","","marc-blue-horse-i","Franz Marc, August Macke and Paul Klee, 1910–1914 · In the Munich circle, Franz Marc turned to animals, which he saw as purer beings than people. August Macke stayed closer to the places and people he saw around him, and Paul Klee kept a link to real places. · Marc's animals, from observed horses to near-abstract forms · Macke's quieter path, and the 1914 journey to Tunisia with Klee · Watercolors from the 1914 trip · Franz Marc · Blue Horse I · 1911"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/horse-in-a-landscape.html","Animals and Color","Horse in a Landscape","Franz Marc","marc-horse-in-landscape","Franz Marc · 1910 · Oil on canvas, 85 × 112 cm. Museum Folkwang, Essen · A red horse with a blue mane stands with its back to us, looking out over yellow fields toward blue-green hills. The museum suggests the pose may echo Caspar David Friedrich's Wanderer above the Sea of Fog, another figure seen from behind. · Look at the edges: the frame cuts off the horse, and the land rises with no horizon line. · Broad zones of strong color sit side by side, with red and blue set straight against yellow. · The viewer shares the animal's view rather than studying it: the landscape it faces, more than its body, becomes the subject. · The Folkwang bought it in 1953; where it was between 1937 and 1953 is still being researched."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/yellow-cow.html","Animals and Color","Yellow Cow","Franz Marc","marc-yellow-cow","Franz Marc · 1911 · Oil on canvas, 140.7 × 189.2 cm. Solomon R. Guggenheim Museum, New York · A leaping cow fills a landscape of orange ground, green plants and blue hills. The Guggenheim, following scholar Mark Rosenthal, reads it as a possible private wedding picture. · In that reading the cow may stand for Maria Franck, whom Marc married that year. · Look at the blue mountains at the top: Rosenthal sees them as a self-portrait of Marc. · Smooth, rounded animal forms play against hard black tree trunks that lock the cow in place. · Marc gave colors set roles, yellow for the feminine and blue for the masculine and spiritual; here the two meet in the cow and the mountains above it. · It belongs to the museum's founding collection; its accession number points to 1949. · Color symbolism · Giving each color a fixed meaning or feeling, so that color carries ideas instead of describing appearances."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/blue-horse-i.html","Animals and Color","Blue Horse I","Franz Marc","marc-blue-horse-i","Franz Marc · 1911 · Oil on canvas, 112 × 84.5 cm. Städtische Galerie im Lenbachhaus und Kunstbau, Munich · With its head bent, the horse fills the front of the picture before rolling pink, violet and yellow hills. Shown close up and on its own, it leaves nothing to compete with its color. · Look for paired opposites: vermilion ground against green plants, and pink-red hills against yellow. · Broad, visible strokes model the body in tones running from pale blue-white to deep blue. · The Lenbachhaus calls it an unusually clear case of Marc's color theory, which he set out in a letter to Macke; the horse takes the color of its essence, the museum's text says, not of its outward look. · A 1965 gift from the Bernhard und Elly Koehler Stiftung brought it to the Lenbachhaus."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/the-large-blue-horses.html","Animals and Color","The Large Blue Horses","Franz Marc","marc-large-blue-horses","Franz Marc · 1911 · Oil on canvas, 105.7 × 181.1 cm. Walker Art Center, Minneapolis · Pressed together across nearly the full width of the canvas, the horses stand before red and yellow hills. The group reads almost as one large body rather than three separate animals. · Look at the pale tree trunks: they slice down through the horses and flatten the space. · Long green leaves rise along the bottom edge, rooting the animals in the plants around them. · The bodies are shaded with deeper blue and violet instead of brown or gray. · The arched backs and bent necks repeat the curves of the hills behind them, so that animals and land seem to move to a single rhythm. · The Walker acquired it in 1942 with money from the T. B. Walker Foundation's Gilbert M. Walker Fund."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/animal-destinies-tierschicksale.html","Animals and Color","Animal Destinies (Tierschicksale)","Franz Marc","marc-animal-destinies","Franz Marc · 1913 · Oil on canvas, 194.7 × 263.5 cm. Kunstmuseum Basel, Basel · A forest splinters into crossing shafts of red, green and blue, with a blue deer, red boars and green animals caught inside. It reads as a vision of violence and collapse in nature. · Probably first shown at the First German Autumn Salon in Berlin, whose catalogue the museum lists. · On the back is a German line saying that all existence is burning pain; it is now covered by a lining added later. · Look at the brownish right third: it burned in a 1917 warehouse fire, and Klee repainted it in 1919, leaving his repair visible. · Prism-like, broken shapes tear the scene apart so that no part of the forest holds still. · The city museum in Halle lost it in the 1937 \"degenerate art\" seizures; Basel acquired it in 1939."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/fighting-forms.html","Animals and Color","Fighting Forms","Franz Marc","marc-fighting-forms","Franz Marc · 1914 · Oil on canvas, 91 × 131.5 cm. Bayerische Staatsgemäldesammlungen – Sammlung Moderne Kunst in der Pinakothek der Moderne, Munich · A red mass and a dark blue mass swirl into each other at the center of the canvas. It marks a turn away from the solid animal bodies he painted only a few years before. · Look for the thin white loops spinning through the dark side, against the jagged points of the red. · Fine drawn lines run over broad areas of color, adding speed to the clash. · No animal is clearly shown, though a beak-like hook appears in the red; the struggle is carried mainly by color, curve and direction, close to full abstraction. · Maria Marc inherited it on the artist's death in 1916 and kept it until the Bavarian state collections bought it from her in 1949."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/lady-in-a-green-jacket.html","Animals and Color","Lady in a Green Jacket","August Macke","macke-lady-green-jacket","August Macke · 1913 · Oil on canvas, 44 × 43.5 cm. Museum Ludwig, Cologne · Strollers in hats and jackets pause under trees beside blue water. Nothing happens beyond people out for a walk, and the picture carries no symbols or story. · Look at the faces: they are turned away or barely marked, so no one meets our eye. · Behind the strollers, pale houses sit on the near bank and distant hills rise across the water. · Color goes on in clear, separate areas, while the trees are soft, loose patches of green and yellow. · Figures become flat, simple silhouettes of strong color, like the woman in green at the center, yet the scene stays easy to read as a real place. · Bought in 1947 with Haubrich-Fonds money for Cologne's Wallraf-Richartz-Museum, it was transferred to its present home in 1976."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/market-in-tunis-ii.html","Animals and Color","Market in Tunis II","August Macke","macke-market-in-tunis","August Macke · 1914 · Watercolor over pencil, 25.5 × 20.8 cm. Kupferstichkabinett, Staatliche Museen zu Berlin, Berlin · A street market seen through awnings, stalls and passers-by, washed in pale light. It comes from the spring trip to Tunisia that Macke made with Paul Klee and their friend Louis Moilliet. · The museum places it among the first watercolors Macke painted outdoors on the trip, partly because of its restrained color. · Under the washes lies a pencil drawing; Klee, working beside Macke, skipped that step and went directly to paint. · Look at the figures in the foreground: the museum reads them as a screen that blocks our way into the market. · Perspective lines pull the eye into depth while flat, see-through washes hold it at the surface; the bare paper glowing through them keeps the light pale and bright. · The title came from his wife and Moilliet; Berlin bought it from his estate in 1921."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/munich/motif-from-hammamet.html","Animals and Color","Motif from Hammamet","Paul Klee","klee-motif-from-hammamet","Paul Klee · 1914 · Watercolor and pencil on paper, mounted on cardboard, 20.3 × 15.7 cm. Kunstmuseum Basel, Kupferstichkabinett, Basel · Squares and strips of watercolor stack up into a grid of color, titled after Hammamet in Tunisia. It comes from the same journey as Macke's market scene. · The mount is inscribed with the title and date; the signature sits on the sheet itself, lower left. · Look for the rows of small dots, which may suggest plants or trees inside the grid. · Thin washes sit side by side and overlap, letting the grain of the paper show through. · The landscape turns into a grid of colored blocks, close to abstraction yet still tied to a real place by its title. · The Basel collector Richard Doetsch-Benziger owned it from at least 1933 and left it to the museum in 1960."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/nerves-exposed.html","Nerves Exposed · Overview","Nerves Exposed","","gerstl-laughing-self-portrait","Gerstl, Schiele and Schoenberg in Vienna, 1905–1918 · In Vienna the break with tradition came through line, the body and the self-portrait more than through color. Younger painters, working in the long shadow of Klimt (see the Post-Impressionism section), turned portraits into studies of nerves and desire. The story here ends in 1918, the year the war ended and influenza swept the city. · Richard Gerstl's short, isolated career · Egon Schiele's self-portraits, double portraits and figure groups, 1912–1918 · A composer who painted, and a portrait print · Richard Gerstl · Self-Portrait, Laughing · 1908"],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/the-sisters-karoline-and-pauline-fey.html","Nerves Exposed","The Sisters Karoline and Pauline Fey","Richard Gerstl","gerstl-sisters-fey","Richard Gerstl · 1905 · Oil on canvas, 175 × 150 cm. Belvedere, Vienna · A double portrait of two sisters in white dresses, seated side by side. The Belvedere credits its painter as the earliest of the young Expressionists to abandon decorative outline and ornament. · Look at the faces: pale and masklike, with neither sister turning toward the other. · The dresses are brushed in fast, open strokes that leave the fabric half dissolved. · The formal pose turns uneasy: the sisters rise, in the Belvedere's words, like phantoms out of a loose, dark, brushy ground, with only a curved chair arm and a red seat back to anchor them. · The Belvedere dates it to winter 1905, three years before his death. · It was lent to the Leopold Museum's Gerstl retrospective in Vienna in 2019."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/self-portrait-laughing.html","Nerves Exposed","Self-Portrait, Laughing","Richard Gerstl","gerstl-laughing-self-portrait","Richard Gerstl · 1908 · Oil on canvas, 40 × 30.5 cm. Belvedere, Vienna · A small self-portrait in which the painter stretches his mouth into a wide grin. The Belvedere reads the expression as closer to menace than to good humor, a rare break from his usual gravity. · The Belvedere calls him self-taught; he kept clear of the city's artist associations and chose not to exhibit. · He died in 1908 at 25; his roughly 80 works became known only decades later. · Look at the bared teeth and narrowed eyes, which make the grin look strained. · Short, separate dabs fill the background and thick ridges build the face, with almost no blending; the raw surface makes the paint itself carry his state of mind. · The Belvedere dates it 1908; some sources place it a year earlier."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/self-portrait-with-chinese-lantern-plant.html","Nerves Exposed","Self-Portrait with Chinese Lantern Plant","Egon Schiele","schiele-self-portrait-physalis","Egon Schiele · 1912 · Oil and opaque color on wood, 32.2 × 39.8 cm. Leopold Museum, Vienna · A bust-length self-portrait in a dark jacket, set beside the thin stems and red fruit of a lantern plant. The edges crop both head and body, and the red fruit answers the dark areas. · Look at the head, which turns to the right while the eyes meet yours. · The twist of the head against the shoulders does the expressive work, turning a plain bust into a picture of strain and self-scrutiny. · Its earliest recorded showing was his 1928 memorial exhibition in Vienna. · Its first owner, a Vienna engineer, is said to have bought it between 1914 and 1918, probably from the painter himself. · Rudolf Leopold bought it in the mid-1950s; it passed to the Leopold Museum in 1994."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/portrait-of-wally-neuzil.html","Nerves Exposed","Portrait of Wally Neuzil","Egon Schiele","schiele-wally-neuzil","Egon Schiele · 1912 · Oil on wood, 32 × 39.8 cm. Leopold Museum, Vienna · A small horizontal portrait of the young woman who was the painter's partner and model at the time. The Leopold Museum calls it arguably the best-known image of her. · Painted alongside his lantern-plant self-portrait as its pendant; the museum ties the pairing to the odd layout of both. · Look at the tilted head, wide eyes and white collar set against a pale ground. · A dealer's catalogue of 1925 listed it simply as a woman's portrait with a flowering tendril. · Tight cropping brings her face close to ours; the picture records intimacy rather than social status. · Lea Bondi Jaray lost it when her Vienna gallery was taken over in 1938. Lent to MoMA in 1997, it was subpoenaed by the Manhattan District Attorney in January 1998; a 2010 settlement with her estate left it with the Leopold. · Pendant · One of a pair of pictures made to hang together as companions."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/death-and-maiden.html","Nerves Exposed","Death and Maiden","Egon Schiele","schiele-death-and-maiden","Egon Schiele · 1915 · Oil on canvas, 150 × 180 cm. Belvedere, Vienna · A man in a dark robe holds a woman who clings to him, the pair perched on a rumpled cloth in a barren landscape. The Belvedere identifies the man as the painter and the woman as Wally Neuzil. · His marriage to Edith Harms ended the relationship. · Neuzil later became a wartime nurse; scarlet fever killed her in 1917. · Look at the unsteady poses: neither figure seems firmly placed, as if they could slide apart. · A flat dark robe sits against the broken, chalky white of the cloth and her patterned, many-colored dress. · An old motif of death claiming a young woman can be read here as a private parting between two real people."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/the-embrace.html","Nerves Exposed","The Embrace","Egon Schiele","schiele-the-embrace","Egon Schiele · 1917 · Oil on canvas, 100 × 170 cm. Belvedere, Vienna · Two nude lovers hold each other on a crumpled white sheet, filling a long horizontal canvas. The Belvedere reads the bodies as carriers of feeling rather than portraits of particular people. · Those feelings, in the museum's reading, include desire, tenderness, fear of parting and despair. · Look at the man's long, curved back and the woman's hair spread across the sheet. · Reddish-brown contours run around each limb; blue lines edge the sheet's quick, angular folds. · Contour locks the two bodies into one tight knot, set off by an acid yellow-green ground, so the drawing itself holds the couple together. · Some sources add the title Lovers II; the Belvedere uses only the shorter name."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/mother-with-two-children-iii.html","Nerves Exposed","Mother with Two Children III","Egon Schiele","schiele-mother-two-children","Egon Schiele · 1915–1917 · Oil on canvas, 150 × 159.8 cm. Belvedere, Vienna · A veiled mother sits between two small children in striped, brightly colored clothes, all set against a dark ground. Her pale, hollow-eyed face is the still center of a crowded, patterned group. · Begun in 1915, it carries only the final year, 1917, in its signature at lower center. · Look at the orange, fringed forms that frame each child like a niche. · The white drapery is built up in thick layers of paint; the Belvedere keeps an X-ray of the canvas. · Doll-like children with staring eyes and a remote, veiled mother turn a traditionally comforting subject toward unease. · The Belvedere bought it in 1951 from Jenny Steiner, then living in New York."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/squatting-couple-the-family.html","Nerves Exposed","Squatting Couple (The Family)","Egon Schiele","schiele-the-family","Egon Schiele · 1918 · Oil on canvas, 150 × 160.8 cm. Belvedere, Vienna · A naked man and woman squat, one behind the other, in a dim interior, and a small clothed child sits at the woman's feet. The man's face resembles the painter's own. · Berta Zuckerkandl was the first to call it The Family. · Spanish flu killed his wife Edith, pregnant with their child, in 1918; he died three days after her. · Look at how the three figures stack into a pyramid; only the man meets our eyes. · Muted browns and greens surround the yellow-ochre of the man's body, whose bony, mottled limbs open out around the woman squatting just below him. · It entered the Belvedere among its new acquisitions of 1947 to 1951."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/blue-self-portrait.html","Nerves Exposed","Blue Self-Portrait","Arnold Schoenberg","schoenberg-blue-self-portrait","Arnold Schoenberg · 1910 · Oil on plywood. Arnold Schönberg Center, Vienna · A head-and-shoulders self-portrait by the composer: a blue-grey head set against a flat salmon-orange ground. Self-portraits form the largest group in his art, with 75 catalogue entries. · He dated it to a single day, 13 February 1910. · His talks with Richard Gerstl, starting in 1906, spurred him to paint. · He insisted that painting never ranked with his music and counted himself an amateur in it. · In a 1938 text he wrote that he painted gazes, not faces; here the head serves mainly to carry a fixed, wide-eyed stare. · In 1911 Kandinsky invited him to the first Blue Rider exhibition in Munich; this picture was not among the four works he showed."],[3,"/study/art-history/fauvism-and-expressionism/vienna/portrait-of-franz-hauer.html","Nerves Exposed","Portrait of Franz Hauer","Egon Schiele","schiele-franz-hauer","Egon Schiele · 1914 · Drypoint, plate 13 × 11 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A small printed head of Franz Hauer, a tavern owner who, in Jane Kallir's account, was the painter's most important collector from 1912 to 1914, buying nine of his oils. · Look at the looping, curled beard and the long parallel lines of the brow. · The head is built from line alone, darker wherever the lines crowd together. · The jittery line is left raw and open, giving the down-turned face a restless, searching look. · The Met holds several other 1914 drypoints by him, including a self-portrait and a portrait of Arthur Roessler. · This impression comes from a 1922 portfolio of his prints, part of the Met's Scofield Thayer bequest of 1982. · Drypoint · A print made by scratching lines into a metal plate with a sharp point, without acid."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/before-1874-the-salon-realism-and-manet.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet · Overview","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","","manet-dejeuner","What the Impressionists rejected, and what they took · Before 1874, a French painter's career ran through the Salon, whose jury rewarded polished, idealized work. In the 1860s Courbet, Manet and the young Monet and Bazille challenged that standard, pointing toward the traits of Impressionism: visible brushwork, bright color, outdoor scenes and subjects from modern life. The younger painters' trouble getting a fair hearing at the Salon led to the independent exhibition of 1874. · A Salon jury chose what was hung, and success brought state purchases, prizes and buyers. · In 1863 works the jury refused went on view at a separate Salon des Refusés. · Courbet's Realism and Manet's modern subjects showed that present-day life could fill a large canvas. · Salon · The official Paris art exhibition, chosen by a state jury. · Academic art · Painting taught by the École des Beaux-Arts: classical subjects, careful drawing, smooth finish. · Realism · Courbet's movement, from about 1850, painting present-day life without idealizing it. · Édouard Manet · Le Déjeuner sur l'herbe · 1863"],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/the-birth-of-venus.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","The Birth of Venus","Alexandre Cabanel","cabanel-venus","Alexandre Cabanel · 1875 · Oil on canvas, 106 × 182.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A smaller replica, by Cabanel himself, of the nude that drew crowds at the 1863 Salon. It shows the polished academic manner the future Impressionists were judged against. · Academic painters smoothed away their brushstrokes, as Cabanel does here. The Impressionists did the opposite and left their strokes visible on purpose. · Napoleon III bought the first version. · The New York collector John Wolfe ordered this replica from Cabanel in 1875. · Look at the cupids overhead. · Wolfe gave it to the Met in 1893. · Replica · A copy an artist makes of his own earlier work. · In Greek myth, Venus was born from the foam of the sea. Salon painters used such stories to show the nude as an ideal, not a real woman."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/woman-with-a-parrot.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","Woman with a Parrot","Gustave Courbet","courbet-parrot","Gustave Courbet · 1866 · Oil on canvas, 129.5 × 195.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A life-size nude sprawled on a rumpled sheet, shown at the 1866 Salon by the leader of Realism. Courbet used the scale of a Salon nude but gave her no myth. · Courbet paints a real body, tangled hair and all, not an ideal one. The Impressionists took from Realism this demand to paint people as they are. · Several critics objected to the awkward pose. · The state briefly thought of buying it, but it stayed with Courbet until 1870. · Manet began his own woman with a parrot the same year. · Louisine Havemeyer's bequest brought it to the Met in 1929. · Courbet refused gods and heroes as subjects. Here he turned that stance on the nude, the academy's most valued subject."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/le-dejeuner-sur-lherbe.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","Le Déjeuner sur l'herbe","Édouard Manet","manet-dejeuner","Édouard Manet · 1863 · Oil on canvas, 207 × 265 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Two clothed men and a naked woman picnic in a wood. Refused by the 1863 Salon jury, it became the main attraction of the Salon des Refusés. · Shown as Le Bain (The Bath), it drew laughter and charges of obscenity. · The theme comes from the Louvre's Concert champêtre, today given to Titian. · Victorine Meurent posed for the nude, who turns to look straight at the viewer. · The woods behind are only roughly sketched, with little depth, on purpose. The Impressionists would push this sketch-like finish much further. · Étienne Moreau-Nélaton gave it to the French state in 1906. · Picnics in the woods near Paris were a common Sunday outing. Set there, beside men in modern dress, the nude reads as a real woman."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/olympia.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","Olympia","Édouard Manet","manet-olympia","Édouard Manet · 1863 · Oil on canvas, 130.5 × 191 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A reclining nude on a white bed, with a Black maid bringing flowers. Manet recast Titian's Venus of Urbino as a Paris prostitute. · Manet lights the pale body flatly and sets it hard against the dark background. This flat, bright light, with little gradual shading, anticipated the Impressionists' lighter, less modeled painting. · First shown at the 1865 Salon, where critics attacked it fiercely. · Émile Zola led the small group that defended it. · She meets the viewer's eye. Look for the black ribbon at her neck. · Monet raised a public subscription in 1890 to give it to the French state. · Odalisque · A harem woman, a common type for 19th-century nudes. · Salon nudes were usually goddesses or harem women in an imagined East."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/a-studio-at-les-batignolles.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","A Studio at Les Batignolles","Henri Fantin-Latour","fantin-batignolles","Henri Fantin-Latour · 1870 · Oil on canvas, 204 × 273.5 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Young painters and writers gather around Manet at his easel. Shown at the 1870 Salon, it presents the future Impressionists as serious, respectable men. · Fantin paints the circle as a group of friends. Monet and Renoir, both here, would later exhibit together, and showing as a group became how the Impressionists made their name. · Named for Les Batignolles, the Paris district where Manet and several younger painters lived. · Among the men: Manet seated, Renoir in a hat, Zola, Bazille standing tall, and Monet. · Look for a statuette of Minerva, a sign of respect for ancient art, and a Japanese-style pot, a sign of taste for Japanese art. · The Musée du Luxembourg acquired it in 1892. · Group portraits of artists were a way to announce a shared cause in public. Within months, the Franco-Prussian War scattered this circle."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/the-railway.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","The Railway","Édouard Manet","manet-railway","Édouard Manet · 1873 · Oil on canvas, 93.3 × 111.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A young woman with a book and a puppy sits by an iron railing while a girl looks through at the steam below. Manet sent it to the Salon of 1874. · Manet kept to the official Salon and did not join the independent exhibition his friends opened in April 1874. · The setting is the rail yard leading into the Gare Saint-Lazare, beside Manet's new studio. · Manet never says whether the two are mother and daughter or nanny and child. Impressionist pictures likewise show a slice of modern life with no story attached. · The figures mirror each other: one faces us, the other turns away. · Horace Havemeyer gave it to the National Gallery in 1956, in memory of his mother, Louisine. · The Gare Saint-Lazare served the western suburbs and the Normandy coast. Monet and Caillebotte also painted its iron bridge, the Pont de l'Europe."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/garden-at-sainte-adresse.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","Garden at Sainte-Adresse","Claude Monet","monet-sainte-adresse","Claude Monet · 1867 · Oil on canvas, 98.1 × 129.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A terrace above the Channel in strong sunlight, painted when Monet was 26. It is one of the boldest pictures he made before Impressionism had a name. · Painted in summer at Sainte-Adresse, a resort by Le Havre, where Monet's family stayed. · The seated man in the Panama hat is the artist's father, Adolphe. · The high viewpoint and flat bands of bright color come from Japanese woodblock prints, a source the Impressionists kept returning to. · Look at the two flags, pulled hard by the same sea wind. · It hung in the fourth Impressionist exhibition in 1879; the Met bought it in 1967. · By the 1860s, rail lines had made the Normandy coast a holiday ground for city families. The picture records that new middle-class leisure."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/la-grenouillere.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","La Grenouillère","Claude Monet","monet-grenouillere","Claude Monet · 1869 · Oil on canvas, 74.6 × 99.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Bathers and boaters at a floating café on the Seine, painted quickly beside Renoir. It points straight toward the style later called Impressionist. · The name means \"the frog pond\"; the spot was at Croissy, west of Paris. · Monet called his pictures here poor rough sketches; this is thought to be one. · Renoir painted almost the same view, now in Stockholm. · Monet paints the rippling water in short, separate strokes of blue, green and black, not blended. This broken brushwork became the hallmark of Impressionism. · It came to the Met in 1929 with Louisine Havemeyer's bequest. · Plein air · French for \"open air\": painting outdoors, in front of the subject. · New resorts on the Seine drew Parisians of every class for weekend boating and swimming. Here women in black bathing suits mix with men in top hats."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/family-reunion.html","Before 1874: The Salon, Realism and Manet","Family Reunion","Frédéric Bazille","bazille-family","Frédéric Bazille · 1867 · Oil on canvas, 152 × 230 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Ten members of the painter's family on a shaded terrace near Montpellier, with Bazille at far left. · Painted in summer and shown at the 1868 Salon, which refused Monet's bolder work. · Bazille admired Monet's outdoor pictures and had bought his Women in the Garden. · Bazille sets his family in deep shade, with sunlight breaking through the leaves onto pale dresses. Showing how outdoor light falls on figures was a core Impressionist aim. · Most sitters gaze out at the viewer, as if posing for a photographer. · The French national museums bought it in 1905 with help from his brother Marc. · The family spent summers at Méric, its estate outside Montpellier. Bazille was killed in the Franco-Prussian War in 1870, at 28."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/1874-the-first-exhibition.html","1874: The First Exhibition · Overview","1874: The First Exhibition","","renoir-loge","An artist-run show, and a mocking new name · In the spring of 1874 Monet, Renoir, Degas, Morisot, Pissarro, Sisley and Cézanne held their own show, alongside two dozen other artists, most of them little known today. · The show ran from 15 April to 15 May 1874 on the boulevard des Capucines. · The artists ran it through a société anonyme, a company they formed, and showed about 200 works. · Little sold, but the press covered the show widely. · catalogue number · A work's number in the 1874 booklet, used to match surviving paintings to the show. · Pierre-Auguste Renoir · La Loge · 1874"],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/impression-sunrise.html","1874: The First Exhibition","Impression, Sunrise","Claude Monet","monet-impression","Claude Monet · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 50 × 65 cm. Musée Marmottan Monet, Paris · Le Havre harbor at dawn, painted in autumn. Its one-word title gave the critic Louis Leroy his mocking name for the group. · Monet painted the mist and rising sun in a few hours and showed the canvas in 1874. Impressionist painters often worked this fast, finishing a picture in a single session. · He painted it early one morning from his room at the Hôtel de l'Amirauté. · Look for cranes, chimneys and masts fading into mist behind the rowing boats and orange sun. · Monet titled it Impression because he judged it did not work as a record of the town. · Given to the Musée Marmottan in 1940 by Eugène and Victorine Donop de Monchy. · impression · Painters' word for a quick record of overall light and atmosphere, not detail. · Le Havre, Monet's hometown, was one of France's main ports for Atlantic ships. Here its smoke and machinery become a study of light and moist air."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/boulevard-des-capucines.html","1874: The First Exhibition","Boulevard des Capucines","Claude Monet","monet-capucines","Claude Monet · 1873–74 · Oil on canvas, 80.3 × 60.3 cm. The Nelson-Atkins Museum of Art, Kansas City · A cold day on one of the new Paris boulevards, seen from high above. Monet painted it from the rooms that housed the 1874 exhibition. · The crowd below is a scatter of dry, brushy dabs, not individual people. The Impressionists painted city crowds as the eye takes them in, as moving marks. · Monet worked from a balcony of Nadar's former studio, in late 1873 or early 1874. · The museum identifies a horizontal view from the same spot, now in Moscow, as the one exhibited. · Look for the pink balloons at lower right and two men in top hats watching from a balcony. · Very likely sold by Monet in 1875, it was bought by the Nelson-Atkins in 1972. · Baron Haussmann redesigned the boulevard under Napoleon III, lining it with new hotels and cafés. Monet's high, tilted view echoes Nadar's balloon photographs of Paris."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/theme-organizing-the-show.html","1874: The First Exhibition · Theme","Organizing the Show","","monet-impression","In April 1874 thirty-one artists bypassed the Salon and showed their work themselves. · On 17 January 1874 the organizers announced an open exhibition with no jury and no prizes. The members avoided a group name that would suggest a school. · About 3,500 people came, against roughly half a million at the 1874 Salon; the artists' company soon closed. · Conservative critics faulted the unfinished look, while friendly ones welcomed scenes of modern Paris."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/la-loge.html","1874: The First Exhibition","La Loge","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-loge","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 80 × 63.5 cm. The Courtauld Gallery, London · A fashionable couple in a theater box, painted as a play of black, white and pink. Shown in 1874, its modern subject drew both praise and suspicion. · Renoir paints up-to-date evening dress and a night at the theater, not history or myth. Scenes of modern Paris pleasures were a core Impressionist subject. · Renoir's brother Edmond and a model named Nini Lopez posed for it in the studio. · He raises his opera glasses toward the audience; she lowers hers and is herself on view. · Look at the broad black and white stripes of her dress against the soft strokes of her face. · Samuel Courtauld bought it in 1925 and bequeathed it in 1948. · loge · A private theater box. · A theater box was for being seen as much as for watching. Fashion and satirical papers both pictured box scenes, so viewers knew the setting."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/the-dancer.html","1874: The First Exhibition","The Dancer","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-dancer","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 142.5 × 94.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A young ballet dancer in fifth position on a canvas nearly five feet tall. It was one of Renoir's seven 1874 entries and among the better received. · Her tulle skirt melts into the hazy green background with no hard outline. Letting edges dissolve into light and air was typical of Impressionist painting. · Shown as no. 141, it struck some critics as a weak sketch and others as graceful. · The National Gallery identifies the model as almost certainly the young actress Henriette Henriot. · The centered, full-length pose borrows from formal portraiture, unlike Degas's dancers caught in rehearsal. · It joined the Widener collection and came to the National Gallery in 1942."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/at-the-races-in-the-countryside.html","1874: The First Exhibition","At the Races in the Countryside","Edgar Degas","degas-races-countryside","Edgar Degas · 1869 · Oil on canvas, 36.5 × 55.9 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · A Normandy family outing that mixes landscape, everyday scene and family portrait. Degas included this small canvas among his 1874 entries. · The man at the reins is Degas's friend Paul Valpinçon, with his wife, baby son and wet nurse. · Degas pushes the race into the distance and leaves the middle empty, like a passing glance. Impressionist painters often aimed for this glimpsed look. · Look for the carriage cropped at the edge and the family dog echoing its master's pose. · The MFA points out that the wet nurse sets paid work beside leisure. · The baritone Jean-Baptiste Faure owned it in 1874; the MFA bought it in 1926. · wet nurse · A woman paid to breastfeed another family's baby. · Horse racing was a fashionable pastime for the French upper classes, in Paris and the provinces. Well-off families commonly hired wet nurses."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/the-cradle.html","1874: The First Exhibition","The Cradle","Berthe Morisot","morisot-cradle","Berthe Morisot · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 56 × 46.5 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Morisot's sister Edma watches her sleeping daughter Blanche through a veil of white gauze. It is Morisot's first mother-and-child picture and was shown in 1874. · Morisot gives a quiet moment between mother and child at home a place in a public exhibition. Everyday private life became a serious Impressionist subject. · With it, Morisot became the first woman to exhibit with the group. · One slanting line runs from the curtain through Edma's lowered eyes and arm to the child. · Edma draws the veil across the cradle, screening the child from the viewer. · Morisot failed to sell it; the Louvre bought it from the family in 1930. · Middle-class home life, with mothers, sisters and children, was the world Morisot knew best. Motherhood became one of her lasting themes."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/the-harbor-at-lorient.html","1874: The First Exhibition","The Harbor at Lorient","Berthe Morisot","morisot-lorient","Berthe Morisot · 1869 · Oil on canvas, 43.5 × 73 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A young woman with a pink parasol sits on a harbor wall above calm, reflecting water. Morisot gave it to Manet in 1869, and it was shown in 1874 as Marine. · Quick, light strokes only suggest the scene, even the woman's face. Impressionist painters often suggested forms like this rather than describing them in detail. · When the National Gallery exhibited it in 1966, the figure was identified as Edma Morisot. · Most of the canvas is water and sky; look at the ships' reflections across the channel. · The pale pink parasol stands out against the blues of water and sky. · Ailsa Mellon Bruce bought it in 1955 and bequeathed it to the National Gallery in 1970. · Lorient is a port town on the south coast of Brittany."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/white-frost.html","1874: The First Exhibition","White Frost","Camille Pissarro","pissarro-hoarfrost","Camille Pissarro · 1873 · Oil on canvas, 65.5 × 93.2 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A plowed field near Pontoise on a frosty morning, with a lone figure on the path. It hung in 1874, the first of the eight group shows Pissarro joined. · Pissarro paints one passing condition instead of a timeless landscape. Catching effects like this became central to Impressionism. · Louis Leroy ridiculed it, while Philippe Burty likened it to the best of Millet. · Look at the furrows climbing the slope and the man walking with a load on his back. · Pissarro laid the paint on thickly, partly with a knife, so the frozen field feels dense. · Jean-Baptiste Faure bought it in 1874; it has been at the Musée d'Orsay since 1986. · Pissarro lived at Pontoise, northwest of Paris, for much of the 1870s. Its fields and farm workers, not suburban leisure, were his main subject."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/orchard-in-bloom-louveciennes.html","1874: The First Exhibition","Orchard in Bloom, Louveciennes","Camille Pissarro","sisley-villeneuve","Camille Pissarro · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 45.1 × 54.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A small spring landscape with a tree in white blossom, a plowed field and two farm workers. Pissarro sent it to the 1874 show as Le verger (The Orchard). · Pissarro paints a working field and a plain fruit tree, not a famous or scenic view. The Impressionists chose ordinary, lived-in country like this. · Listed first among his entries, as no. 136; the museum suggests personal reasons may explain why. · Painted in spring, months after the family returned from wartime London. · Short dabs build the blossoms, and the sky shifts from pale gray to strong blue. · Bought by Ailsa Mellon Bruce in 1960, it came to the National Gallery in 1970. · Pissarro came home in late 1871 to find much of his stored work lost. The museum reads the flowering tree as a sign of recovery."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/beginnings/a-modern-olympia.html","1874: The First Exhibition","A Modern Olympia","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-modern-olympia","Paul Cézanne · 1873–74 · Oil on canvas, 46.2 × 55.5 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A man in a dark suit looks at a nude on a bed as a Black servant pulls back her sheet. Cézanne's answer to Manet's Olympia baffled viewers in 1874. · Cézanne lays on hot reds and pinks in quick, heavy strokes, with no polished finish. Many Impressionist painters used brighter, stronger color than the Salon favored. · Painted at Auvers-sur-Oise in the house of Dr. Paul Gachet, who lent it to the show. · Cézanne adds a clothed visitor who faces the woman from the viewer's side. · A review in L'Artiste in May 1874 went so far as to call the painter mad. · Gachet's son Paul gave it to the French national museums in 1951. · Here Manet's theme becomes a scene about looking."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/modern-paris-boulevards-leisure-and-labor.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor · Overview","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","","caillebotte-rainy-day","Where Parisians worked and played · In the mid-1800s Paris was rebuilt with broad boulevards, railway stations, cafés and theaters. The Impressionists and painters close to them made this new city their subject. These ten pictures, from about 1874 to 1897, show its crowds, pleasures and workers. · Baron Haussmann's rebuilding replaced old streets with wide avenues and uniform apartment blocks. · The third Impressionist exhibition, in April 1877, showed several of these works together. · modern life · Present-day city subjects, in place of history, myth or religion. · grands boulevards · Broad Right Bank streets lined with cafés, theaters and shops. · Gustave Caillebotte · Paris Street; Rainy Day · 1877"],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/bal-du-moulin-de-la-galette.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","Bal du moulin de la Galette","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-galette","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1876 · Oil on canvas, 131.5 × 176.5 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A crowded open-air dance on Montmartre. It gave an ordinary city pleasure the size of a Salon picture and is usually ranked first among his mid-1870s works. · Sunlight through the trees falls in patches on hats, jackets and dusty ground, in quick, broken touches. Impressionist painters often favored dappled light like this, filtered through leaves. · Shown at the third Impressionist exhibition in April 1877. · Friends posed among the dancers, but the subject is the whole crowd, not one figure. · Critics disliked its blurred look, an effect many now see as the point. · Gustave Caillebotte bought it by 1879; his bequest brought it to the French state in 1896. · The Moulin de la Galette was a popular dance garden on the Butte Montmartre, at the northern edge of Paris. The museum stresses the crowd's cheerful mood."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/luncheon-of-the-boating-party.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","Luncheon of the Boating Party","Pierre-Auguste Renoir","renoir-boating","Pierre-Auguste Renoir · 1880–81 · Oil on canvas, 130.2 × 175.6 cm. The Phillips Collection, Washington, DC · Renoir's friends linger after lunch on a restaurant balcony at Chatou, west of Paris. It joins group portrait, still life and landscape in one large canvas. · No preparatory drawings are known, and scans show repainted sitters: Renoir seems to have composed it on the canvas itself. Working directly, rather than from studies, suited the Impressionists' approach. · Painted mostly on the balcony of the Fournaise family's restaurant. · Aline Charigot, Renoir's future wife, plays with a small dog. · The painter Gustave Caillebotte sits backward on his chair. · Duncan Phillips bought it in 1923 for his Washington gallery. · The restaurant owner's son leans on the railing; the woman resting her elbows on it is thought to be his sister."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/paris-street-rainy-day.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","Paris Street; Rainy Day","Gustave Caillebotte","caillebotte-rainy-day","Gustave Caillebotte · 1877 · Oil on canvas, 212.2 × 276.2 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Life-size Parisians cross a wet, newly built intersection near the Gare Saint-Lazare. It is Caillebotte's most ambitious picture of the rebuilt city and dominated the 1877 Impressionist exhibition. · The streets themselves, newly cut through an old quarter under Haussmann's plan, are the subject. Taking the changing city of the moment as material for a major picture was central to Impressionism. · Shown as no. 1 at the third Impressionist exhibition, which Caillebotte largely organized. · Its finish, perspective and size suited Salon taste; its lopsided layout and cropped figures were new. · Look at the green lamppost splitting the scene and the man cut off at right. · Sold by the Caillebotte family in 1954; bought by the Art Institute in 1964. · The figures wear the newest styles of 1877, and their umbrellas were a recent design. Caillebotte, son of a wealthy family, painted the well-dressed middle class."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/the-floor-scrapers.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","The Floor Scrapers","Gustave Caillebotte","caillebotte-floor-scrapers","Gustave Caillebotte · 1875 · Oil on canvas, 102 × 147 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Three workmen plane the floor of a bare room, seen from above. It is one of the first large exhibition pictures of city laborers. · Earlier painters of labor showed peasants and country workers. Caillebotte chose city tradesmen, carrying the Impressionist interest in modern urban life into the world of work. · Rejected by the Salon in 1875, it was shown at the second Impressionist exhibition in 1876. · Trained under Léon Bonnat, Caillebotte drew each part separately, then transferred it with a squared grid. · Look at the curls of wood shaved from the boards, lit from the balcony door. · Given to the French state by Caillebotte's heirs in 1894. · The bare-chested workers look more like classical nudes than real tradesmen. Unlike Millet or Courbet, the museum notes, Caillebotte makes no moral or political point."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/young-man-at-his-window.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","Young Man at His Window","Gustave Caillebotte","caillebotte-window","Gustave Caillebotte · 1876 · Oil on canvas, 116.1 × 81 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · Caillebotte's brother René stands at an open window in the family apartment, looking down at a sunlit street. It was among the most praised works of the artist's 1876 debut. · The street is seen from inside the home, past René's back, toward a lone woman far below. The Impressionists kept looking for new viewpoints on the city, and the window was one. · Shown at the second Impressionist exhibition in 1876, with The Floor Scrapers. · One of three 1876 family interiors, with Young Man Playing the Piano and Luncheon. · Strong outside light turns the dark-suited figure almost into a silhouette. · Bought by the Getty at Christie's in 2021, its first Caillebotte. · The family lived in an upper-floor apartment on a street closed in by tall blocks. The red armchair suggests René has just stood up."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/arrival-of-the-normandy-train-gare-saint-lazare.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","Arrival of the Normandy Train, Gare Saint-Lazare","Claude Monet","monet-saint-lazare","Claude Monet · 1877 · Oil on canvas, 60.3 × 80.2 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A train pulls in under the glass-and-iron shed of the Paris terminal for Normandy. The station pictures were Monet's first series, a method he kept for life. · Monet paints the steam under the roof and the daylight through the glass, not the train. Impressionist painters often treated modern city sites as studies of changing atmosphere. · Eight of his twelve known station canvases were ready for the April 1877 exhibition. · Sold in March 1877 to the collector Ernest Hoschedé. · After this group Monet largely gave up city subjects for landscape. · Left to the Art Institute in 1933 by the Chicago collector Martin A. Ryerson. · series · Paintings of one motif made in sequence to record changing light or weather. · The station stood in the same new quarter as the street in Caillebotte's Paris Street; Rainy Day. Monet's outdoor method had taken shape in Normandy in the 1860s."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/theme-tools-for-painting-outdoors.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor · Theme","Tools for Painting Outdoors","","monet-saint-lazare","Railways, paint in metal tubes and folding easels made it practical to paint a whole picture outdoors. · These tools did not create Impressionism, but they made its outdoor practice far easier. · Rail: a morning train took a painter and kit from Paris to a country motif. · Tube paint: John G. Rand's collapsible tin tube of 1841 replaced leaky pigs’ bladders. · Easels and brushes: folding easels, and flat brushes that laid broad strokes quickly, helped painters keep pace with light."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/in-a-cafe-labsinthe.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","In a Café (L'Absinthe)","Edgar Degas","degas-absinthe","Edgar Degas · 1875–76 · Oil on canvas, 92 × 68.5 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A man and a woman sit side by side in a café, silent and apart, a glass of absinthe before her. It is among Degas's bleakest city scenes. · Degas hems the pair in between empty tabletops and the wall behind them. Unlike most of his Impressionist friends, he preferred enclosed city spaces to open landscape. · Shown at the third Impressionist exhibition in 1877, though not in the catalogue. · Degas painted it in the studio, with friends posing: the actress Ellen Andrée and the engraver Marcellin Desboutin. · Look at the mirror behind them, where their shadows merge into one dark shape. · Came to the Louvre in 1911 with Count Isaac de Camondo's bequest. · Absinthe, a strong anise spirit, was thought harmful and later banned in France. The café, the Nouvelle Athènes on the place Pigalle, was a meeting place for artists."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/the-boulevard-montmartre-on-a-winter-morning.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","The Boulevard Montmartre on a Winter Morning","Camille Pissarro","pissarro-montmartre","Camille Pissarro · 1897 · Oil on canvas, 64.8 × 81.3 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A busy boulevard seen from a hotel window on a gray winter morning. In this late series Pissarro brought the Impressionist study of light and weather to Haussmann's Paris. · Bare trees and gray light tie this canvas to one winter morning. Its fifteen companions from the same window change the hour and weather. · In February 1897 he took a big room in the Grand Hôtel de Russie. · The street climbs from the bottom edge to a distant vanishing point. · Given to the Met in 1960 by Katrin S. Vietor, honoring her husband. · Pissarro, mostly a painter of villages and fields, left Eragny for long city stays in the 1890s."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/a-loge-at-the-theatre-des-italiens.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","A Loge at the Théâtre des Italiens","Eva Gonzalès","gonzales-loge","Eva Gonzalès · c. 1874 · Oil on canvas, 97.7 × 130 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A young woman in pale blue sits in a theater box, a man at her shoulder. Gonzalès, Manet's pupil by her own account, painted it at the same time as Renoir's La Loge, and shares its theme. · Pale faces and light fabrics stand out against the dark box, in Manet's manner. Gonzalès, like Manet, showed at the Salon. · An earlier state may have been refused by the 1874 Salon; reworked, it was well received in 1879. · Look at the bouquet on the ledge, which echoes the flowers in Manet's Olympia. · Given to the Louvre in 1927 by her son, Jean Guérard. · The models were her sister Jeanne and Henri Guérard, whom she married. Their cool detachment, the museum notes, recalls Manet's refusal to explain a scene."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/a-bar-at-the-folies-bergere.html","Modern Paris: Boulevards, Leisure and Labor","A Bar at the Folies-Bergère","Édouard Manet","manet-folies-bergere","Édouard Manet · 1882 · Oil on canvas, 96 × 130 cm. The Courtauld, London · A barmaid stands behind a marble counter at a Paris music hall, the crowded room behind her seen in a mirror. It was Manet's last major painting. · Reflections of the barmaid and the man who seems to be her customer are shifted right. The puzzling mirror conveys the hall's bustle, a night-life subject the Impressionists also painted. · Shown at the Salon of 1882, the year before Manet died. · Manet sketched at the hall, then painted it in the studio with a barmaid called Suzon posing. · Look at the counter: champagne, crème de menthe and Bass ale with its red triangle. · Samuel Courtauld bought it in 1926 and gave it to the Courtauld in 1934. · The Folies-Bergère, opened about a decade earlier, was one of the most popular music halls in Paris. Its barmaids worked in full view as part of the show."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/degas-movement-work-and-the-body.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body · Overview","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","","degas-dance-class","Dancers, laundresses, jockeys and bathers, seen from odd angles. · Edgar Degas showed at nearly every Impressionist exhibition, but he worked mainly indoors, from drawings, models and memory. He returned to ballet, laundresses, racing and bathers for more than thirty years. · He sent work to the official Salon in the 1860s, then joined the independent exhibitions in 1874. · The Paris Opéra was his most frequent setting: first the old house, which burned in 1873, then the new Palais Garnier. · He used the group shows to test new kinds of work: a wax figure in 1881, pastel nudes in 1886. · Some works, such as the steeplechase scene and Four Dancers, stayed in his studio until his estate sales in 1918. · pastel · Sticks of powdered color, used like chalk; Degas often layered it over a print. · petit rat · Nickname for a pupil of the Paris Opéra ballet school. · Edgar Degas · The Dance Class · 1874"],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/the-dance-class.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","The Dance Class","Edgar Degas","degas-dance-class","Edgar Degas · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 83.5 × 77.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A practice room at the Paris Opéra, where a ballet master watches one pupil hold a pose. It is one of Degas's two most fully worked dance-class pictures. · Only one girl dances; the rest of the class waits, stretches and fidgets. Degas paints the in-between moments, the ordinary scenes the Impressionists made their subject. · The singer Jean-Baptiste Faure commissioned a dance-class picture in 1873; Degas delivered this canvas in November 1874. · The teacher leaning on a tall stick is Jules Perrot, a retired dancer and choreographer. · X-rays show that Degas moved the central dancer and changed the mirror as he worked. · It came to the Met by bequest in 1986. · The Opéra ballet had about two hundred performers, some as young as fourteen. Older women acting as chaperones often waited on benches, as at the back here."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/the-rehearsal-of-the-ballet-onstage.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","The Rehearsal of the Ballet Onstage","Edgar Degas","degas-rehearsal-stage","Edgar Degas · c. 1874 · Oil thinned with turpentine, with watercolor and pastel, over pen and ink on paper, mounted on canvas, 54.3 × 73 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Dancers rehearse, wait and yawn on the Opéra stage. · Degas sets us just above the orchestra pit, the tops of two double basses cutting into the bottom edge. Impressionist painters, Degas above all, often chose high, cropped views like this. · It is one of three related versions; a larger gray one hung in the first Impressionist exhibition, 1874. · The writer George Moore said the Illustrated London News refused the drawing as improper; Degas then painted over it. · Louisine and Henry Havemeyer acquired it in 1902, and it came to the Met in 1929. · Season subscribers (abonnés) could enter the wings, and many pursued the young, often poor dancers. Two of them sprawl on chairs at the right."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/ballet-the-star.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","Ballet (The Star)","Edgar Degas","degas-star","Edgar Degas · c. 1876–77 · Pastel over monotype, 58.4 × 42 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A solo dancer leans forward on one leg at the front of the stage, while others wait among the scenery. · Footlights strike the dancer from below, giving her face and arms a pale upward light. Degas studies modern artificial light here as closely as other Impressionists studied daylight. · Shown at the third Impressionist exhibition in 1877. · The stage floor seems to tilt up behind her, as if seen from a high box. · Gustave Caillebotte bought it after the 1877 show; it reached the French national collections through his bequest in 1896. · monotype · A one-off print made by pressing paper onto an inked plate. · In Paris a leading solo dancer was called a star (étoile). She stood above the many dancers who performed in groups."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/little-dancer-aged-fourteen.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","Little Dancer Aged Fourteen","Edgar Degas","degas-little-dancer","Edgar Degas · 1878–1881 · Pigmented beeswax, clay, metal armature, rope, paintbrushes, human hair, silk and linen ribbon, cotton faille bodice, cotton and silk tutu, linen slippers, on wooden base, 98.9 × 34.7 × 35.2 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A wax figure of a pupil of the Paris Opéra ballet school. It is the only sculpture Degas ever exhibited, and the original behind the later bronzes. · Degas gave her real hair, a cloth bodice, a tutu and slippers. He pushed the Impressionist interest in real, unidealized modern people into sculpture and its materials. · Shown at the sixth Impressionist exhibition in 1881; critics called the girl ugly but admitted how real she looked. · The model was Marie van Goethem, whose mother was a laundress. · X-rays show Degas remodeled the head; old paintbrushes inside stiffen the arms. · Paul Mellon bought this wax in 1956 and left it to the National Gallery of Art in 1999. · Most ballet pupils came from working-class families like the model's. The museum notes that these poor girls could become targets for men posing as \"protectors.\""],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/miss-la-la-at-the-cirque-fernando.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","Miss La La at the Cirque Fernando","Edgar Degas","degas-la-la","Edgar Degas · 1879 · Oil on canvas, 117.2 × 77.5 cm. The National Gallery, London · The acrobat Miss La La is hoisted into the roof of a Paris circus on a rope, hanging from a mouthpiece clenched in her teeth. It is Degas's only circus painting. · Degas looks steeply up from the seats, so iron girders fill more of the canvas than she does. Impressionist painters often took up modern iron buildings, from bridges to exhibition halls. · Degas lent it himself to the fourth Impressionist exhibition in 1879. · The Cirque Fernando opened in 1875 near the Place Pigalle, close to Degas's home. · Walter Sickert said an architectural draftsman helped Degas with the ceiling's perspective. · Bought through the Courtauld Fund in 1925; at the National Gallery since 1950. · Miss La La was the stage name of Anna Albertine Olga Brown, born in Prussia to a white mother and an African American father. Her best-known feat was the \"Iron Jaw\" act."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/woman-ironing.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","Woman Ironing","Edgar Degas","degas-woman-ironing","Edgar Degas · begun c. 1876, completed c. 1887 · Oil on canvas, 81.3 × 66 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A laundress leans her weight onto an iron as she presses a shirt. · Her dark figure stands against a bright window hung with drying clothes. How light from behind changes a figure was a typical Impressionist problem. · Edmond de Goncourt said Degas showed him many laundresses and explained the iron's motions. · Look at the stiff white shirt folded on the table. · It was shown in Paris in 1924 as Repasseuse à contre-jour (Ironer Against the Light). · Paul Mellon bought it in 1972 and gave it to the National Gallery of Art the same year. · Realist novels of the day dwelt on laundresses' heavy, poorly paid work. Many people doubted their respectability, partly because they delivered linen alone to men's lodgings."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/scene-from-the-steeplechase-the-fallen-jockey.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","Scene from the Steeplechase: The Fallen Jockey","Edgar Degas","degas-fallen-jockey","Edgar Degas · 1866, reworked 1880–1881 and c. 1897 · Oil on canvas, 180 × 152 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A jockey lies on his back after a fall while horses gallop over him. Degas first showed it at the Salon of 1866, as Steeplechase Scene. · Patches of the first blue sky and a painted-out horse still show through. Degas leaves these traces of his changes visible, as Impressionist painters often let the process of painting show. · He made large changes each time he reworked it. · Mr. and Mrs. Paul Mellon acquired it in 1960; it came to the National Gallery of Art in 1999. · A steeplechase is a horse race over fences and ditches, so falls were common. Jockeys wore bright silk caps, like the pink one lying beside the fallen man."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/woman-bathing-in-a-shallow-tub.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","Woman Bathing in a Shallow Tub","Edgar Degas","degas-shallow-tub","Edgar Degas · 1885 · Charcoal and pastel on light green wove paper, now discolored to warm gray, 81.3 × 56.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A woman bends double over her lower leg in a wide, shallow tub, a cloth in her hand. Degas showed it among his pastel nudes at the last Impressionist exhibition, in 1886. · Her face is hidden and her body folds over the task, as if no one were watching. Impressionist painters often showed people absorbed in private, ordinary tasks like this. · Signed and dated \"Degas / 85\" at upper left. · Look at the tub, seen from above, nearly filling the bottom of the sheet. · Degas traded it to Mary Cassatt; it came to the Met with the Havemeyer bequest in 1929. · His bathers are ordinary women, not the goddesses or nymphs that usually justified a nude in art."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/the-tub.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","The Tub","Edgar Degas","degas-tub","Edgar Degas · 1886 · Pastel on cardboard, 60 × 83 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A woman crouches in a round tub beside a shelf of toilet articles. Shown at the 1886 Impressionist exhibition, it is one of Degas's boldest bathers. · Degas looks straight down, so the shelf at right runs almost flat up the picture. The museum links this device to Japanese prints. · The Musée d'Orsay counts it as one of seven pictures of women washing from the mid-1880s. · The pose is based on the Crouching Aphrodite, an ancient statue type. · Degas had already drawn bathing women about a decade earlier, in monotypes. · Count Isaac de Camondo left it to the Louvre in 1911; it is now at the Musée d'Orsay. · Some critics of 1886 called the bathers' poses animal-like and unflattering."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/four-dancers.html","Degas: Movement, Work and the Body","Four Dancers","Edgar Degas","degas-four-dancers","Edgar Degas · c. 1899 · Oil on canvas, 151.1 × 180.2 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Four dancers in orange bodices adjust their straps before going on, crowded into the left half of a wide canvas. It shows the strong color and simple forms of Degas's late work. · Each figure reaches for a strap at a different angle, so the group can read as one dancer turning. Catching movement, not a fixed pose, was an Impressionist aim. · Thick black outlines surround the bodies. · The tutus and backdrop are loose dabs of green, yellow, blue, pink and gold. · Chester Dale bought it from Durand-Ruel in 1931 and left it to the National Gallery of Art in 1963. · Opéra scenery often included painted flats of trees, so the backdrop can read as a stage set or a real landscape."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/women-impressionists.html","Women Impressionists · Overview","Women Impressionists","","cassatt-loge-mfa","Morisot, Cassatt and Bracquemond · Shut out of much of men's training and unable to move freely around the city, Morisot, Cassatt and Bracquemond made homes, gardens and theater boxes their subjects. · Women could not train at the state École des Beaux-Arts until 1897. · Morisot showed in seven of the eight Impressionist exhibitions and helped pay to keep the group going. · Cassatt, the one American formally linked to the group, exhibited in 1879, 1880, 1881 and 1886. · Bracquemond later largely gave up painting. · Genre scene · A picture of everyday life, not history, myth or a formal portrait. · Japonisme · The European taste for Japanese art, especially woodblock prints, from the 1850s. · Mary Cassatt · In the Loge · 1878"],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/the-mother-and-sister-of-the-artist.html","Women Impressionists","The Mother and Sister of the Artist","Berthe Morisot","morisot-mother-sister","Berthe Morisot · 1869–70 · Oil on canvas, 101 × 81.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Morisot's mother, in black, reads while her sister Edma sits behind her in white. It is one of Morisot's largest canvases, shown at the Salon of 1870. · Morisot's letters record that Manet heavily repainted the mother on the last day for Salon entries. · Manet's broad strokes shape the mother; Morisot's lighter touch shapes Edma and the sofa. For the Impressionists, visible brushwork like this was a mark of each painter's own hand. · Edma's loose white morning robe conceals her pregnancy with her first child. · Chester Dale bequeathed it to the National Gallery of Art in 1963. · Daughters of Morisot's upper-middle-class family were expected to paint only as amateurs. Edma stopped painting after her marriage in 1869."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/summers-day.html","Women Impressionists","Summer's Day","Berthe Morisot","morisot-summers-day","Berthe Morisot · c. 1879 · Oil on canvas, 45.7 × 75.2 cm. The National Gallery, London · Two young women drift in a rowing boat on a lake in the Bois de Boulogne. They sit so close that the viewer seems to share the boat with them. · Quick zigzag strokes build the women, the water and the trees alike, so the whole surface flickers. Broken brushwork like this gave Impressionist pictures their sense of movement. · Almost certainly shown at the fifth Impressionist exhibition, April 1880, as The Lake in the Bois de Boulogne. · The same two women, in the same clothes, appear in a companion picture of the park. · The tree line sits at the top edge, so water fills almost the whole picture. · Part of Hugh Lane's 1917 bequest, shared between London and the Hugh Lane Gallery in Dublin. · Under Napoleon III, the Bois de Boulogne was remade with lakes, islands and winding paths. Its boating made it a ready setting for modern leisure."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/in-the-dining-room.html","Women Impressionists","In the Dining Room","Berthe Morisot","morisot-dining-room","Berthe Morisot · 1886 · Oil on canvas, 61.3 × 50 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A young maid in a dark dress and white apron stands in a dining room and looks straight out. Morisot paints the room around her in loose, rapid strokes. · A maid, not the family she serves, fills the picture in an ordinary working moment. · Shown at the last Impressionist exhibition in 1886, listed as Petite servante (Little Servant). · A cabinet of white and silver pieces stands at left, a round table at right. · Look at the lower edge, where a small spotted dog runs past her feet. · Chester Dale's bequest brought it to the National Gallery of Art in 1963. · Paris households of Morisot's class kept live-in servants. They cooked, cleaned and waited at table."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/little-girl-in-a-blue-armchair.html","Women Impressionists","Little Girl in a Blue Armchair","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-blue-armchair","Mary Cassatt · 1878 · Oil on canvas, 89.5 × 129.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A small girl slumps in an overstuffed blue armchair beside a sleeping dog. Cassatt shows a bored child, not a doll-like model of good manners. · The girl sprawls with one arm behind her head, as no posed portrait sitter would. The Impressionists wanted figures caught off guard, in natural, unposed moments. · Possibly shown at the 1879 Impressionist exhibition. · Degas, a friend of the girl's parents, probably worked on the background. · The chairs crowd the floor and are cut off at the edges of the canvas. · Mr. and Mrs. Paul Mellon gave it to the National Gallery of Art in 1983. · Nineteenth-century portraits usually showed children as small, well-behaved adults or emblems of innocence."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/theme-cassatt-and-degas-an-exchange.html","Women Impressionists · Theme","Cassatt and Degas: An Exchange","","cassatt-blue-armchair","For about a decade Cassatt and Degas traded criticism, techniques and works, with Degas as her main adviser. · Degas met Cassatt in 1877 and invited her to show with the independent group. Their friendship lasted until his death in 1917. · Both came from prosperous banking families and preferred the human figure to landscape. · Both made printmaking central to their work, and for a time they worked on prints together. · Degas kept buying Cassatt's work, and she steered American collectors such as Louisine Havemeyer toward his."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/in-the-loge.html","Women Impressionists","In the Loge","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-loge-mfa","Mary Cassatt · 1878 · Oil on canvas, 81.3 × 66 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · A woman in black leans forward in a theater box and aims her opera glasses across the hall. · Renoir and Degas painted women in theater boxes as attractive objects on display. Cassatt's woman does the looking herself, a new turn on a favorite Impressionist subject. · Shown in Boston in 1878, the first Impressionist painting by Cassatt seen in America. · The theater is the Comédie-Française during an afternoon show, so she wears a day dress. · Look at the upper left, where a man in another box trains his glasses on her. · The Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, bought it in 1910."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/at-the-theater.html","Women Impressionists","At the Theater","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-at-theater","Mary Cassatt · c. 1879 · Pastel on paper, 55.5 × 46.1 cm. The Nelson-Atkins Museum of Art, Kansas City · A red-haired woman in a yellow evening gown sits in a theater box, lit from behind by a chandelier. · Theater and opera scenes were among her main subjects in the late 1870s and early 1880s. · Diagonal pastel strokes run through her hair, dress and the hall, so all share one artificial glow. · At right, a mirror seems to repeat her hair and shoulders. · Signed lower left. The Nelson-Atkins bought it with an anonymous gift. · In the evening, women in boxes wore low-cut gowns under bright gas and chandelier light."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/the-tea.html","Women Impressionists","The Tea","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-tea","Mary Cassatt · c. 1880 · Oil on canvas, 64.8 × 92.1 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Two women sit over afternoon tea; the guest, still in hat and gloves, hides her face behind her cup. · The repeated ovals of cups, tray, hats and faces carry the picture more than the social call does. Impressionists often put color and shape ahead of storytelling. · Shown at the fifth Impressionist exhibition in 1880, where critics were divided. · The sitters are usually called Lydia Cassatt and a family friend. · The silver was first made in Philadelphia around 1813 for Cassatt's grandmother and enlarged by her mother. · The Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, bought it from the dealer Dikran Kelekian in 1942. · Receiving callers for afternoon tea was a regular part of life for upper-middle-class women in Paris. Cassatt painted the custom several times around 1880."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/lydia-crocheting-in-the-garden-at-marly.html","Women Impressionists","Lydia Crocheting in the Garden at Marly","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-lydia-crocheting","Mary Cassatt · 1880 · Oil on canvas, 65.6 × 92.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Cassatt's older sister Lydia sits crocheting in a sunlit walled garden, dressed far more formally than the task requires. Lydia was the relative Cassatt painted most often in these years. · One of Cassatt's few outdoor paintings, it catches the strong sunlight of a summer garden. · The Cassatt family spent the season at Marly-le-Roi. · Hand needlework grew more prized in wealthy homes as factory-made textiles spread. · Look at the large, close figure set against the path and dark flower beds behind. · Mrs. Gardner Cassatt gave it to the Met in 1965. · Well-off Paris families often spent the summer in villages such as Marly-le-Roi. Needlework, reading and visits filled the days of women of Cassatt's class."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/the-childs-bath.html","Women Impressionists","The Child's Bath","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-childs-bath","Mary Cassatt · 1893 · Oil on canvas, 101.3 × 67.3 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A woman holds a child on her lap and washes its feet in a basin. It was number 1 in Cassatt's 1893 solo exhibition at Durand-Ruel's Paris gallery. · From above, the floor tilts up and the dress, rug and chest lie flat as pattern. Like Degas, Cassatt traded the deep space of Salon painting for steep, flattened views. · It followed her color prints of 1890–91, begun after a Japanese print show in Paris. · Look at the child's hand resting on the woman's knee. · The Art Institute of Chicago bought it in 1910. · Well-off households often shared the bathing and feeding of small children with nurses and maids. From the 1890s this care became Cassatt's best-known subject."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/the-bath.html","Women Impressionists","The Bath","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-bath-print","Mary Cassatt · 1890–91 · Drypoint, soft-ground etching and aquatint, printed in color from two plates; image 29.5 × 24.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A woman in a yellow dress steadies a child on her lap beside a basin of water. It is the first of ten color prints Cassatt showed in Paris in 1891. · The everyday subject, a quiet moment at home, is Impressionist, but the flat, unshaded color and firm outlines follow Japanese prints, a step away from Impressionism's broken brushwork. · Cassatt printed it with a professional printer, Monsieur Leroy. · An early printing used one plate; this sheet used two, for lines and for tone. · Look at the plain yellow dress beside the small ripples in the basin. · Paul J. Sachs gave it to the Met in 1916, the year its print department began. · Drypoint · Lines scratched straight into a metal plate with a sharp needle. · Aquatint · An etching method that prints areas of even tone. · Japanese prints of women's daily routines were widely collected in Paris in the 1880s and 1890s."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/modern-life/le-gouter-afternoon-tea.html","Women Impressionists","Le Goûter (Afternoon Tea)","Marie Bracquemond","bracquemond-umbrellas","Marie Bracquemond · c. 1880 · Oil on canvas, 81.5 × 61.5 cm. Petit Palais, Musée des Beaux-Arts de la Ville de Paris · A young woman in a white dress sits at a garden table, looking away from her open book. It is one of the few Bracquemond paintings in a public museum. · The white dress is built from strokes of blue, lilac and pink, not plain white. Impressionists painted whites and shadows in color, as they appear in daylight. · The sitter is the artist's sister, Louise Quivoron. · Bracquemond exhibited with the Impressionists in 1879, 1880 and 1886. · The setting is the garden of the Villa Brancas at Sèvres, just outside Paris. · The City of Paris acquired it in 1919, three years after the artist's death. · The goûter is a light French snack taken in the late afternoon, often outdoors. Historians link such garden scenes to the places women painters could most easily work."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/monets-series-and-the-end-of-the-group.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group · Overview","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","","monet-wheat-stacks","From the last group show to the water lilies · After the last group exhibition in 1886, the Impressionists went separate ways. Monet made the series his main form. · The group held eight independent exhibitions between 1874 and 1886, with between nine (1882) and thirty-one (1874) artists taking part. · Monet settled at Giverny in 1883 and found most of his later subjects close to home. · At the 1886 exhibition Seurat's circle, Pissarro among them, showed a new dot-based method. · Neo-Impressionism · Fénéon's 1886 name for Seurat's method of small, planned touches of color. · One-artist exhibition · A show of one living artist's work. · Claude Monet · Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer) · 1890–91"],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/woman-with-a-parasol-madame-monet-and-her-son.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","Woman with a Parasol – Madame Monet and Her Son","Claude Monet","monet-woman-parasol","Claude Monet · 1875 · Oil on canvas, 100 × 81 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Monet's wife Camille and son Jean, seen from below against a windy sky. It shows how far Monet would push fast, broken brushwork. · Monet caught the blowing veil and the sun on Camille's dress in one outdoor sitting. · Monet showed it at the second Impressionist exhibition in 1876, under the title Promenade. · The low viewpoint sets both figures against open sky. · Quick, loose strokes leave bare canvas showing in parts of the sky. · Bought for Paul Mellon in 1965, it came to the National Gallery as a gift in 1983. · In the 1870s Monet often posed his household outdoors near home. This outing reads more like a snapshot than a formal portrait."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer)","Claude Monet","monet-wheat-stacks","Claude Monet · 1890–91 · Oil on canvas, 60 × 100.5 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Two large wheat stacks beside Monet's house at Giverny, in the low light of late summer. The series brought him praise and strong sales. · Monet painted the same stacks again and again to record how light shifts with the hour and season. Shown together, the series made that change the subject. · This was no. 1 of fifteen stack paintings shown at Durand-Ruel's Paris gallery in 1891. · Monet painted at several easels in the field at once, then refined the color in the studio. · Look at the cone-shaped tops, which cut through the horizon line. · The Palmer family of Chicago gave it to the Art Institute in shares from 1985 to 2007. · Wheat stacks stood in the fields around Giverny at harvest."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/theme-durand-ruel-and-the-market.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group · Theme","Durand-Ruel and the Market","","monet-wheat-stacks","Monet's series reached buyers through one dealer, Paul Durand-Ruel, who helped turn the Impressionists' work into a market. · Durand-Ruel took up the Impressionists in the 1870s and came close to bankruptcy twice. By the 1890s Monet launched each new series as a group at his Paris gallery. Branches in New York, London and Brussels widened his reach. · He paid Monet 3,000 francs for Stacks of Wheat in May 1891 and resold it for 6,000. · His New York branch sold The Four Trees to the Havemeyers and Water Lilies to Martin A. Ryerson. · He sometimes held stock jointly with the Paris firm Bernheim-Jeune, as with Rouen Cathedral and Water Lilies."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/the-four-trees.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","The Four Trees","Claude Monet","monet-poplars","Claude Monet · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 81.9 × 81.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Four poplar trunks on the bank of the river Epte, cut off by the top edge and doubled in the water. · Four trunks and a crossing band of foliage form a flat grid on a nearly square canvas. Here Impressionist close looking at nature edges toward abstraction. · Fifteen of the poplars, this one included, were shown together at Durand-Ruel's Paris gallery in 1892. · The village of Limetz planned to auction the trees, so Monet paid a timber dealer to delay the felling. · He painted this view from a flat-bottomed boat with racks for several canvases. · Bought by the Havemeyers in 1895, it came to the Met with their bequest in 1929. · Poplars grow fast and were planted in rows along French rivers, roads and field edges."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/rouen-cathedral-west-facade-sunlight.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","Rouen Cathedral, West Façade, Sunlight","Claude Monet","monet-rouen-sunlight","Claude Monet · 1894 · Oil on canvas, 100.1 × 65.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The west front of Rouen Cathedral in Normandy, seen at an angle in bright sun. It is one of more than thirty views of the façade that Monet began in 1892–93. · Up close the stone façade breaks into a thick crust of cream, blue and gold dabs. Here the visible Impressionist brushstroke becomes nearly as solid as the building. · Monet rented a room facing the cathedral's west front in 1892 and 1893. · He finished the canvases at Giverny in 1894, the year written beside his signature. · Look at the lower left corner, where a few dark strokes stand for people and give scale. · Chester Dale bought it in 1926 and left it to the National Gallery in 1963. · The Gothic front is crowded with pointed arches, spires and carved figures. Monet made changing light, not its history, the center of attention."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/the-houses-of-parliament-sunset.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","The Houses of Parliament, Sunset","Claude Monet","monet-parliament-sunset","Claude Monet · 1903 · Oil on canvas, 81.3 × 92.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Parliament's towers in London at sunset, repeated in the Thames below. It is one of Monet's London views, reworked for years and shown in Paris in 1904. · The towers are a blue silhouette in a haze of pink, peach and lavender. Monet paints the air as color. · Between 1899 and 1901 Monet made close to one hundred pictures of the Thames. · He developed the London canvases together at Giverny and signed this one in 1903. · Look at the upright strokes that build the towers and the short horizontal strokes of the water. · Chester Dale bought it in 1929 and left it to the National Gallery in 1963. · Monet painted Parliament from St Thomas' Hospital, across the Thames. The Met owns a companion picture of the same view in fog."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/bridge-over-a-pond-of-water-lilies.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","Bridge over a Pond of Water Lilies","Claude Monet","monet-water-lily-bridge","Claude Monet · 1899 · Oil on canvas, 92.7 × 73.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The wooden footbridge over Monet's lily pond at Giverny, above floating lilies and reflections. It comes from his first bridge series. · Monet bought the pond in 1893 and built the bridge to give himself motifs to paint. The Impressionists painted the world around them; here Monet designed his own. · He painted eighteen bridge views in 1899–1900 and showed twelve in 1900. · This is one of only two upright versions, which gives more room to the lilies. · Look for thick dabs for the lily flowers and a dark red curve for the bridge's shadow. · The Havemeyers bought it through Durand-Ruel's New York branch in 1901. · Monet shaped his flower and water gardens at Giverny for decades. In 1901 he enlarged the pond and added a wisteria frame to the bridge."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/water-lilies.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","Water Lilies","Claude Monet","monet-water-lilies-1906","Claude Monet · 1906 · Oil on canvas, 89.9 × 94.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A view straight down onto the Giverny pond, lilies floating among reflections. It is a step toward the mural-size water lilies of Monet's last years. · Bank and sky are gone; reflections of clouds and trees float in a pond with no horizon. Light on water, an early Impressionist subject, becomes the whole picture. · Monet's first water-lily paintings of 1897–99 still showed the bridge and trees below a horizon. · It was shown in Durand-Ruel's 1909 Paris exhibition of Monet's water landscapes. · Martin A. Ryerson of Chicago bought it in 1914 and left it to the Art Institute in 1933."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/weeping-willow.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","Weeping Willow","Claude Monet","monet-weeping-willow","Claude Monet · 1918–19 · Oil on canvas, 99.7 × 120 cm. Kimbell Art Museum, Fort Worth · The twisting trunk and hanging branches of a willow beside Monet's water garden, in shifting greens, yellows and blues. It is one of ten willow pictures Monet completed by 1919. · The museum reads the curling strokes and shadowy color as grief for the war years. Monet's late brushwork moves from recording light, the Impressionist aim, toward expressing feeling. · The willows are among the few easel-size pictures Monet made after 1914. · The war emptied Giverny of most of his family and staff, but Monet stayed on. · He built the picture in layers, and in the foliage he scraped paint away. · Kojiro Matsukata owned it by 1924; the Kimbell bought it in 1996. · This willow stood on the bank of the lily pond. Its trunk and reflection reappear in the huge water-lily panels that occupied Monet until his death in 1926."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/the-artists-garden-at-eragny.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","The Artist's Garden at Eragny","Camille Pissarro","pissarro-eragny","Camille Pissarro · 1898 · Oil on canvas, 73.4 × 92.1 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The vegetable and flower beds of Pissarro's garden at Eragny, with a woman bent over her work. Like Monet, Pissarro in his last years kept returning to the ground around his own home. · Small spots of yellow, pink, coral and red scatter across the greens and browns. · Pissarro lived at Eragny, northwest of Paris, from 1884 until his death in 1903. · An eye infection troubled him outdoors, so he often painted from his windows. · Look at the bending woman, shown with only a few strokes. · Ailsa Mellon Bruce bought it in 1965, and her bequest brought it to the National Gallery in 1970. · Julie Pissarro came from the countryside, and the household lived close to the land. Vegetable beds, an orchard and a meadow lay beside the house."],[4,"/study/art-history/impressionism/late-impressionism/a-sunday-on-la-grande-jatte-1884.html","Monet's Series and the End of the Group","A Sunday on La Grande Jatte — 1884","Georges Seurat","seurat-grande-jatte","Georges Seurat · 1884–86 · Oil on canvas, 207.5 × 308.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Parisians of different classes spend a Sunday on a Seine island. It introduced the method known as neo-impressionism. · Where the Impressionists painted fast to catch a moment, Seurat worked for two years in systematic dots, and his figures stand still. The picture pointed beyond Impressionism. · It made its debut in Paris in 1886, at the group's eighth and final show. · Over short strokes in complementary colors, Seurat laid dots that merge from a distance. · Look at the painted border of blue, orange and red dots around the scene. · Frederic Clay and Helen Birch Bartlett gave it to the Art Institute in 1926. · As Smarthistory points out, the figures do not interact with one another."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/ink-line-to-hand-color.html","Ink Line to Hand Color · Overview","Ink Line to Hand Color","","anchi-courtesan-walking","Pictures for Edo's townspeople, c. 1680–1764 · Single-sheet prints grew out of Edo's illustrated books in the late 1600s and found buyers among the city's townspeople. For most of this period color was added by hand; printed color came only gradually. · Pictures cheap enough for city buyers to own · Courtesans and kabuki stars as favorite subjects · Named designers and the first family studios · Kaigetsudō Anchi · Courtesan Walking · c. 1714"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/a-parade-of-courtesans.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","A Parade of Courtesans","Hishikawa Moronobu (attributed)","moronobu-parade-courtesans","Hishikawa Moronobu (attributed) · c. 1690 · Woodblock print; ink on paper, 31 × 68 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A file of courtesans, each shaded by an attendant's parasol, seems to move through the Yoshiwara. · Unsigned and without a publisher's mark, it is attributed by the museum. · Moronobu is the first ukiyo-e designer known by name. · The figure at left is identified as a Soga brother, so the scene may draw on a kabuki play. · Edo · The old name of Tokyo, seat of the shoguns; it also names the Edo period, 1615–1868. · Ukiyo-e · “Pictures of the floating world”: prints and paintings of the city's pleasures, theater and fashions. · Yoshiwara · Edo's licensed pleasure quarter, a walled district of courtesans' houses, teahouses and entertainment. · Kabuki · Japan's popular stage drama, combining acting, music and dance. · Soga brothers · Twelfth-century heroes of a revenge story staged in many kabuki plays."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/the-entrance-to-ageya-machi.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","The Entrance to Ageya-machi","Hishikawa Moronobu","moronobu-entrance-ageya-machi","Hishikawa Moronobu · 1681–84 · Woodblock print; ink on paper, 25.7 × 38.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A samurai and his servant have just come through the great gate as two men joke with two women nearby. · From a set of Yoshiwara views; that gate, the Ōmon, was the only way in or out. · The figures stand on Nakamachi, the quarter's main street. · Black line alone renders the figures, roofs and lattices. · Ageya-machi · The Yoshiwara street of ageya, houses where clients met and entertained courtesans."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/on-the-yoshida-highway.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","On the Yoshida Highway","Sugimura Jihei (attributed)","sugimura-yoshida-highway","Sugimura Jihei (attributed) · c. 1685 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 59.1 × 29.3 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · An actor plays Miyako no O Kuni, the woman who founded kabuki, seated upstairs while one character shades another below. · Name tags identify the roles; the play and actors remain unknown. · Sugimura also helped popularize literary classics, John Carpenter notes. · Tan-e: orange-red lead color brushed on after printing, the first way color reached these prints."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/courtesan-walking.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Courtesan Walking","Kaigetsudō Anchi","anchi-courtesan-walking","Kaigetsudō Anchi · c. 1714 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 55.2 × 28.8 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Wind lifts a tall courtesan's robe, its fabric written over with a poem. · Look inside the robe: the poem's author is pictured on the lining. · The Art Institute contrasts its freedom with the rule-bound religious images of its day. · In early courtesan prints the costume drew as much attention as the face."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/kabuki-actor.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Kabuki Actor","Torii Kiyonobu I (attributed)","kiyonobu-kabuki-actor","Torii Kiyonobu I (attributed) · c. 1708 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 55.2 × 29.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A dancing actor, probably in a female role, whirls in a robe of swirling patterns and flowers. · Theater advertising was the studio's trade: kabuki posters and signboards. · The color was brushed on by hand over a sheet printed in black. · Thick, rounded outlines and flat pattern carry well from far off, a signboard painter's habit. · Torii · The family school of designers founded by Kiyonobu I. · I, II, V after a name · Generation numbers: stage and studio names passed from holder to holder, so Kiyonobu I is the first to use the name and Danjūrō V the fifth."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/courtesan-from-the-myogaya-house.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Courtesan from the Myōgaya House","Torii Kiyomasu I (attributed)","kiyomasu-courtesan-myogaya","Torii Kiyomasu I (attributed) · c. 1712 · Woodblock print (tan-e); ink and color on paper, 31.1 × 15.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A standing courtesan reads a poem slip; the inscription likens her to Ono no Komachi, the classical woman poet. · Her poem slip breaks off mid-line; its plum blossoms by a window hint at spring hope. · The comparison flattered the literary skill of many Yoshiwara courtesans. · Large ginger (myōga) shapes on her robes advertise her house."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/perspective-view-of-the-interior-of-the-nakamura-theater.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Perspective View of the Interior of the Nakamura Theater","Okumura Masanobu","masanobu-nakamura-theater","Okumura Masanobu · 1740 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 40.2 × 63.7 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · A packed kabuki house seen from the rear, with Ichikawa Ebizō II on stage as Yanone Gorō. · A runway crossed the seats; many patrons stayed all day, eating and calling out praise. · Its hand-brushed palette keeps to yellow, red and red-brown. · Probably the first printmaker to use Western one-point perspective, a novelty for buyers."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/the-actor-sanogawa-ichimatsu-i-as-hisamatsu.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","The Actor Sanogawa Ichimatsu I as Hisamatsu","Ishikawa Toyonobu","toyonobu-sanogawa-ichimatsu","Ishikawa Toyonobu · c. 1748 · Hand-colored woodblock print, 71 × 26.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A star poses in a role from a 1743 Soga play, his jacket in a bold brown-and-black check. · Glossy black mixed with glue, called lacquer picture (urushi-e), deepens the darks. · Signed and sealed, with the publisher Urokogata-ya's seal. · The check caught on as a fashion through this actor."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/ono-no-komachi-washing-the-copybook.html","Ink Line to Hand Color","Ono no Komachi Washing the Copybook","Torii Kiyomitsu I","kiyomitsu-komachi-copybook","Torii Kiyomitsu I · 1764 · Color woodblock print, 30.8 × 14 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · The poet washes an old manuscript to foil a rival who tried to discredit her. · Frank Lloyd Wright owned this impression before it came to the Art Institute. · He headed the Torii actor-print school in his day. · A water picture (mizu-e): colors printed from blocks with no black outlines, a year before full color."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/brocade-pictures-harunobu-and-1765.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765 · Overview","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","","harunobu-plum-blossoms-night","A decade of slender figures and soft color, 1765–1770s · In 1765 Edo printers began printing each color from its own carved block, one after another, so a sheet could carry many hues. Wealthy patrons first ordered them as New Year calendars, and the new prints became known as brocade pictures (nishiki-e). · Calendars, private commissions and the first full-color sheets · A delicate, youthful ideal of beauty · Old poems and legends in modern dress · Suzuki Harunobu · Woman Admiring Plum Blossoms at Night · c. 1766"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/young-woman-riding-a-phoenix.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Young Woman Riding a Phoenix","Suzuki Harunobu (attributed)","harunobu-young-woman-phoenix","Suzuki Harunobu (attributed) · 1765 · Color woodblock print, 20.5 × 27.7 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A girl in fashionable Edo dress rides a phoenix through the air, perhaps playing a Chinese immortal. · Look at her sleeve: numbers for the year's long months suggest a 1765 calendar. · Her panpipes suggest a flute-playing sage; the bird suggests another, often shown riding a phoenix. · If so, the print is a mitate. · Mitate · Playful parody or “likening”: a classical subject or legend recast in contemporary dress."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/visit-to-a-shrine-at-the-hour-of-the-ox.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Visit to a Shrine at the Hour of the Ox","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-shrine-hour-ox","Suzuki Harunobu · 1765 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 28.3 × 20.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Carrying hammer and nails to curse a faithless lover or a rival at about 2 a.m., a woman looks serene. · The straw doll and candle crown this rite usually calls for are missing. · A seal at left marks the private patron who commissioned it. · The carver, Sekine Kaei, is credited by name beside the designer."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/parody-of-minamoto-no-tametomo.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Parody of Minamoto no Tametomo","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-tametomo-calendar","Suzuki Harunobu · 1765 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 26.7 × 20 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A youth with a bow and arrows plays the archer-hero Tametomo as two women kneel beside him. · The year name and “Meiwa 2” flank his neckband. · Long-month numbers sit in his lining; short ones are white bamboo leaves on the dark robe by the screen. · Like other 1765 calendars, it hides the dates in the design. · Year name · One of sixty cyclical names for years, repeating every sixty years. · Meiwa 2 · The second year of the Meiwa era, 1765; Japan counted years by era names."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/evening-chime-of-the-clock.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Evening Chime of the Clock","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-evening-chime-clock","Suzuki Harunobu · c. 1766 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 28.3 × 17.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Fresh from the bath, a woman sits on the veranda; her maid turns as the clock strikes. · Slanting parallel lines carry the eye into the room's private space. · The chime may hint at a lover's coming visit. · From Eight Parlor Views: the temple bell of China's classic Eight Views becomes a household clock."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/woman-admiring-plum-blossoms-at-night.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Woman Admiring Plum Blossoms at Night","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-plum-blossoms-night","Suzuki Harunobu · c. 1766 · Woodblock print with embossing; ink and color on paper, 32.4 × 21 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A young woman raises a lantern to plum blossoms in the dark, an old theme of East Asian verse. · The stylized cloud at top recalls the court art of the Heian period. · His elite merchant and samurai buyers admired Kyoto's literary past. · Blind embossing (karazuri) raises uncolored areas, like her socks and inner robes. · Heian · The era of 794–1185, when the imperial court flourished in Kyoto."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/lovers-walking-in-the-snow-crow-and-heron.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Lovers Walking in the Snow (Crow and Heron)","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-lovers-snow","Suzuki Harunobu · 1764–72 · Woodblock print with embossing; ink and color on paper, 28.6 × 20.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Two lovers share an umbrella in falling snow, one in black, one in white. · Perhaps a michiyuki, a couple's last journey toward a love suicide. · Bright patterned under-robes show at collars, sleeves and hems. · The design was re-cut during or soon after his lifetime; this is an early impression."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/osen-of-the-kagiya-teahouse.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Osen of the Kagiya Teahouse","Suzuki Harunobu","harunobu-osen-kagiya","Suzuki Harunobu · c. 1769 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 65.7 × 12.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A celebrated waitress at an Edo shrine teahouse turns to watch a cuckoo. · The teahouse lantern reads “rest stop,” and a table of cakes stands beside her. · No story is told: her own fame is the subject. · The pillar print (hashira-e), a tall, narrow sheet for hanging, stresses her height."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/the-courtesan-nishikigi-of-the-yotsumeya-brothel.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","The Courtesan Nishikigi of the Yotsumeya Brothel","Isoda Koryūsai","koryusai-courtesan-nishikigi","Isoda Koryūsai · 1776 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 37.8 × 25.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A courtesan shows off a black robe with paulownia flowers, two young attendants beside her. · Look at the attendants' robes: trellised flowers hinted at women behind the quarter's barred windows. · The designer gave up samurai rank and began in Harunobu's manner. · From a series showing the year's first new robe patterns."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/the-floating-world-takes-shape/three-types-of-drunks.html","Brocade Pictures: Harunobu and 1765","Three Types of Drunks","Isoda Koryūsai","koryusai-three-drunks","Isoda Koryūsai · c. 1770 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 69.8 × 11.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Three tipsy figures, stacked up a tall sheet, play a stock trio of Edo art and literature. · The sheet was trimmed at left, cutting into the signature. · Utamaro later took up the same theme in a triptych. · Young fashionable women take the men's usual place, a favorite twist of Koryūsai's."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/beauties-kiyonaga-utamaro-eishi.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi · Overview","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","","utamaro-reflective-love","From full figures to close-ups, 1780s–90s · The last quarter of the 1700s is often called the golden age of the print. Rival designers each offered an ideal of feminine beauty, and new formats such as the bust portrait followed. · A shifting ideal of the beautiful woman · Poetry circles, publishers and picture books · Outings, pastimes and private moments · Kitagawa Utamaro · Reflective Love · c. 1793/94"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/a-boating-party-under-azuma-bridge.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","A Boating Party under Azuma Bridge","Torii Kiyonaga","kiyonaga-boating-azuma-bridge","Torii Kiyonaga · c. 1785 · Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 38.7 × 26 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Pleasure boats tie up in the cool beneath a Sumida River bridge on a summer day. · On the far shore at left, look for a shrine gate's crossbars above the river steps. · He trained under Kiyomitsu and designed actor prints too. · Recognizable landmarks set the beauties in places buyers knew."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/women-watching-a-girl-dance-on-shells.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Women Watching a Girl Dance on Shells","Torii Kiyonaga","kiyonaga-girl-dance-shells","Torii Kiyonaga · 1784 · Color woodblock print, 36.3 × 24.8 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · A small girl dances on a pair of shells, the kind also used as game pieces, as a woman plays the shamisen. · His women are taller and more adult than the slight figures of the 1760s. · Dancing marked festival days and other celebrations. · Tall onlookers frame the small dancer, stressing her size. · Shamisen · A three-stringed plucked lute, the usual accompaniment for song and dance."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/three-beauties-of-the-kansei-period.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Three Beauties of the Kansei Period","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-three-beauties-kansei","Kitagawa Utamaro · c. 1791 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 38.7 × 25.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Three women are pressed close together, their heads and shoulders filling the sheet. · A handwritten note in one corner reads “Three Beautiful Women.” · Sarah Thompson notes that he later faced official limits on close-ups like this. · The bust portrait, or big head (ōkubi-e), brought the viewer face to face with the beauty. · Kansei · An era name covering 1789–1801."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/takashima-ohisa-using-two-mirrors-to-observe-her-coiffure.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Takashima Ohisa Using Two Mirrors to Observe Her Coiffure","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-takashima-ohisa","Kitagawa Utamaro · c. 1795 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 34.9 × 25.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Seen from behind, a young woman checks her hairstyle; her face shows only in the mirror before her. · A second mirror, held behind her head, shows her the back. · Mirrors let the viewer look over her shoulder, a device Utamaro used in other prints too. · Real, named townswomen, not only courtesans, became print celebrities."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/reflective-love.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Reflective Love","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-reflective-love","Kitagawa Utamaro · c. 1793/94 · Color woodblock print, 37.7 × 24.8 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A woman rests her chin on her hand and gazes past us, lost in thought. · From the series Anthology of Poems: The Love Section. · The plain pale ground isolates her face and hand. · Its publisher, Tsutaya Jūzaburō, also issued Utamaro's insect book."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/picture-book-of-crawling-creatures.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Picture Book of Crawling Creatures","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-crawling-creatures","Kitagawa Utamaro · 1788 · Page from a woodblock-printed book; ink and color on paper, 26.7 × 18.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A tree cricket clings to a seed head by comic verses; a firefly climbs the grasses opposite. · Insects and other small creatures fill all fifteen designs. · The verses were chosen, and the preface written, by the poet-scholar Yadoya no Meshimori. · Picture books like this paired prints with verse by comic-poetry (kyōka) circles."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/yamauba-and-kintaro.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Yamauba and Kintarō","Kitagawa Utamaro","utamaro-yamauba-kintaro","Kitagawa Utamaro · c. 1795 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 38.4 × 22.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A legendary mountain woman and the wild boy she raised lean close in a quiet moment. · He returned to mother and boy many times. · Over his 1804 triptych of the Taikō, he was jailed and then kept in hand chains for fifty days. · In legend, Kintarō grew up to become the warrior Sakata no Kintoki. · Taikō · Title of the warlord Toyotomi Hideyoshi (1537–1598)."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/pleasure-boats-on-the-sumida-river-leftmost-sheet.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Pleasure Boats on the Sumida River (leftmost sheet)","Chōbunsai Eishi","eishi-pleasure-boats-sumida","Chōbunsai Eishi · c. 1792 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 39 × 25.6 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Elegantly dressed women gather on a roofed pleasure boat, the far bank and bridge behind them. · He worked in painting as well as prints, known for graceful line and drapery. · Cleveland holds two of its five sheets. · The five sheets join into one long river view."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/ono-no-komachi-visiting-kiyomizu-temple.html","Beauties: Kiyonaga, Utamaro, Eishi","Ono no Komachi Visiting Kiyomizu Temple","Chōbunsai Eishi","eishi-komachi-kiyomizu","Chōbunsai Eishi · c. 1788 · Color woodblock print, 38.7 × 25.4 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · At a Kyoto temple, two women admire cherry blossoms as a boy fastens a poem slip to a branch. · From The Fashionable Seven Komachi, a mitate series. · It alludes to a legend: at this temple a young woman overheard a monk praying for the aged poet and led him to her house. · Published by Izumiya Ichibei; the pale, limited colors keep the mood quiet."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/stars-of-the-stage-actor-prints.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints · Overview","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","","sharaku-otani-oniji","How fans followed their stars on paper, c. 1715–1824 · Women were banned from the kabuki stage in 1629 and boys in 1652, leaving it to adult men. Fans bought pictures of favorite stars and the roles they made famous. · Fan culture and the business of star images · How designers showed who was on stage · Private and regional prints beside the Edo mainstream · Tōshūsai Sharaku · Ōtani Oniji III as Yakko Edobei · 1794"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/ichikawa-danjuro-ii-in-wait-a-moment-shibaraku.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Ichikawa Danjūrō II in “Wait a Moment” (Shibaraku)","Torii Kiyotada","kiyotada-danjuro-shibaraku","Torii Kiyotada · c. 1715 · Woodblock print; ink and hand-applied color (tan-e) on paper, 28.6 × 15.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Striding out to the stage along the runway, the hero flaunts a boldly patterned costume. · He studied with Kiyonobu, in the Torii line of actor-print designers. · The museum's date predates his recorded activity, c. 1720–50. · Fans knew the actor by his family crest, three nested squares, more than by his face."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/ichikawa-danjuro-v-as-a-traveler-beside-a-clump-of-iris.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Ichikawa Danjūrō V as a Traveler beside a Clump of Iris","Katsukawa Shunshō","shunsho-danjuro-iris","Katsukawa Shunshō · c. 1780 · Color woodblock print, 29.9 × 14.4 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · A star in a striped travel robe pauses by a bank of iris, sword at his hip. · Shunshō led the actor-print field in his day and painted as well. · The actor later took the name Ichikawa Ebizō IV. · His Katsukawa studio began drawing actors as recognizable individuals."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/danjuro-v-as-a-skeleton-and-iwai-hanshiro-iv-as-the-cherry-princess.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Danjūrō V as a Skeleton and Iwai Hanshirō IV as the Cherry Princess","Katsukawa Shunshō","shunsho-danjuro-skeleton","Katsukawa Shunshō · c. 1783 · Color woodblock print (diptych), each sheet about 32.9 × 15 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · As a skeleton, a disgraced monk's ghost closes in on the princess he loved, who shrinks away. · In the play he gave up his vows for her, was cast out and died poor. · His stage costume was black, with bones painted on. · The crest on his arm dates the scene to the Nakamura Theater, 1783."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/otani-oniji-iii-as-yakko-edobei.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Ōtani Oniji III as Yakko Edobei","Tōshūsai Sharaku","sharaku-otani-oniji","Tōshūsai Sharaku · 1794 · Woodblock print; ink, color and white mica on paper, 38.1 × 25.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A villainous manservant scowls, hair bristling, his hands thrust out like claws. · Behind the pen name is an artist whose identity is still debated; he issued over 140 prints in about ten months. · Mica in the dark ground gives it a soft shimmer. · Caricature-like features fuse the stage villain with the man playing him."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/ichikawa-ebizo-iv-as-takemura-sadanoshin.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Ichikawa Ebizō IV as Takemura Sadanoshin","Tōshūsai Sharaku","sharaku-ichikawa-ebizo","Tōshūsai Sharaku · 1794 · Color woodblock print, 36 × 23.6 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · A gaunt older man clasps his hands; in the play, he is allowed one last performance with his daughter. · From the same 1794 production as the scowling manservant. · The publisher Tsutaya Jūzaburō's emblem and the censor's seal kiwame, “approved,” share the sheet. · Born in 1741, the actor was in his fifties when he played the role."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/segawa-tomisaburo-ii-as-yadorigi.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Segawa Tomisaburō II as Yadorigi","Tōshūsai Sharaku","sharaku-segawa-tomisaburo","Tōshūsai Sharaku · 1794 · Woodblock print; ink, color and white mica on paper, 37.1 × 25.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Playing a stylish woman, the male actor tugs at his robe with a big theatrical gesture. · Look at the square jaw beneath the woman's hairstyle. · A handwritten note gives the actor's name and the fifth month of 1794. · Audiences were fascinated by onnagata, men who specialized in women's roles."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/sawamura-sojuro-iii-as-shimada-juzaburo.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Sawamura Sōjūrō III as Shimada Jūzaburō","Utagawa Toyokuni I","toyokuni-sawamura-sojuro","Utagawa Toyokuni I · c. 1795 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 35.9 × 24.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A star poses in the play Faithful Courtesans in Yoshiwara, his trousers patterned with stylized water. · From a series of actors on stage published in 1794–96. · The actor's yagō, Kinokuniya, is printed under the series title. · Toyokuni's full-length, composed figures stand apart from Sharaku's tight close-ups. · Yagō · An acting family's house name, shared by its line of actors."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/danjuro-viis-benkei-as-fudo-myoo.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Danjūrō VII's Benkei as Fudō Myōō","Utagawa Kunisada","kunisada-danjuro-benkei-fudo","Utagawa Kunisada · c. 1824 · Woodblock print (surimono); ink and color on paper, 21.4 × 18.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The star's warrior-monk role merges with Fudō, a fierce Buddhist guardian, sword and rosary in hand. · Ichikawa actors looked to this guardian of Narita's Shinshōji temple for protection. · It might have been a New Year gift marking the future Danjūrō VIII's birth. · Surimono were privately commissioned luxury prints in small runs, often exchanged as gifts."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/beauties-and-actors/nakamura-utaemon-iii-as-ishikawa-goemon.html","Stars of the Stage: Actor Prints","Nakamura Utaemon III as Ishikawa Goemon","Shunkōsai Hokushū","hokushu-utaemon-goemon","Shunkōsai Hokushū · 1822 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 40 × 26.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A bandit-hero glares sideways, plotting to steal the Imperial Seal in The Golden Gate and the Paulownia Crest. · Look at the courtly cap and stiff robes, stolen from an imperial messenger as a disguise. · The actor lived from 1778 to 1838. · The designer was based in Osaka, which had its own actor prints beside Edo's."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hokusai.html","Hokusai · Overview","Hokusai","","hokusai-red-fuji","Landscapes, ghosts and poems from a long career, 1810s–1830s · The designer (1760–1849) worked in prints, books and paintings over a very long career. At seventy, around 1830, he turned to Mount Fuji, and his views helped make landscape a favorite with buyers. · Landscape as a subject in its own right · A newly affordable imported blue · Ghosts, poems and sketches beside the views · Katsushika Hokusai · South Wind, Clear Sky (Red Fuji) · c. 1830–32"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/under-the-wave-off-kanagawa-the-great-wave.html","Hokusai","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-great-wave","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 37.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A towering wave curls over three boats, its foam breaking into claw-like fingers. · Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji sold so well that it grew to 46 prints. · Debussy's orchestral work La Mer is said to owe something to it. · Look in the wave's trough: Fuji appears as a small peak, dwarfed by the water."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/south-wind-clear-sky-red-fuji.html","Hokusai","South Wind, Clear Sky (Red Fuji)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-red-fuji","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 24.4 × 35.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The mountain alone, glowing red under fleecy clouds, fills the sheet. · Fuji can look reddish at late-summer dawns, perhaps what he meant to show. · Janice Katz (Art Institute) notes later printings are much redder than the earliest, softer ones. · Bokashi: color wiped by hand on the block shades the cone from green to red-brown."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/storm-below-mount-fuji.html","Hokusai","Storm below Mount Fuji","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-storm-below-fuji","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 38.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Red lightning flickers over the darkened foot of the mountain as the peak climbs into a summer sky. · Another version from the same block shows rain falling below the peak. · The Met counts it among his most celebrated prints. · The series shows one mountain under changing weather and from shifting viewpoints."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/shichirigahama-in-sagami-province.html","Hokusai","Shichirigahama in Sagami Province","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-shichirigahama","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 38.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Fuji rises beyond a wooded headland and a long beach. · Prussian blue is a synthetic pigment; the Met says Dutch traders brought it to Japan. · Blue ties sea, sky and land into one view. · Blue printing (aizuri), in which blue dominates the color scheme, was then in fashion."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/kirifuri-waterfall-at-kurokami-mountain-in-shimotsuke.html","Hokusai","Kirifuri Waterfall at Kurokami Mountain in Shimotsuke","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-kirifuri-waterfall","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1832 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 37.5 × 26 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A waterfall breaks into many streams down a rock face as tiny travelers look up from below. · From A Tour of Waterfalls in Various Provinces. · Look at upper right: two more travelers pick their way across the wooded slope. · Sets of famous sights (meisho) let buyers tour the provinces on paper."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hokusai-manga-sketches-of-hokusai.html","Hokusai","Hokusai Manga (Sketches of Hokusai)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-manga","Katsushika Hokusai · 1812–78 · Woodblock-printed books (11 of 15 volumes), 22.5 × 15.5 cm closed. The Art Institute of Chicago · Flowers, grasses and leaves are scattered across an open spread. · Planned as one volume, it grew to fifteen; the last two appeared after his death. · Western collectors later bought it as a source of Japanese motifs. · Manga here means casual sketches, not the modern comic."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/oiwa-from-one-hundred-ghost-tales.html","Hokusai","Oiwa, from One Hundred Ghost Tales","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-oiwa","Katsushika Hokusai · 1831–32 · Color woodblock print, 25.6 × 16.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Scarred and pale, a betrayed wife stares out of a torn lantern, back for revenge. · Poison she took for beauty cream scarred her face, leaving one eye drooping. · The series is named for a candlelit game of telling a hundred ghost stories. · Her tale comes from a famous ghost play, The Ghost Story of Yotsuya."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/the-emperor-tenji-from-one-hundred-poems-explained-by-the-nurse.html","Hokusai","The Emperor Tenji, from One Hundred Poems Explained by the Nurse","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-emperor-tenji","Katsushika Hokusai · c. 1835/36 · Color woodblock print, 26.2 × 37.7 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Farmers haul rice sheaves past a flimsy hut where, a story says, an emperor sheltered from rain. · He reigned 668–671 and is credited with Japan's first civil law code. · His poem sympathizes with farmers who meet such rain day after day. · The picture turns a court poem into a scene of farm work. · The Nurse · The series imagines an old nursemaid explaining each classic poem in plain terms."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/basket-of-peonies.html","Hokusai","Basket of Peonies","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-basket-peonies","Katsushika Hokusai · 1810 or 1814 · Color woodblock print (surimono), 38.3 × 52.1 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Red and white peonies spill from a woven basket beside a poem. · The signature notes his change of name to Taito; the printer is named too. · Made for an occasion in the life of the actor Danjūrō VII. · The peony, a summer flower, was also the actor's emblem."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hiroshige.html","Hiroshige · Overview","Hiroshige","","hiroshige-kanbara-snow","Travel, weather and the city, 1830s–1858 · The designer (1797–1858) made his name in the early 1830s with views of the Tōkaidō, the highway between Edo and Kyoto. His last great series, on Edo itself, appeared in the late 1850s. · Mood made from weather, season and hour · Bird's-eye and close-up viewpoints · City places and pastimes in his last years · Utagawa Hiroshige · Evening Snow at Kanbara · c. 1833–34"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/morning-view-of-nihonbashi.html","Hiroshige","Morning View of Nihonbashi","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-nihonbashi","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1833–34 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 24.1 × 35.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · At dawn a lord's procession leaves Edo over the bridge that marked the start of the great road. · Fishmongers with the morning's catch walk ahead of the procession. · The first sheet of Fifty-three Stations of the Tōkaidō, published by Hōeidō. · A landmark shown as street life: rank and trade crowd the same crossing."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hara-mount-fuji-in-the-morning.html","Hiroshige","Hara: Mount Fuji in the Morning","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-hara","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1833–34 · Woodblock print, 24.1 × 35.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Two women and a porter halt on the road, apparently struck by the view. · The name Hara means “field”; from here the view of Fuji was almost unbroken. · This edition is often called the First or Great Tōkaidō. · The summit pushes past the top border, too big for its frame."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/evening-snow-at-kanbara.html","Hiroshige","Evening Snow at Kanbara","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-kanbara-snow","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1833–34 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 22.5 × 34.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Snow blankets a village at nightfall as a few travelers struggle on. · Look for three small figures, bent under umbrellas and straw capes. · Records suggest he came through in summer, and deep snow is rare there: the snow is invented. · The snow is bare paper, set against a dark gray sky."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/driving-rain-at-shono.html","Hiroshige","Driving Rain at Shōno","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-shono-rain","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1833 · Color woodblock print, 25.4 × 38 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Travelers bent double hurry along a slope as a sudden squall whips the bamboo. · The publisher's name, Takenouchi, appears on one traveler's umbrella. · Shōno was a minor station on a mostly coastal route. · Three tones of gray print the bamboo, each fainter with distance."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/the-monkey-bridge-in-kai-province.html","Hiroshige","The Monkey Bridge in Kai Province","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-monkey-bridge","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1841–42 · Vertical diptych of woodblock prints, mounted as a hanging scroll; print 73 × 24.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A bridge spans a deep gorge, with a village, far hills and a rushing river below. · Look for travelers crossing the bridge, tiny against cliff and sky. · Look at the moon, framed between the bridge and the cliff. · Two sheets joined top to bottom let landscape run vertically, from treetops to the gorge floor. · Monkey Bridge, Kai Province · Saruhashi, a famous bridge in Kai, a mountainous old province west of Edo (today Yamanashi Prefecture)."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/wild-geese-flying-under-the-full-moon.html","Hiroshige","Wild Geese Flying under the Full Moon","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-geese-moon","Utagawa Hiroshige · c. 1833 · Woodblock print, 38.1 × 12.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Geese slant down across a huge full moon in a tall, narrow sheet. · In East Asian poetry, the eighth month's full moon marked the height of autumn. · The poem at upper left wonders whether so lovely a night will ever return. · Fixed seasonal pairings let picture and poem work as one."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/fukagawa-susaki-and-jumantsubo.html","Hiroshige","Fukagawa Susaki and Jūmantsubo","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-fukagawa-susaki","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Color woodblock print, 33.3 × 22 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A golden eagle swoops over a snowy shore, its wings spanning the top of the sheet. · Below lies Edo Bay, now Tokyo Bay, seen from high above. · One of the most popular designs in One Hundred Famous Views of Edo. · The aerial view is one his viewers could only imagine."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/fireworks-at-ryogoku.html","Hiroshige","Fireworks at Ryōgoku","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-ryogoku-fireworks","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1858 · Color woodblock print, 36 × 24.1 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A rocket bursts in a moonless sky above lantern-lit boats and a crowded bridge. · On late-summer nights, those who could afford it took pleasure boats onto the cool river. · Published in the year he died, thought to be of cholera. · Its dying sparks light up the crowd and the far bank."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/plum-garden-at-kameido.html","Hiroshige","Plum Garden at Kameido","Utagawa Hiroshige","hiroshige-kameido-plum","Utagawa Hiroshige · 1857 · Color woodblock print, 36 × 24.4 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A dark plum trunk sprawls across the foreground: Edo's most famous tree, said to resemble a sleeping dragon. · Van Gogh copied the design in 1887; see Post-Impressionism. · Comparing many impressions, British Museum scientist Capucine Korenberg found the blocks changed over time. · Its surprising color, above all the red sky, makes it easy to recognize."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/heroes-ghosts-and-the-west.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West · Overview","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","","kuniyoshi-taira-ghosts","Late Edo, Meiji, and prints abroad, 1827–1927 · In the 1800s battle scenes, ghosts and foreign newcomers grew into major subjects, even as censors banned actor prints for several years. Abroad, Western painters and printmakers borrowed the prints' ways of composing, and by the 1920s Yoshida Hiroshi had carried the woodblock into modern times. · Heroes and ghosts as censorship tightened · Foreign trade and the Western newcomers · Western borrowings and a modern Japanese revival · Utagawa Kuniyoshi · Ghosts of the Taira at Daimotsu Bay · 1840s"],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/hu-sanniang-from-one-hundred-and-eight-heroes-of-the-popular-water-margin.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Hu Sanniang, from One Hundred and Eight Heroes of the Popular Water Margin","Utagawa Kuniyoshi","kuniyoshi-hu-sanniang","Utagawa Kuniyoshi · c. 1827/30 · Color woodblock print, 39.5 × 27 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A woman warrior of the Chinese outlaw novel braces amid flying arrows, a sword in each hand. · Sarah Thompson (MFA Boston) calls this series his breakthrough, launching the warrior print. · Armor, robes and foliage crowd the sheet in dense, swirling pattern. · She adds that Chinese novels were a fairly safe subject under the censors."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/ghosts-of-the-taira-at-daimotsu-bay.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Ghosts of the Taira at Daimotsu Bay","Utagawa Kuniyoshi","kuniyoshi-taira-ghosts","Utagawa Kuniyoshi · 1840s · Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 35.4 × 25.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York (this impression dated 1849–52) · From a churning sea, a host of ghosts surges toward the ship of the clan that destroyed them. · After the Minamoto victory at sea in 1185, warriors ruled Japan. · Drawn as flat silhouettes, the ghosts suggest how fleeting life is. · Soft-toned ghosts against a crisp, colorful ship and wave set two worlds side by side. · Minamoto and Taira · Rival warrior clans who fought for control of Japan in the 1180s."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/popular-otsu-e-phenomenon.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Popular Ōtsu-e Phenomenon","Utagawa Kuniyoshi","kuniyoshi-otsu-e","Utagawa Kuniyoshi · 1848 · Woodblock print triptych; ink and color on paper, 36.4 × 73.1 cm. Minneapolis Institute of Art · Folk-painting characters spring to life to protect their painter, a scene from a puppet play. · Ōtsu-e were sold to travelers at Ōtsu, a way station on the Tōkaidō. · The figures carry famous actors' faces, slipping past the ban on actor prints. · Look for the warrior-monk Benkei in kabuki face paint, as if mocking censors."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/artisans-from-an-up-to-date-parody-of-the-four-classes.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Artisans, from An Up-to-Date Parody of the Four Classes","Utagawa Kunisada","kunisada-artisans","Utagawa Kunisada · 19th century · Triptych of woodblock prints; ink and color on paper, each sheet 36.8 × 25.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A busy workshop staffed by women prepares blocks and paper for printing. · Artisans were one of the four Edo classes, with warriors, farmers and merchants. · Women were not normally in the trade, so the scene is a playful fiction. · Read right to left: carving through the pasted design, clearing wood, sizing paper, a printer waiting."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/an-american-mercantile-building-in-yokohama.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","An American Mercantile Building in Yokohama","Utagawa Sadahide","sadahide-american-building","Utagawa Sadahide · 1861 · Color woodblock print triptych, 37 × 75.7 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Westerners and their Japanese companions crowd the veranda of a trading house above a busy harbor. · Look for the American flag on a tall flagpole at left. · The Met holds a related 1861 triptych by the same designer, of foreigners indoors. · After Yokohama opened to foreign trade, prints showed buyers the newcomers' clothes and buildings."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/moon-over-daimotsu-bay-benkei-from-one-hundred-aspects-of-the-moon.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Moon over Daimotsu Bay: Benkei, from One Hundred Aspects of the Moon","Tsukioka Yoshitoshi","yoshitoshi-benkei-moon","Tsukioka Yoshitoshi · January 1886 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 35.2 × 24.5 cm. Minneapolis Institute of Art · In a storm, the monk prays from the prow to hold back the Taira ghosts, unseen here. · Benkei and his lord Yoshitsune are fleeing by sea from the lord's half-brother. · Every design in this Meiji-era series (1885–92) builds on a moon. · It followed by two months the kabuki premiere of Funa Benkei, adapted from Noh. · Meiji · The reign of Emperor Meiji, 1868–1912, when Japan rapidly modernized. · Noh · Japan's classical masked drama, older and more stately than kabuki."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/caprice-in-purple-and-gold-the-golden-screen.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Caprice in Purple and Gold: The Golden Screen","James McNeill Whistler","whistler-caprice-golden-screen","James McNeill Whistler · 1864 · Oil on wood panel, 50.1 × 68.5 cm. Freer Gallery of Art, Smithsonian National Museum of Asian Art, Washington, DC · A model in Japanese dress sits before a gilded folding screen, looking through color prints. · The Irish model Joanna Hiffernan is surrounded by Asian objects from Whistler's own collection. · Shown at the Royal Academy in London in 1865. · Look at the print she studies: it comes from Hiroshige's Sixty-odd Provinces series."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/the-letter.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","The Letter","Mary Cassatt","cassatt-letter","Mary Cassatt · 1890–91 · Color aquatint and drypoint, 34.5 × 21.1 cm (plate). The Art Institute of Chicago · A woman seals a letter at her writing desk, her patterned dress pressed against patterned wallpaper. · Printed by the artist with Leroy in an edition of 25. · Domestic life, bound by social custom, was one of her main themes. · Flat pattern and bright color squeeze the space, a lesson she took from Japanese woodblocks."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/boats-at-rest.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Boats at Rest","Arthur Wesley Dow","dow-boats-at-rest","Arthur Wesley Dow · c. 1895 · Oil on canvas, 66 × 91.4 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Rowboats rest on a marshy bank near the painter's home town, Ipswich, Massachusetts. · His bold palette has much in common with Gauguin, Émile Bernard and other French Post-Impressionists. · His Japanese-inspired teaching on composition, at Ipswich and Pratt Institute, was widely influential. · From ukiyo-e came bold cropping, a raised viewpoint and flattened space."],[5,"/study/art-history/japanese-woodblock-prints/landscape-and-legend/evening-on-the-chikugo-river.html","Heroes, Ghosts and the West","Evening on the Chikugo River","Yoshida Hiroshi","yoshida-chikugo-evening","Yoshida Hiroshi · 1927 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 27.6 × 41.1 cm. The Cleveland Museum of Art · Lamplit houses line the river at dusk as a long bridge fades into the blue distance. · The Japanese title names the town, Hita, which the English leaves out. · The artist gave this impression to the museum himself, within a few years. · A seal reads jizuri, “self-printed”; Edo designers had left printing to specialists."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/a-short-history.html","A Short History · Overview","A Short History","","oa-h-plastic","Five centuries of oil; less than one of acrylic · Oil has been the painter's main paint since the 1400s. Acrylic came out of the plastics industry in the mid-1900s. · Oil's long run and the paint tube · Plastic paint · Using both · Diagram · Acrylic paint arrives"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-five-centuries-of-oil.html","A Short History · History","Five Centuries of Oil","","rand-1841-tube-patent","For more than five hundred years, oil has been the paint most painters reached for. · Its early story is told in Pigments and Materials. What matters here is one change in the 1800s: the paint tube, patented by John Rand in 1841. · Before tubes, paint was kept in animal bladders that couldn't be resealed once pricked. · A screw-cap tin tube kept paint fresh and easy to carry outdoors. · Painters already worked outdoors before tubes; many outdoor sketches were finished in the studio."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-plastic-paint.html","A Short History · History","Plastic Paint","","oa-h-plastic","Acrylic paint comes from the same plastics family as Plexiglas, first made by industry, not for artists. · Paint makers adapted it twice: a solvent paint, then a water-based one. · Early ads pushed quick drying and flexibility; mixing with water came near the end. · By 1965 most companies making artists' paint had a water-based acrylic of their own. · For David Hockney it ended the wait: in oil he worked on two or three pictures while each dried; in acrylic, one at a time."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-using-both.html","A Short History · History","Using Both","","oa-h-both","Oil and acrylic now share the studio, and sometimes the same canvas. · Tate reported in 2009 that acrylic paints and primers had been about half of artists' paint sales for 30 years. Painters who use both give each the job it does best. · Roy Lichtenstein's labels list oil and Magna; Art Institute of Chicago scientists found which is where. · Magna gave him fast-drying, flat color. · Through a metal stencil Magna dried too fast and stuck, so he used oil for the dots."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/whats-in-the-tube.html","What's in the Tube · Overview","What's in the Tube","","oa-binders","Same pigment, two very different binders · Both paints can carry the same pigment; the difference is what holds it. Acrylic itself comes in two kinds. · Two binders · Solvent acrylic and water acrylic · Diagram · Oil and acrylic binders"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-two-binders.html","What's in the Tube · Concept","Two Binders","","oa-binders","Oil paint is pigment in a drying oil, a plant oil that turns solid in air. Acrylic is pigment among tiny plastic droplets in water. · The droplets are less than a thousandth of a millimeter across. Binders in general: see Pigments and Materials. · Linseed is the main paint oil; makers use safflower or poppy in whites because they yellow less. · Oil paint holds few extras, such as driers; acrylic holds many, to keep the droplets apart, thicken the paint and stop mold."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/the-harvesters.html","What's in the Tube","The Harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder","bruegel-harvesters","Pieter Bruegel the Elder · 1565 · Oil on oak, 119 × 162 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The same oil binder carries paint thin enough to see through and thick enough to cover, side by side. Thin see-through layers like these are glazes, covered in Color Theory. · Where the paint is thinnest, the drawing underneath is not fully covered. · Thicker, opaque strokes on top describe the details. · The Met's essay on oil in the Low Countries calls this a quicker, more economical way of painting than earlier work."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/two-paints/theme-solvent-acrylic-and-water-acrylic.html","What's in the Tube · Concept","Solvent Acrylic and Water Acrylic","","oa-solvent-water","Magna, the first artists' acrylic, had its plastic dissolved in solvent. Most acrylic sold today is water-based instead. · Magna thinned like oil but often dried in hours rather than days. Solvent or the next layer could dissolve it again. · It could even be mixed with oil paint. · It never sold well and was dropped in the mid-1970s."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/how-they-dry.html","How They Dry · Overview","How They Dry","","oa-acrylic-dries","Air hardens one paint; evaporation sets the other · The two paints become solid in different ways, and on very different clocks. Those clocks shape how each one is handled. · Oil cures · Acrylic dries · Open time · Color shift as it dries · Diagram · How an acrylic film forms"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-oil-cures.html","How They Dry · Concept","Oil Cures","","oa-oil-cures","Oil cures: it reacts with oxygen in the air, and its molecules link into one solid network. · Follow the arrows: oxygen reaches the top first, so the paint underneath lags behind, most of all in thick paint. · Driers added to the paint speed the reaction. · Cured oil can't be thinned back into wet paint. · In a 2022 lab study, oil paint films were still stiffening after 30 years."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-acrylic-dries.html","How They Dry · Concept","Acrylic Dries","","oa-acrylic-dries","Acrylic dries by losing water, not by reacting with air. · The surface dries fast, but the film keeps losing water for about a month and can take up to a year to fully fuse. · For about a week, sometimes longer, freshly dried paint can still dissolve in water. · Even fully dry, it can swell in water and lose some additives. · Dried too cold, the droplets fuse poorly, leaving a porous or powdery film."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/early-still.html","How They Dry","Early, Still","David Nicholson","dn-early-still","David Nicholson · 2025 · Acrylic on canvas, 55.9 × 71.1 cm · Because acrylic sets quickly into a film, each flat block of color could be laid over the last without a long wait. · Look where colors meet: the yellow tree against the red, the lit windows against the dark wall. · None of the color beneath has smeared into the new layer. · Brush marks show inside the red and yellow, yet each block still reads as one color."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-open-time.html","How They Dry · Concept","Open Time","","oa-open-time","Open time is how long paint stays wet enough to move around on the canvas. · Acrylic is dry to the touch in minutes to a few hours; oil takes about two days. That window decides how a painter works. · Oil invites wet-in-wet: new color worked into paint that is still wet, for soft blends. · Once acrylic has set, the next color sits on top with a clean edge. · Acrylic can be blended wet too, but only quickly."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer.html","How They Dry","Stacks of Wheat (End of Summer)","Claude Monet","monet-stacks-of-wheat-end-of-summer","Claude Monet · 1890–91 · Oil on canvas, 60 × 100.5 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Monet used both timings in one canvas: some passages worked wet, others painted over dry paint. · Look at the edges of the large stack: paint built up wet-in-wet. · In the sky, zigzag strokes were blended wet, then more color went on once they had dried. · Some thick strokes look slightly flattened, possibly while still soft; the signature went on over dry paint."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-color-shift-as-it-dries.html","How They Dry · Concept","Color Shift as It Dries","","oa-color-shift","Wet acrylic looks milky; as it dries, the binder turns clear and the color shows through. · Getty-published research explains the change. One paint maker says colors dry slightly darker. How binders change color in general is covered in Color Theory. · A second, different effect: dry paint can look less rich than the wet paint did. · A varnish brings that richness back, conservator Chris Stavroudis notes."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/handling-and-layering.html","Handling and Layering · Overview","Handling and Layering","","oa-blending","Blending, building up and painting over · Drying time shapes every stroke. This part follows each paint from blending and thick paint to layering rules, and what to thin and clean it with. · Blending · Brushwork and impasto · Painting over dry paint · Fat over lean · Mixing the two in layers · Mediums, solvents and cleanup · Diagram · Wet-in-wet and onto dry"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-blending.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Blending","","oa-blending","Soft blends in acrylic depend on keeping the paint wet long enough to work. · Oil needs no such help. · Mist the palette with water to keep acrylic wet. · Add a little retarder, an additive made for acrylic that slows drying. · Too much retarder leaves a weak film that may stay sticky, and slows full drying."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/ginevra-de-benci.html","Handling and Layering","Ginevra de' Benci","Leonardo da Vinci","leonardo-ginevra-de-benci","Leonardo da Vinci · c. 1474/1478 · Oil on panel, 38.1 × 37 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Some of the blending here was done with fingers, not a brush. · Where the sky meets the juniper bush, the paint was softened by hand; the National Gallery of Art notes his fingerprints still show. · Oil dried more slowly than egg tempera and could go on in thin layers, the NGA adds. · That let him paint the soft shading of the face and the distant haze."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-brushwork-and-impasto.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Brushwork and Impasto","","oa-impasto","Impasto is paint laid on thick enough to stand up from the surface and keep the mark of the brush. · Some oil colors come stiff and buttery from the tube; others flow. · Acrylic gels and pastes are thickened acrylic mediums; fillers such as calcite or silica change their texture. · A Jules Olitski impasto at MoMA, probably built from gel, was found still slightly soft and uncracked; the gel had discolored somewhat."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/self-portrait.html","Handling and Layering","Self-Portrait","Rembrandt van Rijn","rembrandt-self-portrait-1659","Rembrandt van Rijn · 1659 · Oil on canvas, 84.5 × 66 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The thickest paint sits where light hits the face. · The lights went in first as stubby dabs of heavy paint. · After they dried, he went back over them with small touches of darker color that he left unblended. · In the hair, lines were scratched into the wet paint with the end of the brush. · The background and hands are thin by contrast."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-painting-over-dry-paint.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Painting Over Dry Paint","","oa-painting-over","Once a layer is dry, a painter can cover it, change it or start over on top. · Changes painted over are called pentimenti. In oil paintings, X-rays and other scans often find them. · Oil: if a fingernail doesn't dent the layer, it's ready to paint over; go easy on solvent. · Both paints: don't start a new painting over a finished one. Dirt, varnish or a slick surface can make the new paint lift or flake."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/venus-with-a-mirror.html","Handling and Layering","Venus with a Mirror","Titian","titian-venus-with-a-mirror","Titian · c. 1555 · Oil on canvas, 124.5 × 105.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Under this goddess lies another painting: a portrait of a man and a woman, turned on its side. · X-rays show the double portrait; in the scholar Shapley's view, it was abandoned only shortly before the canvas was reused. · The man's jacket was left showing and became the red velvet drape over her hip. · Her elbow, her fingers and a cupid's legs were changed too."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/the-milkmaid.html","Handling and Layering","The Milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer","vermeer-milkmaid","Johannes Vermeer · c. 1660 · Oil on canvas, 45.5 × 41 cm. Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam · Scans by the Rijksmuseum and the Mauritshuis in 2022 found objects painted out of this kitchen. · At lower right, a fire basket was later painted over; the foot stove, the tiles and the floor now cover it. · A rack of jugs sketched behind her head was never developed."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/umbrella-reverie.html","Handling and Layering","Umbrella, Reverie","David Nicholson","dn-umbrella-reverie","David Nicholson · 2024 · Acrylic on canvas, 101.6 × 76.2 cm · Painting over dry paint can build an image, not only hide one. · The orange and yellow shapes and the black outlines all went on over dry layers. · Look at the yellow jacket: its black fold lines are drawn on top of the dried color."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-fat-over-lean.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Fat Over Lean","","oa-fat-lean","Fat over lean is the rule for layering oil: the oiliest paint goes on top. · University of Delaware conservators phrase it as flexible over less flexible. · The National Gallery, London, traces wrinkling and cracks to a top layer setting while paint below is still soft. · Acrylic has a similar rule: avoid watery washes over thick, glossy acrylic."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-mixing-the-two-in-layers.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Mixing the Two in Layers","","oa-mixing-layers","Oil can go over acrylic, but acrylic should not go over oil. · Research finds oil on a good-quality, well-applied acrylic ground holds up fairly well. Keep acrylic mediums out of oil paint. · Why oil sometimes peels off acrylic is debated: additives in cheap grounds, or compounds forming in the oil. · In Christina Young's lab tests, fresh oil gripped acrylic relatively poorly; aged oil gripped better but could crack the primer beneath. · Thin acrylic underpainting can go under oil, but only on an acrylic ground."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-mediums-solvents-and-cleanup.html","Handling and Layering · Concept","Mediums, Solvents and Cleanup","","oa-mediums","A medium is added to paint to change how it handles. A solvent thins oil paint and cleans the brushes. · Water does both jobs for acrylic. Oil's solvents call for care. · Turpentine fumes irritate eyes, nose and throat; one university art department treats mineral spirits the same. · Ventilate, cover jars and wear nitrile gloves; latex doesn't block solvents. · Low-odor mineral spirits are safer than regular spirits or turpentine, not harmless. · Oily rags can self-heat and catch fire; keep them in water in a closed can or a metal fireproof bin."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/surfaces.html","Surfaces · Overview","Surfaces","","oa-grounds","What goes under the paint · Under most canvases today is an acrylic ground. A few painters work on bare canvas. · Acrylic gesso · Staining raw canvas · Diagram · Primed and stained canvas"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/working-with-each/theme-grounds-for-each.html","Surfaces · Concept","Grounds for Each","","oa-grounds","The most common canvas ground today is acrylic, sold as gesso, and both paints go over it. · The name is borrowed from traditional gesso, a different ground covered in Pigments and Materials. Some painters skip the ground altogether and stain the canvas. · Morris Louis stained with Magna, the solvent acrylic. · Helen Frankenthaler's first stains, in 1952, were oil; she moved to acrylic in 1962."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/aging-and-care.html","Aging and Care · Overview","Aging and Care","","oa-oil-ages","How each paint changes, and how to look after it · Oil hardens, yellows and cracks with age. Acrylic stays flexible but soft, and needs different care. · How oil ages · How acrylic ages · Varnishing and cleaning · Choosing between them · Diagram · Young and old oil"],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/theme-how-oil-ages.html","Aging and Care · Concept","How Oil Ages","","oa-oil-ages","Old oil paint is stiffer than new, and it can yellow and turn see-through. · Brittle paint cracks as the canvas swells and shrinks with humidity; paint on a rigid panel cracks less. The example that follows is an extreme case. · Transparency comes mainly from metal soaps, which form when lead or zinc in pigments reacts with the oil. · Yellowing that builds up in the dark largely clears after a short time in daylight, one study found."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/moonlight-marine.html","Aging and Care","Moonlight Marine","Albert Pinkham Ryder","ryder-moonlight-marine","Albert Pinkham Ryder · 1870–90 · Oil and possibly wax on wood, 29.2 × 30.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Thick paint laid on layer after layer has moved here: since the painting was finished, the Met reports, the edges of the clouds, sails and hull have changed shape. · Look at the wide cracks running across the whole surface. · His biographers say he wanted glass over his pictures so their still-tacky surfaces would not be touched."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/theme-how-acrylic-ages.html","Aging and Care · Concept","How Acrylic Ages","","oa-acrylic-ages","Acrylic ages gently but stays soft, and how soft depends on the temperature. · It yellows and cracks far less than oil, Tate finds, with no sign that it is less stable. Its weak points are different. · It scratches easily, and warm acrylic can take surface dirt into the paint. · A soap-like additive, a surfactant, can drift to the surface as a cloudy, greyish film. It can come back after wiping."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/theme-varnishing-and-cleaning.html","Aging and Care · Concept","Varnishing and Cleaning","","oa-varnish","Taking off an old varnish is usually simpler on oil than on acrylic. · Solvents that lift varnish from oil, such as xylene, can soften dry acrylic; Tate warns oil-painting cleaners can damage it for good. · Give an oil painting six months or more to dry before varnishing it. · For acrylic, paint makers recommend an isolation coat: a permanent clear layer of acrylic medium beneath the varnish. · Whether to varnish acrylic at all is still debated."],[6,"/study/oil-vs-acrylic/over-time/theme-choosing-between-them.html","Aging and Care · Concept","Choosing Between Them","","oa-choosing","Neither paint is better; each suits a different way of working. · Choose by how you like to paint and where the work will live. · To blend and rework over days, pick oil. · To build crisp layers fast and clean up with water, pick acrylic. · To use both, keep thin acrylic underneath and oil on top, on an acrylic ground."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/a-short-history-of-paint.html","A Short History of Paint · Overview","A Short History of Paint","","egyptian-blue-lump","From colored earth and furnaces to the chemist's lab · For thousands of years painters worked from a short list of colors, most dug from the ground and a few made with fire. Some were priced like treasure. Then chemistry widened the palette. · The first palettes, and the first synthetic pigment · Ultramarine and the apothecary's shop · The chemists' colors and the paint tube · Egyptian, New Kingdom, Dynasty 18 · Lump of Blue Paste (Egyptian blue) · about 1473–1458 BCE"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-earth-and-fire-the-first-palettes.html","A Short History of Paint · History","Earth and Fire: The First Palettes","","pm-h-first-palettes","The first palettes held what could be dug up or charred: red and yellow iron earths, black charcoal and white chalk. · Fire also made Egyptian blue, at right, the oldest known synthetic pigment: a new compound cooked in a furnace, not dug up. It is very stable, though a binder that yellows around it changes how it looks. · At Blombos Cave in South Africa, a kit for mixing red ochre paint is about 100,000 years old. · Egyptian blue fell out of use in the Middle Ages; Raphael's Galatea fresco (1511–12) is a rare late use."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/lump-of-blue-paste-egyptian-blue.html","A Short History of Paint","Lump of Blue Paste (Egyptian blue)","Egyptian, New Kingdom, Dynasty 18","egyptian-blue-lump","Egyptian, New Kingdom, Dynasty 18 · about 1473–1458 BCE · Paste, L. 1 × H. 0.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · This is the pigment before it became paint: a crumb about 1 cm long, found in a burial inside the tomb of Senenmut at Thebes. · Copper gives it its blue."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-gold-lapis-and-the-guild-shop.html","A Short History of Paint · History","Gold, Lapis and the Guild Shop","","pm-h-costly-blue","Some pigments were luxury goods. The finest, ultramarine, came from lapis lazuli mined in Badakhshan, in present-day Afghanistan. · The chart compares top grades in mid-1400s Florence: ultramarine could cost as much as gold leaf, so it was usually saved for the most important figure, often the Virgin's mantle. · Grinding alone gave a grayish powder; the blue had to be kneaded out of a dough of wax and resin. · Painters bought colors from apothecaries, and in Florence joined their guild. · Cennino Cennini's handbook (about 1400) calls ultramarine \"perfect beyond all other colours\" (Herringham translation, 1899)."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-the-chemists-palette.html","A Short History of Paint · History","The Chemist's Palette","","rand-1841-tube-patent","After centuries with few new colors, chemistry began producing them, most of them after 1800. · Prussian blue came first, found by accident in Berlin early in the 1700s. The rest came in a rush after 1800; several, such as cadmium yellow, came from metals discovered around that time. · Synthetic ultramarine is chemically the same as the natural pigment but far cheaper, so the costliest blue became an everyday one. · The patent drawing shows the collapsible tin tube (1841) of John Goffe Rand, an American portrait painter; it replaced paint stored in pig bladders."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/pigments-by-color.html","Pigments by Color · Overview","Pigments by Color","","pm-blues","The main historical pigments in each color, and how each behaves · Each color family had a few workhorse pigments. These pages say what each was, when it was used, and which ones change color or are poisonous. · Whites · Blacks · Earth pigments · Reds · Blues · Greens · Yellows and oranges · Diagram · The main blues, by date"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-whites.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Whites","","pm-whites","For most of history the painter's white was lead white, made by letting vinegar fumes corrode strips of lead. · It is dense, covers well and helps oil dry. Zinc white covers less and in oil can contribute to cracking; titanium white covers best of all. · Lead white is poisonous; the US banned lead in household paint in 1978. · Tube \"titanium white\" often contains some zinc white too."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/self-portrait-at-the-age-of-63.html","Pigments by Color","Self Portrait at the Age of 63","Rembrandt van Rijn","rembrandt-self-portrait-63","Rembrandt van Rijn · 1669 · Oil on canvas, 86 × 70.5 cm. The National Gallery, London · The lit cheek is built up thick, in lead white warmed with ochres, over a brown underlayer. · Thick, raised paint like this is called impasto."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-blacks.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Blacks","","pm-blacks","Painters' blacks get their color from carbon: soot collected from a flame, or charred vine twigs or bone. · Lampblack is soft and bluish and can leave oil slow to dry, so umber was often mixed in to speed it up. · \"Ivory black\" today is almost always bone black, which is mostly bone mineral (calcium phosphate) with only some carbon. · Cima da Conegliano used vine black for a cool gray robe and bone black for warmer areas (1502–04). · Carbon blacks barely change in light."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/music-in-the-tuileries-gardens.html","Pigments by Color","Music in the Tuileries Gardens","Édouard Manet","manet-music-tuileries","Édouard Manet · 1862 · Oil on canvas, 76.2 × 118.1 cm. The National Gallery, London · The crowd's top hats and coats are mostly ivory black, mixed with cobalt blue and orange-brown ochre. · The gray clothes mix the same black with cobalt blue, viridian and ochre. · Black even goes into the glazes of the foliage (see Color Theory, Glazing and Direct Painting), so it works as a color, not just for shadow."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-earth-pigments.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Earth Pigments","","pm-earths","Heat turns yellow earth red: it drives the water out of goethite, the yellow iron mineral, and leaves hematite, the red one. · People used this trick about 40,000 years ago. For earth colors in general, see Color Theory, Neutrals and Earth Colors. · Hilary Becker (Art Institute of Chicago lecture, 2024) notes that the heat of Vesuvius turned some yellow walls at Herculaneum red."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/self-portrait.html","Pigments by Color","Self-Portrait","Rembrandt van Rijn","rembrandt-self-portrait-1659","Rembrandt van Rijn · 1659 · Oil on canvas, 84.5 × 66 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Almost everything here is earth: red, yellow and brown ochres, with lead white, carbon black and tiny traces of vermilion. · Scientists at the National Gallery of Art identified the ochres area by area: flesh, collar, hatband and background (2014). · Umber, the browner earth, shows at the edge of the collar."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-reds.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Reds","","pm-reds","Reds came in two kinds: mineral and made reds such as vermilion and red lead, and lakes, which turn root or insect dyes into pigment (see Color Theory, Where Pigments Come From). · Vermilion is mercury sulfide, and toxic; red lead is made by heating lead white. By Cennini's time vermilion too was made chemically and bought ready-made from the apothecary. He warned that on walls it turns black. · Cochineal insects, raised on prickly-pear cactus, earned Spain more than almost anything it took from the Americas except silver. · Synthetic alizarin, the madder dye made from coal tar in 1868, quickly ended the madder-growing trade."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/venus-and-adonis.html","Pigments by Color","Venus and Adonis","Titian","titian-venus-adonis","Titian · about 1555–60 · Oil on canvas, 160 × 196.5 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · Both kinds of red sit side by side: vermilion in Adonis's orange-red sleeve, a red lake in his pinker tunic and in the cloth over Venus's seat. · The lake contains textile fibers. It was probably made from clippings of red-dyed cloth. · Tests matched the dye to cochineal, with some madder-type colorant too."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-blues.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Blues","","pm-blues","From the Middle Ages to 1700, painters had only a few blues: azurite, ultramarine, smalt (powdered cobalt glass) and indigo, a plant dye. · Each had a weakness. Ultramarine survives light and lime, so it works in fresco, but acid destroys it. Azurite can turn green, and smalt can lose its color in oil (see Fading and Darkening). · Violet pigments came late (cobalt violet 1859, manganese violet 1868). Before that, painters mixed purples, such as lake with ultramarine."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/girl-with-a-pearl-earring.html","Pigments by Color","Girl with a Pearl Earring","Johannes Vermeer","vermeer-girl-pearl-earring","Johannes Vermeer · about 1665 · Oil on canvas, 44.5 × 39 cm. Mauritshuis, The Hague · The turban is natural ultramarine of high quality, mixed with lead white where the light falls. · In the shadow, the blue is cut with about half chalk and laid over a black underlayer."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/under-the-wave-off-kanagawa-the-great-wave.html","Pigments by Color","Under the Wave off Kanagawa (The Great Wave)","Katsushika Hokusai","hokusai-great-wave","Katsushika Hokusai · about 1830–32 · Woodblock print; ink and color on paper, 25.7 × 37.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The museum's record names a palette of indigo and imported Prussian blue, which probably reached Japan in the early 1800s. · Marco Leona, a Met scientist, argues that its strong, saturated blue helped make landscape prints popular. · Tests on other Met impressions found the blue outlines mix Prussian blue with indigo. · Despite cyanide in its structure, Prussian blue is safe enough to be an approved drug for removing radioactive cesium."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-greens.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Greens","","pm-greens","Most old greens were copper: malachite, a ground mineral, and verdigris, made by letting vinegar corrode copper. · Cennini called verdigris and lead white \"mortal enemies\" and kept them apart. The 1800s added emerald green, bright but full of arsenic, and viridian, a cool chromium green that hardly changes. · Emerald green also colored wallpapers; by the 1850s doctors were reporting illness in the people who lived with them."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/the-battle-of-san-romano.html","Pigments by Color","The Battle of San Romano","Paolo Uccello","uccello-battle-san-romano","Paolo Uccello · probably about 1438–40 · Egg tempera with some oil on poplar, 182 × 320 cm. The National Gallery, London · The hedge behind the horsemen was green. The museum's record says it has darkened to almost black. · The rose leaves mix verdigris with lead-tin yellow under a copper-green glaze, and those glazes have darkened. · Later additions at the corners use a different copper green, artificial malachite."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/theme-yellows-and-oranges.html","Pigments by Color · Concept","Yellows and Oranges","","pm-yellows","Many of the brightest early yellows were poisons: orpiment is an arsenic mineral, and lead-tin yellow and Naples yellow are lead compounds. · The bars show yellows coming and going. Lead-tin yellow was a favorite for centuries, then seems to have dropped out of use and was forgotten until analysts rediscovered it in 1941. Chrome and cadmium yellows added vivid new yellows and oranges. · Cennini calls orpiment \"like gold in colour\" and warns painters to keep it away from the mouth. · Adding selenium to cadmium yellow shifts it to orange and red."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/the-painters-colors/sunflowers.html","Pigments by Color","Sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 92.1 × 73 cm. The National Gallery, London · The yellows and oranges are several grades of chrome yellow, from pale lemon to deep orange. Soon after, in September 1888, he ordered three grades of it by letter. · Chrome yellow is lead chromate; small changes in its chemistry gave the different shades. · It can darken in light; see Fading and Darkening."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/binders-and-media.html","Binders and Media · Overview","Binders and Media","","pm-binder","What holds the pigment, and how each binder changes the paint · These pages take the main binders one by one: what each is made of, and what it lets a painter do. · What a binder does · Egg tempera · Fresco · Oil · Watercolor and gouache · Pastel · Encaustic · Diagram · One pigment in four binders"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-what-a-binder-does.html","Binders and Media · Concept","What a Binder Does","","pm-binder","A binder turns loose colored powder into paint: it glues the grains to each other and to the surface. · For how a binder changes a pigment's look, see Color Theory, How Pigments Behave. · Glue paints (distemper) were once common, but they are fragile and few survive."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-egg-tempera.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Egg Tempera","","pm-tempera","Egg tempera binds pigment with egg yolk. In medieval and early Renaissance Italy it was the usual paint for panels. · Egg dries quickly, so wet colors can't be blended as in oil. Painters modeled form with many small strokes side by side, as on the sphere. · By the late 1400s Italians were often combining egg with oil, and after about 1500 tempera fell out of favor."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/madonna-and-child.html","Binders and Media","Madonna and Child","Giotto","giotto-madonna-child","Giotto · about 1310–15 · Tempera on poplar panel, 85.4 × 61.8 cm (painted surface). National Gallery of Art, Washington · In the flesh, a green underpaint sets the shadows, then fine hatching and broader strokes model the form. · Under the gold is red bole, a layer of clay that warms the gold's color. · The dotted patterns in the halos were drawn freehand with a point, not stamped with punches."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-fresco.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Fresco","","pm-fresco","In fresco the painter works on lime plaster while it is still wet; as it sets, the color becomes part of the wall. · Follow the arrows: carbon dioxide from the air turns the lime back into a kind of stone, with the color inside it. Paint added once the plaster has dried, called secco, rests on the surface and may flake off. · Each day's patch of fresh plaster, a giornata, was only as big as the painter could finish before it dried. · Not every color suits wet lime: Pliny says indigo does not."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/lion-from-the-chapter-house-of-san-pedro-de-arlanza.html","Binders and Media","Lion, from the Chapter House of San Pedro de Arlanza","Spanish (Castile)","arlanza-lion","Spanish (Castile) · after 1200 · Fresco, mounted on canvas, 332.7 × 335.3 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art (The Cloisters), New York · This lion was painted on a monastery wall, taken down in the early twentieth century and mounted on canvas. · Lifting paintings off walls goes back to at least the 1500s. · Other wall paintings from the same monastery are in Barcelona, at the Museu Nacional d'Art de Catalunya."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-oil.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Oil","","stradanus-color-olivi","Oil paint binds pigment in an oil that dries, usually linseed; it hardens slowly as it takes up oxygen from the air. · Slow drying gives the painter time: colors can be blended on the surface, laid on thick or put on as glazes. · Stradanus's print (about 1600) repeats Vasari's legend that Van Eyck invented oil; it was used centuries earlier. · Oil then moved south; Giovanni Bellini used it in Venice by the 1460s."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/the-magdalen-reading.html","Binders and Media","The Magdalen Reading","Rogier van der Weyden","vanderweyden-magdalen-reading","Rogier van der Weyden · about 1435 · Oil on wood, transferred to mahogany, 62.2 × 54.4 cm. The National Gallery, London · Tests on this panel and four related ones found the paint is bound mostly in linseed oil. · Look at the lips, blended wet into wet, and the fur trim, where the brush dragged through paint that was still wet. · In one red glaze of the group, pine resin had been added to make the oil clearer."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-watercolor-and-gouache.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Watercolor and Gouache","","pm-watercolor","Watercolor is pigment in gum arabic, thinned with water. Its glow comes from the white paper under the color. · Gouache carries more pigment for its gum, so it hides the paper. · Highlights are often bare paper, left unpainted. · Watercolor paper is usually coated with gelatin to control how fast it drinks in the wash."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/the-return-tynemouth-recto.html","Binders and Media","The Return, Tynemouth (recto)","Winslow Homer","homer-return-tynemouth","Winslow Homer · 1881 · Transparent and opaque watercolor with gum glaze over graphite on paper, 34.2 × 34.3 cm. Art Institute of Chicago, Chicago · Look at the pale pink in the clouds and on the boat. Homer used colors that resist light here; in many of his other watercolors, similar washes made with less stable reds have faded. · The museum lists rewetting, blotting and scraping on this sheet: ways of taking color off the paper to win back lights."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-pastel.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Pastel","","pm-pastel","Pastel is pigment mixed with a white filler such as chalk, held in a stick by a trace of weak gum. · With so little binder, the color rests on the paper's tooth as loose powder. The powder scatters light, giving pastel its soft, velvety look, and it smudges at a touch. · Fixative sprays hold the powder, but they darken the colors and flatten the surface, so many pastellists did without. · Pastel needs no drying time, and a portrait took fewer sittings. It became a fashion after Rosalba Carriera visited Paris in 1720–21."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/portrait-of-maria-frederike-van-reede-athlone-at-seven-years-of-age.html","Binders and Media","Portrait of Maria Frederike van Reede-Athlone at Seven Years of Age","Jean-Étienne Liotard","liotard-maria-frederike","Jean-Étienne Liotard · 1755–56 · Pastel on vellum, 54.9 × 44.8 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · The pastel is laid on vellum, a fine prepared animal skin, and blended with a stump, a pointed blending tool. · Liotard disliked showing his strokes; look at the face and the velvet, where none can be seen."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-encaustic.html","Binders and Media · Concept","Encaustic","","pm-encaustic","Encaustic binds pigment in beeswax; the name is Greek for \"burnt in\". · The wax must be kept warm to brush, so painters worked quickly and went over the surface with heated tools. Some wax paint may have been worked without heat; the sources disagree. · Around 1,000 painted portraits survive from mummies of Roman Egypt, mostly from about the 1st to 3rd centuries CE; some are in glue tempera rather than wax. · These thin wooden panels survived because Egypt's climate is so dry."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/portrait-of-a-young-woman-in-red.html","Binders and Media","Portrait of a young woman in red","Roman Egypt","fayum-young-woman-red","Roman Egypt · 90–120 CE · Encaustic on limewood with gold leaf, 38.1 × 18.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Met study (published by the Getty) identifies the paint as pigment in beeswax, laid straight onto a sliver of linden wood only millimeters thick, with no ground. · Its colors: lead white, iron ochres, a see-through red lake for the garment and gold leaf; a copper pigment darkens the shadows of the face. · Look around the nose and under the lower lip: parallel marks left by a heated tool."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/supports-and-grounds.html","Supports and Grounds · Overview","Supports and Grounds","","pm-layers","What the paint is laid on, and the layer that prepares it · A panel or canvas painting rests on a support, wood or cloth, usually coated with a ground before any paint goes on. Both shape how the picture looks and how it ages. · Wood panels · Canvas · Grounds · Diagram · Layers of a panel painting"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-wood-panels.html","Supports and Grounds · Concept","Wood Panels","","pm-panel","From the 1200s to the early 1500s, most surviving portable paintings are on wood: one plank or several glued and doweled into a board. · Wood takes up moisture and gives it back as the air changes, swelling and shrinking. Over centuries a panel can warp and split, as at right, and loosen the paint on it. · Earlier restorers planed warped panels thinner and glued wooden lattices (cradles) to the back; conservators now use gentler methods."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/portrait-of-a-lady.html","Supports and Grounds","Portrait of a Lady","Rogier van der Weyden","vanderweyden-portrait-lady","Rogier van der Weyden · about 1460 · Oil on oak panel, 37 × 27 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · The panel is a single oak board, and its tree rings can be dated. · Its 265 rings end in 1438. Adding the missing outer rings and time for the wood to season, the panel was probably not painted before about 1463, so it may be a few years later than its usual date. · The oak probably came from the Baltic region. · A bare margin around the paint shows it was painted with a frame already fixed to the panel."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-canvas.html","Supports and Grounds · Concept","Canvas","","encyclopedie-peinture-pl4-chassis","Canvas is a tough cloth, usually linen, pulled tight over a wooden frame. · It weighs less than wood and can be rolled up. Venice switched to canvas from panels and walls between about 1450 and 1550; damp air and the sailcloth trade are the usual reasons given, though recent research doubts any single one. · Seen from the back: a crossbar and corner braces keep the frame square. · Until the 1800s, big canvases were usually sewn from strips; the Titian on the next page is an exception."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/the-vendramin-family-venerating-a-relic-of-the-true-cross.html","Supports and Grounds","The Vendramin Family, venerating a Relic of the True Cross","Titian","titian-vendramin-family","Titian · about 1540–45 · Oil on canvas, 206.1 × 288.5 cm. The National Gallery, London · Nearly 3 m wide, this canvas is a single piece with no seam, woven in a patterned twill. · Patterned cloth like this was woven far wider than a meter. · It has been lined, backed with a second canvas, probably three times, most recently with wax-resin in 1961."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/theme-grounds.html","Supports and Grounds · Concept","Grounds","","pm-layers","A ground is the coat laid on the wood or canvas before any paint, giving the paint a smooth, even surface to hold on to. · Its color glows through thin paint and affects the colors laid over it. · Gesso: white gypsum and glue, the Italian panel ground, sanded glass-smooth. · North of the Alps, chalk and glue did the same job. · From about 1600 many painters chose warm brown or red grounds instead of white. · In the 1800s some painters, the Impressionists above all, preferred white."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/paint-and-its-surface/samson-and-delilah.html","Supports and Grounds","Samson and Delilah","Peter Paul Rubens","rubens-samson-delilah","Peter Paul Rubens · about 1609–10 · Oil on oak, 185 × 205 cm. The National Gallery, London · Over the white chalk ground, Rubens brushed a streaky yellow-brown layer, and he let it show. · In the folds of Delilah's white sleeve he left the layer bare as a warm half-shadow. · The layer is an earth color like umber, with a little lead white, in oil. · A 1983 National Gallery study by Joyce Plesters traced the layers, down to the six oak planks."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/how-paintings-change.html","How Paintings Change · Overview","How Paintings Change","","pm-fading","Fading, darkening and yellow varnish, and how scientists see beneath the surface · No painting looks exactly as it did when it left the studio. Some pigments fade or darken, varnish yellows, and scientists use X-rays, infrared and tiny samples to work out what has changed. · Fading and darkening · Varnish · Looking beneath the surface · Diagram · Four pigments, then and now"],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/theme-fading-and-darkening.html","How Paintings Change · Concept","Fading and Darkening","","pm-fading","Some pigments change color because they react with light, air or the oil around them. · Red lakes fade in light; see Color Theory, How Pigments Behave. As potassium seeps out of smalt's glass into the oil, the blue fades and the oil yellows, leaving gray-brown. Copper greens darken in light. Lead white can turn black in polluted air, mainly on paper and in water-based paint. · Copper greens hidden under a frame often stay green. · Chrome yellow can darken too: a 2011 X-ray study of two Van Gogh paintings found light had likely altered a thin surface layer. · Darkened lead white on drawings can be turned white again with hydrogen peroxide, though it may darken again."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/the-bedroom.html","How Paintings Change","The Bedroom","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-bedroom","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 73.6 × 92.3 cm. Art Institute of Chicago, Chicago · Researchers suspect the walls and doors, now light blue (cobalt blue with zinc white), were once violet, and that the red in the mix has faded. · Dark red lake particles turned up in the door paint. · The floor contains geranium lake, a red that has probably faded. · In the Amsterdam version, a strip under tape since 1931 keeps a peachier floor, and one stroke hidden by the frame is still lilac (Hendriks and colleagues, 2011)."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/theme-varnish.html","How Paintings Change · Concept","Varnish","","pm-varnish","Varnish is a clear top coat that evens out the gloss, deepens the colors and protects the paint. · Natural-resin varnishes such as mastic and dammar yellow, cloud and crack with age, as the middle panel shows. Because varnish is the top layer, conservators can usually remove it and apply fresh varnish. · The National Gallery of Art suggests the look of varnish matters more than its protection. · Cleaning is not always safe: Reynolds mixed mastic into his paint, so varnish solvents attack it too (David Peggie, National Gallery scientist, in a 2014 Royal Society of Chemistry lecture)."],[7,"/study/pigments-and-materials/aging-and-analysis/theme-looking-beneath-the-surface.html","How Paintings Change · Concept","Looking Beneath the Surface","","pm-analysis","Conservators identify materials with methods that see through paint or read its chemical elements, most of them without taking a sample. · X-rays show changes the painter covered over, where heavy paint such as lead white is involved. Infrared can reveal a carbon drawing under the paint. X-ray fluorescence (XRF) names the elements at a spot, and the pigment is worked out from them. · Scanning XRF across a whole painting can map hidden earlier versions. · When a sample is needed, it is smaller than a pinhead and comes from an already damaged spot, as a last resort."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/neo-impressionism-seurat-signac-and-the-science-of-color.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color · Overview","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","","seurat-circus","Theory turned into a working method. · From the mid-1880s a group of painters set pure color down in separate touches. Within a few years the approach had followers across France and in Belgium. · Seurat's large figure paintings, from the riverbank to the circus ring · Signac, the movement's champion · Followers: Cross, Luce and Van Rysselberghe · Neo-Impressionism · The critic Félix Fénéon's 1886 name for the color method of Seurat, Signac and their circle. · Georges Seurat · The Circus · 1891"],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/bathers-at-asnieres.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","Bathers at Asnières","Georges Seurat","seurat-bathers","Georges Seurat · 1884 · Oil on canvas, 201 × 300 cm. The National Gallery, London · Young men and boys rest and swim on a riverbank at the edge of an industrial suburb. It was the painter's first picture on the scale of a major museum canvas. · The figures sit in profile, still and solid, like a frieze on a classical building. Ordinary leisure on the Seine is given the calm weight usually kept for history painting. · The official Salon rejected it, so it hung at the first Indépendants show that same year. · Look across the water: factory chimneys at Clichy rise behind the bathers. · He later repainted some passages in dots, adding yellow and blue spots to the orange hat. · It was bought with the Courtauld Fund in 1924. · Unlike Impressionist pictures made outdoors, it was planned through oil sketches and drawings and painted in the studio. The working-class bathers contrast with a well-dressed couple in a boat."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/circus-sideshow-parade-de-cirque.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","Circus Sideshow (Parade de cirque)","Georges Seurat","seurat-parade","Georges Seurat · 1887–88 · Oil on canvas, 99.7 × 149.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Musicians and performers line up on a raised platform outside a fair tent as onlookers gather. It was the painter's first night scene and first picture of popular entertainment. · The dot method moves from sunlight to artificial light, its separate touches blending into a dim, hazy glow at night. · Its debut came at the 1888 Indépendants, beside Models, an indoor daylight scene. · Look for the trombone player standing at the very center of the line. · The small lights along the top and the round lamps at upper right are built from tiny touches. · Stephen C. Clark left it to the Met in his 1960 bequest. · Optical mixing · Small touches of different colors meant to blend in the viewer's eye, not on the palette. · It shows the Circus Corvi's free outdoor show at a Paris fair in spring 1887, staged to lure ticket buyers inside."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/le-chahut.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","Le Chahut","Georges Seurat","seurat-chahut","Georges Seurat · 1889–90 · Oil on canvas. Kröller-Müller Museum, Otterlo · Dancers perform on a dance-hall stage while a double-bass player and a conductor work in the foreground. A rowdy night out becomes a carefully planned, almost stiff design. · A popular dance becomes the subject of a large, serious canvas. Painters in this circle often treated city nightlife as part of modern life, a scene of freedom and spectacle. · Look at the lines: legs, the conductor's hand, the bass neck and even the flowers in the background point upward. · The dancers repeat one another like types rather than individual portraits. · Warm artificial light is built from tiny dots, short lines and combinations of color. · This bold offshoot of the cancan was performed by both sexes. Its female dancers raised their hems and showed their legs, which made it a risqué spectacle."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-channel-of-gravelines-petit-fort-philippe.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","The Channel of Gravelines, Petit Fort Philippe","Georges Seurat","seurat-gravelines","Georges Seurat · 1890 · Oil on canvas, 73.3 × 92.1 cm. Indianapolis Museum of Art at Newfields · A curving quay leads the eye across a channel to a white lighthouse. It is one of four harbor views from the artist's last summer. · A painted band of dots runs around the edge, turning red beside the green water and orange against the blue sky. The border becomes part of the color scheme. · The four harbor views were sent to the Les XX exhibition in Brussels in early 1891. · A sturdy bollard in the foreground replaces a two-masted boat seen in the oil study. · A tilted boat tied up below the lighthouse grew out of a hazy patch of dots in the study. · The series' original painted blue frames have not survived. · The small port sits where a river reaches the sea, on the coast near Belgium. Each summer the painter left his Paris studio to work alone in a quiet Channel town."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-circus.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","The Circus","Georges Seurat","seurat-circus","Georges Seurat · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 186 × 152 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A bareback rider, an acrobat, a ringmaster and a clown perform before tiers of spectators. It followed the sideshow and the cancan as his third scene of popular entertainment at night. · The ring and performers are drawn in curves and spirals, while the stands and audience stay rigid and square. The contrast follows Charles Henry's ideas on how lines affect feeling. · It hung unfinished at that spring's Indépendants, and diphtheria killed him within days of the opening. · Look at the flat blue frame: he began painting it, and friends completed it. · The palette is largely white, broken by small touches of red, yellow and blue. · The American collector John Quinn left it to the French national museums. · Its color draws on Chevreul and Rood's color theory. After the painter's death it belonged to his mother, then to Signac by 1900."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/opus-217-against-the-enamel-of-a-background-rhythmic-with-beats-and-angles-tones-and-tints-portrait-of-m-felix-feneon-in-1890.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","Opus 217. Against the Enamel of a Background Rhythmic with Beats and Angles, Tones, and Tints, Portrait of M. Félix Fénéon in 1890","Paul Signac","signac-feneon","Paul Signac · 1890 · Oil on canvas, 73.5 × 92.5 cm. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · A man holding a top hat and a flower stands against a spinning background of colored bands and stars. · A friend of the group is posed against a pattern this close to abstract design, so the portrait becomes a tribute to the method itself. · The sitter had defended the dot technique in print in 1887, urging viewers to step back. · Look at the pinwheel behind the sitter, which may nod to the color theorist Charles Henry. · The figure stands in strict left profile, set sharply against the flat pattern. · Mr. and Mrs. David Rockefeller gave it to the museum. · Besides writing criticism, he edited, translated and dealt in art, and he held anarchist views. He later owned Bathers at Asnières."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/evening-calm-concarneau-opus-220-allegro-maestoso.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","Evening Calm, Concarneau, Opus 220 (Allegro Maestoso)","Paul Signac","signac-concarneau","Paul Signac · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 64.8 × 81.3 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Fishing boats rest on a still evening sea beyond rounded coastal rocks. Here the dot method is carried out onto open water. · The opus number and musical subtitle suggest the series works like a symphony, with each canvas one movement of a larger piece. · It belongs to a group of five related views of fishing boats off Concarneau. · One boat with a raised sail stands out against a row of distant sails. · Precise hues sit in separate strokes, which he compared to brightly colored lights. · It came to the Met with the Robert Lehman Collection in 1975. · Divisionism · The working practice of separating color into individual strokes or dots. · The harbor lies on the coast of Brittany. The painter was a social anarchist, and two curators in a museum lecture read its fishing cooperatives as the harmony anarchists hoped for."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-evening-air-lair-du-soir.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","The Evening Air (L'Air du soir)","Henri-Edmond Cross","cross-evening-air","Henri-Edmond Cross · c. 1893 · Oil on canvas, 116 × 166 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Women rest and stroll under pines by the sea as the heat of the day fades. Matisse saw it hanging in Signac's dining room and drew on it for an early key work. · The small dot grows into broad, rectangular strokes that read like mosaic tiles. Enlarging the touch gave the method a decorative force suited to a big wall-sized canvas. · It answered Signac's 1893 idea that each paint a big decorative canvas praising the South. · Its first showing came in 1894, at the group's third joint exhibition. · Level and upright lines offset each other, and the figures hold still in easy poses. · Ginette Signac donated it to the French national collections in 1976. · The setting is the South of France, where the painter had lived for two years. The classical grouping of figures in an ideal landscape draws on Puvis de Chavannes's Sweet Land."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-river-sambre-in-charleroi.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","The River Sambre in Charleroi","Maximilien Luce","luce-sambre","Maximilien Luce · 1896 · Oil on canvas, 54.6 × 64.8 cm. Yale University Art Gallery, New Haven · Lit windows glow along a river and an arched bridge as night falls. The dot method, often used for sunny leisure, here records a working city. · The subject is a coal and steel town. Painters in the group's anarchist circle often turned to factories and workers, bringing industry into the movement's subject matter. · It dates from his second and most productive stay in Charleroi. · Look at the reflected lights, broken into vertical streaks on the dark water. · Deep blues and violets dominate, with small warm touches for the lamps. · Arthur G. Altschul, a Yale graduate, gave it to the university's gallery. · Charleroi lies in the Pays Noir, or Black Country, which the painter first visited in 1895, invited by Émile Verhaeren and Théo van Rysselberghe. He painted more than thirty canvases there."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/maria-sethe-at-the-harmonium.html","Neo-Impressionism: Seurat, Signac and the Science of Color","Maria Sèthe at the Harmonium","Théo van Rysselberghe","rysselberghe-sethe","Théo van Rysselberghe · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 120 × 86 cm. Royal Museum of Fine Arts Antwerp · A young woman sits sideways at a harmonium, her face turned toward light from the left. The dot method gives a quiet domestic moment the presence of a formal portrait. · The painter helped found Les XX, the Brussels exhibiting society that showed Seurat in 1887. That show brought the method to Belgium, where he and other avant-garde artists took it up. · He saw La Grande Jatte in Paris in 1886, with the poet Émile Verhaeren. · Look at the purple dress against purple curtains, set close together in tone. · He switched to divisionism in 1888 while keeping up his work as a portrait painter. · The portrait later hung in the houses her husband designed and built. · The sitter (1867–1942) was a pianist whom he painted more than once. In 1894 she married the Belgian designer and architect Henry van de Velde."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/cezanne-building-with-color.html","Cézanne: Building with Color · Overview","Cézanne: Building with Color","","cezanne-viaduct","How one painter turned the Impressionist touch into structure, from Auvers to Aix. · From 1882 Paul Cézanne often painted landscapes around Aix-en-Provence and L'Estaque. His slow method made him a model for the painters who came after him. · Early work beside Pissarro · Provençal landscapes, portraits and still lifes organized by planes and color · The late mountain views, bathers and portraits · Constructive stroke · Short, deliberate strokes that build form. · Paul Cézanne · Mont Sainte-Victoire and the Viaduct of the Arc River Valley · 1882–85"],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-house-of-the-hanged-man-auvers-sur-oise.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","The House of the Hanged Man, Auvers-sur-Oise","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-hanged-man","Paul Cézanne · 1873 · Oil on canvas, 55.5 × 66.3 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A steep village lane drops between old houses toward distant rooftops. Puzzling to critics at first, it became the first canvas the painter ever sold to a collector. · Thick, grainy paint is laid on like masonry, so walls, roofs and slopes feel built rather than glimpsed, each plane pressed close against the next. · It was one of three canvases he showed at the first Impressionist exhibition, Paris, 1874. · Look for the road, roofs and slopes, which radiate like spokes from one central point. · Its light colors and broken touch show what he learned while painting beside Pissarro. · It reached the Louvre in 1911 through the bequest of Count Isaac de Camondo. · Its first buyer, Comte Doria, later traded it to the collector Victor Chocquet. When Chocquet's collection was sold in 1899, Camondo bought it on Monet's advice."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/mont-sainte-victoire-and-the-viaduct-of-the-arc-river-valley.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","Mont Sainte-Victoire and the Viaduct of the Arc River Valley","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-viaduct","Paul Cézanne · 1882–85 · Oil on canvas, 65.4 × 81.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A river valley stretches toward a lone mountain, split down the middle by one great tree, with a railroad viaduct crossing it. It shows his search for a lasting order beneath nature's surface. · Fields, roads and hills are laid out as layered horizontal planes that step back toward the mountain. The depth comes from this ordered structure, not from passing effects of light. · He painted it from a hillside at Montbriand, the estate his sister owned. · Look at the viaduct: its arches recall a Roman aqueduct, as in Poussin's classical landscapes. · Of his several views from this spot, it is the most fully worked. · It entered the Met in 1929 with the H. O. Havemeyer Collection. · The valley lies near Aix-en-Provence, his hometown, and he had known it since childhood. Between 1882 and 1890 he drew and painted this site again and again."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-bay-of-marseille-seen-from-lestaque.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","The Bay of Marseille, Seen from L'Estaque","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-marseille","Paul Cézanne · c. 1885 · Oil on canvas, 80.2 × 100.6 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Red-roofed houses give way to a deep blue bay and a low line of hills. He painted this bay over a dozen times in the 1880s. · The water turns into a single dense blue plane that binds the whole picture. It reads as flat pattern and as deep space at the same time. · It is likely the final canvas he painted at L'Estaque. · Look for four zones stacked up the canvas: buildings, water, mountains and sky. · Cracks in the sea expose many layers of blue, often brushed on before the last had dried. · It came to the Art Institute with the Martin A. Ryerson Collection. · It was shown in Amsterdam in 1911 and in the Museum of Modern Art's first loan show in 1929. In a letter to Pissarro, he compared the view to a playing card."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/madame-cezanne-in-a-red-dress.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","Madame Cézanne in a Red Dress","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-red-dress","Paul Cézanne · 1888–90 · Oil on canvas, 116.5 × 89.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A woman in red sits upright in a yellow chair with a tall back. He painted his wife in this dress four times; only this version places her in a furnished room. · She is the steady center of a room whose chair, curtain and fireplace tilt and bend around her. · The setting is his Paris apartment on the quai d'Anjou (1888–90). · Look for the mirror above the fireplace at the far left. · The blue wall is mottled, painted in uneven patches rather than one flat tone. · The Met bought it in 1962 through the Mr. and Mrs. Henry Ittleson Jr. Purchase Fund. · Hortense Fiquet was working as a bookbinder when they met in Paris in 1869. They married in 1886, and she sat for 29 known portraits."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-card-players.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","The Card Players","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-card-players","Paul Cézanne · 1890–92 · Oil on canvas, 65.4 × 81.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Three men play cards at a small table while a fourth stands behind them and watches. An everyday rural pastime is painted with unusual calm and dignity. · The figures are fixed in a steady, balanced arrangement, and nothing happens. Older card-game pictures often warned against cheating; here the only drama is silent concentration. · He built it from separate studies of laborers, each of whom posed alone. · Look for the four pipes hanging on the back wall. · Conservation research shows repeated changes to the outlines and furniture before the figures settled. · It came to the Met in 1960 through the bequest of Stephen C. Clark. · The models worked at the Jas de Bouffan, the estate near Aix he inherited from his father. The series grew to five paintings; the later ones show only two players."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-basket-of-apples.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","The Basket of Apples","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-basket","Paul Cézanne · c. 1893 · Oil on canvas, 65 × 80 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Apples, a basket and a bottle crowd a draped table. Everything is arranged for the balance of the whole picture, not for a faithful record of the scene. · The tabletop's back edge breaks into three parts, each at a different height. Writers have read the picture as combining several viewpoints rather than one fixed view. · It was first shown in 1895, at the artist's first solo exhibition, at Ambroise Vollard's Paris gallery. · Look at the basket: it tips forward, as if about to spill its apples. · He reworked the left table edge, which now warps downward. · It came to the Art Institute in the Helen Birch Bartlett Memorial Collection. · Still life sat at the bottom of the traditional ranking of subjects. That very neglect seems to have attracted him, since it left him free to invent."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/still-life-with-plaster-cupid.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","Still Life with Plaster Cupid","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-cupid","Paul Cézanne · c. 1894 · Oil on paper on board, 70.6 × 57.3 cm. The Courtauld Gallery, London · A small plaster cupid stands among apples and onions in a crowded studio corner, with canvases leaning on the wall behind it. The painter's own workroom and props become a puzzle about seeing. · The floor tilts steeply upward, and the depth of the room is hard to judge. · A sprouting onion sits where real produce and the fruit painted in the canvases behind seem to blur. · Look at the far corner, where a green apple seems much too large. · It came to the Courtauld Gallery with Samuel Courtauld's bequest in 1948. · The cast copies a small seventeenth-century sculpture. Samuel Courtauld bought the painting in 1923 from the Glasgow dealer Alex Reid."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/mont-sainte-victoire.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","Mont Sainte-Victoire","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-sainte-victoire","Paul Cézanne · 1902–04 · Oil on canvas, 73 × 91.9 cm. Philadelphia Museum of Art · The mountain, built from blues, violets and grays, rises over a broad plain beneath an open sky. Small strokes of green bring life to both the peak and the sky. · Patches of color laid side by side build space: blue-browns up front, reds and greens across the middle, blues at the back. Color, not linear perspective, sets the distance. · Look at the mountain: it keeps a dark outer contour and lines across its face. · Rough strokes of ochre and two kinds of green make the middle plain look like a quilt. · The dealers Ambroise Vollard and Bernheim-Jeune bought it in February 1907. · It entered Philadelphia's Elkins Collection in 1936, bought from the dealer Paul Rosenberg. · In 1901 he bought land on Les Lauves, a hill just north of Aix, and built a studio there. Over two dozen late works show the mountain from there."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-large-bathers.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","The Large Bathers","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-bathers","Paul Cézanne · 1900–06 · Oil on canvas, 210.5 × 250.8 cm. Philadelphia Museum of Art · Nude women gather on a riverbank beneath tall, leaning trees. It is the largest and last of his pictures of bathers in a landscape, a lifelong theme. · The figures are simplified into sturdy, block-like shapes and grouped into a broad pyramid. Younger painters, Picasso among them, studied this kind of structure closely. · It was shown at a Paris retrospective in 1907, the year after the artist died. · Look at the trees, which lean inward to form a pointed arch over the river. · He worked on it for years and left it unfinished at his death. · The museum bought it in 1937 through the W. P. Wilstach Fund. · He made three large bathers pictures late in life; the others are at the Barnes Foundation and the National Gallery, London. He almost never worked from a live nude model."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/order-and-science/the-gardener-vallier.html","Cézanne: Building with Color","The Gardener Vallier","Paul Cézanne","cezanne-vallier","Paul Cézanne · 1906 · Oil on canvas, 107.4 × 74.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A bearded old man in a dark cap and jacket sits on a wooden seat against a deep green ground. It is one of a late series of portraits of the same sitter. · Loose, thick patches of green, blue and brown stay visible across the whole surface. The open, mottled handling lets the paint itself carry the structure of the figure. · Its earliest listed showing was at the Met in 1920, titled A Sailor. · Look at the pose: legs crossed, fingers laced together in his lap. · Small touches of bright blue, orange and red light up the dark clothes and face. · It came to the National Gallery in 1959 as a gift of Eugene and Agnes E. Meyer. · The sitter also worked as handyman at the painter's house near Aix. The artist died in October of the year this was painted."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/van-gogh-from-the-netherlands-to-saint-remy.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy · Overview","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","","vangogh-starry-night","Five years, four places, and the turn from earth tones to expressive color · Across about five years, from the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy, each move brought a change in Van Gogh's color and handling. · Nuenen and the dark, earthy palette of his peasant pictures · Paris, where Japanese prints and the dot painters lightened and broke up his touch · Arles and Saint-Rémy, where heightened color, outline and thick paint express mood · Vincent van Gogh · The Starry Night · 1889"],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/the-potato-eaters.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","The Potato Eaters","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-potato-eaters","Vincent van Gogh · 1885 · Oil on canvas, 82 × 114 cm. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam · A peasant family shares a meal of potatoes under a hanging lamp. He planned it as a showpiece, a difficult group scene meant to prove he could paint figures. · The coarse faces and bony hands are deliberate. He put the message about honest labor ahead of correct anatomy and technical polish. · Look at the five figures gathered close around one shared dish. · He kept to earth colors, comparing the figures to a dusty, unpeeled potato. · Critics faulted its dark palette and awkward drawing, though he was pleased with it. · Related studies and sketches for it are in the same museum. · He painted it in the village of Nuenen in April and May. Today it ranks among his best-known paintings."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/self-portrait-with-a-straw-hat.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","Self-Portrait with a Straw Hat","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-straw-hat","Vincent van Gogh · 1887 · Oil on canvas, 40.6 × 31.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A small head-and-shoulders view painted in Paris. It shows how quickly his dark Dutch manner gave way to light and color. · Short, separate strokes of color build the face, hat and background. The Met links this to his growing grasp of Neo-Impressionist methods and color theory. · Look at the hat brim, where yellow strokes are laid in curving, radiating rows. · Bare canvas shows between the strokes in many places, especially in the background. · He painted it on the reverse of a peasant study from 1885, The Potato Peeler. · It came to the Met in 1967 through the bequest of Adelaide Milton de Groot. · In Paris, from 1886 to 1888, he painted over twenty self-portraits. Short of money for models, he worked from a mirror and used himself instead."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/portrait-of-pere-tanguy.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","Portrait of Père Tanguy","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-tanguy","Vincent van Gogh · 1887 · Oil on canvas, 92 × 75 cm. Musée Rodin, Paris · An older man in a straw hat sits before a bright patchwork of pictures. The portrait gives a modest neighborhood figure the steady calm of a sage. · Behind the sitter hangs a wall of vivid woodblock prints from Japan, collected by the two brothers. Here the Japanese influence is put in plain view. · Notice how still he sits, facing us squarely, his hands folded together at his waist. · Unmixed colors, set against their opposites, go down in visible, carefully placed strokes. · Rodin acquired it, possibly in 1894, perhaps drawn by a shared love of Japanese art. · Rodin also owned two other paintings by the same artist. · Japonisme · The European taste for Japanese art, especially woodblock prints, in the later 1800s. · The sitter, Julien-François Tanguy (1825–94), sold art materials from a small shop on the Rue Clauzel. Painters short of cash often paid him in pictures."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/garden-with-courting-couples-square-saint-pierre.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","Garden with Courting Couples: Square Saint-Pierre","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-courting-couples","Vincent van Gogh · 1887 · Oil on canvas, 75 × 113 cm. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam · Couples stroll and sit under young chestnut trees in a Paris park on a spring day. It shows him testing the newest Paris technique on a scene of modern leisure. · He swapped the Pointillists' dots for short dashes of uneven size, each pointing its own way. The borrowed method becomes a source of movement and light rather than a strict system. · He called it his garden-with-lovers picture. · Look at the sky, made of slanting blue and white dashes across the whole upper half. · Red touches mark the chestnut blossom, scattered through the green of the trees. · It is owned by the foundation that bears the painter's name. · The theme of couples together reflected his own longing for a wife and family. That hope went unmet, and he gave himself to his art instead."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/the-night-cafe.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","The Night Café","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-night-cafe","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 72.4 × 92.1 cm. Yale University Art Gallery, New Haven · A few drinkers slump at tables in a harshly lit room at night. He meant the place to feel like somewhere a person could go to ruin. · Blood-red walls clash with a green billiard table and ceiling. As he told Theo, the clash was meant to express dark human passions rather than describe the room. · Look at the lamps, each ringed by halos of yellow and orange strokes. · He studied gaslight's effect: the owner's white clothes shift toward lemon yellow and pale green. · The title is painted below the signature at lower right. · Once owned by Ivan Morozov in Moscow, it came to Yale in 1961 by Stephen Carlton Clark's bequest. · It was the Café de l'Alcazar in Arles, an all-night haunt of drifters and prostitutes. He took his meals there."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/sunflowers.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","Sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-sunflowers","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 92.1 × 73 cm. The National Gallery, London · A vase of sunflowers stands on a table. The series was one of the earliest results of the expressive style he worked out in Arles. · Yellows of many shades fill the flowers, vase, wall and table. The museum links that yellow to happiness and the bright sunshine of Provence. · It was meant for Gauguin's bedroom in the Yellow House, ahead of his visit. · Look for every stage of life, from tight buds to drooping, seed-heavy heads. · He signed it on the vase itself, in blue paint. · The National Gallery bought it in 1924 with the Courtauld Fund. It hangs in a 1600s Italian frame. · Flower pieces of this kind draw on Dutch still lifes of the 1600s about life's brevity. Five versions survive in public collections; this is one."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/portrait-of-the-postman-joseph-roulin.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","Portrait of the Postman Joseph Roulin","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-roulin","Vincent van Gogh · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 81.3 × 65.4 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · A bearded postal worker sits in uniform cap and coat. It opens a run of six portraits he painted of this man. · The blue uniform, with yellow buttons and braid, carries the picture. Strong color contrast, not modeled shadow, gives the sitter his presence. · Look at the large, heavy hands resting on the chair and table. · The thick beard is built from short vertical strokes; he likened it to Socrates'. · In 1889 it was left in Arles with Joseph and Marie Ginoux, and Vollard had it by 1897. · It reached the MFA in December 1935 as a gift of Robert Treat Paine, 2nd. · They became friends once the painter settled in Arles in February 1888. He went on to portray Roulin's wife and children, hoping to paint a whole family."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/the-bedroom.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","The Bedroom","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-bedroom","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 73.6 × 92.3 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A narrow room holds a wooden bed, two chairs and pictures on the walls. This canvas is the second of three versions he painted. · Flat, bold colors and simple shapes were meant to calm the eye. Yet the steep, rushing lines of floor and bed can read as restless, a tension the museum notes. · A letter lists the colors: lilac walls, chrome yellow bed, blood-red bedspread, green window. · Thick, broken brushwork stays visible across the floorboards. · It was first exhibited at Ambroise Vollard's Paris gallery in 1895. · Frederic Clay Bartlett bought it in 1926 and gave it to the museum that year. · The room was his own, in the Yellow House in Arles, first painted in October 1888. At Saint-Rémy he repainted it, adding a smaller copy for his mother and sister."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/irises.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","Irises","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-irises","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 74.3 × 94.3 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · A bed of blue irises crowds the foreground against a field of orange flowers. He started it during his opening week as a patient at Saint-Rémy. · The view is close and cropped, with flowers spilling past the edges. The museum suggests Japanese woodblock prints shaped this framing and the broad areas of color. · Look for the single white iris at left, set apart from the blue mass. · Dark contour lines edge many of the leaves and petals. · In September 1889 his brother Theo showed it at the Salon des Indépendants in Paris. · The critic Octave Mirbeau was the first to own it. · The asylum of Saint-Paul-de-Mausole had a garden where he could paint from nature. He thought of this canvas as a study and made no known drawings for it."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/the-starry-night.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","The Starry Night","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-starry-night","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 73.7 × 92.1 cm. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · A village sleeps under a sky of swirling stars beside a towering cypress. It began with the view from his asylum window. · The village was put together from other views, and the cypress was moved closer. The motif is reshaped to carry feeling rather than to record the scene. · Look for the moon blazing at right and Venus glowing at center left. · The scene is nocturnal, but he worked on it across several sessions by day. · He painted it in mid-June, a month or so after he entered the asylum. · MoMA acquired it by exchange through the Lillie P. Bliss Bequest. · He stayed at the asylum for a full year, 1889–90, hoping treatment would ease his mental illness. That single year produced hundreds of works."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/wheat-field-with-cypresses.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","Wheat Field with Cypresses","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-wheat-cypresses","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 73.2 × 93.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Ripe wheat, green shrubs and two dark cypresses sit under a sky of rolling clouds. He ranked it among the strongest canvases of that summer. · Curving strokes of thick impasto carry wind and afternoon heat through wheat, trees and clouds. Brushwork, not outline, sets the rhythm. · It is the first version, painted outdoors on the spot. · To him the cypresses resembled an Egyptian obelisk, a dark accent in a sunny landscape. · In a letter he likened the sky to a piece of Scotch plaid. · The Met bought it in 1993 with a gift from the Annenberg Foundation. · Impasto · Paint laid on thickly enough to stand up from the surface and keep the marks of the brush. · That June he made cypresses the theme of an early Saint-Rémy series. He repeated this view in a drawing, a London studio copy, and a replica for his family."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/self-portrait.html","Van Gogh: From the Netherlands to Saint-Rémy","Self-Portrait","Vincent van Gogh","vangogh-self-portrait-1889","Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 65 × 54 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · The painter turns three-quarters toward us against a pale blue-green ground. · Swirling arabesques fill the background and repeat the waves of his hair and beard. The pattern turns an ordinary backdrop into a carrier of energy and feeling. · Look at the clothes: a suit, not his usual work jacket. · Green and pale turquoise surround the orange of the hair and beard. · The face is gaunt and hard-edged, and the eyes have an intense, anxious look. · Dr. Gachet's son and daughter, Paul and Marguerite, donated it in 1949. · Over roughly a decade he produced upward of forty self-portraits, counting paintings and drawings. He wrote to his sister that he wanted a deeper likeness than a photograph gives."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/gauguin-pont-aven-and-synthetism.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism · Overview","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","","gauguin-vision","Brittany, then Tahiti: flat color and dark contour put to work for belief and dream. · In the late 1880s, Gauguin and younger painters turned away from observed light. Their ideas spread through friendships, a café exhibition and one small panel. · The Pont-Aven circle: Gauguin, Bernard and Sérusier in Brittany · Self-portraits, a ceramic and a talisman · Tahiti: paintings and prints built from many cultures, read with their colonial context · Synthetism · The Pont-Aven style of simplified drawing, firm contours and flat, intense color, often painted from memory. · Paul Gauguin · Vision of the Sermon (Jacob Wrestling with the Angel) · 1888"],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/madeleine-in-the-bois-damour.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","Madeleine in the Bois d'Amour","Émile Bernard","bernard-madeleine","Émile Bernard · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 137 × 163 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A young woman lies full length on the grass of a wood, head propped on one hand. The life-size canvas is signed with the name of the Breton town where it was made. · She is lost in a daydream, and the museum sees a portrait with allegorical overtones. The pose stands for a state of mind, not a moment observed. · It was painted in the studio, with the woods worked up from studies made on the spot. · Look at her body: it lies parallel to the river running behind the trees. · Figure and landscape differ in light, brushwork and color, as if painted separately. · The Orsay bought it in 1977 from a private collection. · The sitter was the painter's sister, then 17; he was 20. The museum points out that Sérusier's small panel, the Nabis' talisman, made this same wood famous."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/vision-of-the-sermon-jacob-wrestling-with-the-angel.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","Vision of the Sermon (Jacob Wrestling with the Angel)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-vision","Paul Gauguin · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 72.2 × 91 cm. Scottish National Gallery, Edinburgh · Women in white caps pray in the foreground while, beyond a tree trunk, a man wrestles an angel. Here he set aside the parallel strokes of his earlier work. · A flat field of unnatural red replaces grass and holds both the women and what they imagine. Vision and real world share one space, with no boundary between them. · Writing to Van Gogh, he explained that the struggle and its setting exist only in the women's imagination. · Look for the small cow at upper left, grazing beside the struggle. · The wrestlers are drawn small and far off, while the women's heads crowd the near edge. · The National Galleries of Scotland bought it in 1925. · The sermon was on Genesis 32, the story of a night-long struggle after the crossing of the river Jabbok. The women wear the regional dress of Brittany."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/self-portrait-with-portrait-of-emile-bernard-les-miserables.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","Self-Portrait with Portrait of Emile Bernard (Les Misérables)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-miserables","Paul Gauguin · 1888 · Oil on canvas, 44.5 × 50.3 cm. Van Gogh Museum, Amsterdam (Vincent van Gogh Foundation) · A painter shows himself close up, his face turned toward the viewer. It was made for an exchange of self-portraits among friends. · He presents himself as Jean Valjean, outcast hero of a well-known French novel. Through that role he stands for all painters misunderstood by their society. · Van Gogh proposed the exchange, after Japanese printmakers who traded their work. · Look at upper right for the small framed portrait of the younger painter. · The face sits against flat yellow wallpaper scattered with flowers, painted as pure pattern. · Van Gogh's family kept it until their foundation took it over in 1962. · The younger painter had joined him at Pont-Aven in Brittany that summer. Their meeting there fed the flat, outlined style of 1888."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/the-talisman-landscape-in-the-bois-damour.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","The Talisman (Landscape in the Bois d'Amour)","Paul Sérusier","serusier-talisman","Paul Sérusier · 1888 · Oil on wood, 27 × 21.5 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A riverside wood is painted in a handful of strong hues. Dense trees fill the upper left, with a path below them. · Trees, path and mill shrink to patches of strong color. Each color is an equivalent for what he saw, not a copy. · A note on the back says it was made at Pont-Aven under Gauguin's direction. · Look for the mill at right. · Gauguin's advice, as Maurice Denis recorded it, was to paint shadow pure ultramarine. · It stayed with Denis and his heirs until the Orsay bought it in 1985. · Nabis · A group of painters inspired by Gauguin; the name comes from the Hebrew word for prophets. · On his return to Paris, young painter friends saw the panel and adopted it as their guiding example. They soon called themselves the Nabis."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/self-portrait-in-the-form-of-a-grotesque-head-pot-anthropomorphe.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","Self-Portrait in the Form of a Grotesque Head (Pot anthropomorphe)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-pot","Paul Gauguin · 1889 · Glazed stoneware, height 28.4 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A jar is modeled as a human head, its face bowed and brooding. The museum classes it as a domestic pot, but a label he attached names it The Sincerity of a Dream. · The group's taste for simplified form carries over into clay. The features are reduced to a few heavy masses rather than worked into a detailed likeness. · It was a gift to the painter Émile Schuffenecker, named on the label under the base. · Look at the half-shut eyes and the hand pressed up under the chin. · The glaze pools and streaks over the face, leaving the surface uneven. · It was given to the Louvre in 1938 and later passed to the Orsay. · The label salutes this fellow painter as an idealist. He kept the pot until 1934, and it then passed through his family."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/the-yellow-christ.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","The Yellow Christ","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-yellow-christ","Paul Gauguin · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 92.1 × 73.3 cm. Buffalo AKG Art Museum, Buffalo, NY · Three peasant women kneel at a crucifix set in open autumn fields. The museum sets the scene at the evening Angelus. · Dark contours close each shape, and the colors inside stay flat. The scene simplifies into a few strong areas. · The model for the Christ was a carved, painted crucifix from the 1600s, kept in the Trémalo Chapel near Pont-Aven. · Look at the trees: their reds and oranges echo the yellow body. · He said the yellow was meant to express the peasants' isolation and piety. · After wartime seizure by German forces, it was bought by Buffalo in 1946. · The Angelus is a Catholic prayer said three times a day, at six in the morning, noon and six in the evening. Early owners included the painter Émile Schuffenecker and the collector Gustave Fayet, before the dealer Paul Rosenberg."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/the-seaweed-gatherer.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","The Seaweed Gatherer","Paul Sérusier","serusier-seaweed","Paul Sérusier · c. 1890 · Oil on canvas, 46 × 54.9 cm. Indianapolis Museum of Art at Newfields · A stone wall runs across a stretch of Breton coast below rolling fields. · The land rises in rolling bands of color stacked up the canvas, with little sense of deep space. The coast becomes a flat, patterned surface. · The view shows the shoreline close to Le Pouldu. · Look at the two rust-colored mounds: seaweed hauled up off the shore to feed the fields. · The worker is a flat shape, his curved back echoing the hills. · It came to Indianapolis in 1998 with the Samuel Josefowitz collection of Pont-Aven art. · After his death the canvas passed to his widow, who left it to her friend Paule Boutaric. He signed this canvas only with his initials, in reddish paint."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/ia-orana-maria-hail-mary.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","Ia Orana Maria (Hail Mary)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-ia-orana","Paul Gauguin · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 113.7 × 87.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A mother carries a child on her shoulder while two women in wrapped cloths approach through a garden. It was the first important canvas he made in Tahiti, ahead of his series on Polynesian beliefs. · Mary and the Christ Child appear as Tahitian figures. The scene recasts a familiar Christian subject with a Polynesian setting and cast. · In a March 1892 letter, he said he was fairly pleased with it. · Look for the angel with yellow wings, half hidden among the flowers at left. · The title is painted in Tahitian at lower left, like a caption. · Sam A. Lewisohn bequeathed it to the Met in 1951. · The title puts the angel's greeting to Mary into Tahitian. Much of the design comes from a photograph he owned of a relief at Borobudur, a temple in Java."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/merahi-metua-no-tehamana-tehamana-has-many-parents.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","Merahi metua no Tehamana (Tehamana Has Many Parents)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-tehamana","Paul Gauguin · 1893 · Oil on jute canvas, 75 × 53 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A girl in a striped, high-necked dress is posed before a wall of glyphs and figures. She holds a fan from Samoa and wears white flowers in her hair. · Behind her, glyphs derived from a tablet from Easter Island meet a goddess mixing Polynesian with Hindu imagery. The encounter of cultures is one he staged. · It was first shown at his Durand-Ruel exhibition in Paris in November 1893. · Look at her upright posture and steady look toward the viewer. · The title is lettered at lower left, the signature at lower center. · Mr. and Mrs. Charles Deering McCormick gave it to the Art Institute in 1980. · The Art Institute identifies the sitter, named in the title, as a Tahitian girl of 13. The museum says the picture turns her into an image of his own desire."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/nave-nave-fenua-delightful-land.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","Nave Nave Fenua (Delightful Land)","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-nave-nave","Paul Gauguin · 1893–94 · Woodblock print in color with stenciling, image 35.9 × 20.7 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A nude woman stands among palms and flowers in a rough-cut print. It belongs to a set that critics of the time called a revolution in printmaking. · The artist's own impressions were each inked and printed differently. Each sheet behaves more like a single work than one copy in an edition. · It is one of ten woodcuts made for Noa Noa, his account of Tahiti. · Look at the paper: the cream sheet has grayed with age. · It was pressed twice on a yellow tone block, and red was added by stencil. · Edward McCormick Blair gave this impression to the Art Institute. · Woodcut · A print made by carving a wood block, inking its raised surface and pressing paper onto it. · He began Noa Noa after returning to Paris from his first stay in Tahiti. In a lecture, curator Cornelia Homburg calls the book largely fiction."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/color-as-feeling/where-do-we-come-from-what-are-we-where-are-we-going.html","Gauguin, Pont-Aven and Synthetism","Where Do We Come From? What Are We? Where Are We Going?","Paul Gauguin","gauguin-where","Paul Gauguin · 1897–98 · Oil on canvas, 139.1 × 374.6 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · Figures, animals and an idol fill a wide Tahitian landscape. Three questions about human life, written at upper left, give the picture its subject. · Read from right to left, the scene moves from a baby asleep to an old woman close to death. A philosophical program is set out as the stages of a single life. · It was first shown at Ambroise Vollard's Paris gallery in late 1898. · Look for the blue idol at left, which his letters say points to the Beyond. · Yellow corners make the canvas look like an aged fresco on a wall. · The museum bought it in 1936 for $80,000. · He painted it in Tahiti during a period of decline and psychological crisis. He then shipped it to Daniel de Monfreid in France, and it was consigned to the dealer."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/montmartre-and-the-nabis-posters-prints-and-interiors.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors · Overview","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","","lautrec-moulin-rouge","From café-concert billboards to quiet rooms covered in pattern · Montmartre's dance halls and cabarets hired painters to design posters for their stars. At the same time, a group of young friends made small, private pictures of family rooms. · Posters and paintings of Montmartre nightlife, its performers and its audiences · The Nabis' decorative panels and self-portraits · Color prints published by Ambroise Vollard, and a group portrait · Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · At the Moulin Rouge · 1892–95"],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/equestrienne-at-the-cirque-fernando.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Equestrienne (At the Cirque Fernando)","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-equestrienne","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1887–88 · Oil on canvas, 100.3 × 161.3 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A trick rider circles a circus ring while the ringmaster urges her horse on. It is an early large painting of Montmartre entertainment, made before the posters. · Faces lean toward exaggeration: the rider's heavy makeup and snarl, the ringmaster's answering glare. Pushing features this far sharpens character instead of flattering the sitter. · Joseph Oller, owner of the Moulin Rouge, bought it to hang in his dance hall. · Look at the ringmaster's long whip, curving across the empty floor toward the horse. · The ring's curve sweeps diagonally across the canvas, carrying the eye along with the horse. · In 1925 the Art Institute purchased it from Paul Rosenberg & Co. · The Cirque Fernando was a Montmartre circus that Renoir also painted. Suzanne Valadon may have modeled for the rider."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/moulin-rouge-la-goulue.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Moulin Rouge: La Goulue","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-goulue","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1891 · Lithograph printed in four colors on three sheets, 190 × 116.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A dancer kicks high above a crowd of black silhouettes on a street poster. This advertisement launched the artist's career as a poster designer. · A painter's eye is put to work selling a dance hall: flat color, bold lettering and a cropped crowd. Art and advertising now shared the same street wall. · The dance hall commissioned the poster. · Look for the gray dancer in front, Valentin le Désossé, whose stage name means boneless. · The Paris printer Affiches Américaines, of Charles Lévy, produced the sheets. · The Met acquired this complete impression in 1932. · Color lithograph · A print made by drawing each color on its own stone, then printing the stones in turn on one sheet. · The hall first opened its doors in 1889, on the boulevard de Clichy. Its star dancer went by a nickname meaning the glutton."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/ambassadeurs-aristide-bruant.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Ambassadeurs: Aristide Bruant","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-bruant","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1892 · Color lithograph on tan wove paper, 147.2 × 99.9 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A cabaret singer in a wide black hat and red scarf turns to glare past the viewer. The poster sells a performer's stage persona as much as his show. · The figure is cut down to a few flat shapes: hat, cloak and scarf. Posters like this traded modeled detail for shapes that read at once from across a street. · The singer commissioned it himself to advertise his shows at an upscale club. · Look at the low viewpoint, which makes the figure loom over the passer-by. · The design was printed in five colors, with the scarf one flat red. · It came to the Art Institute in the Mr. and Mrs. Carter H. Harrison Collection. · The singer played a rough outsider who mocked upper-class audiences at his own cabaret, Le Mirliton. The artist reused and adapted this image in later designs."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/at-the-moulin-rouge.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","At the Moulin Rouge","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-moulin-rouge","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1892–95 · Oil on canvas, 123 × 141 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · Performers and friends crowd a table in a famous dance hall. The canvas doubles as a portrait of the artist's own circle. · One performer's face glows a harsh acid green under the hall's electric light. Here artificial glare becomes a subject in itself, even at the cost of a flattering likeness. · It may have been shown first at Les XX, in Brussels, in January 1892. · Look for the artist in the background, dwarfed by his cousin Gabriel Tapié de Céleyran, who towers beside him. · A railing cuts diagonally across the foreground, pulling the viewer into the scene. · Museum text published online says the canvas was once cut down to remove that performer, then made whole again by 1914. · The artist kept a permanently reserved table there, and La Goulue stands behind, fixing her hair. The woman at the right edge is the performer May Milton."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/divan-japonais.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Divan Japonais","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec","lautrec-divan","Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec · 1892–93 · Lithograph printed in four colors, 80.8 × 60.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A dancer in black sits in the audience while a singer performs on the stage above. Both women were stars of the city's nightlife. · The singer's head is cut off by the frame, yet she is known by her long black gloves alone. A performer's trademark detail worked like a brand. · It was made to advertise a Montmartre café-concert of the same name. · Look at the orchestra pit, where instrument necks rise between audience and stage. · Broad areas of flat black and yellow come from just four printed colors. · It entered the Met in 1958 as a bequest of Clifford A. Furst. · The seated dancer is Jane Avril, one of the artist's favorite subjects, with the writer Édouard Dujardin. The singer on stage is Yvette Guilbert."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/self-portrait-with-waroquy.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Self-Portrait with Waroquy","Édouard Vuillard","vuillard-waroquy","Édouard Vuillard · 1889 · Oil on canvas, 92.7 × 72.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A young painter studies himself while a friend looms close behind him. Curator Mary Weaver Chapin pairs it with two other self-portraits from that year to introduce the group. · The scene is a mirror image, given away only by a small bottle's reflection at lower right. A quiet clue like this makes an ordinary room feel slightly dreamlike. · His journal places the sitting before his grandmother's bedroom mirror. · Look at the friend behind, with a cigarette between his lips. · The friend is painted more vaguely than the sitter, so he seems to drift back. · It came to the Met in 1955 as a gift of Alex M. Lewyt. · The painter was about 21, in a high collar that could suit a clerk or a cleric. Only the palette and brushes mark him as an artist."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/decorative-panels-women-in-the-garden.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Decorative Panels: Women in the Garden","Pierre Bonnard","bonnard-garden","Pierre Bonnard · 1890–91 · Distemper over carbon, crayon and chalk on paper, mounted on canvas; each panel 155 × 45 cm. Kunsthaus Zürich · Across four panels, figures walk and rest among leaves. Zurich's museum sees the set as his first major statement in the group's manner. · White dots cover one red dress and lilac checks another, lying flat across the folds. The Nabis took bold patterning like this partly from Japanese prints. · The panels were first conceived as one folding screen in four parts, then separated. · Look at the first panel, where a pale dog leaps up beside the walking woman. · The second panel stays largely unpainted, so its bare sketch lines show. · A Zurich art society donated it in 1984; the Musée d'Orsay holds a revised second version. · His younger sister Andrée appears in the second panel, and his cousin Berthe Schaedlin in the two figures shown from behind. The woman in the first panel has not been identified."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/woman-in-a-striped-dress.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Woman in a Striped Dress","Édouard Vuillard","vuillard-striped","Édouard Vuillard · 1895 · Oil on canvas, 65.7 × 58.7 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Two women arrange flowers in a room. Cut flowers and greenery fill the right half of the canvas. · The sitter's features blur into the patterned room around her. Such panels sometimes hung unframed on patterned wallpaper, as if continuing the room. · It is one of five decorative panels Thadée Natanson ordered for himself and Misia. · Look at the red-and-white stripes, which echo the flower patterns beside them. · The color range stays small, mostly deep reds set off by cream, green and gold. · Paul Mellon gave it to the National Gallery of Art in 1983. · Intimism · A label for small paintings of family and friends in cramped, patterned rooms, a style two of the group pursued after 1899. · Thadée Natanson published the magazine La Revue blanche. The red-haired woman may be Misia, a musician."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/interior-with-pink-wallpaper-i.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Interior with Pink Wallpaper I","Édouard Vuillard","vuillard-pink","Édouard Vuillard · 1899 · Color lithograph on China paper, image 35.3 × 27.4 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A corner of the artist's home is covered almost from ceiling to floor in printed pattern. The sheet comes from an album of private rooms and landscapes. · The room feels close and crowded. Pattern, more than any figure, sets the mood here. · It is plate five of an album Vollard released in 100 copies. · Look at the doorway at right, where a small figure stands half hidden. · The grayish-ivory sheet is slightly larger than the printed image, leaving a narrow margin. · It came to the Art Institute as a gift of Walter S. Brewster. · Figures in these interiors are absorbed in daily tasks, which makes them seem distant. Collectors ignored the album, and Vollard still had unsold copies two decades on."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/houses-in-the-courtyard.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Houses in the Courtyard","Pierre Bonnard","bonnard-courtyard","Pierre Bonnard · 1895–96 · Lithograph in four colors, image 34.5 × 25.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York (impression shown: Petit Palais, Paris) · A window frames the back of an apartment building, with shutters, chimneys and one woman leaning out. The window's own dark frame runs down the left side and across the bottom. · The everyday city appears mostly in soft grays and beiges. A few muted inks are enough to hold the whole mood. · It belongs to Vollard's 1899 album of Paris life: 12 prints, issued in 100 copies. · Look for the tiny touches of orange on the chimney pots. · A trial proof at the Art Institute adds violet and hand-applied yellow. · The Met bought its impression in 1928 through the Harris Brisbane Dick Fund. · The album set out to show the private Paris that residents knew. In hand-colored proofs, he tried moving the season from a wintry gray to a warm olive."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/homage-to-cezanne.html","Montmartre and the Nabis: Posters, Prints and Interiors","Homage to Cézanne","Maurice Denis","denis-homage","Maurice Denis · 1900 · Oil on canvas, 182 × 243.5 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Nine men and one woman crowd a room hung with other paintings. It works as a group portrait. · The younger generation names its model in public, gathering around a still life by the older master. It links this circle directly to his way of building pictures. · The 1901 Salon of the Société nationale des beaux-arts gave it its first showing. · Look at the far left, where Odilon Redon listens to Paul Sérusier. · The figures stand in a row, following earlier group homages by Fantin-Latour. · The writer André Gide gave it to the Musée du Luxembourg, Paris, in 1928. · The scene is set in Ambroise Vollard's gallery in Paris. The still life on the easel once belonged to Paul Gauguin."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/symbolism-in-france-puvis-redon-and-rousseau.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau · Overview","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","","redon-closed-eyes","Painters who turned from the visible world to dreams, ideas and imagined places. · In the 1880s and 1890s some French artists left the viewer to complete the meaning. · A calm, simplified figure painting. · Prints and drawings in black that picture visions, evolution and literature, then a late turn to color. · Jungle and desert scenes by a self-taught painter, built from sources found in Paris. · Symbolism · A movement in art and poetry that sought to evoke ideas and inner states instead of describing the visible world. · Odilon Redon · Closed Eyes · 1890"],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/the-poor-fisherman.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","The Poor Fisherman","Pierre Puvis de Chavannes","puvis-poor-fisherman","Pierre Puvis de Chavannes · 1881 · Oil on canvas, 154.7 × 192.5 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A thin man stands with bowed head in a moored boat on a grey shore. Little happens in the scene, and its stillness sets the tone. · The color is pale and flat, with almost no modeling, and the forms are kept simple. Younger painters, among them Seurat, Gauguin, Denis and Picasso, admired the result. · It made its debut at the Salon, where it found no buyer. · Look past the boat: a figure gathers flowers while a baby lies on the grass. · Critics attacked its odd perspective and greenish palette. · The French State bought it in 1887, its first purchase of a work by the painter. · The figures are a widowed fisherman and his two children, a theme with Biblical echoes. It hung in the Musée du Luxembourg until 1929, then the Louvre."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/guardian-spirit-of-the-waters.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","Guardian Spirit of the Waters","Odilon Redon","redon-guardian","Odilon Redon · 1878 · Charcoal and black chalk on paper, 46.6 × 37.6 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A huge head with a single wide eye drifts above the sea. The drawing takes ordinary elements, a face and a horizon, and makes them strange. · Rubbed, erased and scratched charcoal builds the head out of shadow. Here black serves imagination rather than observation, a shift toward picturing inner life. · It appeared in the Art Institute's Strange Realities exhibition (2025–26). · Look lower right: a tiny sailboat gives the head its vast scale. · He also used sponging and touches of white chalk. · The Art Institute bought it in 1950 from Henri Petiet. · Noirs · The artist's name for his black charcoal drawings and lithographs of the 1870s to 1890s. · Black remained his chief medium into the 1890s. The MoMA brochure connects that preference to the shadows that had fascinated him as a boy."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/the-eye-like-a-strange-balloon-moves-towards-infinity.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","The Eye, Like a Strange Balloon Moves Towards Infinity","Odilon Redon","redon-eye-balloon","Odilon Redon · 1882 · Lithograph on China paper, image 26.3 × 19.8 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · A giant eye, fringed with lashes, floats over a dark plain. Its pupil rolls upward, toward the open sky. · The caption points toward infinity, something no one can see. The print gives that abstract idea a strange, concrete shape. · It is the first plate of the album To Edgar Poe. · Look below the eye: a small head sits in the basket-like gondola hanging from it. · He learned transfer lithography in 1878, and most of his prints were made for albums. · It came to the Art Institute in 1920 as part of the Stickney Collection. · The album was dedicated to the American writer Edgar Allan Poe. The Los Angeles County Museum of Art holds a complete copy of it."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/there-was-perhaps-a-first-vision-attempted-in-the-flower.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","There Was Perhaps a First Vision Attempted in the Flower","Odilon Redon","redon-first-vision","Odilon Redon · 1883 · Lithograph on China paper, image 22.5 × 17.7 cm. The Art Institute of Chicago · An eye opens at the top of a plant stalk, ringed by lashes like petals. · Human anatomy and plant growth merge into one creature. Hybrid forms like this recur often in his early prints, which imagine stages of life no one could witness. · It is the second of eight plates in the album Les Origines. · Look at the speckled ground: the stalk rises from it like a seedling. · The plates were first made without captions, which came later because a patron asked for them. · A second impression is in Washington's National Gallery of Art, from the Rosenwald Collection. · The album traces life from simple organisms toward human form. It draws on Darwin and on French theories of evolution."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/death-my-irony-surpasses-all-others.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","Death: My Irony Surpasses All Others","Odilon Redon","redon-death-irony","Odilon Redon · 1889 · Lithograph on China paper. The Art Institute of Chicago · A pale, hollow-eyed figure rises from a coiling dark mass. One arm is raised behind its head, and dark tones fill most of the sheet. · The series follows no continuous story, and its plates do not simply illustrate the text. Each one pairs a caption with an image that stands on its own. · It is the third of six plates in an album dedicated to Gustave Flaubert. · Look at the top: small flowers trail through the figure's hair. · A 1913 catalogue of his prints lists it as number 97. · The Art Institute also owns a second impression, which it dates to 1888. · The plates respond to The Temptation of Saint Anthony, a novel by Flaubert. This was the second of three albums he devoted to the book, in 1888, 1889 and 1896."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/closed-eyes.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","Closed Eyes","Odilon Redon","redon-closed-eyes","Odilon Redon · 1890 · Oil on canvas mounted on cardboard, 44 × 36 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A head with lowered lids rises above a band of pale light. The Musée d'Orsay treats it as a defining image of Symbolist painting. · The museum reads the closed lids as a sign of inward life, of dreaming and absence. · It repeats a motif from one of the artist's earlier drawings. · Look at the space around the bust: it is left undefined, so the figure seems to float. · The paint is thin enough for the canvas weave to show through. · Bought from his studio in 1904, it was the first of his works in the French national collections. · The model was most likely his wife, Camille Falte. The face recalls the Dying Slave by Michelangelo, now in the Louvre, whose shut eyes he wrote about in his journal."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/bouquet-of-flowers.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","Bouquet of Flowers","Odilon Redon","redon-bouquet","Odilon Redon · c. 1900–1905 · Pastel on paper, 80.3 × 64.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Blooms of many kinds burst from a blue vase against a warm, hazy ground. Stems and blossoms reach well beyond the rim of the vase. · Around 1900 he took up pastel and oil, and color became the center of his art. He said pastel let him give his dreams more substance. · Look among the blooms: butterflies hover at the edges of the bouquet. · The vase was made by the ceramicist Marie Botkin, who presented it to him as a gift around 1900. · Loose pastel strokes leave patches of the tan paper showing. · It came to the Met in 1956, a gift of Mrs. George B. Post. · As a young man he befriended the Bordeaux botanist Armand Clavaud. The MoMA brochure credits Clavaud with showing how science could feed the imagination."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/surprised.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","Surprised!","Henri Rousseau","rousseau-surprised","Henri Rousseau · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 129.8 × 161.9 cm. National Gallery, London · Lightning splits a stormy sky while a tiger crouches low in bending grass. His jungle pictures, some twenty in all, start here; none of his work is better loved. · Every leaf gets its own sharp edge, painted by a man with no formal training. Some critics mocked him, but Picasso and other avant-garde artists admired him. · Its debut was the Salon des Indépendants, a Paris exhibition with no jury. · Look at the tiger: it is unclear whether it is startled or stalking. · A copying device, the pantograph, transferred his outlines; the museum suggests this gives the tiger its weightless look. · The National Gallery bought it in 1972 with help from Walter H. Annenberg. · Years spent as a clerk in the Paris customs office earned him the nickname le Douanier. He also told of soldiering in Mexico, a tale with no basis in fact."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/the-sleeping-gypsy.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","The Sleeping Gypsy","Henri Rousseau","rousseau-sleeping-gypsy","Henri Rousseau · 1897 · Oil on canvas, 129.5 × 200.7 cm. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · Under a full moon, a lion stands over a figure asleep in the desert. A mandolin and a water jug lie beside her on the sand. · Nothing happens here, and nothing is explained. MoMA's essay treats the picture as another of his dreams, one that feels lovely and menacing at the same time. · It was first shown at the Salon des Indépendants. · Look at her dress: bands of many bright colors run along its length. · The sleeper lies flat along the front of the picture, parallel to its surface. · It came to MoMA in 1939, a gift of Mrs. Simon Guggenheim. · Romani people were widely thought in France to trace their roots to Egypt, a belief carried by his word for the sleeper, bohémienne. A fascination with North Africa shaped the desert setting."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/the-dream.html","Symbolism in France: Puvis, Redon and Rousseau","The Dream","Henri Rousseau","rousseau-dream","Henri Rousseau · 1910 · Oil on canvas, 204.5 × 298.5 cm. The Museum of Modern Art, New York · A nude reclines on a sofa in the middle of a moonlit jungle. At roughly two by three meters, no jungle scene by him is bigger, and none came later. · Its plants and animals came from Paris sources: sketches at the Jardin des Plantes and the zoo, popular literature and colonial expositions. The nude and musician are invented. · It was shown at the Salon des Indépendants. · Look into the leaves: lions, birds, monkeys and a musician hide among them. · In February 1910 the dealer Ambroise Vollard purchased it directly from the painter. · Nelson A. Rockefeller gave it to the Museum of Modern Art in 1954. · Asked about the sofa, he explained that the sleeping woman dreams of being carried off to the forest. The museum notes the picture's later influence on Dalí and Magritte."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/beyond-france.html","Beyond France · Overview","Beyond France","","munch-scream","How painters from Brussels to London reworked the new ideas. · New exhibiting groups in Brussels, Munich and Vienna gave painters outside France a stage. The label for the whole movement came later, from Roger Fry's 1910 exhibition in London. · Belgium and Norway: masks, silence and dread in Ensor, Khnopff and Munch. · Vienna: the Secession, and Klimt's gold and ornament. · Switzerland, Italy and Britain: rhythmic figures, divisionist light and the London music hall. · Secession · A group of artists who broke away from an official academy or exhibition society to show new work on their own terms. · Edvard Munch · The Scream · 1893"],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/christs-entry-into-brussels-in-1889.html","Beyond France","Christ's Entry into Brussels in 1889","James Ensor","ensor-christ","James Ensor · 1888–early 1890s · Oil on canvas, 252.6 × 431 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · The canvas packs a city street to its edges, with a haloed Christ almost lost in the procession. · Masks, clowns and grotesque faces stand in for the public, turning society into a mob of frauds. The satire takes aim at religion, politics and art. · Les XX, which he had helped found, rejected it. · Look for the red banner over the crowd, with a slogan for the socialist cause. · He spread paint with palette knives, spatulas, and the brush handle as well as its tip. · It was not shown in public until 1929, long after it was signed. · The figures mix public, historical and allegorical characters with the painter's family and friends, and Christ is in part the painter himself. Several preparatory drawings survive, one of them now at the Getty."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/i-lock-my-door-upon-myself.html","Beyond France","I Lock My Door upon Myself","Fernand Khnopff","khnopff-door","Fernand Khnopff · 1891 · Oil on canvas, 72.7 × 141 cm. Neue Pinakothek, Munich · A pale, red-haired woman rests her chin on her hands. The picture builds a mood of withdrawal rather than telling a story. · An empty doorway, a blank window and an inward stare shut out the world beyond the room. Here the Symbolist turn inward is staged as a sealed room. · It keeps its original gilded frame. · Look for the bust of Hypnos, Greek god of sleep, on a high shelf beside a red poppy. · Soft greys fill the room; only her hair, the poppy and withered flowers add warmth. · The Munich collection bought it from the artist at the city's 1893 annual exhibition. · The title is a line from a Christina Rossetti poem about inner conflict. Her brother, the Pre-Raphaelite painter Dante Gabriel Rossetti, strongly influenced the artist."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/the-scream.html","Beyond France","The Scream","Edvard Munch","munch-scream","Edvard Munch · 1893 · Tempera and oil crayon on cardboard, 91 × 73.5 cm. Nasjonalmuseet, Oslo · A figure with a skull-like face clutches its head on a road above a fjord. It became a lasting image of modern anxiety. · Sky and water bend into swirling bands that echo the figure's panic. Feeling reshapes the landscape, one reason it is widely read as a turn toward Expressionism. · It was first shown in Berlin, under the title Despair. · Look for faint pencil writing at top left, claiming only a madman could paint it. · An 1892 version showed a clearly drawn man; here he shrinks to a schematic face. · Collector Olaf Schou gave it to the national collection in 1910. · Expressionism · Art that distorts form and color to convey inner feeling rather than outward appearance. · A figure without age or identity invites viewers' own fears. Other versions include a painting at MUNCH, two pastels (1893, 1895) and an 1895 lithograph."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/anxiety.html","Beyond France","Anxiety","Edvard Munch","munch-anxiety","Edvard Munch · 1894 · Oil on canvas, 93.5 × 73 cm. MUNCH, Oslo · A row of pale, staring townspeople walks toward the viewer along a fjord road under a red sky. A man in a top hat and a woman in a bonnet lead the group. · The motif moved across media, from this canvas to an 1896 lithograph printed by Clot in black and in red. Painting and printmaking fed each other. · A watercolour and pencil study of the same scene also dates from 1896. · Look at the faces, set side by side like masks and all turned outward. · MUNCH conservator Inger Grimstad found raw canvas under a hand-applied ground, and an original tinted glaze once taken for dirt. · It was recently shown in the museum's Infinite display. · Grimstad links this crowd to the Sunday stroll in his earlier painting of Karl Johan. That picture, now at KODE, sets the walkers in town."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/pallas-athene.html","Beyond France","Pallas Athene","Gustav Klimt","klimt-pallas","Gustav Klimt · 1898 · Oil on canvas, 75 × 75 cm. Wien Museum, Vienna · An armored goddess stares straight out at the viewer, gripping a tall spear. The canvas sits in a frame lettered with her name. · An ancient goddess is recast as an allegory, shown as a patroness rather than acting in a scene from myth. An old subject becomes a modern emblem. · It is signed and dated in the upper left corner. · Look at her breastplate, where a Medusa head sticks out its tongue. · Gold on the helmet and scaled armor catches the light against a dark ground. · It now hangs in the museum's fin-de-siècle Vienna galleries. · The goddess goes by her Greek name here, while the museum's subject index uses her Roman name, Minerva. The work has appeared in the museum's own catalogues since 1985."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/the-kiss.html","Beyond France","The Kiss","Gustav Klimt","klimt-kiss","Gustav Klimt · c. 1907–09 · Oil and metal leaf on canvas, 180 × 180 cm. Upper Belvedere, Vienna · Two lovers embrace at the edge of a flowered meadow, wrapped together in one golden cloak. She kneels with eyes closed as he bends to kiss her cheek. · Gold leaf and dense pattern press the figures flat against a shimmering ground. Ornament does more than decorate here: it builds the space and binds the couple together. · It was first shown, still unfinished, at the 1908 Kunstschau exhibition in Vienna. · Look at his robe of black and white rectangles against her circles and flowers. · The figures carry gold, silver and platinum leaf over a background of brass and metal flakes. · Austria's Ministry for Culture and Education bought it in 1908–09, in the artist's lifetime. · Gold mosaics the artist saw in Ravenna, Italy, are often linked to this golden phase. The picture was first shown simply as Lovers, a title the museum still records."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/the-dream-of-the-shepherd.html","Beyond France","The Dream of the Shepherd","Ferdinand Hodler","hodler-shepherd","Ferdinand Hodler · 1896 · Oil on canvas, 250.2 × 130.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A kneeling shepherd in an Alpine landscape dreams of eight pale women who appear in a row above him. The picture divides into two zones, earth below and vision above. · The women repeat related poses in a steady, frieze-like rhythm. · It was first shown in Geneva. · Look at the women's stylized gestures, which recall Puvis de Chavannes. · Red-brown contour lines outline the women over pale, thinly brushed color. · The Metropolitan Museum bought it in 2013. · Parallelism · The painter's later name for his principle of repeating similar forms in symmetrical order to express unity. · According to the Met, the women may stand for spiritual insight, oneness with nature and physical longing. The picture helped build the painter's name as an explorer of sex, death and the unconscious."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/spring-in-the-alps.html","Beyond France","Spring in the Alps","Giovanni Segantini","segantini-spring","Giovanni Segantini · 1897 · Oil on canvas, 116 × 227 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · A girl leads two draught horses across a sunlit Alpine field, with snowy peaks behind. The wide view sets farm work inside a calm, high mountain world. · Strands of unmixed color are set next to each other, so field and sky shimmer. Italian divisionists drew the method out into threads. · Munich saw it first, at the seventh Secession exhibition. · Look for the sower working the plowed field behind the horses. · Flecks of gold leaf and thick paint catch the light across the surface. · On loan in San Francisco for over 70 years, it was sold in 1999 and reached the Getty in 2019. · The view is near Soglio in Switzerland's Val Bregaglia, toward the Sciora peaks. It was painted for a San Francisco collector, Jacob Stern."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/the-fourth-estate.html","Beyond France","The Fourth Estate","Giuseppe Pellizza da Volpedo","pellizza-fourth-estate","Giuseppe Pellizza da Volpedo · 1898–1901 · Oil on canvas, 283 × 550 cm. Galleria d'Arte Moderna (GAM), Milan · A broad line of working men and women strides out of shadow toward the viewer. On a huge canvas, a workers' march takes on the scale of history painting. · Here the divisionist method serves a political subject rather than a landscape. In Italy, painters of the group used effects of light to heighten feeling. · The 1902 Turin Quadriennale showed it first, and it did not sell there. · Look at the woman beside the two leading men, carrying a baby as she steps forward. · It grew out of about a decade of studies and three earlier versions. · Milan bought it by public subscription in 1920. · The museum says the final title drew on Jean Jaurès's writings on the French Revolution, casting working people as a fourth estate. It was painted in years of strikes, protests and workers' demands."],[8,"/study/art-history/post-impressionism/dream-decoration-and-the-wider-world/gattis-hungerford-palace-of-varieties-second-turn-of-katie-lawrence.html","Beyond France","Gatti's Hungerford Palace of Varieties. Second Turn of Katie Lawrence","Walter Sickert","sickert-gatti","Walter Sickert · c. 1888 · Oil on canvas, 38.7 × 47 cm. Yale University Art Gallery, New Haven · A singer in a pale dress stands alone in the footlights, under a gilded arch hung with red. The crowd below sits in shadow. · Degas and Manet had made the café-concert a worthy subject in Paris, and the young painter knew Degas. Here that city theme moves to a London variety stage. · He first showed at the New English Art Club with a picture of this act, and critics called the subject vulgar. · Look at the man in a bow tie in the front row, facing out while the rest watch the stage. · He sketched in the halls almost nightly, and paintings like this grew from those sketches. · Yale acquired it through the Mary Gertrude Abbey Fund. · The singer was a popular star of the halls, later known for making Daisy Bell a hit. Her name survives mostly through his pictures of her."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/barbizon-and-the-open-air.html","Barbizon and the Open Air · Overview","Barbizon and the Open Air","","corot-forest-of-fontainebleau","Landscape painters around Fontainebleau and the Oise, 1830s–1860s · Beginning before Realism had a name, a group of landscape painters worked from close study in the forest near the village of Barbizon, south of Paris, and along the Oise to the north. They painted real French places, and their example opened the way for painting ordinary life. · Corot's forest and a remembered lake · Rousseau, Diaz and Daubigny: named places, real light · A State-commissioned farm scene · A photographer in the same woods · Camille Corot · Forest of Fontainebleau · 1834"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/forest-of-fontainebleau.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Forest of Fontainebleau","Camille Corot","corot-forest-of-fontainebleau","Camille Corot · 1834 · Oil on canvas, 175.6 × 242.6 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A large forest view, painted for the Salon of 1834 from sketches made outdoors. · It was still framed as a historic landscape: viewers took the woman reading in the foreground for Mary Magdalene. · The National Gallery notes the \"clarity and freshness of vision\" that set Corot apart from formula landscapes. · The forest, not the small figure, holds the eye. · Salon · the official annual or biennial art exhibition in Paris, where artists built their reputations. · Historic landscape · a landscape with a story from history, myth or the Bible, a category meant to raise landscape's status."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/souvenir-of-mortefontaine.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Souvenir of Mortefontaine","Camille Corot","corot-souvenir-of-mortefontaine","Camille Corot · about 1864 · Oil on canvas, 65 × 89 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A silvery lake scene recalled rather than recorded; the title means a remembrance. · Napoleon III bought it at the Salon of 1864; it entered the Louvre in 1889. · Look at the feathery trees and soft gray-green light, Corot's late manner. · It shows the road Realism did not take: a dreamy, remembered landscape, the kind of official taste Realism pushed against."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/sunset-from-the-forest-of-fontainebleau.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Sunset from the Forest of Fontainebleau","Théodore Rousseau","rousseau-sunset-fontainebleau","Théodore Rousseau · 1848 · Oil on paper over black chalk or conté crayon, laid down on canvas, 32.2 × 45.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A small study of the setting sun through the trees, with cattle near water. · Rousseau settled at Barbizon and was a leading figure of the group. · It is painted on paper over a chalk drawing, a small, quick format suited to recording light. · Its subject is one hour of light in one real place, not an ideal or a story. · Barbizon school · landscape painters, among them Rousseau, Diaz, Daubigny and Troyon, who worked in and around the Forest of Fontainebleau from the 1830s."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-edge-of-the-forest-at-les-monts-girard-fontainebleau.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","The Edge of the Forest at Les Monts-Girard, Fontainebleau","Narcisse Diaz de la Peña","diaz-edge-of-forest","Narcisse Diaz de la Peña · 1868 · Oil on canvas, 97.8 × 125.5 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Storm light breaks over dark trees at a named spot in the forest. · Look at the heavy, broken paint in the foliage. · Dark, dense paint gives the trees weight and the light its brightness by contrast."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/washerwomen-at-the-oise-river-near-valmondois.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Washerwomen at the Oise River near Valmondois","Charles-François Daubigny","daubigny-washerwomen-oise","Charles-François Daubigny · 1865 · Oil on wood, 24 × 46 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A wide, quiet river with two small figures doing laundry at the water's edge. · The panel is small, the size of a study, though it is signed and dated. · Valmondois is on the Oise, north of Paris, the river Daubigny painted again and again. · A low horizon gives most of the panel to sky and water."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/oxen-going-to-work-morning-effect.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Oxen Going to Work, Morning Effect","Constant Troyon","troyon-oxen-going-to-work","Constant Troyon · 1855 · Oil on canvas, 262 × 391 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris (on deposit from the Louvre) · Oxen head out to plow at sunrise, painted on a canvas nearly four meters wide. · The State commissioned it in 1854 and showed it at the 1855 Exposition Universelle. · Look at the low sun behind the team and the long shadows thrown toward the viewer. · A farm-animal subject on a canvas nearly four meters wide, at the scale of history painting. · History painting · large pictures of history, religion or myth, ranked highest by the academies."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/carrefour-de-lepine.html","Barbizon and the Open Air","Carrefour de l'Epine","Eugène Cuvelier","cuvelier-forest-scene","Eugène Cuvelier · early 1860s · Salted paper print, 19.3 × 25.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A photograph of bare trees at the Carrefour de l'Épine, a named crossing in the Forest of Fontainebleau. · Cuvelier worked in the circle of Corot and photographed the same woods the painters used. · The Met's Cuvelier study notes that he printed from paper negatives, which give the soft, grainy tone. · The photograph shares the painters' aim: a real spot, recorded without arranging it. · Salted paper print · an early photographic print on paper coated with salt and silver, with a matte surface."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/courbet.html","Courbet · Overview","Courbet","","courbet-painters-studio","The painter who claimed the name Realist · Gustave Courbet, from the village of Ornans in eastern France, painted laborers, villagers and his own friends, often at the size of history painting. Critics attacked him, and he answered with his own exhibition and a manifesto. · The road workers and the village funeral · The great studio allegory · Village girls, a roadside meeting and two young women by the Seine · Landscape and sea from his own region and the coast · Gustave Courbet · The Painter's Studio · 1854–55"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-stonebreakers.html","Courbet","The Stonebreakers","Gustave Courbet","courbet-stonebreakers","Gustave Courbet · 1849 · Oil on canvas. Formerly Gemäldegalerie, Dresden; lost during the Second World War · Two road workers, one too old and one too young for the job, break stones by a roadside. · Shown at the Salon of 1850–51; the Dresden picture is known today from photographs, and a smaller version survives in Winterthur. · In a letter he said the two men he saw by the road were \"the most complete expression of poverty.\" · Their faces are turned away or shaded, so they stand for a kind of work rather than two individuals. · Jonathan Ribner of Boston University contrasts its stiff, piece-by-piece composition with the flowing unity of Millet's figures. · One of the first pictures to show manual labor plainly, without uplift."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/a-burial-at-ornans.html","Courbet","A Burial at Ornans","Gustave Courbet","courbet-burial-at-ornans","Gustave Courbet · 1849–50 · Oil on canvas, 315 × 668 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A village funeral, with Courbet's family and neighbors as the mourners, on a canvas almost seven meters long. · Shown at the same Salon as The Stonebreakers; critics called it vulgar and ugly. · The Musée d'Orsay points to the visible brushwork and deep blacks, applied with brush and palette knife, against academic polish. · Who is being buried is unknown. · Ordinary villagers painted life-size, a scale normally given to religious and mythic subjects, made it the manifesto of the new style."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-painters-studio.html","Courbet","The Painter's Studio","Gustave Courbet","courbet-painters-studio","Gustave Courbet · 1854–55 · Oil on canvas, 361 × 598 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Courbet paints a landscape at the center of a crowded studio. · Its full title calls it a \"real allegory\" of seven years of his artistic life. · On the right are friends and backers, among them Baudelaire reading; a hunter on the left may have Napoleon III's features. · From 1899 to 1919 it hung as the backdrop of a private amateur theater in Paris. · He turns his own studio and career into the subject of a monumental picture."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/theme-the-pavilion-of-realism.html","Courbet · Theme","The Pavilion of Realism","","courbet-burial-at-ornans","In 1855, after the Exposition Universelle jury turned down The Painter's Studio, Courbet built his own pavilion and showed his work there. · He paid for it himself and put it within sight of the official exhibition. The show included The Painter's Studio and A Burial at Ornans, with more than forty works in all. · Its catalogue carried his Realist manifesto, promising subjects from modern life. · In the catalogue preface he wrote that the title of Realist had been imposed on him. · It was a step outside the official system that later independent shows, including the Impressionists' in 1874, took further."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/young-ladies-of-the-village.html","Courbet","Young Ladies of the Village","Gustave Courbet","courbet-young-ladies-village","Gustave Courbet · 1851–52 · Oil on canvas, 194.9 × 261 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Courbet's three sisters, out walking near Ornans; one gives alms to a young cowherd. · Salon critics in 1852 found it crude and mocked the models' plain features and country dress. · X-rays show Courbet enlarged the figures as he worked. · Calling country girls \"demoiselles\", young ladies, blurred class lines, part of what unsettled Paris critics."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-meeting-bonjour-monsieur-courbet.html","Courbet","The Meeting (Bonjour Monsieur Courbet)","Gustave Courbet","courbet-bonjour-monsieur-courbet","Gustave Courbet · 1854 · Oil on canvas, 132 × 150 cm. Musée Fabre, Montpellier · Alfred Bruyas, a collector, and his servant greet Courbet on a country road; the painter has his gear strapped to his back. · Courbet himself reported the nickname \"Bonjour Monsieur Courbet\" in an 1855 letter. · Unlike The Painter's Studio, it was accepted for the 1855 Exposition Universelle, where caricaturists mocked its vanity. · Bruyas gave it to the Musée Fabre in 1868. · Painter and patron meet as equals on a dusty road, with the artist shown as a working traveler."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/young-ladies-on-the-banks-of-the-seine-summer.html","Courbet","Young Ladies on the Banks of the Seine (Summer)","Gustave Courbet","courbet-young-ladies-seine","Gustave Courbet · 1856–57 · Oil on canvas, 174 × 206 cm. Petit Palais, Musée des Beaux-Arts de la Ville de Paris · Two young Parisian women lie on the riverbank on a summer day, one half asleep. · It caused a critical scandal at the Salon of 1857. · The Petit Palais finds its realism in \"the frankness of the faces and postures.\" · Rumpled clothes and unguarded poses, with no classical pretext, carry the picture."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-stream-le-ruisseau-du-puits-noir-vallee-de-la-loue.html","Courbet","The Stream (Le Ruisseau du Puits-Noir; vallée de la Loue)","Gustave Courbet","courbet-stream-puits-noir","Gustave Courbet · 1855 · Oil on canvas, 104 × 137 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A shaded stream in a rocky valley near Ornans, thought to be the first of several views of the site. · Shown at the 1855 Exposition Universelle. · Courbet laid on paint with a palette knife in irregular layers, the National Gallery notes. · Rough paint for rough rock: the handling itself feels like the place."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-wave.html","Courbet","The Wave","Gustave Courbet","courbet-the-wave","Gustave Courbet · 1869–70 · Oil on canvas, 65.6 × 92.4 cm. Städel Museum, Frankfurt am Main · A wave breaks under a stormy sky on the Normandy coast near Étretat. · The Städel says he painted the scene about sixty times between 1865 and 1870. · Look at the thick paint of the foam against the dark water. · Sea and weather alone, with no figures or story, are the whole subject."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/peasants-and-labor.html","Peasants and Labor · Overview","Peasants and Labor","","millet-gleaners","Millet, Bonheur and the painters of rural France, 1849–1884 · After the Revolution of 1848, peasants and farm work became a major subject. Some painters gave farm labor dignity and grandeur; others showed its toll, and were accused of politics or ugliness. · Millet's sower, gleaners, angelus, hoe and rake · Rosa Bonheur's working animals · Breton's and Bastien-Lepage's later naturalism · Jean-François Millet · The Gleaners · 1857"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-sower.html","Peasants and Labor","The Sower","Jean-François Millet","millet-sower","Jean-François Millet · 1850 · Oil on canvas, 101.6 × 82.6 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · At dusk a peasant flings seed as he strides downhill, oxen harrowing the field behind him. · Ribner sees a near-biblical dignity in the sower that the stonebreakers lack. · The MFA quotes a 19th-century critic: he \"seems painted with the earth that he sows.\" · The American painter William Morris Hunt bought it from Millet around 1851. · One field worker given the scale and stride of a hero, painted soon after the 1848 Revolution gave male farm laborers the vote."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-gleaners.html","Peasants and Labor","The Gleaners","Jean-François Millet","millet-gleaners","Jean-François Millet · 1857 · Oil on canvas, 83.5 × 110 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Three women bend to pick up stray grain left after the harvest, while the main harvest goes on far behind them. · Shown at the Salon of 1857, after nearly a decade spent on the subject. · A rider at the right, probably an estate overseer, marks who is in charge. · Ribner notes that the women never look capable of revolt, though some viewers read the picture as subversive. · Three bent figures given the weight of sculpture, not the pathos of charity. · Gleaning · gathering what the harvesters leave behind, a right traditionally allowed to the rural poor."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-angelus.html","Peasants and Labor","The Angelus","Jean-François Millet","millet-angelus","Jean-François Millet · 1857–59 · Oil on canvas, 55.5 × 66 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · A man and woman stop digging potatoes to pray as the evening bell rings. · Millet said the idea came from his grandmother, who stopped work to pray whenever the bell rang. · It became so famous that its sale abroad in 1889 caused an outcry in France; a man slashed it in 1932. · Prayer appears as part of the working day, in a real field with real tools. · Angelus · a Catholic prayer said at morning, noon and evening when a church bell rings."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/man-with-a-hoe.html","Peasants and Labor","Man with a Hoe","Jean-François Millet","millet-man-with-a-hoe","Jean-François Millet · 1860–62 · Oil on canvas, 81.9 × 100.3 cm. J. Paul Getty Museum, Los Angeles · An exhausted laborer leans on his hoe in a stony field full of thistles. · At the Salon of 1863 it caused a storm; Parisian viewers found the man brutish and frightening. · It was read as socialist protest; Millet denied being a socialist or a political agitator. · Millet wrote that he tried to say \"what I saw and felt\" at work in the fields. · No ennobling here: the cost of labor is written on the body."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/woman-with-a-rake.html","Peasants and Labor","Woman with a Rake","Jean-François Millet","millet-woman-with-rake","Jean-François Millet · probably 1856–57 · Oil on canvas, 39.7 × 34.3 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A single field worker rakes hay beside a haystack, the harvest going on behind her. · Millet first used the subject in a woodcut for a popular series on farm work, published in 1853. · It was shown in 1860 at the Martinet gallery, where Courbet and Manet also exhibited. · A subject first made for cheap prints became an easel painting."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/ploughing-in-the-nivernais.html","Peasants and Labor","Ploughing in the Nivernais","Rosa Bonheur","bonheur-ploughing-nivernais","Rosa Bonheur · 1849 · Oil on canvas, 133 × 260 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Two teams of oxen make the first ploughing of fallow land in central France, known as sombrage. · The State commissioned it in 1848; shown at the Salon of 1849, it was praised almost unanimously. · The Musée d'Orsay calls it a hymn to work in the fields. · The animals are studied as closely as portraits, and the land is a real region."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-horse-fair.html","Peasants and Labor","The Horse Fair","Rosa Bonheur","bonheur-horse-fair","Rosa Bonheur · 1852–55 · Oil on canvas, 244.5 × 506.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Grooms lead and ride horses at the Paris horse market on the boulevard de l'Hôpital. · Shown at the Salon of 1853, where it was widely praised; she retouched it in 1855. · The dome of the Salpêtrière shows in the left background. · A working Paris market scene, painted about five meters wide."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-song-of-the-lark.html","Peasants and Labor","The Song of the Lark","Jules Breton","breton-song-of-the-lark","Jules Breton · 1884 · Oil on canvas, 110.6 × 85.8 cm. Art Institute of Chicago · A young peasant woman with a sickle stops at sunrise to listen to a lark. · First shown at the Salon of 1885. · In 1934 it was named \"America's Best Loved Picture\" in a Chicago newspaper contest. · Breton was raised in Courrières, northern France, and painted its fields for much of his career. · A softer, more idealized vision of rural life than Millet's or Courbet's."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/haymaking.html","Peasants and Labor","Haymaking","Jules Bastien-Lepage","bastien-lepage-haymaking","Jules Bastien-Lepage · 1877 · Oil on canvas, 180 × 195 cm. Musée d'Orsay, Paris · Two hay-makers rest in a mown field near his home village of Damvillers. · A great success at the Salon of 1878; the State bought it from his heirs in 1885. · Émile Zola called him \"grandson of Millet and Courbet\" and the picture the masterpiece of naturalism in painting. · The horizon sits near the top and the figures crowd the front edge, a cropping close to photography. · A later generation's naturalism: the workers' exhaustion shown plainly. · Naturalism · a later, more detailed form of Realism; the name Zola used for art that recorded life as observed."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/joan-of-arc.html","Peasants and Labor","Joan of Arc","Jules Bastien-Lepage","bastien-lepage-joan-of-arc","Jules Bastien-Lepage · 1879 · Oil on canvas, 254 × 279.4 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A peasant girl in a farm garden in Lorraine starts up as the saints appear to her. · Shown at the Salon of 1880. · After France lost part of Lorraine to Germany in 1871, Joan became a patriotic symbol. · Critics praised her expression but thought the ghostly saints clashed with the realism of the rest. · A history subject painted as if observed on an ordinary farm."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-city-and-the-street.html","The City and the Street · Overview","The City and the Street","","daumier-third-class-carriage","Daumier, early Manet and the changing face of Paris · In Paris, Realism meant the people of the street and the politics of the day. Daumier drew both for the press; Manet painted the city's poor and passers-by; a photographer recorded streets about to be torn down. · Daumier's political prints · Daumier's paintings of working Paris · Manet's street people, singer and dead bullfighter · Photographing old Paris · Honoré Daumier · The Third-Class Carriage · 1864"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/le-ventre-legislatif-the-legislative-belly.html","The City and the Street","Le Ventre législatif (The Legislative Belly)","Honoré Daumier","daumier-ventre-legislatif","Honoré Daumier · 1834 · Lithograph, image 28.2 × 43.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The government ministers and deputies of 1834, sitting in tiers, each drawn as a recognizable caricature. · Daumier had modeled many of these politicians as small painted clay busts, which curator John Daniel Tilford of the Oglethorpe University Museum of Art describes as models for his lithographs. · The French title puns on the legislative body: ventre means belly. · Satire in the press made current politics a subject for art. · Lithograph · a print drawn in greasy crayon on stone, so it could be printed quickly in large editions for the illustrated press and print series."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/rue-transnonain-april-15-1834.html","The City and the Street","Rue Transnonain, April 15, 1834","Honoré Daumier","daumier-rue-transnonain","Honoré Daumier · 1834 · Lithograph, image 28.6 × 44.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A man lies dead beside his bed, a child crushed under him, in an apartment after soldiers stormed the building and killed people inside. · It records a real event of the April 1834 riots in Paris. · After it appeared in a print-seller's window, Smarthistory reports, police tried to track down copies and confiscated the stone. · No hero and no symbol: one room, one family, as a witness might have found it."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-third-class-carriage.html","The City and the Street","The Third-Class Carriage","Honoré Daumier","daumier-third-class-carriage","Honoré Daumier · 1864 · Oil on canvas, 65.4 × 90.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A nursing mother, an old woman with a basket and a sleeping boy ride on a crowded wooden bench. · The Met says it takes up a lithograph from about ten years earlier. · It is unfinished; look for the grid of squaring lines used to transfer the design. · Daumier showed how ordinary people lived with the new railways and the industrial city."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-laundress.html","The City and the Street","The Laundress","Honoré Daumier","daumier-laundress","Honoré Daumier · about 1863 · Oil on wood, 48.9 × 33 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A washerwoman with a heavy bundle helps a child up the steps from the Seine. · Daumier could see laundresses returning from the river's laundry boats from his studio on the quai d'Anjou. · The paint has decayed, so the final digit of the date can no longer be read. · The figure is a dark mass against a bright city wall, a type of the urban laborer rather than a portrait."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-old-musician.html","The City and the Street","The Old Musician","Édouard Manet","manet-old-musician","Édouard Manet · 1862 · Oil on canvas, 187.4 × 248.2 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Five ragged figures gather around a bearded street violinist on waste ground. · First shown at the Galerie Martinet in Paris in 1863. · His models came from a poor neighborhood at the city's edge, just as Paris was being rebuilt around new boulevards. · Street people painted at a scale the National Gallery says was normally kept for the wealthy and powerful."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/the-dead-toreador.html","The City and the Street","The Dead Toreador","Édouard Manet","manet-dead-toreador","Édouard Manet · probably 1864 · Oil on canvas, 75.9 × 153.3 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A bullfighter lies dead on the ground, painted alone against a plain dark background. · It began as part of a larger picture shown at the Salon of 1864. · After critics and cartoonists mocked that canvas, Manet cut it up; the other piece, The Bullfight, is in the Frick Collection. · A contemporary death treated like a fact, not a drama."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/street-singer.html","The City and the Street","Street Singer","Édouard Manet","manet-street-singer","Édouard Manet · about 1862 · Oil on canvas, 171.1 × 105.8 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · A singer with a guitar steps out of a café, eating cherries from a paper parcel. · Manet saw a singer leave a café and later had Victorine Meurent pose for the figure in his studio. · The MFA quotes Zola praising Manet's ability to \"state frankly what he sees.\" · A chance moment of city life, with an awkward, unguarded gesture."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-in-france/rue-saint-jacques.html","The City and the Street","Rue Saint-Jacques","Charles Marville","marville-rue-saint-jacques","Charles Marville · 1864–67 · Albumen silver print, 30.6 × 27 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A narrow street in the Latin Quarter, photographed from a corner, looking down its length. · From 1862 Marville was the city of Paris's official photographer. · He photographed old streets slated for demolition in Baron Haussmann's rebuilding of the city. · Peeling posters and painted shop signs on the walls: the old street itself is the subject. · Albumen print · a photographic print made with egg white to hold the image, giving fine detail; the usual process from the 1850s to the 1890s."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/britain-and-europe.html","Britain and Europe · Overview","Britain and Europe","","repin-barge-haulers","Realism in Britain, Germany and Russia · Painters across Europe took up modern life and working people, each in their own way: moral storytelling in Britain, close detail in Germany, social witness in Russia. · Britain: work, emigration, poverty and the railway · Germany: the factory, the village church, a mocked debut · Russia: the haulers and the returning exile · Ilya Repin · Barge Haulers on the Volga · 1870–73"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/work.html","Britain and Europe","Work","Ford Madox Brown","brown-work","Ford Madox Brown · 1852–65 · Oil on canvas, 137 × 197.3 cm. Manchester Art Gallery · Navvies dig up a Hampstead street while people of every class pass by. · Brown showed it at his own exhibition in 1865, with a catalogue essay explaining the figures. · The two men at the right are portraits of the writers Thomas Carlyle and F. D. Maurice. · A street scene packed with real types becomes an argument about the value of labor. · Navvy · a laborer digging roads, canals or railways."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-last-of-england.html","Britain and Europe","The Last of England","Ford Madox Brown","brown-last-of-england","Ford Madox Brown · 1852–55 · Oil on panel, 82.5 × 75 cm. Birmingham Museums Trust · A young couple stare grimly ahead as their ship leaves England, the woman holding a baby's hand under her shawl. · The circular picture sits inside a painted gold border carrying its title. · Emigration, a real choice facing many Victorians, painted as a modern drama."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/applicants-for-admission-to-a-casual-ward.html","Britain and Europe","Applicants for Admission to a Casual Ward","Luke Fildes","fildes-casual-ward","Luke Fildes · 1874 · Oil on canvas, 137.1 × 243.7 cm. Royal Holloway, University of London · Homeless men, women and children wait in the snow outside a London night shelter. · It grew from Fildes's 1869 illustration Houseless and Hungry for the weekly The Graphic. · At the Royal Academy in 1874, Royal Holloway says, the painting needed \"a barrier and a police officer\" to protect it from the crowds. · It was bought for Thomas Holloway in 1883. · The homeless poor, unidealized, given the finish of an Academy picture."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-railway-station.html","Britain and Europe","The Railway Station","William Powell Frith","frith-railway-station","William Powell Frith · 1862 · Oil on canvas, 116.7 × 256.4 cm. Royal Holloway, University of London · Crowds board a train at Paddington Station: families, a wedding party, porters and passengers of every kind. · The dealer Louis Flatow bought the painting together with the rights to exhibit and reproduce it; more than 21,000 people came to see it in seven weeks. · At the right, two real, well-known London detectives arrest a man about to board. · Modern public life in all its variety, sold as a popular spectacle."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-iron-rolling-mill-modern-cyclopes.html","Britain and Europe","The Iron Rolling Mill (Modern Cyclopes)","Adolph Menzel","menzel-iron-rolling-mill","Adolph Menzel · 1872–75 · Oil on canvas, 158 × 254 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Workers drag a white-hot iron bloom through the rollers to make a railway rail. · In 1872 Menzel traveled to an ironworks in Upper Silesia to draw the machines and the men. · The Berlin museum notes that no factory owner commissioned it. · The museum calls a factory scene on this scale unprecedented in Europe."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/three-women-in-church.html","Britain and Europe","Three Women in Church","Wilhelm Leibl","leibl-three-women-in-church","Wilhelm Leibl · 1878–82 · Oil on mahogany panel, 113 × 77 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle · A young woman and two older ones sit in a pew in a Bavarian village church, one praying, two reading their prayer books. · Leibl worked on it for some three and a half years inside the church, very close to his models. · Look at the detail: embroidered clothes, rough hands, dust on the carved pew. · Real country women in an upright, altar-like format that honors Holbein; the museum calls it a main work of Realism."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/women-plucking-geese.html","Britain and Europe","Women Plucking Geese","Max Liebermann","liebermann-women-plucking-geese","Max Liebermann · 1871–72 · Oil on canvas, 118 × 172 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Women sit in a dim shed stripping feathers from geese while an old man brings more. · It was the first picture he showed in public, at about twenty-five, and it was mocked for its shabby subject and dark colors. · The sale paid for a journey to Paris, where he saw work by Millet and Courbet for the first time. · He took the broad, dark handling from the Hungarian painter Mihály Munkácsy, whose studio he had visited in 1871."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/barge-haulers-on-the-volga.html","Britain and Europe","Barge Haulers on the Volga","Ilya Repin","repin-barge-haulers","Ilya Repin · 1870–73 · Oil on canvas, 131.5 × 281 cm. State Russian Museum, St Petersburg · Eleven men in harness drag a barge along the riverbank under a hot sky. · Repin first saw haulers on the Neva in 1868 and made two trips to the Volga to study them. · Unlike Courbet's faceless stonebreakers, each hauler is based on a portrait of a real man. · Shown at the Vienna World's Fair in 1873; the Russian Museum says it opened a new era in genre painting. · Labor shown as a long, grinding line of exhaustion across the whole width of the canvas."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/they-did-not-expect-him.html","Britain and Europe","They Did Not Expect Him","Ilya Repin","repin-did-not-expect-him","Ilya Repin · 1884–88 · Oil on canvas, 160.5 × 167.5 cm. State Tretyakov Gallery, Moscow · A political exile walks into his family's sitting room; the maid and the family freeze. · First shown in 1884 with the Wanderers. · Repin kept reworking the exile's face until 1888. · Family and friends posed, which keeps the scene grounded in a real home. · A political subject told through one ordinary moment of surprise. · The Wanderers (Peredvizhniki) · Russian Realist painters who formed in 1870 independent of the Academy, touring their exhibitions from 1871."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/america.html","America · Overview","America","","eakins-gross-clinic","Homer, Eakins and Tanner after the Civil War · American painters applied Realism to the decades after the Civil War: soldiers, schoolboys, a rower, a surgeon at work, a music lesson, and the sea. · Homer's war and its aftermath · Childhood and the sea · Eakins's rowers and surgeons · Tanner's banjo lesson · Thomas Eakins · Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross (The Gross Clinic) · 1875"],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/prisoners-from-the-front.html","America","Prisoners from the Front","Winslow Homer","homer-prisoners-from-the-front","Winslow Homer · 1866 · Oil on canvas, 61 × 96.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Union officer faces three captured Confederate soldiers on a stripped battlefield. · The Met says it was inspired by the courage of Barlow, a Union general and friend of Homer's. · The critic Eugene Benson saw the prisoners as types standing for \"the elements in our Southern society that fomented and fed the rebellion.\" · The war is shown through faces and clothes rather than battle."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-veteran-in-a-new-field.html","America","The Veteran in a New Field","Winslow Homer","homer-veteran-new-field","Winslow Homer · 1865 · Oil on canvas, 61.3 × 96.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A man with his back to us scythes ripe wheat; his Union jacket and canteen lie in the corner. · Finished in the months after Lee's surrender and Lincoln's assassination. · The Met notes the single-bladed scythe also recalls the Grim Reaper. · A soldier's return to farm work, shown plainly, which the Met reads as both grief and hope."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/snap-the-whip.html","America","Snap the Whip","Winslow Homer","homer-snap-the-whip","Winslow Homer · 1872 · Oil on canvas, 30.5 × 50.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Barefoot boys play a rough chain game outside a red country schoolhouse. · This is the smaller of two versions; the larger is at the Butler Institute of American Art. · The Met reads the linked boys as a hopeful image of the reunited nation. · A schoolyard game, observed, becomes a picture of national memory as America grew more urban."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-gulf-stream.html","America","The Gulf Stream","Winslow Homer","homer-gulf-stream","Winslow Homer · 1899, reworked by 1906 · Oil on canvas, 71.4 × 124.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Black sailor lies on the deck of a dismasted boat, sharks circling, a waterspout behind. · The Met notes that the current links the sugarcane on board to the history of transatlantic slavery. · Homer gives no outcome: whether the sailor is rescued is left open."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-champion-single-sculls-max-schmitt-in-a-single-scull.html","America","The Champion Single Sculls (Max Schmitt in a Single Scull)","Thomas Eakins","eakins-champion-single-sculls","Thomas Eakins · 1871 · Oil on canvas, 81.9 × 117.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · His friend Max Schmitt rests his oars on the Schuylkill River in Philadelphia, in clear, raking light. · It is believed to mark Schmitt's racing victory of October 1870. · Eakins put himself in the picture, rowing farther back. · The Met calls it the work that launched his career. · Modern sport and a real place, worked out with careful perspective and light."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/portrait-of-dr-samuel-d-gross-the-gross-clinic.html","America","Portrait of Dr. Samuel D. Gross (The Gross Clinic)","Thomas Eakins","eakins-gross-clinic","Thomas Eakins · 1875 · Oil on canvas, 243.8 × 199.4 cm. Philadelphia Museum of Art and Pennsylvania Academy of the Fine Arts · The surgeon Samuel Gross pauses, scalpel in his bloody hand, to lecture during an operation. · Made for the 1876 Centennial Exhibition; the fair's art jury turned it down, so it hung in a model army hospital. · Many viewers, the Philadelphia Museum of Art says, were repelled by its \"ugly and inartistic realism.\" · In a 2009 lecture, Kathleen Foster related its dark ground and dramatic light to old masters such as Rembrandt and Velázquez. · A modern operation, painted from close knowledge of the clinic, with nothing softened."],[9,"/study/art-history/realism/realism-abroad/the-banjo-lesson.html","America","The Banjo Lesson","Henry Ossawa Tanner","tanner-banjo-lesson","Henry Ossawa Tanner · 1893 · Oil on canvas, 124.5 × 90.2 cm. Hampton University Museum, Hampton, Virginia · An older man helps a boy hold a banjo, cool light from one side and warm firelight from the other. · It was admitted to the Paris Salon of 1894. · The same year Robert C. Ogden, chair of Hampton's board, bought it and gave it to Hampton. · In the same 2009 University of Pennsylvania lecture, Tanner was introduced as Eakins's \"great student.\" · An intimate lesson between generations, observed closely, offered a respectful picture of Black life."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-dark-side-of-reason.html","The Dark Side of Reason · Overview","The Dark Side of Reason","","fuseli-nightmare","Wright of Derby, Fuseli, Blake, Martin, Palmer and Turner, 1768–1835 · In the decades around 1800, British artists turned to dreams, visions and catastrophe, often against the confident science of their age. Some made pictures for London's crowded public exhibitions; others, like Blake, worked mainly for a few patrons and friends. · A forerunner: a science lesson lit by a single candle · Fuseli's bedroom demon and Blake's visionary prints and watercolors · A defiant poet, a moonlit harvest and a city fire as subjects for awe · Henry Fuseli · The Nightmare · 1781"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/an-experiment-on-a-bird-in-the-air-pump.html","The Dark Side of Reason","An Experiment on a Bird in the Air Pump","Joseph Wright of Derby","wright-air-pump","Joseph Wright of Derby · 1768 · Oil on canvas, 183 × 244 cm. National Gallery, London · A lecturer draws the air out of a glass bowl while a household watches a trapped bird struggle for breath. The National Gallery calls it a modern vanitas, a picture about the fragility of life. · First shown at the Society of Artists in London, then again that September for the visiting King of Denmark. · Look at the young couple beside the lecturer, Wright's friends Thomas Coltman and Mary Barlow, absorbed in each other. · A first idea for the composition survives, painted on the back of a Wright self-portrait. · Science becomes drama: candlelight picks faces out of the dark, each reacting differently to the bird's fate. Romantic artists took up this staging of feeling around a scene of reason. · Presented to the National Gallery by Edward Tyrrell in 1863."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-nightmare.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Nightmare","Henry Fuseli","fuseli-nightmare","Henry Fuseli · 1781 · Oil on canvas, 101.7 × 127.1 cm. Detroit Institute of Arts, Detroit · A sleeping woman lies sprawled across a bed while a demon squats on her chest and a horse's head pushes through the curtains. The picture made the Swiss-born painter's name in London. · Shown at the Royal Academy in 1782. In a 2022 lecture, Martin Myrone of the Paul Mellon Centre notes that, unusually for Fuseli, it cited no poem or play. · Look at the woman's head and arm, hanging limp off the edge of the bed toward the floor. · Myrone links the curtained room to fashionable bedroom scenes by painters such as Jean-Baptiste Le Prince; the demon was Fuseli's twist. · The theme is the sleeping mind. Putting dreams and irrational fear on a public exhibition wall was a bold step that made the inner life a fit subject for painting. · First owned by Sir Brooke Boothby; the Detroit Institute of Arts bought it in 1955. · Royal Academy · Britain's royal academy of art, founded in London in 1768, with a yearly exhibition and a school; a separate body from the Society of Artists."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-ancient-of-days.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Ancient of Days","William Blake","blake-ancient-of-days","William Blake · 1794 · Color-printed relief and white-line etching with oil, watercolor, and pen and black ink on paper, plate 23.2 × 16.8 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · A bearded figure leans out of a blazing disk and stretches a pair of compasses down into the dark void. It opens one of the illuminated books Blake wrote, printed and colored himself. · Made as the frontispiece to Europe: A Prophecy; this sheet comes from Copy A. · Look at the wind sweeping the figure's white hair and beard to one side, though he crouches in a still circle of light. · The Tate catalogue reads the design as an answer to the frontispiece of a 1729 edition of Newton's Principia. · Blake invented his own gods. This creator, measuring out the world, belongs to a personal mythology from his prophetic books rather than to church teaching. · Part of the Paul Mellon Collection; kept in the print Study Room and shown on request."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/newton.html","The Dark Side of Reason","Newton","William Blake","blake-newton","William Blake · 1795–c. 1805 · Color print, ink and watercolor on paper, 46 × 60 cm. Tate, London · A muscular nude scientist sits hunched on a rock, drawing with compasses on a sheet at his feet. Blake turns the hero of the Enlightenment into a man shut inside his own diagram. · First shown in public at the Burlington Fine Arts Club, London, in 1876, long after Blake's death. · Look at the murky green setting and the patterned rock, which the Tate catalogue reads as a scene under water. · The pose comes from Michelangelo's Abias in the Sistine Chapel, which Blake knew from an engraving. · Blake's attack on Newton is an early sign of a lasting Romantic distrust of science that reduces the living world to measurement. · Presented to the Tate by W. Graham Robertson in 1939."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-great-red-dragon-and-the-woman-clothed-with-the-sun.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Great Red Dragon and the Woman Clothed with the Sun","William Blake","blake-great-red-dragon","William Blake · c. 1805 · Pen and gray ink with watercolor over graphite, 40.8 × 33.7 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A horned, winged dragon hovers over a woman wrapped in light, who kneels on a dark, rough mass at the foot of the sheet with her arms flung wide. The scene comes from the Book of Revelation, where the dragon waits to devour her newborn child. · Made for Thomas Butts, Blake's loyal patron, as part of a long series of Bible pictures. · Look at the dragon's spread wings, which arch across the top of the sheet like a canopy. · One of four Great Red Dragon subjects Blake painted, each with a different composition. · A watercolor on a Bible theme is given the ambition of history painting. Blake treated a medium then ranked below oil as fit for the grandest visionary subjects. · Given to the National Gallery of Art by Lessing J. Rosenwald in 1943."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-bard.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Bard","John Martin","martin-bard","John Martin · c. 1817 · Oil on canvas, 127 × 101.6 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · From a crag high above a river, a white-bearded harper curses the English army winding below. The scene comes from Thomas Gray's ode of 1757 about the last Welsh bard, who then leaps to his death. · Martin painted another, much larger version, over two meters tall, now in the Laing Art Gallery in Newcastle upon Tyne. · Look at the tiny soldiers at lower left, dwarfed by the cliffs, torrent and peaks that climb out of the top of the frame. · The castle across the river has been identified as Conwy, in North Wales. · A lone poet stands against an army in a sublime mountain setting. The artist as prophet, defying worldly power, became a favorite Romantic hero. · Sublime · The awe mixed with fear felt before something vast or overwhelming, such as mountains, storms or great height."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-harvest-moon.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Harvest Moon","Samuel Palmer","palmer-harvest-moon","Samuel Palmer · c. 1833 · Oil on paper laid on panel, 22.2 × 27.6 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · Farm workers gather and bundle sheaves of grain late into the night under a full moon and a scatter of stars. Palmer made this small picture near the end of his years in the Kent village of Shoreham. · Harvesting by night was common practice at the time, the museum notes, so the scene itself is not invented. · Look at the workers: they show no strain, and seem to move in step, almost dancing. · The oil is laid on in small, thick touches, and the moon, high among the trees at upper left, washes the field in glowing orange-gold. · The countryside becomes a vision of plenty. For Palmer the natural world was a sign of God's handiwork, so an ordinary English farm gains an almost sacred calm."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-burning-of-the-houses-of-lords-and-commons-16-october-1834.html","The Dark Side of Reason","The Burning of the Houses of Lords and Commons, 16 October 1834","Joseph Mallord William Turner","turner-burning-lords-commons","Joseph Mallord William Turner · 1835 · Oil on canvas, 92 × 123.2 cm. Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland · Fire tears through the old Parliament buildings at Westminster, lighting up the Thames and the bridge beyond. Turner painted it the year after the blaze, raising the flames above their real height. · This version looks at the fire from downriver; the other, looking across Westminster Bridge, is in the Philadelphia Museum of Art. · Look at the small craft pushing toward the blaze at lower right; the museum adds that low water at first held up the steam vessels hauling fire-fighting gear. · Loose strokes of color, glinting reflections and drifting smoke leave the buildings and bridge only half defined. · A real disaster becomes public spectacle. The dark mass of onlookers packed along the near bank is part of the subject, so the picture is as much about watching as about the fire. · Bought from Turner by a Leeds collector; bequeathed to Cleveland by John L. Severance."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/goya-witness.html","Goya, Witness · Overview","Goya, Witness","","goya-third-of-may","Francisco de Goya, 1799–1823 · Court painter to the Spanish crown, Goya lived through Napoleon's invasion of Spain and the brutal war that followed. His work moved from royal portraits to prints and private murals that face cruelty, fear and illness head on. · A state portrait at the court of Charles IV · Etched series on folly, war and its civilian cost · Late works made for himself and a friend · Francisco de Goya · The Third of May 1808 · 1814"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-family-of-charles-iv.html","Goya, Witness","The Family of Charles IV","Francisco de Goya","goya-family-of-charles-iv","Francisco de Goya · 1800 · Oil on canvas, 280 × 336 cm. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · The Spanish royal family lines up across the canvas in fashionable dress, jewels and royal orders. Painted soon after his promotion to First Chamber Painter, it shows how close he now stood to the throne. · Painted at Aranjuez and Madrid over one spring and summer, and probably hanging in the Royal Palace the same year. · Look at the centre: the king and queen stand with their two youngest children. · The Prado names two models: Velázquez's Las Meninas and Van Loo's group portrait of Philip V's family. · The Prado praises how clearly each sitter's character comes through. Even in an official state portrait, the royals appear as distinct individuals rather than idealized types. · Palace inventories still record it there in 1814; ten years later it belonged to the Prado."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-sleep-of-reason-produces-monsters-el-sueno-de-la-razon-produce-monstruos.html","Goya, Witness","The Sleep of Reason Produces Monsters (El sueño de la razón produce monstruos)","Francisco de Goya","goya-sleep-of-reason","Francisco de Goya · 1799 · Etching and aquatint; plate 21.2 × 15.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A sleeping artist slumps over his desk while owls and bats crowd in behind him. It became the best-known plate of the Caprichos, an 80-print series of social satire sold as a set. · Before becoming plate 43, the design was probably meant to open the series, judging from a preparatory drawing now in the Prado. · Look at the lynx at lower right: it stays wide awake while the sleeper is swarmed. · Aquatint lays the grainy dark tone behind the swarm of creatures. · Prints let the artist put his own satirical commentary before a wide public. Frederick Ilchman (Museum of Fine Arts, Boston talk, 2014) notes that sueño means both sleep and dream, so the title reads two ways. · Given to the Met by the dealers M. Knoedler & Co. in 1918. · Aquatint · An etching method that uses a dusting of resin on the plate to print areas of tone rather than lines."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-second-of-may-1808.html","Goya, Witness","The Second of May 1808","Francisco de Goya","goya-second-of-may","Francisco de Goya · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 268.5 × 347.5 cm. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · Madrid townspeople attack French cavalry with knives in a crowded street fight. Painted at the war's end, it looks back to the uprising that opened Spain's war against Napoleon's occupation. · Commissioned through the Regency Council; frame invoices billed to the King's Bedchamber suggest the king paid to frame it for the Royal Palace. · Look at the turbaned Mamelukes of Napoleon's Imperial Guard being dragged from their horses. · A truck carrying it crashed in 1938. Work in 2007–08 made the colors bright again and hid the losses, guided by old photographs. · Bodies and blades tangle around the white horse at centre, and nobody in particular leads the fight. Janis Tomlinson (UC Berkeley talk, 2011) reads the Spanish attackers as committing atrocities too, so neither side looks heroic. · Moved from the Royal Palace to the Prado, whose catalogue lists it from 1872."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/the-third-of-may-1808.html","Goya, Witness","The Third of May 1808","Francisco de Goya","goya-third-of-may","Francisco de Goya · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 268 × 347 cm. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · At night a French firing squad shoots Madrid civilians seized after the uprising. Tomlinson (Berkeley) observes that this pair of canvases brings the brutality of the war etchings to the full size of a history picture. · Painted as the companion to the street-fight canvas; the Prado rejects the old story that both were made for street display. · Look at the man in white with raised arms, lit by a lantern and facing soldiers whose faces are hidden. · Broad brushwork and stark light and dark replace careful finish. · The scene honors anonymous townspeople as martyrs. That dignity, granted to people history would not otherwise name, made it a lasting model for later images of political violence. · Registered in the Prado's Large Deposit in 1834; the French writer Théophile Gautier saw it there in 1840."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/and-there-is-nothing-to-be-done-y-no-hai-remedio.html","Goya, Witness","And There Is Nothing to Be Done (Y no hai remedio)","Francisco de Goya","goya-y-no-hai-remedio","Francisco de Goya · 1810, published 1863 · Etching, drypoint, burin and burnisher; plate 14 × 16.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Two bodies lie in front, a firing squad shoots other prisoners behind, and a blindfolded man bound to a post waits his turn. The Met reads it as several moments of one execution in a single scene. · Plate 15 of the Disasters of War; the Real Academia de San Fernando published the first edition long after his death. · Look at the three rifle barrels poking in from beyond the right edge, held by shooters we never see. · The Met notes it was etched on the back of a plate already used for a landscape print. · Killing appears as cold routine, seen low and close among the dead and the next to die: war from the victims' side. Ilchman (MFA Boston) contrasts Jacques Callot's 1633 war prints, which keep such brutality at a distance. · Given to the Met by Mrs. Grafton H. Pyne in 1951."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/i-saw-it-yo-lo-vi.html","Goya, Witness","I Saw It (Yo lo vi)","Francisco de Goya","goya-yo-lo-vi","Francisco de Goya · 1810, published 1863 · Etching, drypoint and burin; plate 15.8 × 23.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Townspeople stream away from a hilltop town while a woman hauls a small child along by the arm. Whatever they are fleeing lies outside the frame. · Tomlinson (Berkeley) notes that the 1863 edition followed the order of the Ceán Bermúdez album of 85 proofs, now in the British Museum. · Look at the two men at left racing ahead, one clutching a bundle, while women and children follow behind. · Fine lines alone build the figures; the pale sky and hillside are left almost bare. · The caption claims eyewitness authority. Tomlinson (Yale University Art Gallery talk, 2016) thinks the artist probably imagined most scenes, though he did see the Madrid famine of 1811–12. · Entered the Met in the same 1951 gift as plate 15."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/saturn-devouring-his-son.html","Goya, Witness","Saturn Devouring His Son","Francisco de Goya","goya-saturn","Francisco de Goya · 1820–1823 · Mixed technique, mural transferred to canvas, 143.5 × 81.4 cm. Museo Nacional del Prado, Madrid · A wild-eyed giant tears at a headless body. The late murals it belongs to shaped the modern image of the artist and, says the Prado, fed into Expressionism, Surrealism and film. · Placed on the ground floor of the Quinta del Sordo, facing an image of Leocadia Zorrilla. · Look at the staring eyes and the darkness that swallows everything except the two bodies. · Chemical analysis found oil in the paint, which is why the Prado calls the technique mixed. · Made for the house he lived in rather than for a client or a public showing, it is art kept for the artist alone. The Prado suggests the god may stand for the fear of losing power. · Baron Émile d'Erlanger bought the house in 1873, had the murals moved to canvas at a heavy loss of paint, and gave them to the Spanish state. · Black Paintings · The later name for the dark murals painted directly on the walls of the Quinta del Sordo in Madrid, near the end of his life in Spain."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/revolution-and-nightmare/self-portrait-with-dr-arrieta.html","Goya, Witness","Self-Portrait with Dr. Arrieta","Francisco de Goya","goya-self-portrait-arrieta","Francisco de Goya · 1820 · Oil on canvas, 114.6 × 76.5 cm. Minneapolis Institute of Art · A doctor props up a gravely ill patient and offers him a glass of medicine. This is the artist's last self-portrait, a gift of thanks to the physician who saved his life. · The doctor was Eugenio García Arrieta, who treated him through an acute, dangerous illness late in 1819. · Look at the dim faces behind the bed; the Minneapolis Institute of Art (Mia) suggests they may be hallucinations. · An inscription along the lower edge calls the doctor a friend and gives the painter's age as 73. · Mia notes that pairing picture and inscription recalls the ex-voto, a devotional offering made in Spain to give thanks after surviving a calamity. Here the painter turns his own brush with death into the subject. · Bought by Mia through the Ethel Morrison Van Derlip Fund."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/painting-the-headlines.html","Painting the Headlines · Overview","Painting the Headlines","","gericault-raft-medusa","Gros, Géricault, Delacroix and Rude, 1804–1836 · This part follows French art from the official painting of Napoleon's reign to the generation that broke with it. It closes with a sculpture carved for a public monument, the Arc de Triomphe. · Gros and a state commission from Napoleon's campaigns · Géricault's soldiers, horses, castaways and a portrait of madness · Delacroix and the revolution of July 1830 · Théodore Géricault · The Raft of the Medusa · 1818–19"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/bonaparte-visiting-the-plague-stricken-in-jaffa.html","Painting the Headlines","Bonaparte Visiting the Plague-Stricken in Jaffa","Antoine-Jean Gros","gros-plague-jaffa","Antoine-Jean Gros · 1804 · Oil on canvas, 523 × 715 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A mosque courtyard serves as a military hospital for the Army of the Orient, and its commander walks among the dying. The picture links the grand style of David's school to the new regime of Napoleon. · Shown at the Salon of 1804. Bonaparte himself prompted the commission, which went through the interior ministry. · Look at his bare hand touching a sore on a patient's chest while an officer covers his nose. · Painted in a studio in the Jeu de Paume at Versailles and signed on a step at the left. · A living commander appears as a near-sacred healer, calm and upright among the dying, while a blind soldier at the right gropes his way toward him. · Paid for by the state in 1805, it sat in storage by 1821, reportedly deteriorating, then hung at the Luxembourg from 1830. · History painting · Large pictures of heroic, religious or historical scenes, ranked highest by the academies. · Salon · The official, juried art exhibition in Paris, where an artist's reputation was made or lost."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-charging-chasseur.html","Painting the Headlines","The Charging Chasseur","Théodore Géricault","gericault-charging-chasseur","Théodore Géricault · 1812 · Oil on canvas, 349 × 266 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A mounted officer of Napoleon's Imperial Guard rides into battle smoke with his sabre drawn. At its first showing, the catalog identified the sitter only by the initials M. D. · Shown at the Salon of 1812–13, then renamed A Hussar Charging when it returned to the Salon in 1814. · Look at the rider glancing back over his shoulder while the horse lunges the other way. · The smoky sky and ground are brushed in loosely, leaving the detail to the uniform and harness. · Energy wins over order: horse and rider are built on a steep, twisting diagonal, caught mid-turn, instead of the steady, balanced pose of earlier equestrian portraits. · Kept by the artist until his death, then owned by the duc d'Orléans, later King Louis-Philippe."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/wounded-cuirassier-leaving-the-field.html","Painting the Headlines","Wounded Cuirassier Leaving the Field","Théodore Géricault","gericault-wounded-cuirassier","Théodore Géricault · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 358 × 294 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A heavy cavalryman leads his horse down a slope on foot, his eyes turned up toward the sky. The canvas is even larger than the charging officer's, yet it shows no combat. · Shown at the Salon of 1814 under the same title it carries today. · Look at the sheathed sabre he leans on like a crutch and the rearing horse he struggles to hold. · The Louvre also holds a related oil sketch for the composition. · Defeat replaces victory: a wounded soldier turns his back on the battle, smoke still rising behind him. Shown in the year of Napoleon's first abdication, it puts doubt and loss where glory used to be. · Kept as a pair with the Chasseur, it entered the Louvre in 1851; the two cost 23,400 francs together at Louis-Philippe's estate sale."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-raft-of-the-medusa.html","Painting the Headlines","The Raft of the Medusa","Théodore Géricault","gericault-raft-medusa","Théodore Géricault · 1818–19 · Oil on canvas, 491 × 716 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Survivors of a French navy shipwreck crowd a makeshift raft and strain toward a sail on the horizon. Of about 150 people put on the raft in 1816, fifteen were alive when rescued. · First shown at the Salon of 1819 under the neutral title Scène de naufrage (Shipwreck Scene). · Look at the bodies: they rise in a pyramid from the dead at the front to a man waving a cloth. · Begun in a rented Paris studio in 1818 and finished in a theater foyer in July 1819. · No king, saint or general appears: a recent disaster and its anonymous victims are given the size of history painting, the most prestigious kind of art. · Shown to paying crowds in London and Dublin in 1820–21, then bought for the Crown at his estate sale in 1824."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-monomaniac-of-envy.html","Painting the Headlines","The Monomaniac of Envy","Théodore Géricault","gericault-monomaniac-envy","Théodore Géricault · c. 1819–22 · Oil on canvas, 72 × 58 cm. Musée des Beaux-Arts, Lyon · An elderly woman in a white cap stares sideways with red-rimmed eyes and a tight mouth. Her name and story are unknown; only the painter's close attention survives. · One of five surviving portraits of mentally ill sitters; the museum treats the story of a doctor's commission as unproven. · Look at the eyes, which avoid ours and fix on something outside the frame. · Painted close up against a plain dark ground, with no setting or props to explain her. · Mental illness becomes a subject for portraiture. The sitter is studied as an individual, not a curiosity, in the years when doctors in Paris were beginning to classify madness. · The Lyon museum bought it in 1908 at the sale of the collector Auguste Chéramy."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-derby-at-epsom.html","Painting the Headlines","The Derby at Epsom","Théodore Géricault","gericault-derby-epsom","Théodore Géricault · 1821 · Oil on canvas, 92 × 123 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Four jockeys race across open turf under a low, heavy sky. Horses were a lifelong subject for the painter, and this small canvas dates from the period of his stay in England. · Known as the Derby of 1821, though the museum says that name is only traditional. · Look at the legs, flung out front and back at once, with every hoof off the ground. · The horses' near-identical strides repeat across the canvas like a frieze, above a plain, low landscape. · Speed becomes a feeling. The flying gallop is physically impossible, as photography later showed, yet it conveys rush and excitement better than an accurate pose would. · Bought for the Louvre in 1866; the museum does not record the seller."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-barque-of-dante.html","Painting the Headlines","The Barque of Dante","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-barque-dante","Eugène Delacroix · 1822 · Oil on canvas, 189 × 241.5 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Two poets cross a river in hell in a small boat while the damned claw at its sides. The National Gallery's Christopher Riopelle says this first Salon picture made its young painter famous almost overnight. · Shown at the Salon of 1822 and bought from it by the royal museums for 2,000 francs. · Look at Dante in his red hood, recoiling, while a calm Virgil steadies him by the hand. · The crowded, tilting boat on dark water owes a clear debt to Géricault's raft, shown three years before. · Literature becomes a source: a scene from Dante's Inferno is painted with Rubens-like color and heavy, twisting muscle instead of the cool, drawn line taught in David's school. · Displayed at the Luxembourg for as long as Delacroix lived; transferred to the Louvre in 1874."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/scenes-from-the-massacres-at-chios.html","Painting the Headlines","Scenes from the Massacres at Chios","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-massacres-chios","Eugène Delacroix · 1824 · Oil on canvas, 419 × 354 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Greek families sit stunned and dying before an Ottoman horseman after the 1822 killings on the island of Chios. The event was only about two years old when the canvas went on view. · Shown at the Salon of 1824, where the booklet sent visitors to newspaper reports of the massacre. · Look at the baby reaching for its dead mother at the lower right. · Painted between January and July 1824, just in time for the Salon jury in August. · The canvas is often read as a plea for French support of the Greek war of independence. There is no single focus or climax; suffering spreads across the whole foreground. · Bought from the artist by the King's Household in 1824 for 6,000 francs; at the Louvre since 1874."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/liberty-leading-the-people.html","Painting the Headlines","Liberty Leading the People","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-liberty-leading","Eugène Delacroix · 1830 · Oil on canvas, 260 × 325 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A bare-breasted woman with the tricolor flag leads armed Parisians over a barricade and the bodies of the fallen. It records the three days of street fighting in July 1830 that toppled King Charles X. · Shown at the Salon of 1831; its other title was simply A Barricade. · Look at the boy with two pistols at her side and the man in a top hat holding a gun. · Photographed in May 2024, just after a cleaning and retouching of the paint surface. · An allegory walks beside real people: Liberty is a symbol, yet the men around her wear the everyday clothes of Parisians from different classes. · The state bought it in 1831, soon stored it and later entrusted it to the artist; it reached the Louvre in 1874. · Allegory · A figure or story that stands for an abstract idea."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-departure-of-the-volunteers-of-1792-la-marseillaise.html","Painting the Headlines","The Departure of the Volunteers of 1792 (La Marseillaise)","François Rude","rude-departure-volunteers","François Rude · 1833–36 · Stone, high relief. Arc de Triomphe de l'Étoile, Paris · A winged figure shouts and brandishes a sword above armed citizens rushing to defend France against foreign armies. It is one of four colossal groups carved for the piers of the Arc de Triomphe. · Commissioned under Adolphe Thiers as a political choice of the July Monarchy; the arch was inaugurated in 1836. · Look below her: a bearded veteran raises his arm in a rallying gesture and hugs a naked young recruit to his side, with an elder at the back offering counsel. · Ancient-style armor and nudity replace 1792 uniforms, so that the struggle reads as universal, not local. · Public sculpture gains a Romantic voice: violent motion and raw emotion take the place of the calm, balanced poses expected of official monuments. · It remains on the arch, which the Centre des monuments nationaux now manages. · Relief · Sculpture that projects from a flat background instead of standing free."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/passion-and-the-exotic.html","Passion and the Exotic · Overview","Passion and the Exotic","","delacroix-death-sardanapalus","Ingres, Delacroix, Chassériau and Barye, 1814–1861 · French painting in these decades split between Delacroix's color and loose handling and Ingres's polished line. Many of the subjects come from North Africa and the Middle East, places artists knew through travel, books or imagination. · Delacroix's 1832 journey to Morocco and what he made of it at home · Bible stories retold through Eastern dress or a storm at sea · Storms, lion hunts and animal combat in paint and bronze · Eugène Delacroix · The Death of Sardanapalus · 1827"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/la-grande-odalisque.html","Passion and the Exotic","La Grande Odalisque","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","ingres-grande-odalisque","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · 1814 · Oil on canvas, 91 × 162 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A nude harem woman lies on cushions and turns to look back over her shoulder. Its cool, exact surface makes it the calm opening to a part full of violent scenes. · Made in Rome for Caroline Murat, Queen of Naples, and signed with the place name Rom; Paris saw it at the Salon of 1819. · Look at the back and the right arm: both run longer than a real body would allow. · The brushstrokes vanish under a smooth, enamel-like finish, the kind of surface the academies prized. · The turban, peacock-feather fan and pipe dress the scene as a harem, but the harem is an invented one; Ingres never traveled to the East. · The Louvre bought it in 1899."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-death-of-sardanapalus.html","Passion and the Exotic","The Death of Sardanapalus","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-death-sardanapalus","Eugène Delacroix · 1827 · Oil on canvas, 392 × 496 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · A defeated Assyrian king watches from his bed as his servants kill his women and horses before his own death. Inspired by a play by Lord Byron, the picture caused a scandal. · A late addition to the Salon of 1827–28, it went on view in February 1828. · Look at the huge red bed, which slides diagonally down toward the viewer. · Delacroix worked on it in the second half of 1827, on canvas from the Haro firm; the Louvre restored it in 2022–23. · Bodies, jewels and animals tumble in every direction, with nothing to anchor the center. Christopher Riopelle of the National Gallery, London, in a 2016 lecture, describes the disorder as running through both the subject and the composition. · Unsold until 1846, it passed through several collections before the Louvre bought it in 1921."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/women-of-algiers-in-their-apartment.html","Passion and the Exotic","Women of Algiers in Their Apartment","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-women-algiers","Eugène Delacroix · 1834 · Oil on canvas, 180 × 229 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Three women rest on the floor of a dim, tiled room while a servant walks out at the right. Glowing reds, greens and golds against shadowed walls hold the eye more than any story. · First shown at the Salon of 1834. · Look at the water pipe on the floor and the slippers kicked off beside it, signs of a slow, private afternoon. · A watercolor study of Arab women from his travel album may have been used for the figures. · His costumes and objects rest on what he saw, but the closed, timeless harem is a European idea of the East. That blend of observation and fantasy runs through much Orientalist art. · The French State bought it at that Salon. · Orientalism · Western art and writing that pictured North Africa and the Middle East, often as exotic, sensual or violent."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-convulsionists-of-tangier.html","Passion and the Exotic","The Convulsionists of Tangier","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-convulsionists-tangier","Eugène Delacroix · 1837–38 · Oil on canvas, 95.57 × 128.59 cm. Minneapolis Institute of Art, Minneapolis · Members of the Aissawa, a Sufi brotherhood, surge through a street in Tangier in a religious trance while townspeople look on. Here North Africa appears as a crowd in motion, not a quiet interior. · During the 1832 mission, he and the Comte de Mornay watched the procession pass from a boarded-up window under the roof. · Look at the raised arms and bowed heads at the center, and the onlookers crowding the rooftops above. · Painted in France about five years after the event. · Riopelle (National Gallery) notes that in Morocco Delacroix made only drawings and watercolors; the paintings came later, from memory, and here he heightened the color of the scene he had glimpsed. · Long known as The Fanatics of Tangier, it was once owned by James J. Hill and came to Mia as a bequest of J. Jerome Hill."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-toilette-of-esther.html","Passion and the Exotic","The Toilette of Esther","Théodore Chassériau","chasseriau-toilette-esther","Théodore Chassériau · 1841 · Oil on canvas, 45.5 × 35.5 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Two attendants help the Biblical heroine Esther prepare to be presented to the Persian king Ahasuerus. This small cabinet picture was painted when Chassériau was still in his early twenties. · Shown at the Salon of 1842. · Look at the pale, cleanly outlined body set between the deep blue robe at left, the red-orange dress at right, and the sky and trees behind. · A pencil study for the painting is also in the Louvre. · Chassériau, a pupil of Ingres, keeps his teacher's firm contour on the body but gives the robes and jewels the strong color linked with Delacroix. The two rival camps meet in one Eastern beauty scene. · Baron Arthur Chassériau bequeathed it to the Louvre in 1934."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/christ-on-the-sea-of-galilee.html","Passion and the Exotic","Christ on the Sea of Galilee","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-christ-galilee","Eugène Delacroix · 1854 · Oil on canvas, 59.8 × 73.3 cm. Walters Art Museum, Baltimore · Wind and waves toss a small boat while frightened disciples fight the sail and their teacher sleeps, a story told in the Gospels. Religious subjects took up much of Delacroix's late career. · It belongs to a group of versions from 1853–54, a time when French Catholic taste favored this episode. · Look at the haloed figure asleep at the left, the only still point in a boat heeled over by the swell. · Signed at the lower right with his name and the short date 54. · The critic Roger Kimball, in a 1998 lecture at the New York Studio School, links the stormy sky in this series to the disciples' crisis of faith. Sea and clouds set the emotional pitch. · William T. Walters bought it through the agent George A. Lucas in 1886; it reached the museum with his son Henry's bequest in 1931."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/the-lion-hunt.html","Passion and the Exotic","The Lion Hunt","Eugène Delacroix","delacroix-lion-hunt","Eugène Delacroix · 1860–61 · Oil on canvas, 76.5 × 98.5 cm. Art Institute of Chicago, Chicago · Hunters on horseback and on foot attack two lions in hilly, grassy country under a heavy, cloudy sky. It is one of several hunt scenes Delacroix returned to in his last years. · First exhibited after his death, at the 1885 Delacroix exhibition at the École des Beaux-Arts, Paris. · Look at the lion leaping onto a fallen horse at the left and the second lion roaring over the men beneath it. · The scene is invented: having never watched such a hunt, he assembled it from earlier studies of terrain, Islamic dress and lions at the zoo. · Strokes stay loose and visible even in manes and faces, so men, horses and lions blur into one tangled mass. The quick handling adds to the violence. · Bought by Mr. and Mrs. Potter Palmer in 1892 and bequeathed to the museum in 1922."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/france/lion-and-serpent-no-1.html","Passion and the Exotic","Lion and Serpent no. 1","Antoine-Louis Barye","barye-lion-serpent","Antoine-Louis Barye · model 1838, cast possibly about 1850 · Bronze (sand cast), 22.9 × 34.3 × 17.1 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A crouching lion pins a snake beneath one forepaw and lowers its head toward it as the snake rears to strike. This tabletop bronze is a reduced version of Barye's large group for the Tuileries Garden in Paris. · The large version stood in the Tuileries from 1835, after a purchase by the French government. · Look at the lion's wrinkled snout, open jaws and bristling mane as it faces the snake. · Issued in an edition by the publisher Émile Martin; the base is signed BARYE. · Animal combat, long a minor subject, here gets the care once kept for heroes and gods. A struggle in nature becomes worthy of serious sculpture. · The museum bought it in 1910 with money from its Rogers Fund."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/britain-weather-and-light.html","Britain: Weather and Light · Overview","Britain: Weather and Light","","constable-hay-wain","Turner, Constable, Barry and Pugin, 1812–c. 1870 · In these decades British painters made sky, storm and changing daylight serious subjects for large exhibition pictures. Constable worked from close study of places he knew, while Turner pushed toward catastrophe, night and the machines of his own time. · Constable's Suffolk, Essex, Hampstead and Salisbury, sketched outdoors and worked up in the studio · Turner's Royal Academy pictures of war, the sea and the railway · A new Parliament built in a medieval style · John Constable · The Hay Wain · 1821"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/snow-storm-hannibal-and-his-army-crossing-the-alps.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Snow Storm: Hannibal and his Army Crossing the Alps","J.M.W. Turner","turner-hannibal","J.M.W. Turner · exhibited 1812 · Oil on canvas, 146 × 237.5 cm. Tate Britain, London · A dark storm cloud curls over the Carthaginian army as it struggles through a mountain pass. The soldiers are tiny, and the weather, not the general, fills most of the canvas. · At the Royal Academy the painter insisted it hang low on the wall and threatened to withdraw it otherwise. · Look at the small orange sun, caught inside the dark arc of cloud. · The catalogue carried verses credited to his own poem Fallacies of Hope, the first time he did this; the poem was probably never finished. · Here the sublime comes from weather, a force no general can command or escape. Critics at the exhibition already praised the picture in exactly those terms. · Part of the Turner Bequest, the paintings he left to the nation, accepted in 1856; at Tate since 1910."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/wivenhoe-park-essex.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Wivenhoe Park, Essex","John Constable","constable-wivenhoe-park","John Constable · 1816 · Oil on canvas, 56.1 × 101.2 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A red-brick house sits beyond a wide lake under a sky of moving cloud, with swans on the water. It is a portrait of a real country estate. · Made for the owner, Major-General Francis Slater Rebow, his first important patron; shown at the Royal Academy in 1817. · Look at the cow at far left and the boat at right, where two men are setting a net. · He stayed on the estate to paint it. To take in more of the park for the family, he added strips at each side and masked the joins with that cow and boat. · The gallery's curator Franklin Kelly notes that he shifted trees and strengthened shadows and reflections. The gallery also stresses the play of light across it. · Kept by the Rebow family, bought by Peter A. B. Widener in 1906, and given to the gallery in 1942."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-hay-wain.html","Britain: Weather and Light","The Hay Wain","John Constable","constable-hay-wain","John Constable · 1821 · Oil on canvas, 130.2 × 185.4 cm. The National Gallery, London · A horse-drawn cart fords a shallow millpond on the river Stour on a summer day. The familiar name came later from his friend Archdeacon John Fisher. · Shown at the Royal Academy as Landscape: Noon; it was well reviewed but did not sell. · Look at the cottage at left, home of the farmer Willy Lott, at Flatford in Suffolk. · Painted in his London studio from earlier studies. In a 2017 gallery talk, the National Gallery's Colin Wiggins points out that the fields are yellower and bluer greens laid over a mid-green layer that shows through. · Ordinary farm work fills a canvas about six feet wide, a size normally kept for history painting. Scale claims serious attention for the native countryside. · The dealer John Arrowsmith took it to the 1824 Paris Salon, where Charles X awarded the painter a gold medal; Henry Vaughan gave it in 1886."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/cloud-study.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Cloud Study","John Constable","constable-cloud-study","John Constable · ca. 1821 · Oil on paper laid on card, 20.3 × 27.3 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · Grey storm clouds pile up over a low strip of land, painted at speed on a small sheet of paper. Earlier catalogues gave it longer titles that also named the landscape and its trees. · Made on Hampstead Heath, outside London, as private study and never meant as a finished work. · Look at the trees, a dark silhouette at lower left, leaving most of the sheet to the sky. · He called this practice skying; notes on the back record the time, the direction he faced, the wind and the weather. · Studies like this taught him how skies actually form and move, from life rather than from older pictures. Wiggins (National Gallery) notes that weather experts can name the cloud types in his paintings. · Came to Yale with Paul Mellon's collection of British art. · Oil sketch · A quick painting in oil made to study a subject or plan a larger work, not to be sold or exhibited."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/salisbury-cathedral-from-the-bishops-grounds.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Salisbury Cathedral from the Bishop's Grounds","John Constable","constable-salisbury-cathedral","John Constable · ca. 1825 · Oil on canvas, 87.9 × 111.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · This is the Met's full-scale study, the same size as the finished picture of 1826 now in the Frick Collection, New York. The design exists in several versions; this one shows the painter working it out. · The finished picture was made for John Fisher, Bishop of Salisbury, uncle of the painter's close friend Archdeacon John Fisher; the commission goes back to 1822. · Look at the small figure of the bishop at lower left, gesturing toward the spire. · As he developed the design, he opened up the canopy of trees and brightened the sky around the spire. · Tall trees arch over the scene and frame the cathedral like a gateway, so nature sets the stage for the medieval building rather than serving as background. · Bequeathed to the Met by Mary Stillman Harkness in 1950."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/hadleigh-castle-the-mouth-of-the-thames-morning-after-a-stormy-night.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Hadleigh Castle, The Mouth of the Thames—Morning after a Stormy Night","John Constable","constable-hadleigh-castle","John Constable · 1829 · Oil on canvas, 121.9 × 164.5 cm. Yale Center for British Art, New Haven · Two shattered towers of a medieval castle stand on a hill above a wide estuary, under clouds still heavy with rain. Light breaks over the distant water, but the foreground stays dark and broken. · Shown at the Royal Academy, his first show there after election as a full Academician that February. · Look at the figure and dog at lower left, small beside the crumbling stone, and a second figure with cows on the slope beyond. · One of his six-footers; he worked out the design first in a full-size study, now at Tate Britain. · Painted in the months after his wife Maria died in November 1828, and its bleak mood is often linked to that grief. A real place becomes a picture of feeling. · Sold from his studio after his death; Paul Mellon bought it in 1961 and gave it to Yale in 1977."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/keelmen-heaving-in-coals-by-moonlight.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Keelmen Heaving in Coals by Moonlight","J.M.W. Turner","turner-keelmen","J.M.W. Turner · 1835 · Oil on canvas, 92.3 × 122.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · At night, workers in small boats load coal into tall sailing ships crowded along a quay. Smoke from the work drifts across the moonlit river. · Painted for Henry McConnel of Manchester and shown at the Royal Academy and the Royal Manchester Institution. · Look at the full moon near the center and the orange fires at right; his initials are on the buoy at lower left. · Keelmen were the boatmen who carried coal out to larger ships in shallow barges called keels. · Hard labor and nature share one river at night, each with its own light, and both receive the same care. · Bought by Peter A. B. Widener in 1910 and given to the gallery with the Widener Collection in 1942."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-fighting-temeraire.html","Britain: Weather and Light","The Fighting Temeraire","J.M.W. Turner","turner-fighting-temeraire","J.M.W. Turner · 1839 · Oil on canvas, 90.7 × 121.6 cm. The National Gallery, London · A pale warship that fought at Trafalgar in 1805 is towed by a small dark steam tug to a shipbreaker's yard. The ship had been sold for scrap in 1838. · He most likely never saw the tow and worked from reports; the picture went to the Royal Academy the next year. · Look at the old ship, the palest shape in the picture, behind the dark tug, with the sunset blazing at right. · In a 2016 gallery talk, the National Gallery's Matthew Morgan points out that the water holds almost no blue and that the sunset paint is thick and heavily worked. · Critics then read it as a lament for the age of sail. Morgan (National Gallery) notes the painter had drawn steamboats with interest since 1815 and treats the meaning as open. · He kept it until his death and called it his darling."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/slave-ship-slavers-throwing-overboard-the-dead-and-dying-typhoon-coming-on.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Slave Ship (Slavers Throwing Overboard the Dead and Dying, Typhoon Coming On)","J.M.W. Turner","turner-slave-ship","J.M.W. Turner · 1840 · Oil on canvas, 90.8 × 122.6 cm. Museum of Fine Arts, Boston · A sailing ship heads into a typhoon while chained bodies sink and struggle in the foreground. The cruelty of the slave trade is shown with no hero and no rescue. · Shown at the Royal Academy with catalogue lines from his own Fallacies of Hope describing the scene. · Look at the lower right: a shackled leg rises from the water among fish and gulls. · The ship itself is small and set back in the middle distance, not the focus of the scene. · Outrage comes through color and weather rather than story detail: blood-red light and a heaving sea make the crime felt rather than described. · John James Ruskin bought it in 1843 as a gift for his son, the critic John Ruskin; the museum bought it in 1899."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/rain-steam-and-speed-the-great-western-railway.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Rain, Steam and Speed – The Great Western Railway","J.M.W. Turner","turner-rain-steam-speed","J.M.W. Turner · 1844 · Oil on canvas, 91 × 121.8 cm. The National Gallery, London · A train rushes toward the viewer across a bridge over the Thames, half dissolved in rain and mist. It is an early and famous image of the railway age. · Shown at the Royal Academy; the painter George Leslie later reported watching him finish it. · Look at the dark diagonal bridge the train is on, not the arched bridge at left: it is Brunel's crossing between Taplow and Maidenhead, in use from 1839. The engine's dark front is a Firefly-class locomotive. · A hare runs ahead of the train, added late in thin paint that has turned nearly transparent; Robert Brandard's 1859 engraving still shows it. · Speed itself is the subject: the engine seems to tear through rain and space. The new railway draws the kind of awe once kept for mountains and storms."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/palace-of-westminster-houses-of-parliament.html","Britain: Weather and Light","Palace of Westminster (Houses of Parliament)","Charles Barry and A.W.N. Pugin","barry-pugin-palace-of-westminster","Charles Barry and A.W.N. Pugin · designed 1836; built 1840–c. 1870 · Architecture, Perpendicular Gothic style. Westminster, London · After the fire of 1834, Parliament was rebuilt as a vast complex on the Thames, keeping the surviving medieval Westminster Hall. Its towers and river front became a symbol of Britain itself. · A commission of 1835 required a Gothic or Elizabethan design; Barry's entry won the 1836 competition from 97 entries. · Look at the river front: rows of narrow vertical panels and pinnacles in late medieval style, with the clock tower at one end. · Barry was the architect; Pugin designed the Gothic detailing and interiors, down to furniture and small metalwork. · The Gothic requirement tied the nation's government to its medieval past rather than to the Greek and Roman models usual for public buildings. Romantic interest in history became a national style. · The Lords first sat in their new chamber in 1847 and the Commons in 1852; E. M. Barry finished the work about 1870. · Gothic Revival · The reuse of medieval Gothic forms, such as pointed arches and pinnacles, in buildings of the 1700s and 1800s."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/germany-and-the-north-nature-as-scripture.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture · Overview","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","","friedrich-monk-by-the-sea","Runge, Friedrich, Kersting and Dahl, 1805–1839 · In Hamburg and Dresden, a handful of painters used portraits and landscapes to think about God, death and the self. Their pictures usually hold few figures, but they were carefully built from studies and loaded with meaning. · Runge's portraits of children and his private symbolic system · Friedrich's lone figures, ruins, ice and moonlight · The circle around him: Kersting's studio portrait and Dahl's Dresden at night · Caspar David Friedrich · The Monk by the Sea · 1808–10"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-hulsenbeck-children.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","The Hülsenbeck Children","Philipp Otto Runge","runge-hulsenbeck-children","Philipp Otto Runge · 1805–06 · Oil on canvas, 131.5 × 143.5 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · Three small children play outdoors on the edge of Hamburg. The Kunsthalle sees the picture as a turning point for the way German painters portrayed the young. · The sitters are three of the five children of Friedrich August Hülsenbeck, a business partner of the painter's brother Daniel. · Look at the horizon: church towers mark Hamburg, seen from the family's house in Eimsbüttel, then outside the city. · The viewpoint is set low, at a child's eye level, so adults look across at the sitters rather than down. · The children stand for stages of awareness, from a baby grasping at things to an older sister keeping watch. Childhood is treated as a state with its own worth, not as adulthood in miniature. · Given to the Kunsthalle in 1862 by Richard Hülsenbeck."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/morning-first-version-small-morning.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Morning (first version, \"Small Morning\")","Philipp Otto Runge","runge-morning","Philipp Otto Runge · 1808 · Oil on canvas, 109 × 85.5 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · Aurora, goddess of dawn, floats above a wide landscape inside an ornamental painted border. Of a planned four-part cycle on the times of day, it is the only part ever carried out in oil. · The cycle began as the painter's own print series, The Times of Day, issued in editions of 1805 and 1807. · Look at the frame: the arabesque border is painted as part of the picture and carries its own imagery. · This is the smaller first version; a larger second version of 1808–09, also in Hamburg, survives only in fragments. · The center carries a cosmic, nature-based meaning and the border a Christian one about the soul rising to God. The painter built this personal language of symbols to renew Christian art. · Given to the Kunsthalle in 1891 by Paul Runge."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-monk-by-the-sea.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","The Monk by the Sea","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-monk-by-the-sea","Caspar David Friedrich · 1808–10 · Oil on canvas, 110 × 171.5 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · One small figure stands on a pale strip of dune under an enormous sky. The Met's Alison Hokanson and Joanna Sheers Seidenstein (curator talk, 2025) note that it shocked viewers in 1810 and still looks ahead of its time. · Shown at the Berlin Academy in 1810. Heinrich von Kleist's newspaper response, adapted from Clemens Brentano, is the best-known reaction to it. · Look at the bands of land, sea and sky: there are no framing trees and no path leading into depth. · About two years of repeated overpainting stripped the composition down and set aside normal perspective. · Emptiness is the subject. With almost nothing to hold the eye, the viewer shares the monk's position before a space too large to measure or explain. · King Friedrich Wilhelm III bought it in 1810 at the request of his son, the crown prince."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/abbey-in-the-oakwood.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Abbey in the Oakwood","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-abbey-oakwood","Caspar David Friedrich · 1809–10 · Oil on canvas, 110.4 × 171 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · Monks carry a coffin through a snowy graveyard toward a ruined Gothic church among bare oaks. The painter Carl Gustav Carus, a friend, rated it among the deepest achievements of modern landscape. · Painted as the companion to The Monk by the Sea; the two were meant to be seen as a pair. · Look at the window: its model was the Eldena abbey ruin outside Greifswald, made more perfect here and fitted with a doorway. · Around 1830 the real ruin was restored to look much like the painted one, possibly under the crown prince's influence. · Faith and death meet here, and the painting works as a meditation on mortality rather than a record of a real place. · The crown prince kept it for about twenty years; it entered the Berlin museums in 1957 from the destroyed royal palace."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/caspar-david-friedrich-in-his-studio.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Caspar David Friedrich in His Studio","Georg Friedrich Kersting","kersting-friedrich-studio","Georg Friedrich Kersting · c. 1812 · Oil on canvas, 53.5 × 41 cm. Alte Nationalgalerie, Berlin · The landscape painter is shown at work in a Dresden room stripped almost bare. It is one of three studio portraits of him by the same artist, now in Hamburg, Berlin and Mannheim. · The two were friends from 1810; Hokanson and Seidenstein (Met) describe their joint sketching trips. · Look at the walls: two palettes, a straightedge and a drawing triangle are all that hang there. Shutters close half of one window and all of the other. · He holds brushes, palette and a mahlstick, a rod that steadies the hand, before a canvas turned away from us. · The bare studio suggests painting from the inner eye, not from the view outside. The sitter held that a painter should paint what he sees within himself. · Bought in 1906 from Johanna Friedrich of Greifswald, a descendant of the sitter, after the Berlin Centenary Exhibition brought it to notice."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/wanderer-above-the-sea-of-fog.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Wanderer above the Sea of Fog","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-wanderer","Caspar David Friedrich · c. 1817 · Oil on canvas, 94.8 × 74.8 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · A man in a dark coat stands on a rocky summit above valleys filled with mist. Hokanson and Seidenstein (Met) call it a textbook case of the Romantic sublime. · Look at the figure: one large person fills the center of an upright canvas, both rare in the painter's work. · The view was put together in the studio: the foreground rock comes from a drawing made in Saxon Switzerland in June 1813, other landforms from studies of 1808. · The painting carries no signature or date, so its year is an estimate. · Because he turns away, we watch a person looking instead of meeting the scene directly, and seeing itself becomes the subject. Who he is cannot be established. · On permanent loan from a Hamburg foundation that bought it in 1970. The museum describes its ownership history as unresolved and a matter of concern. · Rückenfigur · German for a figure seen from behind, placed so that the viewer looks along with it."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/chalk-cliffs-on-rugen.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Chalk Cliffs on Rügen","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-chalk-cliffs","Caspar David Friedrich · 1818 · Oil on canvas, about 90 × 70 cm. Kunst Museum Winterthur, Reinhart am Stadtgarten, Winterthur · Three figures on a grassy clifftop, framed by leaning trees, look over white pinnacles and a calm sea. The museum counts it among the painter's most radical compositions, and among his most colorful and festive. · He and Caroline Bommer married that year; the picture followed their wedding journey, which his brother joined. · Look at the figures: the woman points downward, one man crawls to peer over the edge, and the other stands gazing out to sea. · Depth is almost abandoned. The museum reads the flat zones as life (green ground), death (white cliffs) and the infinite (sea fading into sky). · A private story may sit inside the landscape: the museum suggests the figures could be those three travelers, which would make the view a personal record. · Purchased in 1930; now in the Oskar Reinhart Foundation, whose collection the Winterthur museum shows."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-sea-of-ice.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","The Sea of Ice","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-sea-of-ice","Caspar David Friedrich · 1823–24 · Oil on canvas, 96.7 × 126.9 cm. Hamburger Kunsthalle, Hamburg · Slabs of broken ice pile up into a jagged pyramid under a pale sky. The museum reads the clearing at the top as a hint of hope, or of something beyond this world. · Once called The Wreck of Hope, a name taken from a ship in a lost earlier version. · Look at the right: the dark hull of a crushed ship lies tipped on its side, half swallowed by the ice. · Built up from three small oil sketches of ice breaking up on the Elbe in winter 1820–21, all now in Hamburg. · Nature's force overwhelms human effort, and the ice is heaped up like a monument. The museum names William Edward Parry's Arctic voyages as a possible source and notes readings from religious to political. · Acquired by the Kunsthalle in 1905, the same year as one of the ice sketches."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/two-men-contemplating-the-moon.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","Two Men Contemplating the Moon","Caspar David Friedrich","friedrich-two-men-moon","Caspar David Friedrich · c. 1825–30 · Oil on canvas, 34.9 × 43.8 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · On a rocky path beside a gnarled tree, two companions face a crescent in the evening sky. This small canvas is the last of three versions of the composition. · The famous first version, of 1819, is a separate painting in Dresden; a second, of about 1824, is in Berlin. · Look at the sky: the Met's Sabine Rewald (New York Studio School talk, 2002) found it pinker than Berlin's, its paint the thinnest, and this version the closest to Dresden. · She adds that he repeated small pictures, but not large ones with difficult skies. · Rewald (Studio School) reads the composition as partly about friendship: in the Dresden first version the pair are Friedrich and his friend August Heinrich. · Given to the Dresden doctor Otto Friedrich Rosenberg in exchange for medical care. His descendants kept it until 1999; the Met bought it in 2000."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/view-of-dresden-by-moonlight.html","Germany and the North: Nature as Scripture","View of Dresden by Moonlight","Johan Christian Dahl","dahl-dresden-moonlight","Johan Christian Dahl · 1839 · Oil on canvas, 78 × 130 cm. Galerie Neue Meister (Albertinum), Dresden · The city's churches and bridge stand dark against a night sky above the Elbe. The view had been famous since Bellotto's large painting of it in 1748. · The Norwegian-born painter settled in Dresden in 1818 and stayed nearly forty years, apart from trips to Italy and Norway. He was a close friend of Friedrich. · Look at the skyline: the Frauenkirche dome, the Hofkirche tower and the Augustus Bridge. · Light is the real subject: the moon itself, the bright edges of the clouds and the reflections on the river. · The city becomes a Romantic nocturne, or night piece: a well-known place offered as a mood to share, not as a guide to its buildings. · The gallery acquired it in 1937 through the Berlin dealer Karl Haberstock, in exchange for a painting by Nikolaus Gysis."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/america-wilderness-and-nation.html","America: Wilderness and Nation · Overview","America: Wilderness and Nation","","cole-oxbow","Cole, Durand, Leutze, Lane, Church and Bierstadt, 1836–1863 · In the United States, painters used landscape and history scenes to raise questions about the young country's land and its future. This part moves from Cole's moral allegories to the giant single-picture shows of Church and Bierstadt. · Wild land and cleared land as a question about the future · Painted cycles and history scenes that carry a lesson · Quiet harbor light and spectacular views of distant wonders · Thomas Cole · View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm—The Oxbow · 1836"],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/view-from-mount-holyoke-northampton-massachusetts-after-a-thunderstorm-the-oxbow.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","View from Mount Holyoke, Northampton, Massachusetts, after a Thunderstorm—The Oxbow","Thomas Cole","cole-oxbow","Thomas Cole · 1836 · Oil on canvas, 130.8 × 193 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A storm moves away from a sharp bend in the Connecticut River, seen from a mountain lookout. Wild forest lies on one side of the valley and cleared, sunlit farmland on the other. · Look at the tiny figure beside the parasol in the lower foreground: Cole painted himself at his easel, glancing back at us. · The Met also owns a sketch for the composition, made the same year in oil and pencil on board. · Storm clouds and a shattered tree hang over the wild side; haystacks and sheep dot the fields, and the river loops around a flat tongue of farmland. The Met reads this contrast as settlers moving into land then thought of as untamed wilderness. · Given to the Met in 1908 by Mrs. Russell Sage. · Hudson River School · A later name for American landscape painters, about 1825–1875, centered on Cole and on scenery of the Hudson Valley and nearby mountains."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-course-of-empire-destruction.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","The Course of Empire: Destruction","Thomas Cole","cole-course-of-empire-destruction","Thomas Cole · 1836 · Oil on canvas, 99.7 × 161.3 cm. New-York Historical Society, New York · Invaders storm a grand classical city as fires burn and its people flee. It is the fourth stage in a five-picture cycle about how an empire rises and falls. · Commissioned in 1833 by the merchant Luman Reed for his home gallery; the full series was shown in New York in 1836. · Look at the headless giant statue of a warrior, which replaces the goddess that stood there in the previous canvas. · Cole carved his signature, unusually large, into a broken pedestal inside the scene. · Cole took the cycle's title from a 1729 poem by Bishop George Berkeley and its motto from Byron's Childe Harold. The Society also suggests echoes of John Martin's disaster scenes and of New York's Great Fire of 1835. · Reed died before it was finished; his collection later passed to the Society in 1858."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-voyage-of-life-childhood.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","The Voyage of Life: Childhood","Thomas Cole","cole-voyage-of-life-childhood","Thomas Cole · 1842 · Oil on canvas, 134.3 × 195.3 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A baby on a bed of flowers drifts out of a dark cave in a golden boat, guided by an angel. It opens a four-part series that follows one traveler down a river. · Painted in Rome and first shown there in 1842, at the Annual Exhibition of Modern Artists. · Look at the prow: a carved angel figurehead holds up an hourglass. · Cole signed and dated it at lower left, alongside the place where he made it. · Each stage of life, from childhood to old age, gets its own landscape and weather. The National Gallery points out that Cole presents prayer as the traveler's only hope of salvation. · Sold by Cole to a Cincinnati collector; the National Gallery bought it in 1971."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/kindred-spirits.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Kindred Spirits","Asher B. Durand","durand-kindred-spirits","Asher B. Durand · 1849 · Oil on canvas, 111.8 × 91.4 cm. Crystal Bridges Museum of American Art, Bentonville, Arkansas · Two men stand on a rock ledge high in the wooded Catskill Mountains. The picture honors Durand's fellow landscape painter Thomas Cole, whose death in 1848 it commemorates. · Look at the pair: Cole holds a portfolio and talks with the poet William Cullen Bryant. · Durand signed and dated it at lower left; the names Bryant and Cole are lettered at center left. · The tall, upright canvas stacks overhanging trees, cliffs, a distant waterfall and sky, keeping the two men small on their ledge above a rocky stream. · Crystal Bridges presents the scene as a place where people could feel close to God through nature. The museum also notes that Durand partly invented the view rather than copying a single spot."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/washington-crossing-the-delaware.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Washington Crossing the Delaware","Emanuel Leutze","leutze-washington-crossing","Emanuel Leutze · 1851 · Oil on canvas, 378.5 × 647.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · The general stands in a crowded boat as his men push through floating river ice. A turning point of the Revolution is painted on a monumental scale. · It won acclaim in the United States and in Germany, where Leutze was born. · Look at the mixed crew named by Scott Manning Stevens, an Akwesasne Mohawk cultural historian at Syracuse University, in the Met's audio guide: a Native American man at the stern, an African American man, farmers and soldiers. · It hangs in a copy of its original frame, rebuilt from a Mathew Brady photograph. · Stevens says Leutze meant to show the nation at its best, all of society aboard except women, yet calls it not reportage but near propaganda. The Met's narrator calls it accurate but ennobling of the war's violence, including against Native peoples. · Given to the Met in 1897 by John Stewart Kennedy."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/lumber-schooners-at-evening-on-penobscot-bay.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Lumber Schooners at Evening on Penobscot Bay","Fitz Henry Lane","lane-lumber-schooners","Fitz Henry Lane · 1863 · Oil on canvas, 62.6 × 96.8 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · A schooner stacked with lumber rests on a calm bay in Maine as the evening sky turns pink. The tiny crew and a far-off ship leave most of the canvas to sky and water. · First exhibited in 1963–64 in Maine and Its Artists, shown at Colby College, the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, and the Whitney. · Look at the four small sailors on deck working the sails. · Lane used thin, see-through glazes instead of thick paint, so his brushwork is hard to see. · The National Gallery sets this stillness against the more explosive style of Cole and Church. It links Lane's quiet, self-effacing method to Emerson's idea that artists should let nature speak through them. · Given to the National Gallery in 1980 by Mr. and Mrs. Francis W. Hatch, Sr. · Luminism · A later name for American landscapes of about 1850–1875 marked by still water, soft light and hidden brushwork."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/niagara.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Niagara","Frederic Edwin Church","church-niagara","Frederic Edwin Church · 1857 · Oil on canvas, 101.6 × 229.9 cm. National Gallery of Art, Washington · Horseshoe Falls stretches across a long, low canvas, seen from the Canadian side. The National Gallery notes that artists had thought the falls almost unpaintable, and that reviewers praised how Church caught their scale and beauty. · First shown at a New York dealer's gallery in May 1857, titled The Great Fall, Niagara. · Look for one faint rainbow in two pieces: a short arc at upper left, picked up again in the spray in front of the falls. · Church sketched the falls from both the American and Canadian banks the year before. · Church left out any ground at the bottom edge, so the viewer seems suspended above the rushing water rather than standing on a bank. · Toured Britain, where John Ruskin saw it, and U.S. cities; the Corcoran bought it in 1876, and it joined the National Gallery in 2014. · Panorama · A very wide view that sweeps across a scene; in the 1800s also a huge painting shown in its own hall."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/heart-of-the-andes.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","Heart of the Andes","Frederic Edwin Church","church-heart-of-the-andes","Frederic Edwin Church · 1859 · Oil on canvas, 168 × 305.3 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A tropical valley climbs through forest and plain to a snowy summit, showing hot, mild and frozen zones in one view. The German naturalist Alexander von Humboldt's studies of the region inspired it. · It was displayed in a darkened room, lit by gas jets behind silver reflectors, inside a window-like frame that is now lost. · Look closely at the plants; Church advised visitors to bring opera glasses for the detail. · It combines pencil and oil sketches from Ecuador, made on trips in 1853 and 1857, rather than one real view. · Presented on its own as a ticketed show at 25 cents, it drew a reported 12,000 to 13,000 paying visitors a month. · Margaret E. Dows bequeathed it to the Met in 1909."],[10,"/study/art-history/romanticism/landscape-and-the-sublime/the-rocky-mountains-landers-peak.html","America: Wilderness and Nation","The Rocky Mountains, Lander's Peak","Albert Bierstadt","bierstadt-rocky-mountains","Albert Bierstadt · 1863 · Oil on canvas, 186.7 × 306.7 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A Shoshone camp spreads across a valley floor beneath a waterfall and a snow-capped peak in the Wind River Range. Karen Quinn of the Museum of Fine Arts, Boston, notes that its huge scale was meant to overwhelm eastern audiences. · Bierstadt saw the region in 1859, traveling with Frederick W. Lander's government survey. · Look at the center: the waterfall drops into a still lake that mirrors it, just behind the camp. · Painted later in New York, working from sketches made on that trip. · The Met links the picture to Manifest Destiny: it presented the West as a frontier for White settlers to claim. The Met adds that this belief disregarded Indigenous peoples already there, with grave results, including for the Shoshone the painting shows. · Bought by the Met through the Rogers Fund in 1907."],[11,"/study/value-and-light.html","Start","About this site","","","About this site"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/a-short-history.html","A Short History · Overview","A Short History","","merrifield-pl01","From Leonardo to the drawing manual · Painters have studied how light builds form for five centuries: Leonardo in his notes, Caravaggio with one strong light, and later teachers with white models and printed plates. · Leonardo's Relief · Caravaggio's Single Light · The Drawing Manual · Mary P. Merrifield · Plate 1, Handbook of Light and Shade · about 1880"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-leonardos-relief.html","A Short History · History","Leonardo's Relief","","cross-fig21","Leonardo da Vinci treated light and shade as the way a flat picture gains relief: the look of solid, rounded form. · He studied how light falls on faces and folds of cloth and wrote about it at length. · Studies of drapery like this one let him work out light on folds in detail. · In 1897 the drawing teacher Anson Cross reproduced this study to show the care great artists took over light and shade."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-caravaggios-single-light.html","A Short History · History","Caravaggio's Single Light","","caravaggio-calling-of-saint-matthew-detail","Around 1600 Caravaggio lit his figures with one strong light against deep darkness. · He painted from live models posed under that light. Curator Letizia Treves of the National Gallery, London, says the light \"always really underpins the meaning\" in his pictures. · His first public works, in the Contarelli Chapel in Rome, caused a sensation when they were unveiled about 1600. · Visiting artists took the style home. The candlelit night scene grew out of his dark manner, though, as Treves notes, he never painted a candle himself. · He fell out of favor by the mid-1600s and was rediscovered only in the 1900s."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/the-calling-of-saint-matthew.html","A Short History","The Calling of Saint Matthew","Caravaggio","caravaggio-calling-of-saint-matthew","Caravaggio · 1599–1600 · Oil on canvas, about 324 × 340 cm. Contarelli Chapel, San Luigi dei Francesi, Rome · The story is told by the light: it enters from high at the right, beyond the frame, and slants down toward the tax collector Christ is calling. · The figures at the table are caught in the beam; the room around them stays dark. · The painted window, which one source describes as covered with oilskin, adds a diffused light; the same source suggests it recalls a painter's studio window."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-the-drawing-manual.html","A Short History · History","The Drawing Manual","","merrifield-pl01","In the 1800s, drawing manuals taught light and shade with plain white models: cubes, cylinders, spheres and plaster casts. · White models show light and shade without color getting in the way. Mary Merrifield's handbook, published by the London colormen George Rowney & Co., was in its twelfth edition by about 1880; Anson Cross published his in Boston in 1897. · Cross's book prints photographs of one white group under six kinds of light. This course uses them and Merrifield's plates. · Both books build up from a single object under a single light."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/value.html","Value · Overview","Value","","zurbaran-still-life-lemons","The ruler for light and dark · Value is how light or dark a tone is, from white to black. Light and shade are built from it. For value in color, see Color Theory. · The Value Scale · Local Value and Light · High Key and Low Key · Francisco de Zurbarán · Still Life with Lemons, Oranges and a Rose · 1633"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-the-value-scale.html","Value · Concept","The Value Scale","","merrifield-pl02-scale","A value scale runs in steps from the lightest light to the darkest dark. · It gives a painter a ruler for tone. Merrifield also named five broad steps: light, half light, middle tint, half dark and dark. · Teachers use five, seven, nine or ten steps; even spacing matters more than the count. · On any lit object, Merrifield observed, middle tints take up most of the surface; the brightest and darkest spots are small."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-local-value-and-light.html","Value · Concept","Local Value and Light","","cross-fig07","Local value is a surface's own lightness or darkness, apart from the light falling on it; light and shade are added on top. · Cross's photograph shows a white sphere and a red one under the same light. He notes both show the same pattern of light and dark, but the range on the red one is narrower. · Cross pointed out that the lit side of a dark object can look lighter than the shadow side of a light one. · The highlight shows more strongly on the dark sphere, against its deep color. · To judge a tone, ask two questions: how light is this surface, and how much light is it getting?"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/still-life-with-lemons-oranges-and-a-rose.html","Value","Still Life with Lemons, Oranges and a Rose","Francisco de Zurbarán","zurbaran-still-life-lemons","Francisco de Zurbarán · 1633 · Oil on canvas, 62.2 × 109.5 cm. Norton Simon Museum, Pasadena · Light from the left falls on objects of very different local value: pale yellow citrons, darker oranges, a white cup and reflective pewter plates. · Each object keeps its own value in light and in shadow, so the row reads clearly against the dark background. · The museum notes that the orange leaves create a \"rhythm of light and shadow\". · A 2008 cleaning removed opaque varnish; this older photograph probably shows the duller surface from before it."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/theme-high-key-and-low-key.html","Value · Concept","High Key and Low Key","","key-high-low","A picture's key is where most of its values sit: mostly light is high key, mostly dark is low key. · Key does much to set the mood. It is separate from contrast: a high-key picture can still hold a few strong darks, and a low-key one a few bright lights."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/summers-day.html","Value","Summer's Day","Berthe Morisot","morisot-summers-day","Berthe Morisot · about 1879 · Oil on canvas, 45.7 × 75.2 cm. The National Gallery, London, in partnership with Hugh Lane Gallery, Dublin (on long loan to Dublin, 2026–31) · Almost every value here is light, so the picture reads as bright outdoor air. · The dark blue coat at the left is the largest dark in the picture. · The National Gallery says her zigzag strokes catch \"the play of light on the water\"."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/foundations/philosopher-in-meditation.html","Value","Philosopher in Meditation","Rembrandt","rembrandt-philosopher-in-meditation","Rembrandt · 1632 · Oil on wood, 28 × 34 cm. Musée du Louvre, Paris · Most of this small panel is dark. · Broad window light falls on the old man; the fire at the right is a small, separate glow. · Yellowed varnish probably makes the whole picture warmer and dimmer than it was painted."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/light-on-form.html","Light on Form · Overview","Light on Form","","merrifield-pl09","What one light does to a solid · Under one light, every rounded solid shows the same families of light and dark. Once you can name them, you can find them on anything. · The Light and Shadow Sequence · Highlights · The Core Shadow · Reflected Light · Cast Shadows · Flat Planes and Round Forms · Mary P. Merrifield · Plate 9, Handbook of Light and Shade · about 1880"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-the-light-and-shadow-sequence.html","Light on Form · Concept","The Light and Shadow Sequence","","merrifield-pl09","On a round form under one light, values come in a set order: highlight, light, halftone, core shadow, reflected light, and the cast shadow on the ground. · Surfaces facing the light get the most; the rest turn away and get less. Some drawing courses call this light logic. · The order holds on an apple, a head or a fold of cloth. · Merrifield noted that a line through the highlight and the center of a sphere crosses the middle of its darkest band. · Halftone · the grays between full light and shadow, where the surface begins to turn away. · Terminator · the line on a form where light gives way to shadow; also called the shadow edge."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/quince-cabbage-melon-and-cucumber.html","Light on Form","Quince, Cabbage, Melon and Cucumber","Juan Sánchez Cotán","sanchez-cotan-quince-cabbage","Juan Sánchez Cotán · about 1602 · Oil on canvas. The San Diego Museum of Art · Simple fruits and vegetables against black show the sequence plainly. · The cabbage and melon turn from lit fronts into deep shadow on their far sides. · The cut melon shows flat cut faces and the round rind taking light differently."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-highlights.html","Light on Form · Concept","Highlights","","cross-fig16","A highlight is the light source reflected off a surface. · Cross's black bottle shows two highlights because the window it faced was divided in two. · Highlights appear only where a surface reflects the light straight to the eye, so they shift as the viewer moves."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/still-life-with-oysters-a-silver-tazza-and-glassware.html","Light on Form","Still Life with Oysters, a Silver Tazza, and Glassware","Willem Claesz Heda","heda-still-life-oysters","Willem Claesz Heda · 1635 · Oil on wood, 49.8 × 80.6 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Glass, silver and oyster shell each catch the light in their own way. · The Met notes Heda worked \"almost exclusively in shades of gray\". · Small, sharp highlights on the tall glass and the overturned silver cup read as hard, polished surfaces; the oysters' wet sheen is broader and softer."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-the-core-shadow.html","Light on Form · Concept","The Core Shadow","","merrifield-pl07","The core shadow is the darkest band on the form itself, just past the turn into shadow. · In Merrifield's first cylinder this \"extreme dark\" sits a little in from the edge, not at the outline. · Cross stressed careful drawing of \"the dividing line of light and shade\". · Under the same light, a soft core means a gentle curve and a crisp one a sharper turn."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-reflected-light.html","Light on Form · Concept","Reflected Light","","merrifield-pl10","Reflected light is light bounced back into a shadow from nearby surfaces. · It lightens the shadow near the edge and helps the form turn. Cross reminded students that, seen as part of the whole picture, reflected lights \"are not lights but darks\". · Merrifield found it often \"warmer in colour\" than the rest of the object. · Cross warned that students tend to exaggerate it."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-cast-shadows.html","Light on Form · Concept","Cast Shadows","","merrifield-pl03","A cast shadow is the shape an object blocks out on another surface. · It is usually darkest and sharpest where the object meets the surface, and softer farther away. · Its direction and length show where the light is."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/dust-motes-dancing-in-the-sunbeams.html","Light on Form","Dust Motes Dancing in the Sunbeams","Vilhelm Hammershøi","hammershoi-dust-motes","Vilhelm Hammershøi · 1901 · Oil on canvas, 63 × 52.5 cm. Ordrupgaard, Copenhagen · Here the light itself is the subject: an empty room, a window, and shafts of sun. · Ordrupgaard says the light lies \"like a geometrical form across the floor\". · The shafts show in the air, crossing the room diagonally from the window."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-flat-planes-and-round-forms.html","Light on Form · Concept","Flat Planes and Round Forms","","merrifield-pl05-pl07","Flat faces change value mostly at their edges; curved surfaces change value gradually. · Compare Merrifield's Plate 5 prisms, above, with her Plate 7 cylinders, below. · Many painters block in round forms as a few flat planes, then soften the joins."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/light-sources.html","Light Sources · Overview","Light Sources","","cross-figs-09-12","Where the light comes from and what kind it is · The same objects look different under every light. Direction, size and number of sources each change the pattern of light and dark. · Direction of Light · Hard and Soft Light · Backlight · Candlelight · Two Lights at Once · Anson K. Cross · Figs. 9 and 12, Light and Shade · 1897"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-direction-of-light.html","Light Sources · Concept","Direction of Light","","cross-figs-08-10-11","Where the light comes from decides how much of a subject is in light and how much in shadow. · Cross's photographs show one white group lit three ways: from the upper left (top), from behind the viewer (middle) and from in front of the viewer (bottom). · Side light divides each form into light and shadow, which makes its shape easy to read. · Light from behind the viewer flattens form; Cross wrote that its contrasts come mostly from local color."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-hard-and-soft-light.html","Light Sources · Concept","Hard and Soft Light","","cross-figs-09-12","A source that looks small from the subject, such as the distant sun, gives hard light; one that looks large, such as an overcast sky or a nearby big window, gives soft light. · Hard light makes sharp-edged shadows and strong contrast. Soft light wraps around forms and its shadows fade at the edges. · Outdoors, Cross noted, light reflected from every direction makes shadows lighter than indoors."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/sewing-the-sail.html","Light Sources","Sewing the Sail","Joaquín Sorolla","sorolla-sewing-the-sail","Joaquín Sorolla · 1896 · Oil on canvas, 222 × 300 cm. Ca' Pesaro, International Gallery of Modern Art, Venice · Sun filtering through plants scatters hard-edged patches of light and shade across a white sail. · The sunlit patches on the sail are among the lightest values in the picture; the figures, largely in shade, stay readable against them."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-backlight.html","Light Sources · Concept","Backlight","","cross-fig11","Backlight comes from behind the subject, toward the viewer. · Most of what we see is in shadow; light mainly catches the tops and outlines. · Cast shadows run toward the viewer and become large, dark shapes."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-candlelight.html","Light Sources · Concept","Candlelight","","latour-magdalen-smoking-flame-detail","A candle or lamp inside the picture lights nearby things strongly; its light fades quickly with distance. · When the flame is in view it becomes part of the subject, and the picture gets one bright center. · Candlelight was ordinary evening light in Wright of Derby's day, Christine Riding of the National Gallery, London, points out, so such scenes showed something familiar. · A hidden source can still be read from what it lights and the shadows it throws."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/the-magdalen-with-the-smoking-flame.html","Light Sources","The Magdalen with the Smoking Flame","Georges de La Tour","latour-magdalen-smoking-flame","Georges de La Tour · about 1635–37 · Oil on canvas, 117 × 91.8 cm. Los Angeles County Museum of Art · Her face, hand and sleeve, nearest the flame, are brightest; the lap and skull are dimmer, and the legs fade into shadow. · LACMA calls the candle the painting's \"chief symbol and true subject\". · The skull's polish and the books' leather answer the flame in different ways. · His sharp turns from light to dark descend from Caravaggio, whose manner reached him through the painter Jean Le Clerc."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/an-experiment-on-a-bird-in-the-air-pump.html","Light Sources","An Experiment on a Bird in the Air Pump","Joseph Wright of Derby","wright-air-pump","Joseph Wright of Derby · 1768 · Oil on canvas, 183 × 244 cm. The National Gallery, London · One candle the viewer cannot see, hidden behind a glass on the table, lights a ring of faces. · The National Gallery traces colors darkening with distance: the lecturer's robe is \"salmon pink in the candlelight\", then magenta farther off. · Lighting this forceful, the Gallery says, was \"new in British art\"."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/theme-two-lights-at-once.html","Light Sources · Concept","Two Lights at Once","","sargent-carnation-lily-detail","When two sources light a scene, each makes its own lights and shadows, often of different color. · Cross warned that daylight and lamplight together make effects so varied that rules become \"impossible\". · Sort out which surfaces face which source. · A warm source and a cool one make the difference easy to see."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/seeing-light/carnation-lily-lily-rose.html","Light Sources","Carnation, Lily, Lily, Rose","John Singer Sargent","sargent-carnation-lily","John Singer Sargent · 1885–6 · Oil on canvas, 174 × 153.7 cm. Tate, London · Two lights meet here: the fading mauve of dusk and the orange glow of paper lanterns. · Tate notes that a lily or leaves in front of a lantern become back-lit shapes. · The effect lasted only minutes a day: ten by Sargent's own letter, up to twenty-five by a friend's account."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/edges.html","Edges · Overview","Edges","","cross-fig13","Sharp, soft and lost · An edge is the line where one value meets another. How sharp it is helps show form and space. · Hard, Soft and Lost Edges · Sfumato · Anson K. Cross · Fig. 13, Light and Shade · 1897"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-hard-soft-and-lost-edges.html","Edges · Concept","Hard, Soft and Lost Edges","","cross-fig13","A hard edge is sharp, a soft edge is blended, and a lost edge disappears where two values match. · In Cross's photograph the cylinder's outline is lost for a short stretch inside the block's shadow. He wrote that a contour should not be drawn where it cannot be seen. · Losing some edges, Cross argued, is part of \"the charm of the best artistic work\"."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/juan-de-pareja.html","Edges","Juan de Pareja","Diego Velázquez","velazquez-juan-de-pareja","Diego Velázquez · 1650 · Oil on canvas, 81.3 × 69.9 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Compare the edges: crisp at the white collar, soft where the face and beard turn into the hair and where the dark coat meets the dark background. · The white collar is the lightest value in the picture and sets off the face."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-sfumato.html","Edges · Concept","Sfumato","","leonardo-virgin-of-the-rocks-detail","Sfumato, from the Italian for \"to turn to smoke\", blends light into shadow with no visible line. · Cross quoted Leonardo's advice that lights and shadows should meet \"as smoke loses itself in the air\". In a 2019 lecture Caroline Campbell, then of the National Gallery, London, stressed that his lighting is a set of deliberate choices, not a copy of nature. · Cross added that even Leonardo's advice should be tested against nature, not taken literally."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/the-virgin-of-the-rocks.html","Edges","The Virgin of the Rocks","Leonardo da Vinci","leonardo-virgin-of-the-rocks","Leonardo da Vinci · about 1491/2–9 and 1506–8 · Oil on wood, 189.5 × 120 cm. The National Gallery, London · Out of the dark grotto the figures appear by soft degrees; the Virgin's temples and nose have no hard outline. · A shaft of light enters from the upper left. · The far mountains are painted blue, with softened edges, so they look hazy and distant."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/styles-of-light.html","Styles of Light · Overview","Styles of Light","","rembrandt-aristotle","Balanced or extreme · Two terms describe painting built on strong light and shade. They differ in how far they push the contrast. · Chiaroscuro · Tenebrism · Rembrandt · Aristotle with a Bust of Homer · 1653"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-chiaroscuro.html","Styles of Light · Concept","Chiaroscuro","","rembrandt-aristotle-detail","Chiaroscuro, Italian for \"light-dark\", is the balance and pattern of light and shade that models forms. · Lit and shadowed parts are arranged into one design; the turns between them can be gradual or abrupt. · The word also names drawings and prints made in light and dark tones."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/aristotle-with-a-bust-of-homer.html","Styles of Light","Aristotle with a Bust of Homer","Rembrandt","rembrandt-aristotle","Rembrandt · 1653 · Oil on canvas, 143.5 × 136.5 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · Light picks out the face, the white sleeves, the gold chain and the hand resting on the bust; the rest sinks into brown shadow. · The Met describes his \"dark palette and almost sculptural buildup of paint\". · Shadow falls across part of the face, so the eyes stay half hidden."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-tenebrism.html","Styles of Light · Concept","Tenebrism","","gentileschi-judith-detroit-detail","Tenebrism pushes chiaroscuro to an extreme: figures lit sharply against near-black, with few middle tones. · The name comes from the Italian tenebroso, \"dark\". · The darkness hides the setting and pushes the figures toward us."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/judith-and-her-maidservant-with-the-head-of-holofernes.html","Styles of Light","Judith and Her Maidservant with the Head of Holofernes","Artemisia Gentileschi","gentileschi-judith-detroit","Artemisia Gentileschi · about 1623–25 · Oil on canvas, 187.2 × 142 cm. Detroit Institute of Arts · A single candle lights the scene; Judith raises a hand beside the flame, as if to screen its light. · The Detroit Institute of Arts says its theatrical lighting echoes Caravaggio. · Almost everything outside the candle's reach is lost in darkness."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/working-in-value.html","Working in Value · Overview","Working in Value","","cross-figs-22-23","Planning light and dark before color · Draftsmen and painters have practical ways to work in value first: simplify, start from a middle tone, or paint in grays. · Simplifying What You See · Drawing on Toned Paper · Grisaille · Anson K. Cross · Figs. 22 and 23, Light and Shade · 1897"],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-simplifying-what-you-see.html","Working in Value · Concept","Simplifying What You See","","cross-figs-22-23","Simplifying groups many small values into a few large ones. · Cross set a photograph of shingled buildings beside a sepia sketch of the same view: the shingles are only suggested, while the masses of light and shadow remain. · Cross told students to blur their eyes and decide whether each detail reads as part of the light or the dark. · For planning a picture in values, see Color Theory and Composition."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-drawing-on-toned-paper.html","Working in Value · Concept","Drawing on Toned Paper","","prudhon-blue-paper-detail","On toned paper the paper's own color serves as a middle value; the artist works down to the darks and up to the lights. · Black and white chalk on a mid-toned sheet cover the whole range of values. · Artist Pamela Lawton's drawing demonstration for the Met does the same in graphite: tone the whole sheet, erase out the lights, add the darks."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/study-of-drapery.html","Working in Value","Study of Drapery","Pierre-Paul Prud'hon","prudhon-blue-paper","Pierre-Paul Prud'hon · about 1813 · Black and white chalk with stumping on blue paper, 25.5 × 26 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · A study for the hem of a gown falling over a chair: white is kept for the ridges, black for the deepest folds, and the bare paper shows between. · Stumping, or rubbing the chalk to blend it, gives what the Met calls \"a velvety yet radiant quality\"."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/theme-grisaille.html","Working in Value · Concept","Grisaille","","ingres-odalisque-grisaille-detail","Grisaille is painting in shades of a single color, usually gray. · Used as an underpainting, it sets the values before color goes on; Leonardo's figures in the Virgin of the Rocks were built this way, over black and white. · A grisaille can also stand on its own as a finished work."],[11,"/study/value-and-light/light-in-painting/odalisque-in-grisaille.html","Working in Value","Odalisque in Grisaille","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres","ingres-odalisque-grisaille","Jean-Auguste-Dominique Ingres · about 1824–34 · Oil on canvas, 83.2 × 109.2 cm. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York · An unfinished, reduced repeat of his Grande Odalisque of 1814, with the form modeled almost entirely in grays. · The Met notes grisailles often guided engravers making black-and-white prints; this one's purpose is uncertain, and it may have served his teaching studio. · A faint blush of color shows on her back and right ear."]]};
